{"data": {"briefing_version": "1", "job_id": "0f7e0818-5def-49dd-bec9-a16b1b534979", "topic": "The full story behind a prospector's horrifying 1874 ordeal in Colorado's San Juan Mountains \u2014 what really happened, beyond what this video tells", "meta": {"source_count": 11, "independent_source_count": 11, "raw_words": 27100, "quote_verification_rate": 0.9948186528497409, "confidence": "medium", "generated_on": "2026-08-30"}, "read": {"lede": "Read all eleven entries. Here\u2019s what is actually in the pile, told straight. The sources repeat one core story, but they do not provide eleven independent verdicts. The pile contains one narrative video, one archival collection page, several institutional and local-history accounts, a long Wikipedia entry, a forensic news report, and two magazine-style investigations. Nearly all repeat the same account: in February 1874, Alferd\u2014also recorded as Alfred\u2014Packer led five men into the snowbound San Juan Mountains, emerged alone two months later with their belongings and enough money to attract suspicion, admitted eating human flesh, and spent the rest of his life insisting that he killed only Shannon Bell in self-defense. The Wikipedia article repeats that account in grade-level versions, so those repetitions are not additional evidence. The sources offer different emphases: SRC_1 is the fullest chronology; the Colorado State Archives page points to surviving confessions, court files, parole records, governor\u2019s papers, and Supreme Court case numbers; James Starrs supplies the strongest forensic prosecution.", "paragraphs": [{"label": "Expedition", "text": "Packer\u2019s disaster had begun before the cannibalism. In late 1873, roughly twenty prospectors\u2014accounts vary between twenty and twenty-one\u2014left Utah for reported Colorado goldfields. Robert McGrue, leader of the larger prospecting party, led the larger party; Packer joined after the journey began, presenting himself as a guide familiar with the route. Born January 21, 1842, near Pittsburgh, Packer had served briefly in the Union Army before epilepsy caused two honorable discharges, and had worked as a hunter, teamster, ranch hand, miner, and guide. The accounts describe him as badly suited to guiding: he had little experience, got lost, quarreled, lied, stole, and fixated on how much money other men carried. Heavy snow slowed the expedition, provisions ran out, and by January 21, 1874, the prospectors reached Chief Ouray\u2019s Ute encampment near Montrose already eating horse feed and considering the horses themselves. Ouray supplied food and shelter, offered to let them remain until spring, and warned that no Ute would cross the mountains in those conditions. Accounts differ over whether he said they would die or gave directions around the mountains. Most stayed with Ouray or took the safer Gunnison River route, although that party also nearly starved and needed cowhands\u2019 help. Gold fever led six men onward."}, {"label": "The crossing", "text": "On February 9, Packer, Israel Swan, George Noon, Frank Miller, James Humphrey, a prospector who left Ouray\u2019s camp, and Shannon Wilson Bell left Ouray\u2019s camp. Sources differ over whether their provisions were meant to last seven or fourteen days, but all agree they were inadequate. They had little warm clothing, no snowshoes, few matches or no flint, and only a few rifles, a pistol, knives, and a hatchet. Ouray advised following the river; Packer argued that a direct mountain route was faster and led the group upward. The expedition leader, Robert McGrue, took them along the river until the horses could go no farther, unloaded their provisions, and turned back, knowing they lacked enough supplies. Packer later said that within days they had eaten their food, rawhide moccasins, rosebuds, and pine gum. He described cold, hunger, snow blindness, prayer, crying, and a vanished trail. After nine weeks, or sixty-five days, he reached the Los Pi\u00f1os Agency on April 16 carrying a rifle, knife, coffee pot, and other equipment, with live coals in the pot in one account\u2014yet looking far better fed than his story suggested."}, {"label": "Suspicion and confession", "text": "At the agency Packer vomited when first given food. In Saguache he bought a $70 horse, spent roughly $100 at Dolan\u2019s saloon on drinking, gambling, or poker, spent another $78 at a general store, and appeared to possess several wallets despite arriving supposedly penniless. The rifle belonged to an older missing prospector; a knife or pipe belonged to other party members. Preston Nutter, a prospector who rejected Packer\u2019s account, and Oliver Loutsenhizer, men who had stayed with Ouray, rejected Packer\u2019s claim that the others had abandoned him. The discovery of human flesh near the agency caused him to panic, faint, beg for mercy, and provide his first official confession, transcribed by clerk Herman Lauter. Packer gave three major confessions: the May 1874 statement, the March 1883 statement after his capture, and a final account sent in an August 1897 letter to the Rocky Mountain News. In the first, Swan died of hunger and exposure, Humphrey died days later, Miller was killed while Packer gathered wood, Bell shot Noon, and Packer killed Bell; he took money and flesh. In the 1883 version, Bell killed Miller, Humphrey, Noon, and possibly Swan while Packer was scouting, then attacked Packer with a hatchet. Packer said he shot Bell through the stomach, struck him in the head, and lived on the bodies for most of sixty days. His later letters kept that basic account: starvation made Bell violent, Packer killed him in self-defense, and cannibalism sustained him. He called the ordeal \u201cthe midnight horrors of starvation as well as those of utter isolation\u201d and \u201cthe greatest wonder\u201d that he returned to rationality."}, {"label": "Bodies and escape", "text": "In August 1874, John A. Randolph, an illustrator traveling for Harper\u2019s Weekly, found all five bodies together in a pine-shaded gulch near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison, later called Deadman\u2019s Gulch, and sketched the scene for the October 17 Harper\u2019s Weekly issue. A twenty-person recovery party found severe violence: skull injuries consistent with blows, bodies stripped at meaty locations, deliberate removal of flesh, and cloth replacing shoes, suggesting the leather had been eaten. A path led to Packer\u2019s crude shelter, where possessions remained. The remains contradicted his account of men dying one by one in scattered places, though they did not establish the exact sequence. Contemporary observers theorized that Packer killed the men for their belongings, became trapped by the storm, and used their bodies as a \u201cnatural refrigerator.\u201d Before the charges could be upgraded to first-degree murder, Packer escaped the Saguache log-cabin jail with a key, penknife, and food; who helped him remains unknown. He hid for nine years under the name John Schwartze or Swartze. Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon recognized him in Cheyenne, Wyoming, on March 11, 1883, reported him, and helped return him for trial."}, {"label": "1. Murder for money versus killing in extremity.", "text": "The prosecution argued that Packer falsely presented himself as a guide, led the men onto a dangerous route, watched their finances, killed them for money, and invented a survival story. Swan was reportedly carrying cash\u2014his family later said about $6,000\u2014and a valuable Winchester. Packer\u2019s contradictory accounts, spending spree, stolen-looking possessions, and attempted attack on agency clerk Herman Lauter damaged him further. At the second trial, hunters testified that deer, antelope, rabbits, and other game remained in the area, undermining his claim that starvation made cannibalism unavoidable. Packer\u2019s narrower defense, later championed by Polly Pry and museum curator David Bailey, was that the men died or were killed by Bell, Packer shot Bell only to save himself, and he ate the dead because otherwise he would have died. Starrs\u2019s modern middle position is that Packer probably killed the men for their belongings but resorted to cannibalism afterward out of necessity."}, {"label": "2. What the bones prove.", "text": "On July 17, 1989, James Starrs\u2019s team exhumed the five men, who had been buried only about thirteen inches below ground. Starrs declared Packer \u201cguilty as sin,\u201d reporting blunt-force injuries, knife or defleshing marks, and a winter \u201cnatural refrigerator\u201d; UPI said four men had been killed by hatchet blows and the fifth skull was missing. Walter Birkby, the forensic anthropologist who disputed Starrs, the forensic anthropologist assisting him, disagreed: \u201cWe found nothing to corroborate or refute anything Packer said. We\u2019ll never know who did it based on any solid physical evidence.\u201d The fuller account records defensive hacking marks on the forearms of three skeletons, including Bell, and possible bullet holes in pelvic bones, but no definitive conclusion. The evidence supports violence and postmortem butchering more firmly than it proves who attacked whom. It does not show that Packer fired the weapon, that the gun was his, or that the shooting was self-defense. In 1994, David Bailey found an 1862 Colt revolver with three .38-caliber rounds loaded and an accession note saying it came from the crime site. A lead fragment beneath Bell\u2019s remains chemically matched the bullets, and an elk-hip test produced a similar hole. That is the strongest evidence for Packer\u2019s claim that Bell was shot. Grant Houston and Michelle Pierce question the gun\u2019s provenance because visitors routinely found old firearms and bullets near the site. The men were reburied after the exhumation."}, {"label": "Trials, parole, and death", "text": "Packer\u2019s first trial in 1883 ended in a murder conviction and death sentence. The conviction was overturned because applying the later murder law to the 1874 territorial crime violated the applicable legal rules; the Colorado Supreme Court reversed the sentence in October 1885. His second trial, moved to Gunnison amid local prejudice, ended June 8, 1886, in five voluntary-manslaughter convictions and a forty-year sentence, eight years per victim\u2014the longest U.S. prison sentence then reported. The famous political insult about Packer eating \u201cfive of the seven Democrats\u201d is apocryphal or later retelling; the surviving court record gives a conventional judgment ordering him hanged \u201cuntil you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d Packer filed five unsuccessful appeals; the Colorado Supreme Court upheld the sentence June 19, 1899. Governor Charles Thomas paroled\u2014not pardoned\u2014him on February 8, 1901, after he had served eighteen years, on the condition that he not profit from his story. Sources otherwise vary between fifteen and eighteen years and between 1901 and 1905. Packer worked as a guard, ranch hand, or laborer, made horsehair bridles and dollhouses in prison, returned to prospecting, and was later described as modest and charitable. He died April 23, 1907, aged sixty-five, in Jefferson County, Colorado."}, {"label": "Legacy and limits", "text": "Polly Pry, whose real name was Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan and who became the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter, portrayed Packer as a Civil War veteran and victim of a prejudiced system. Her campaign, aided by petitions and supporters including Duane Hatch, helped secure parole, not a pardon; the State Archives preserves the records that could test the campaign in detail. Packer\u2019s letters maintained that he killed only Bell, while women including Rebecca Newman wrote seeking his account and sympathy. After release, some Lake City residents saw a calculating killer; others saw a damaged survivor. Popular culture chose the joke: Alferd Packer restaurants and grills, \u201cHave a friend for lunch,\u201d coffin races, mock trials, a Packer burger, Cannibal! The Musical, and other lore. The strongest sources establish that five men died violently, Packer ate them, he was convicted of manslaughter, and deliberate killing is plausible. They do not definitively reconstruct the killings: no eyewitness survives; the chain of custody, every skull and pelvic injury, and the revolver\u2019s ownership remain unsettled; the Archives page lists confessions and court files without presenting them in full; and no source reconciles Starrs\u2019s certainty with Birkby\u2019s agnosticism. The case therefore answers neither exactly who killed whom nor why."}]}, "players": [{"name": "Denver Post", "role": "Newspaper that publicized Packer\u2019s case and employed him after prison", "body": "The Denver Post published Polly Pry\u2019s reporting and campaign on Packer\u2019s behalf. It petitioned for his release after he had served seventeen years, and Packer later worked there as a guard. Many believed the job came through Pry and the newspaper\u2019s efforts to secure his release.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Polly Pry", "role": "Denver Post reporter who led a campaign for Packer\u2019s release", "body": "Polly Pry partly believed Alfred Packer after hearing his story and visited him at Colorado State Penitentiary in 1899. She used his Army service to portray him as a common man caught in a regrettable situation, then publicized his plight. Packer was paroled in 1901 after a campaign led first by Duane Hatch and later by Pry; he called her his \u201cLiberator.\u201d Polly Pry was the pen name of Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Los Pi", "role": "Variant name for the agency where Packer emerged alone in April 1874", "body": "The supplied material uses \u201cLos Pi\u201d for the Los Pi\u00f1os or Los Pinos Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado. Packer arrived there alone on April 16, 1874, after traveling for 65 days, carrying a Winchester rifle, a skinning knife, and a coffee pot with live coals. Other members of the expedition reached the agency later or were said to have disappeared.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Colorado Supreme Court", "role": "State court associated with the reversal of Packer\u2019s sentence", "body": "The Colorado Supreme Court reversed Packer\u2019s sentence in October 1885 on ex post facto grounds. Packer\u2019s lawyers had argued that Colorado Territory had no murder statute when the deaths occurred, before statehood and before Colorado established the relevant laws. The court therefore recurs in accounts of the legal technicality that affected his prosecution.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Israel Swan", "role": "Prospector identified in Packer\u2019s confessions as the first person to die", "body": "Packer said Israel Swan died from hunger and exposure after the party\u2019s supplies ran out, and that he ate Swan. In another account, Packer said Swan was struck in the head with a hatchet, after which the other men butchered him. Packer\u2019s first signed confession said Swan died first and was eaten by the other five men about ten days after they left camp.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Harper\u2019s Weekly", "role": "National publication that illustrated and reported the 1874 deaths", "body": "John A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly, found the site of the incident in August and reported it to authorities in Lake City. Harper\u2019s Weekly covered the story in its October 17, 1874 issue with Randolph\u2019s illustration, \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d The coverage drew national attention to the crime, although most historians conclude that the illustrator found the remains while on assignment in the area.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Lake Fork", "role": "Area near the Gunnison River where Packer said the party became lost", "body": "Packer said that after traveling with little food, he and Bell camped beside a large lake near hemlock trees. After two weeks, near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, he said he was lost and no longer recognized the surroundings. The area northeast of Lake City where the party became lost is labeled \u201cCannibal Plateau,\u201d and Lake City later developed Packer-related attractions including Al Packer Days and the Packer Burger.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Jefferson County", "role": "Colorado county where Packer died in 1907", "body": "Alfred Griner Packer died in Jefferson County, Colorado, on April 23, 1907, at age 65. The supplied material also connects Packer\u2019s escape, assumed identity, and arrest in Wyoming with his eventual return to Hinsdale County for trial. Accounts of his imprisonment and prosecution recur alongside descriptions of the Hinsdale County Museum\u2019s Packer memorabilia.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Colorado State Archives", "role": "State archival source associated with the Packer case and forensic evidence", "body": "The supplied material links the Colorado State Archives with accounts of Packer\u2019s journey, prosecution, the ex post facto legal issue, his parole in 1901, and objects made or carried during his imprisonment. It also records that analysis at Mesa State College\u2019s Electron Microscopy Facility matched bullet fragments from the burial site to lead from Packer\u2019s old pistol. Packer\u2019s pistol is now displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Los Pinos Indian Agency", "role": "Agency where Packer arrived alone and where the separated party sought help", "body": "Los Pinos Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado, was the destination chosen by the expedition after the party split. Chief Ouray advised the men to follow the Gunnison River north to the agency; the party that followed that route ran out of food, while Packer arrived alone on April 16, 1874. General Charles Adams gave Packer food there, and Preston Nutter later arrived and sought answers in Saguache. Two Ute men also brought pieces of flesh they said came from a human found near the agency.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Colorado State Penitentiary", "role": "Prison where Packer was held before his 1901 parole", "body": "Packer was imprisoned at Colorado State Penitentiary and visited there by Denver Post reporter Polly Pry in 1899. Pry wrote about his desire for freedom and launched a media campaign on his behalf. While in prison, Packer made horsehair bridles; one is displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Hinsdale County Historical Society", "role": "Historical organization associated with the Hinsdale County Museum\u2019s Packer collection", "body": "The supplied material connects the Hinsdale County Historical Society with the Hinsdale County Museum, which travel magazines describe as having the largest known collection of Packer memorabilia. The reported collection includes skull fragments, victims\u2019 clothing buttons, and shackles used when Packer was imprisoned. The material also places Packer\u2019s detention, prosecution, and intended execution in Hinsdale County.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Chief Ouray", "role": "Ute leader who sheltered Packer\u2019s expedition and warned it against winter travel", "body": "Chief Ouray\u2019s camp near Montrose gave the party food and lodging after it arrived on January 21, 1874. He advised the prospectors to remain until spring, warned that the mountain journey would mean certain death in those conditions, and recommended following the Gunnison River to the agency. Packer ignored the warning and left Ouray\u2019s camp with five other prospectors on February 9; the party had only enough food for one grown man for a week.", "source_ids": []}, {"name": "Gunnison River", "role": "Route followed by one branch of Packer\u2019s divided expedition", "body": "After the party split, five men followed Oliver Loutsenhizer along the Gunnison River while Packer and five others headed through the San Juan Mountains. Chief Ouray had recommended the river route, and Bob McGrue guided Packer\u2019s men along it until the horses could go no farther. Two of the five men on that route eventually reached the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, while the river\u2019s tributaries and nearby terrain later figured in accounts of the deaths and the discovery of the remains.", "source_ids": []}], "record": [{"when": "1842", "what": "Alferd G. Packer was born in 1842 and died in 1907.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "This establishes the lifespan attributed to Packer in one account. It provides a chronological boundary for interpreting his military service, western travels, and later notoriety.", "sort_key": 1842.0}, {"when": "1842", "what": "Alferd E. Packer lived from 1842 to 1909 and was also called Alfred.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2"], "context": "This account introduces a variant middle initial and the alternate first name Alfred. The differing name and death year matter because records about Packer may identify him inconsistently.", "sort_key": 1842.0}, {"when": "January 21, 1842", "what": "Alfred Griner Packer was born on January 21, 1842, in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania, U.S.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "This gives a precise birthplace and birth date for the man at the center of the chronology. The Pennsylvania origin places his early life before his later movement west.", "sort_key": 1842.0}, {"when": "1842", "what": "Packer was born near Pittsburgh in 1842.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "This places Packer\u2019s origins in the Pittsburgh area without supplying a precise locality. It connects the subject to western Pennsylvania before the events that brought him west.", "sort_key": 1842.0}, {"when": "1846", "what": "Rebecca A. Newman was born in 1846 in Herkimer, New York.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "This establishes Rebecca A. Newman as a contemporary born in upstate New York. Her presence supplies another personal connection within the chronology, though the entry does not identify her relationship to Packer.", "sort_key": 1846.0}, {"when": "1857", "what": "Polly Pry was born to a wealthy family in 1857.", "source_ids": ["SRC_9"], "context": "This introduces Polly Pry as someone whose family background provided considerable wealth. That status may help explain her significance in the surrounding story, although no connection or later event is supplied here.", "sort_key": 1857.0}, {"when": "1862", "what": "In the 1950s, a rusted 1862 Colt Police Model .32 five-shot revolver was found on Cannibal Plateau with two shots missing.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2"], "context": "The discovery provides a physical object associated with the Cannibal Plateau episode. The missing shots and the weapon\u2019s condition make it suggestive evidence, while leaving its owner and exact use unresolved.", "sort_key": 1862.0}, {"when": "April 22, 1862", "what": "Packer enlisted on April 22, 1862, in Winona, Minnesota, and was assigned to Company F of the 16th Infantry Regiment.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "This marks Packer\u2019s first listed wartime enlistment and places him in a specific infantry company. It establishes a military phase before his later western travels.", "sort_key": 1862.0}, {"when": "1862", "what": "His grave has a veteran's tombstone listing his original regiment in 1862, replacing a marker that was stolen.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The replacement marker preserves a connection between Packer\u2019s grave and his original military service. Its history also shows that the surviving memorial is partly a response to the loss of an earlier record.", "sort_key": 1862.0}, {"when": "June 25, 1863", "what": "Packer enlisted in the 8th Iowa Cavalry Regiment at Ottumwa, Iowa, on June 25, 1863.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "This records a later enlistment in a cavalry regiment, showing that Packer\u2019s military service was not limited to the earlier infantry assignment. The Iowa location also adds another geographic point to his wartime movements.", "sort_key": 1863.0}, {"when": "April 22, 1864", "what": "Packer was discharged a second time on April 22, 1864, because of epilepsy.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The discharge ended this period of service for medical reasons. Epilepsy therefore became a documented interruption in Packer\u2019s military career before the later events in the chronology.", "sort_key": 1864.0}, {"when": "1873-1874", "what": "In winter 1873-1874, the prospect party led by Alferd Packer entered the San Juan region.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "This places Packer\u2019s party in the San Juan region during the harsh winter season. It marks the setting in which the expedition\u2019s later crisis and Packer\u2019s notoriety developed.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "November 8, 1873", "what": "Packer led a party of 21 prospectors that departed from Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 8, 1873.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The departure involved a substantial prospecting group and placed Packer in a leadership role from the outset. Its Utah starting point frames the expedition as a movement toward Colorado\u2019s mining country.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "November 1873", "what": "Packer said that in November 1873 he lied to five men and 15 other miners near Bingham Canyon, Utah Territory, claiming he could guide them to Colorado Territory\u2019s rich precious metal region.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "Packer\u2019s own account presents the expedition as beginning with deliberate deception about his guiding ability. That claim casts doubt on the circumstances under which the group formed and makes his later leadership especially consequential.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "November 20, 1873", "what": "Several months earlier, on November 20, 1873, the party Alfred eventually joined set out from Bingham Canyon, Utah, as part of a group of 20.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"], "context": "This account presents a slightly different size and departure date for the group Packer eventually joined. The discrepancy signals that the chronology contains competing versions of how his participation began.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "November 1873", "what": "The party left in November 1873.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "This supplies only the broad month of departure, leaving the expedition\u2019s exact date and party size unspecified. It nevertheless fixes the beginning of the journey within the late-1873 period.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "1873", "what": "In the fall of 1873, Packer left on an expedition toward San Juan country with 20 prospectors.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "This version emphasizes the expedition\u2019s destination and the number of prospectors accompanying Packer. It supports the broader picture of a sizable party traveling toward the San Juan country before winter conditions closed in.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "1873-1874", "what": "Alfred Packer was accused of cannibalism during the winter of 1873-1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_6"], "context": "The accusation places cannibalism at the center of Packer\u2019s historical notoriety. Its timing links the charge directly to the winter expedition, although the entry does not state what evidence or testimony supported it.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "1873", "what": "After the war, Packer traveled around the country and arrived in Utah in 1873.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "This connects Packer\u2019s arrival in Utah with the period immediately preceding the prospecting expedition. It helps explain how he came to be in the region from which the later journey proceeded.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "1873", "what": "In late 1873, Packer joined a group of 20 prospectors headed to southwestern Colorado.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "This describes Packer as joining an already organized group rather than forming the expedition alone. The destination identifies the journey\u2019s economic purpose and sets up the later events in southwestern Colorado.", "sort_key": 1873.0}, {"when": "August 1874", "what": "In August 1874, a prospecting party discovered the bodies of the five missing men.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The discovery ended months of uncertainty about the missing party and established that the other five men had died. It also provided the physical basis for the accusations that followed Packer\u2019s return.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Packer appealed for a retrial on the grounds that the crime was committed on the Ute Reservation in early 1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The appeal shifted attention from the deaths themselves to the legal setting in which they occurred. Packer\u2019s lawyers argued that the reservation location affected which law and court could govern the case.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "January 21, 1874", "what": "The party reached the Ute encampment near present-day Montrose on January 21, 1874, with their food and supplies exhausted.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "Reaching Ouray\u2019s camp placed the exhausted party among the Utes after its attempt to cross the mountains had failed. Their lack of food and supplies made the group dependent on the winter encampment, but they soon continued onward.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Alferd G. Packer was the prospector accused of committing murder during the winter of early 1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "Packer\u2019s role as the sole surviving member made his account the central explanation for what happened. His later confession transformed an obscure prospecting disaster into a notorious criminal case.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "January 1874", "what": "Packer and five others ventured into the wilderness of the San Juan Mountains in January 1874 against the advice of Chief Ouray.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The decision to continue despite Ouray\u2019s warning set the party on the route toward its winter ordeal. The group\u2019s choice connected the search for gold with the hazards that ultimately separated Packer from the others.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "October 17, 1874", "what": "Harper\u2019s Weekly published an October 17, 1874 issue with an illustration by John A. Randolph of the scene of \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The illustration carried the incident beyond Colorado and presented it to a national readership as a dramatic frontier tragedy. Its publication helped turn the episode into a widely recognizable story rather than a local disappearance.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "February 9, 1874", "what": "Those five men followed Packer into a storm on February 9, 1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "The departure marked the party\u2019s final movement into the storm and away from the relative safety of Ouray\u2019s camp. Afterward, the six men were no longer seen together, and Packer eventually became the only one to emerge.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Packer committed cannibalism during the winter of 1874 after guiding five men into Colorado Territory\u2019s San Juan Mountains during a snowstorm.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "The episode combined a failed winter crossing, the deaths of five companions, and Packer\u2019s account of survival. Cannibalism became the most sensational element of the story and the feature that fixed his name in public memory.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "In 1874, six men set off into Colorado's San Juan Mountains in the middle of winter hoping to reach newly discovered goldfields on the other side.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"], "context": "The men were pursuing the promise of gold by attempting a winter crossing rather than waiting for safer conditions. Their timing placed them in the San Juans when food, supplies, and travel conditions were already becoming critical.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "April 16, 1874", "what": "On the morning of April 16, 1874, men at Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado were eating breakfast when Alfred Packer burst into the mess hall during a snowstorm.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"], "context": "Packer\u2019s sudden arrival at the agency brought the missing-party story directly to officials and witnesses. His condition and account could then be compared with the later discovery of the five bodies.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "February 1874", "what": "Packer was a prospector who became famous after confessing to eating his dead comrades while trapped in the San Juan Mountains in February 1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2"], "context": "His confession supplied the most notorious explanation for how he had survived while the others did not. The admission made him both the principal witness to the disaster and the person suspected of causing it.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "February 9, 1874", "what": "On February 9, 1874, Packer and five other prospectors left the Ute leader Ouray\u2019s winter camp.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2"], "context": "The group\u2019s departure from Ouray\u2019s winter camp began the final stage of the journey. It also showed that the men left the one place where they had reached help and supplies before entering the storm.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Packer was spared the death penalty when his lawyers found that the 1874 murder statutes had been repealed and replaced with a savings clause.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The ruling prevented the state from carrying out the death penalty, but it did not erase the murder conviction or the underlying controversy. A change in the statutes became decisive in determining Packer\u2019s punishment.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Local hunters and officials said the winter of 1874 was severe but the San Juans where Packer's party camped still had abundant deer, antelope, and smaller game.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "Reports of plentiful wildlife complicated any claim that starvation alone explained the deaths or the resort to cannibalism. The availability of game became part of the broader debate over Packer\u2019s conduct in the mountains.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "January 21, 1874", "what": "On January 21, 1874, the party reached Chief Ouray\u2019s camp near Montrose, Colorado, in the Uncompahgre Valley.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The party\u2019s arrival at Ouray\u2019s camp briefly brought it within reach of assistance after its supplies had run out. Its later departure meant that this refuge did not end the expedition\u2019s crisis.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "April 16, 1874", "what": "On April 16, 1874, 65 days after leaving, Packer emerged alone and crossed a frozen lake bed to the Los Pin\u00f3s Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "Packer\u2019s emergence after 65 days made him the sole firsthand survivor of the expedition. His crossing to the agency opened the official investigation and supplied the first account of the party\u2019s fate.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Packer confessed to cannibalism during the winter of 1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The confession made survival itself evidence in the public mind, linking Packer to the deaths of his companions. It also ensured that the case would be remembered for the act he admitted rather than only as a failed expedition.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Only Packer returned in the winter of 1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "Packer\u2019s solitary return left no surviving companion to confirm or challenge his version of events. That imbalance made his changing statements especially important once the bodies were found.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "May 1874", "what": "Packer gave three confessions, in May 1874, March 1883, and a final confession in a letter to the Rocky Mountain News in August 1897.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "The three accounts showed that the story did not remain fixed after Packer\u2019s return. Differences across the confessions kept questions about his actions and credibility alive for decades.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Packer was a prospector who set out for Colorado\u2019s gold fields with five other men across the San Juan Mountains in the winter of 1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The expedition joined the pursuit of Colorado\u2019s goldfields to an exceptionally dangerous winter route. Its outcome made the journey a story of isolation, failed survival, and the disappearance of nearly the entire party.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Packer was the only member of that winter 1874 expedition who survived.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "His survival left no fellow member of the party to offer a competing firsthand account. That absence helped make his later statements and the surrounding theories central to the story.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1874", "what": "Starrs said that over the years since 1874, Packer has been lionized, apologized for, and sympathized with, and that people have tried to have him pardoned posthumously.", "source_ids": ["SRC_7"], "context": "The case never settled into a single lasting public interpretation. Its changing reputation kept Packer\u2019s name in public discussion long after the events and legal proceedings.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "February 1874", "what": "Packer was one of six prospectors who headed into the Colorado mountains in February 1874 after being flush with gold fever and ignoring the dangers of winter.", "source_ids": ["SRC_7"], "context": "The party\u2019s decision to enter the mountains under severe winter conditions established the immediate danger surrounding the expedition. Their gold fever helps explain why warnings and practical risks were discounted.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "April 1874", "what": "Packer became the source of legend after he wandered out of the mountains alone in April 1874.", "source_ids": ["SRC_7"], "context": "His solitary return transformed a failed expedition into a mystery that attracted lasting attention. The unexplained gap between the party\u2019s departure and his return became the basis for competing accounts.", "sort_key": 1874.0}, {"when": "1876", "what": "Alfred's lawyers found a legal technicality because Colorado became a state in 1876 and Colorado Territory had no murder statute under which he could be tried for murder at the time of the crime.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"], "context": "The lawyers\u2019 argument shifted the case from the facts of the deaths to the legal authority of the territory and state. It exposed a procedural weakness that affected how the prosecution could proceed.", "sort_key": 1876.0}, {"when": "1877", "what": "The Hinsdale County Courthouse in Lake City was built in 1877 and is the oldest operating courthouse in Colorado.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "The courthouse provided the setting for the proceedings and links the case to one of Colorado\u2019s oldest continuing civic institutions. Its survival gives the later trial a physical connection to the territory\u2019s early legal history.", "sort_key": 1877.0}, {"when": "1883", "what": "Packer was apprehended in Wyoming in 1883.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The discovery ended years in which Packer remained at large and apparently moved through the region. His capture made a formal reckoning possible nearly a decade after the expedition.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "1883", "what": "Packer was first found guilty of murder in 1883 in Lake City.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The verdict converted a long-standing disappearance and survival story into a criminal prosecution. It also began the sequence of legal challenges and arguments over the precise basis for punishment.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "March 1883", "what": "In March 1883, Packer was discovered in Douglas, Wyoming, living under the alias John Swartze.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "The alias shows that he was living under an assumed identity when authorities or acquaintances located him. His presence in Wyoming supplied the link between his years of freedom and his return to court.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "1883", "what": "Packer told Newman that he was not arrested or censured until 1883 and that he was in the country and seen by all men during all that time.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "Packer presented the intervening years as evidence that he had not been hiding from public view. His claim was meant to challenge the idea that he had successfully evaded notice or punishment.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "1883", "what": "In 1883, one of the original prospectors who had stayed the winter with Chief Ouray ran a shop selling supplies in Cheyenne, Wyoming.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"], "context": "The shopkeeper\u2019s earlier connection to the expedition created a possible route by which knowledge of Packer\u2019s whereabouts could circulate. Cheyenne therefore became part of the chain leading from the old expedition to his discovery.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "March 11, 1883", "what": "On March 11, 1883, Jean \"Frenchy\" Cabazon found Packer in Cheyenne, Wyoming, living under the alias John Schwartze.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The alias and the Cheyenne location tied the discovery to a specific witness, rather than leaving it as an anonymous report. Cabazon\u2019s identification helped bring the dormant case back into motion.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "May 19, 1883", "what": "After seven days of testimony and examinations, he was found guilty of the premeditated murder of Israel Swan and sentenced to death by hanging on May 19, 1883.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The proceedings ended with the harshest available outcome after the court focused on a deliberate killing. The date of execution fixed an immediate consequence, even though later developments prevented that timetable from standing unchanged.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "May 19, 1883", "what": "Court records state that Judge M.B. Gerry sentenced Packer to be confined in Hinsdale County jail until May 19, 1883, then taken by the sheriff to an execution site in Lake City and hanged by the neck until dead.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The order laid out the full procedure for carrying out the punishment, from confinement to removal by the sheriff. It shows that the judgment was intended to be implemented through ordinary county authority.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "1883", "what": "In his 1883 confession, Packer said he went back to the fire, covered the men up, and brought to camp the piece of meat that was near the fire.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "This detail became part of the evidentiary record used to connect Packer\u2019s own account with the treatment of the dead. It also reinforced the grim physical circumstances surrounding the camp.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "1883", "what": "District Court Judge Melville Gerry, the judge who condemned Packer, reportedly told Packer at sentencing in 1883 to stand up because he was a \u201cvoracious, man-eating\u201d man.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "The judge\u2019s language made the sentencing a moral condemnation as well as a legal judgment. It helped shape the memorable image of Packer that survived beyond the courtroom.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "1883", "what": "Packer was found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The verdict attached criminal responsibility to a named victim rather than leaving the prosecution at the level of a general accusation. It illustrates how the broader charge against the group could be narrowed in the courtroom.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "May 19, 1883", "what": "The actual sentence ordered Packer confined in the Hinsdale County jail until May 19, 1883.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The formal order specified imprisonment before the planned execution, clarifying that death was not to occur immediately after judgment. This distinction matters because the sentence involved a defined sequence of custody and execution.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "1883", "what": "In 1883, Packer was charged with murdering all five companions.", "source_ids": ["SRC_7"], "context": "The prosecution initially treated the episode as a series of killings involving the entire party. That broad charge framed the trial as an account of the group\u2019s destruction, not merely a dispute over one death.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "April 9, 1883", "what": "On April 9, 1883, Packer\u2019s trial began in a courtroom filled to capacity.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "The packed courtroom showed that the case had become a major public event rather than a routine local prosecution. The testimony that followed would determine which version of the expedition the court accepted.", "sort_key": 1883.0}, {"when": "October 1885", "what": "In October 1885, the Colorado Supreme Court reversed the sentence on ex post facto grounds.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The ruling removed the original capital punishment on a constitutional ground rather than resolving the underlying accusations. It opened the way for the case to continue through another trial and sentencing.", "sort_key": 1885.0}, {"when": "1885", "what": "Packer\u2019s death sentence was overturned by the Colorado Supreme Court in 1885 because of a \u201cgrandfather clause.\u201d", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "This account gives a different explanation for the reversal than the ex post facto rationale elsewhere in the chronology. The disagreement shows that the legal history of the case was later described in competing ways.", "sort_key": 1885.0}, {"when": "1885", "what": "After a second trial in 1885, Packer received a 40-year sentence.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The second proceeding replaced the death penalty with a lengthy prison term. That outcome kept Packer in custody while leaving the case open to further legal and public attention.", "sort_key": 1885.0}, {"when": "1886", "what": "Packer\u2019s retrial in 1886 found him guilty of five counts of manslaughter.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The retrial narrowed the criminal finding to five manslaughter convictions. It marked a shift away from the earlier capital sentence while still treating the deaths as criminally culpable.", "sort_key": 1886.0}, {"when": "1886", "what": "Packer was eventually sentenced to 40 years in prison after being convicted of manslaughter in a second trial in Gunnison in 1886.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The Gunnison proceeding established the punishment that would define the remainder of Packer\u2019s imprisonment. The repeated reference to the second trial reflects the importance of that later conviction in accounts of the case.", "sort_key": 1886.0}, {"when": "1886", "what": "According to The Case of Alferd Packer by Paul H. Gantt, Gunnison photographer Frank E. Deane took a portrait of Alferd Packer during the 1886 trial.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "The portrait connected Packer\u2019s public image to the courtroom drama surrounding the retrial. Its attribution to a named local photographer also provides a contemporaneous visual record of the proceedings.", "sort_key": 1886.0}, {"when": "1886", "what": "Packer was resentenced in 1886 to 40 years in prison.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "The resentencing confirmed that the reduced punishment was not merely temporary. A forty-year term became the practical consequence of the manslaughter conviction.", "sort_key": 1886.0}, {"when": "1887", "what": "In 1887, Adams relented and forwarded her letter to the State Penitentiary.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "Adams\u2019s change of mind allowed her letter to enter the penitentiary record or correspondence. That act helped preserve a personal intervention in the continuing effort around Packer\u2019s imprisonment.", "sort_key": 1887.0}, {"when": "1898", "what": "In 1898, Frederick Bonfils hired Pry for the Denver Post.", "source_ids": ["SRC_9"], "context": "Pry\u2019s hiring placed her at the Denver Post, where she had a platform to pursue stories beyond routine prison reporting. Her later involvement with Packer\u2019s case grew out of that journalistic position.", "sort_key": 1898.0}, {"when": "1899", "what": "In 1899, Denver Post writer Polly Pry visited Packer at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Canon City.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "The prison visit brought Packer\u2019s story into direct contact with a prominent newspaper writer. It became the point at which his imprisonment attracted the attention that would fuel a public campaign on his behalf.", "sort_key": 1899.0}, {"when": "January 7, 1901", "what": "The retrial eventually led to a pardon for Packer on January 7, 1901.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "The retrial did not end the matter; it eventually became part of the path toward executive clemency. The pardon represented the culmination of sustained pressure after the conviction and imprisonment.", "sort_key": 1901.0}, {"when": "1901", "what": "After enough pressure, the Colorado governor paroled Alfred in 1901.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"], "context": "The governor\u2019s action converted years of advocacy into a release from ordinary imprisonment. It also shows that Packer\u2019s eventual freedom came through political intervention rather than a complete clearing of the case.", "sort_key": 1901.0}, {"when": "February 8, 1901", "what": "Packer was paroled on February 8, 1901, after a campaign led by an old acquaintance named Duane Hatch, who petitioned for nearly a decade before Polly Pry took notice.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "Hatch\u2019s long petitioning supplied the campaign with an older personal connection, while Pry\u2019s attention broadened it through the press. The parole therefore emerged from both private persistence and public advocacy.", "sort_key": 1901.0}, {"when": "1901", "what": "Former Gov. Charles Thomas paroled Packer in 1901.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "This account places the decision directly with a former governor, emphasizing the role of executive authority. It presents the release as a political act rather than the result of another judicial reversal.", "sort_key": 1901.0}, {"when": "1901", "what": "Packer was paroled in 1901 after a Denver Post columnist crusaded on his behalf.", "source_ids": ["SRC_7"], "context": "The newspaper campaign made Packer\u2019s imprisonment a public cause rather than a matter known only through court records. Pry\u2019s writing helped turn sympathy for him into pressure for release.", "sort_key": 1901.0}, {"when": "1901", "what": "Pry met Alfred Packer in 1901 while writing an article on the prison where he was incarcerated.", "source_ids": ["SRC_9"], "context": "The meeting gave Pry firsthand access to the prisoner whose case she would champion. Her article on the prison became the immediate setting in which that relationship began.", "sort_key": 1901.0}, {"when": "1905", "what": "Packer received parole in 1905.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"], "context": "This later parole date conflicts with the earlier accounts placing his parole in 1901. The chronology therefore preserves two different claims about when Packer was released under parole.", "sort_key": 1905.0}, {"when": "1907", "what": "Packer died alone in 1907 in Littleton, Colorado, a suburb of Denver, at age 69.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "His death ended the life of a figure whose case had moved from a capital conviction to imprisonment and parole. The location and description emphasize the isolation of his final years.", "sort_key": 1907.0}, {"when": "1907", "what": "Alfred died in 1907 at age 65.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"], "context": "The stated age differs from the age given elsewhere in the chronology. That discrepancy is part of the surviving uncertainty surrounding basic details of his final years.", "sort_key": 1907.0}, {"when": "1907", "what": "The text says Alferd Packer died in 1907 and that his misspelled name and unique reputation live on.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2"], "context": "The account treats the misspelled name as an enduring part of Packer\u2019s public identity. His reputation outlasted both the legal proceedings and his isolated death.", "sort_key": 1907.0}, {"when": "April 23, 1907", "what": "Alfred Griner Packer died on April 23, 1907, in Jefferson County, Colorado, U.S., at age 65.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "This provides a precise endpoint for Packer\u2019s life and closes the chronology of his personal story. The location also connects his death to the Colorado setting central to his later memorialization.", "sort_key": 1907.0}, {"when": "1907", "what": "Packer died in 1907 in Jefferson County, Colorado.", "source_ids": ["SRC_7"], "context": "This abbreviated account confirms the same final setting while omitting the more specific details. It functions as a concise corroboration of the death record.", "sort_key": 1907.0}, {"when": "1907", "what": "Packer died in 1907 at age 65.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "This version preserves only the age at death, making the biographical endpoint especially compact. Together with fuller accounts, it reinforces the consistency of the reported year.", "sort_key": 1907.0}, {"when": "1926", "what": "Newman died in 1926 at age 80.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11"], "context": "This marks Newman\u2019s death as a later endpoint in the surrounding cast of figures. The age gives the chronology a measure of his longevity without adding further biographical detail.", "sort_key": 1926.0}, {"when": "1931", "what": "The plaque also serves as a memorial to Stanley Weston, a USDA employee who lived from 1931 to 1984.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The memorial\u2019s purpose extends beyond Packer to include a USDA employee whose life belonged to a later period. This makes the plaque a shared memorial rather than a single-subject tribute.", "sort_key": 1931.0}, {"when": "July 16, 1938", "what": "Leonel Campbell Ross O'Bryan was born in 1857 and died on July 16, 1938, at age 80 or 81.", "source_ids": ["SRC_9"], "context": "This supplies the full name and lifespan associated with the person identified elsewhere by a shorter surname. The uncertainty over whether he was eighty or eighty-one shows a minor discrepancy in the surviving account.", "sort_key": 1938.0}, {"when": "16 July 1938", "what": "Pry died on 16 July 1938, aged eighty.", "source_ids": ["SRC_9"], "context": "This is a shorter version of the same death record, with the age expressed in words. It helps preserve the alternate naming used in the chronology.", "sort_key": 1938.0}, {"when": "1964", "what": "Phil Ochs, a 1960s folk-music songwriter, wrote 'The Ballad of Alferd Packer' in 1964 about the expedition and its aftermath.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "Ochs\u2019s song carried the episode into the 1960s folk-music culture, giving the story a new form of public afterlife. Its existence also shows that Packer\u2019s reputation was being interpreted artistically rather than only through historical accounts.", "sort_key": 1964.0}, {"when": "January 1965", "what": "The Arrow reported in January 1965 that a blizzard trapped the six men without sufficient food.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "The report presents the expedition\u2019s crisis as the result of a blizzard combined with inadequate provisions. That framing supports the starvation circumstances underlying later accounts of what happened to the group.", "sort_key": 1965.0}, {"when": "1968", "what": "In 1968, students at the University of Colorado in Boulder renamed the student union restaurant the Alferd E. Packer Memorial Grill.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2"], "context": "The university naming turned Packer into a piece of student culture and institutional folklore. It also helped shift his image from a historical figure to a recurring campus reference.", "sort_key": 1968.0}, {"when": "1968", "what": "In 1968, students at the University of Colorado Boulder named their new cafeteria grill the Alferd G. Packer Memorial Grill.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "This parallel wording records the same campus memorialization with a different middle initial and description of the facility. The variation illustrates how the name circulated in differing forms while retaining its commemorative purpose.", "sort_key": 1968.0}, {"when": "1973", "what": "The Littleton Cemetery Association cemented over Packer's grave in 1973 to deter grave robbing and vandalism.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The grave\u2019s alteration was a practical response to fears of desecration, showing that public fascination had extended to Packer\u2019s burial site. It marks preservation and deterrence as part of the later story.", "sort_key": 1973.0}, {"when": "1976", "what": "The song later appeared on several compilations released after Ochs's death in 1976, including the 2010 On My Way compilation of demos from 1963.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The song remained available to listeners through posthumous compilations, extending its reach beyond Ochs\u2019s lifetime. The later demo collection also preserved an earlier stage of the composition\u2019s history.", "sort_key": 1976.0}, {"when": "1977", "what": "In 1977, U.S. Secretary of Agriculture Bob Bergland tried to terminate a contract for the USDA cafeteria food service but was blocked by the General Services Administration.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The failed attempt to end the cafeteria contract created the immediate conflict behind the later publicity. It placed Bergland in opposition to the GSA and turned an administrative dispute into a symbolic campaign.", "sort_key": 1977.0}, {"when": "August 10, 1977", "what": "To embarrass the GSA, Bergland and his employees held a press conference on August 10, 1977, to unveil a plaque naming the executive cafeteria The Alferd Packer Memorial Grill.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The plaque was presented as a deliberate embarrassment to the GSA, linking Packer\u2019s name to a federal workplace controversy. This transformed the memorial grill idea into a public political gesture.", "sort_key": 1977.0}, {"when": "1980", "what": "The 1980 film The Legend of Alfred Packer was described as an almost completely fictitious biopic of Packer\u2019s exploits.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The film brought Packer\u2019s story to a broader audience but did so through extensive invention. Its description underscores the gap between popular entertainment and the historical record.", "sort_key": 1980.0}, {"when": "1981", "what": "In 1981, then-Gov. Richard Lamm declined Packer\u2019s posthumous pardon.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "Lamm\u2019s decision kept Packer\u2019s legal and moral status unresolved despite renewed public interest. It also set up the controversy that surfaced openly at the later bust unveiling.", "sort_key": 1981.0}, {"when": "1982", "what": "The United Press International reported in 1982 that Lamm had declined the pardon the year before.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "This report independently places the pardon refusal in the preceding year, preserving the chronology of the controversy. Its wording shows that the decision remained newsworthy after it was made.", "sort_key": 1982.0}, {"when": "1982", "what": "At a bust unveiling of Packer at the Capitol Rotunda in 1982, Lamm said he knew some people had \u201ca bone to pick\u201d with him because he refused to grant Packer an unconditional pardon.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "Lamm\u2019s remark acknowledged that the refusal had created opposition among those attending or following the commemoration. The bust unveiling therefore became not only a tribute but also a public reminder of the unresolved pardon question.", "sort_key": 1982.0}, {"when": "1982", "what": "The New York Times reported in 1982 that Lamm congratulated sculptor Tom Miller at Packer\u2019s bust unveiling.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "The report records official recognition of the sculptor alongside the unveiling, giving the commemoration an artistic dimension. It shows the event functioning as a formal civic presentation rather than merely a protest over the pardon.", "sort_key": 1982.0}, {"when": "April 1984", "what": "The Washington Post reported in April 1984 that Gerry made those remarks.", "source_ids": ["SRC_4"], "context": "The report places Gerry\u2019s comments in the public record during a period when Packer\u2019s story remained a subject of historical discussion. Their significance depends on the remarks themselves, which are not included here.", "sort_key": 1984.0}, {"when": "July 17, 1989", "what": "On July 17, 1989, 115 years after Packer allegedly consumed his companions, James E. Starrs led an exhumation of the five bodies near the Lake Fork river upstream from Lake City.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The exhumation reopened the physical investigation into the deaths more than a century after the alleged killings. Starrs\u2019s team focused on recovering evidence from the burial site rather than relying solely on surviving accounts.", "sort_key": 1989.0}, {"when": "July 17, 1989", "what": "WH/G visitors can access Packer books, including The Alfred G. Packer Victims Exhumation Project Lake City, Colorado, July 17, 1989.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The collection gives visitors direct access to documentation of the 1989 project and related Packer literature. It preserves the exhumation as part of the story\u2019s continuing public and historical treatment.", "sort_key": 1989.0}, {"when": "1989", "what": "In 1989, Starrs concluded his investigation by calling Packer \u201cguilty as sin.\u201d", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "Starrs\u2019s conclusion gave the exhumation a clear public verdict, despite the investigation\u2019s forensic uncertainties. It reinforced the view that Packer bore responsibility for the deaths.", "sort_key": 1989.0}, {"when": "1989", "what": "During the 1989 exhumation, investigators found a hole about the size of a thimble in Bell\u2019s hip bone.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "The defect supplied a physical detail for investigators examining the victims\u2019 remains. Its location in Bell\u2019s hip became part of the evidence considered in the dispute over how Bell died.", "sort_key": 1989.0}, {"when": "1989", "what": "Bailey got the 1989 forensic samples shared with Richard Dujay at Mesa State College.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "Sharing the samples extended the investigation beyond the original exhumation team. It created the basis for later scientific examination at Mesa State College.", "sort_key": 1989.0}, {"when": "1993", "what": "In 1993, CU film students Matt Stone, a CU film student, and Trey Parker, a CU film student, produced Cannibal! The Musical.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2"], "context": "The film brought Packer\u2019s story into a new popular medium through the work of two CU students. Its production helped shift the subject from historical controversy toward comic entertainment.", "sort_key": 1993.0}, {"when": "1993", "what": "The 1993 film Cannibal! The Musical was described as a more comedic and heavily fabricated take on Packer\u2019s story.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The film treated the historical material loosely rather than presenting it as a factual reconstruction. Its fabricated and comedic approach shaped how many viewers encountered Packer\u2019s story.", "sort_key": 1993.0}, {"when": "1993", "what": "The comedy musical Cannibal! The Musical was released exclusively to Boulder, Colorado, in 1993 and released in the rest of the United States in 1996.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The staggered release first connected the film with Boulder before it reached audiences across the country. The later national release expanded the story\u2019s reach beyond Colorado.", "sort_key": 1993.0}, {"when": "1994", "what": "In 1994, an 1862 Colt revolver was found at the site of the murders.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"], "context": "The discovery introduced a potentially relevant weapon into the historical investigation. Because it was identified as an 1862 Colt, it could be compared with accounts involving firearms used during the murders.", "sort_key": 1994.0}, {"when": "1994", "what": "In 1994, David P. Bailey, curator of history at the Museum of Western Colorado, began an investigation to find more conclusive results than Starrs regarding Packer's claim of having shot Bell.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "Bailey\u2019s project sought to move beyond Starrs\u2019s conclusion by testing the specific question of whether Packer shot Bell. The investigation therefore shifted attention from general guilt to a narrower claim supported by physical evidence.", "sort_key": 1994.0}, {"when": "1994", "what": "In 1994, David Bailey found an 1862 Colt revolver while inventorying historic firearms.", "source_ids": ["SRC_8"], "context": "The firearm emerged during routine museum inventory rather than from a newly excavated crime scene. Its identification created a possible connection to the murders, though the discovery alone did not establish ownership.", "sort_key": 1994.0}, {"when": "1998", "what": "In 1998, author James E. Bank wrote Alferd Packer\u2019s Wilderness Cookbook.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2"], "context": "The book extended Packer\u2019s subject into a culinary and literary format. Its publication reflects the continued transformation of the case into popular culture.", "sort_key": 1998.0}, {"when": "2000", "what": "By 2000, Bailey had not yet proven a link between the antique pistol and Alfred Packer.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The absence of a proven connection left the revolver\u2019s relevance unresolved. Bailey\u2019s investigation had produced a possible artifact, but not the conclusive identification needed to support the claim.", "sort_key": 2000.0}, {"when": "2001", "what": "In 2001, Dr. Richard Dujay at Mesa State College analyzed archived forensic samples from the 1989 exhumation with an electron microscope.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The analysis subjected preserved material from the earlier exhumation to more advanced laboratory scrutiny. It represented a later attempt to extract additional evidence from samples that had already been collected.", "sort_key": 2001.0}, {"when": "2008", "what": "As of 2008, the skull was reported to be at the Ripley's Museum in San Antonio, Texas, after relocation from New Orleans following Hurricane Katrina.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3"], "context": "The skull\u2019s reported location shows how the physical remains had become part of a museum display history. Its relocation after Hurricane Katrina also illustrates the vulnerability of the surviving evidence to events unrelated to the original case.", "sort_key": 2008.0}], "files": [{"title": "The investigation", "chips": [{"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}], "body": "The investigation began when a council was formed to investigate Alfred Packer\u2019s story. As the meeting was beginning, two members of the Ute tribe arrived with pieces, or strips, of dried flesh that they said came from a human found on a hill near the agency. Alfred panicked, fainted, woke up, begged for mercy, and told a different story. He reportedly said, \u201cIt would not be the first time that people had been obliged to eat each other when they were hungry.\u201d Alfred said that ten days into the journey the group ran out of food and the oldest man died first. According to Alfred, the remaining men ate the dead man and took his rifle. Five days later, another man died, the others ate him too, and they found $133 in his pockets. Alfred said another man died after that while he was gathering wood for the fire, and the remaining men ate him too. Alfred claimed Shannon Bell then shot the remaining men. He said that he and Shannon carried some of the dead man for food, hid the rest, and continued toward the agency. Alfred claimed Shannon later tried to kill him too, went mad, attacked him, and was shot by Alfred in self-defense. General Adams wrote Alfred\u2019s confession down and had him sign it as a sworn statement; agency clerk Herman Lauter transcribed Alfred\u2019s first official statement. Preston did not believe the confession and was sure Alfred had killed the men and stolen their things. Preston became so angry that he dragged Alfred outside, and others had to intervene to stop him from killing Alfred.\n\nThe historical accounts describe several reasons for Preston Nutter\u2019s suspicion. Preston arrived in Saguache with two other original party members and found Packer at Dolan\u2019s Saloon. Packer played high-stakes poker there and bought a $70 horse. Nutter found Packer\u2019s story odd because Packer looked well-fed and because abandoning a guide in snowbound mountains seemed unlikely. Nutter also thought it was strange that Israel Swan would give Packer his rifle and leave four men with only one rifle for hunting. Nutter said Packer was sulky, obstinate, quarrelsome, and a petty thief. The accounts also say that Packer had been a poor guide, often got lost, argued with clients, and had a reputation for stealing and lying. Nutter said Packer had been constantly trying to find out how much money the men had. Packer had carried a Winchester rifle belonging to one of the missing men and a skinning knife belonging to Frank Miller, and he explained the knife by saying Miller had stuck it in a tree and walked away without it. Nutter and Loutsenhizer later tried to discredit Packer\u2019s claimed qualifications as a guide, emphasizing his character flaws, inconsistent stories, and contradictions. The accepted theory at the time was that Packer had joined the party by overstating his qualifications as a mountain guide familiar with the area to accompany the men to Breckenridge; at best, that theory held, he led the party to miserable deaths through gross incompetence. A more popular theory was that he had left Ouray\u2019s camp with the five men with a premeditated plan to lead them into the wilderness, kill them, and rob them.\n\nThe men persuaded General Adams to send a mounted agency officer to Saguache to retrieve Packer for questioning while pretending to recruit him for a search party. Packer was preparing to leave when Nutter and other men approached him again, and the agency officer arrived in time to separate the men. The officer told Packer he had been sent to enlist him as a guide for the search party. Packer agreed to return to Los Pin\u00f3s because he had little alternative besides vigilante justice. At the agency, Packer met General Adams and five men he had not seen since February. Loutsenhizer demanded an explanation, and Packer repeated his earlier story. Packer acted amazed that the men had not been heard from and said he was concerned for their well-being. When asked where his money came from, Packer said he had received a cash loan from a man in Saguache. The officer also reported that Packer had arrived in Saguache with plenty of cash, several wallets, and possessions belonging to the missing men, while no one in town claimed to have lent him any money. Adams convened a council of himself, Packer, and the agency officers. The two Ute tribesmen then arrived with the strips of dried human flesh they had found on a hill near the agency while hunting. Packer begged for mercy and promised a full confession. General Adams called a conference of the five men and the agency officers to decide what to do next, and the five original party members said they did not believe a single part of Packer\u2019s story.\n\nThe two Ute men said they knew of a place next to a lake that Packer had described, about 50 miles away across the hills. A search party led by Herman Lauter and consisting of the five Utah miners, several agency officers, and Packer as guide was assembled at once. After two weeks, near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, Packer said he was lost and did not recognize the surroundings. Nothing was found and the party returned to the agency. On the way back, Packer tried to murder Herman Lauter with a knife. Packer was caught, restrained, and arrested. General Adams, who had been willing to believe Packer until then, concluded that Packer was dangerous after the attack on Lauter. Packer was taken to Saguache and jailed outside town by the sheriff for his own protection. His jail was little more than a log cabin on ranch property belonging to the Saguache County Sheriff.\n\nWhile Packer was in jail, observers challenged his credibility. The condition of the bodies contradicted Packer\u2019s version of events. The bodies were all together in one spot rather than scattered across miles. Humphrey and Noon still had large portions of flesh, muscle, and organs that could have been eaten long before Bell allegedly tried to murder Packer. The men had tattered cloths lashed to their rotting feet, replacing shoes they had probably eaten. Moldy and tattered blankets lay beneath and beside the bodies. A beaten path led from the corpses to a crude shelter used by Packer. There was evidence that the deaths had occurred before supplies were totally exhausted, and inside the shelter were possessions of the men that Packer had left behind. The theory at the time was that Packer killed the men before supplies ran out to rob them, got snowed in, lived for months in his shelter, and walked to the bodies to cut off meat as needed.\n\nIn summer, General Adams led a search in the mountains to find the bodies, but the search failed. In August, illustrator John Randolph was crossing the mountains near Lake City when he noticed something sticking out of the ground near a tributary of the Gunnison River. John smelled something terrible and found five decomposed bodies that had met violent ends. One man was little more than bones, suggesting animals had gotten to him afterward. Two of the men had blunt force injuries to the head. Shannon Bell had been stripped to the bones except for his hands and feet and also had a blunt force head injury. The remains suggested deliberate removal of edible portions, and except for the first man, it did not look like animals had done it. John made a sketch and told authorities in Lake City. The local coroner and law enforcement, along with a team of 20 volunteers, later retrieved the bodies. The coroner concluded that the men had been killed by blows to the head while asleep. None of the bodies had defensive wounds or injuries, and all had similar markings on their skulls. The bodies had rags under their feet, suggesting they may have eaten the leather from their shoes after running out of food. There was also a track to a makeshift shelter, leading investigators to think Alfred may have killed the men for their possessions and eaten them to survive.\n\nOther accounts identify Randolph as John A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly, and place the discovery at the foot of Slumgullion Pass, two miles southeast of Lake City, in a pine-shaded gulch above the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, a location later known as Deadman\u2019s Gulch. One account says that the snow covering the bodies and campsite had melted during the intervening four months, and that the local coroner and law enforcement went to the site with about 20 volunteers. The bodies were in various states of decomposition and had been left to the elements and animals. The illustrator\u2019s sketch was later published with the October 17, 1874, edition of Harper\u2019s Weekly under the title \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d Most historians conclude that an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly found the remains near the Gunnison River while on assignment in the area. An illustrator who accompanied the group recorded the scene in visual graphic detail, helping draw national attention to the crime. The corpses showed intense violence and skull fractures consistent with blows from a hatchet. After officials examined the bodies, they buried the remains at the site, and the search party returned to Packer\u2019s makeshift jail to confront him, only to find him missing.\n\nPacker\u2019s charges were expected to be upgraded from manslaughter to first-degree murder. Before that happened, someone gave Alfred a key, a penknife, and some food so he could escape. Packer allegedly received a makeshift key for his irons and some supplies, and escaped easily. Alfred slipped out of his cell and was not seen again for nine years. Months had passed with no definitive evidence of a crime, no bodies discovered, and no formal charges against Packer other than the attempt on Lauter\u2019s life. Saguache County authorities were reportedly unhappy about taxpayer money being spent to keep Packer under guard. Nearly all of Saguache believed through rumor or deduction that Packer was guilty of robbery or murder, and Packer\u2019s life was constantly threatened by nearby townspeople. Packer never said who helped him escape, how it happened, or why. It was presumed that his guard had been bribed by Packer or by someone else.\n\nIn March 1883, Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon encountered Packer when Packer approached him to buy supplies. Packer was living under the alias \u201cJohn Schwartze\u201d or \u201cJohn Swartze.\u201d Cabazon reported Packer to the local sheriff, who apprehended him and contacted General Adams. Cabazon was summoned to Cheyenne, confirmed Packer\u2019s identity, and accompanied him by train to Denver for Packer\u2019s second confession, which Packer signed on March 16. Packer said he fled because he feared mob justice from the people of Saguache. Because the actual crime was committed in Hinsdale County rather than Saguache County, Packer was sent to Lake City for detention and prosecution. Other accounts say Packer was jailed in Saguache, escaped, changed his identity, and was arrested in Wyoming before being returned to Hinsdale County for trial. After this arrest, Packer was tried and convicted of murder and sentenced to death, but the conviction was overturned on a technicality because Colorado became a state in 1876 and, before statehood, Colorado Territory had no statute under which he could be tried for murder. He was retried for manslaughter instead of murder and was eventually convicted of voluntary manslaughter. He received 40 years in prison, the maximum of eight years per man, which was described at the time as the longest prison sentence handed down in United States history. Other accounts say he was convicted of manslaughter in a second trial in Gunnison in 1886. He maintained that he had eaten the flesh of his companions to survive, but held to his story that Shannon Bell had killed the other men and that Packer killed Bell in self-defense.\n\nIn the century after the story, several groups conducted forensic analyses of the remains and compared them with Alfred\u2019s account. Those groups tended to conclude that Alfred was guilty. Some of the forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group. One group found marks across Shannon Bell\u2019s forearms, suggesting that he may have defended himself from an attack. In Alfred\u2019s account, Shannon was only shot once and then finished quickly. The revolver was still loaded with three .38-caliber bullets, and the bullets matched some fragments of lead found in Shannon Bell\u2019s hip. That evidence suggested that at least part of Alfred\u2019s story may have been true.\n\nThe later forensic record is not consistent. On July 17, 1989, grad students dug up the skeletons in Lake City, Colorado, under hot, muggy conditions. When the first skull was exposed, it had a gaping hole in its forehead. Reporters rushed to Lake City\u2019s four public pay phones to file a story headlined \u201cProfessor Exhumes 100-Year-Old Victims of Colorado Cannibal.\u201d The team led by James Starrs, a professor of law and forensic science at George Washington University, unearthed the skeletons of five men from a mass grave in the San Juan mountains. Starrs led the exhumation project, which cost an estimated $10,000 to $15,000 and used modern forensic techniques including ground-penetrating radar and laboratory inspection of the remains. Starrs said the team\u2019s examination showed that none of the victims had been shot, that four had been killed by hatchet blows to the head, and that the fifth skull had not been found. He said all five sets of bones revealed numerous knife marks indicating defleshing. He cited \u201cunmistakeable proof of cannibalism,\u201d said Packer had used the \u201cnatural refrigerator\u201d of the Colorado winter to preserve his victims and eat them until spring, and declared, \u201cHe was having human flesh filets morning, noon and night.\u201d More than a century after Packer was first accused of killing and eating five fellow prospectors in Colorado, Starrs said he had proven that Packer was \u201cguilty as sin.\u201d Starrs said Packer had \u201cbutchered his five fellow prospectors to death and then skinned them\u201d and feasted on their flesh, and that the added scientific evidence convicted Packer \u201cbeyond any shadow of a doubt\u201d in his opinion. Starrs said his aim was to settle the debate over Packer\u2019s guilt and to \u201cput forensic science on parade.\u201d He believed Packer\u2019s motive was initially to steal his companions\u2019 belongings and then to turn to cannibalism.\n\nThe exhumation account says the remains were found at the end of a residential driveway at a home belonging to a local surgeon and were buried only 13 inches below ground. Starrs and Walter H. Birkby said they did not think cannibalism could ever be scientifically demonstrated because it would require a picture of the person actually eating human flesh. Packer never denied cannibalism, so the investigation did not focus on that point. The evidence was sufficient for Starrs to conclude that Packer had murdered his comrades. Starrs believed Packer more likely murdered his companions for their belongings and resorted to cannibalism out of necessity rather than intent. The remains were re-interred and given a proper burial with funeral rites.\n\nExhumation showed blunt force trauma to the skulls of two men, with no other notable disturbance except damage from butchering. All skulls had damage, to varying extents, in their upper craniums, and fabric fibers were found in some skulls, suggesting their heads may have been covered with a blanket at death. The three other skeletons showed defensive hacking marks across the radial and ulna bones of their forearms, including Shannon Bell\u2019s, consistent with shielding the face and body from an attack. Shannon Bell was one of the skeletons with defensive forearm wounds, which could have been sustained while shielding his face and body from an attack. The skeletons showed no other noteworthy disturbance beyond damage made during butchering. All five skeletons had numerous differing post-mortem injuries, including depressed fractures, butterfly fractures, butchering trauma, and hacking trauma. Those post-mortem injuries appear to contradict Packer\u2019s claim of minimal cannibalism.\n\nTwo skeletons other than Shannon Bell\u2019s were found to have cylindrical puncture wounds in the pelvic bone. Some people have questioned whether all three pelvic marks were scavenging marks from a bear or possibly bullet wounds. No definitive conclusion has been made about the pelvic puncture wounds. One scenario suggested that two of the men were bludgeoned in their sleep by Packer. In the same scenario, the three remaining men awoke and Packer shot them in the hips to incapacitate them if the punctures were bullet wounds. The remaining men then may have tried to fight off Packer while he wielded a hatchet, and he killed them with blows to the head. Packer then butchered the men and used their flesh to survive in his snowbound state and during his journey. The different states of decomposition contradict that theory, although it is described as plausible. It seems unlikely that Packer shot three of the five men in the hip unless he was trying to debilitate them before killing them. Further analysis of entry and exit geometry could clarify the initial cause, but no such examination has been done to date. Packer\u2019s ultimate motivation for the murders has been heavily debated and has several possible theories.\n\nA different line of investigation concerns the pistol. In the Audrey Thrailkill collection of firearms at the museum was a Colt revolver reportedly found at the site of Packer\u2019s alleged crime. An accession card said, \u201cThis gun found at the site where Alferd Packer killed and ate his companions.\u201d Exhaustive investigation into the pistol\u2019s background uncovered trial-era documents. A Civil War veteran who visited the crime scene said Shannon Bell had been shot twice and the other victims had been killed with a hatchet. The veteran said that, after studying Bell, he noticed a severe bullet wound to the pelvic area and a bullet hole through Bell\u2019s wallet. This evidence seemed to corroborate Packer\u2019s claim that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer had shot Bell in self-defense.\n\nIn 1994, David P. Bailey, curator of history at the Museum of Western Colorado, began or reported an investigation into the pistol and the case; Bailey spent 10 years investigating the gun and the case. The pistol was described in one account as an 1862 Colt revolver, still loaded with three .38-caliber bullets, and in another as a rusted 1862 Colt Police Model .32 five-shot revolver found on Cannibal Plateau in the 1950s, with two shots missing. Dujay found microscopic lead fragments in soil taken from under Shannon Bell\u2019s remains. Spectrograph analysis matched those fragments to the bullets remaining in Packer\u2019s pistol. Analysis at Mesa State College\u2019s Electron Microscopy Facility proved that bullet fragments exhumed from the burial site matched lead from the old pistol. Dujay\u2019s team examined Bell\u2019s samples and found a tiny lead fragment whose chemical composition matched the bullets in Bailey\u2019s Colt revolver. Using a similar 1860s pistol, the team fired a bullet into an elk hip bone, and the resulting hole matched the hole in Bell\u2019s hip bone. The analysis suggested that Packer did shoot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and Bailey believed the evidence supported Packer\u2019s claim that he shot Bell. It now appears certain that at least Bell was shot, but whether Bell was shot in self-defense remains unanswered. Grant Houston remained skeptical because tourists had often found old guns and bullets near the massacre site and claimed they belonged to Packer or the other prospectors. Michelle Pierce was also unconvinced that Bailey\u2019s pistol was the smoking gun.\n\nMuseum curator David Bailey said Packer did not deny eating the bodies, killed only in self-defense, and was wrongly convicted. That view conflicts with Starrs\u2019s conclusion that Packer was guilty as sin and with the forensic interpretation that he murdered his companions for their belongings before resorting to cannibalism. Walter Birkby, the forensic anthropologist who disputed Starrs, said there was nothing to corroborate or refute anything Packer said and that no solid physical evidence would ever prove who did it. Michelle Pierce, Lake City\u2019s former town clerk, believes Packer did not murder the five men. Pierce\u2019s archival research turned up details that do not match the official narrative and portrays Packer as a pitiable soul whose social problems came from a misunderstood disease and harsh contemporary judgment. Pierce said, \u201cPeople really disliked him,\u201d probably because they were afraid of him. The text says that no clear or definitive evidence has yet been found for Packer\u2019s deeds.\n\nThe Denver Public Library\u2019s Western History/Genealogy Department is presented as a place to research Packer\u2019s life and crimes. Researchers can use its materials to prove or disprove parts of the Packer legend. The collection includes microfilm of Denver and Colorado newspapers from the era, including complete runs of the Denver Post and Rocky Mountain News, as well as copies of Packer\u2019s confession. The Colorado State Archives holds several legal and historical records concerning Alfred Packer. The Alfred Packer Collection includes penitentiary records, mugshot photos, parole records, confessions, letters to the governor concerning Packer\u2019s parole, a governor\u2019s pardon, original case files from Gunnison and Hinsdale District Court, Gunnison District Court cases 238\u2013241, Hinsdale District Court cases 238\u2013241, and Supreme Court case numbers 1198 from 1883, 1753 from 1885, 1980 from 1886, 3239 from 1893, and 3905 from 1898. The collection can now be viewed online: users are instructed to click the button for the online exhibits, choose \u201cBrowse Archive\u201d at the top left below the State Archives logo, and select the Alfred Packer option.\n\nBy mid-April, only Packer emerged alive from the mountains. Ripley\u2019s Vice-President of Exhibits and Archives, Edward Meyer, claimed that the museum possessed Packer\u2019s dissected skull, bought from an anonymous party for a reported $20,000. No statement was made about how the museum verified the skull\u2019s authenticity, other than that the seller\u2019s information about its origin was sufficient. If the skull belonged to Packer, his head was removed shortly before or after burial, dissected with the brain removed for study, preserved by an arsenic curing process, displayed by a traveling sideshow, sold to a private party, and later sold to Ripley\u2019s; as of 2008, it was reported to be at Ripley\u2019s Museum in San Antonio, Texas, after being moved from the New Orleans facility following Hurricane Katrina. During the 1989 exhumation, a hole about the size of a thimble was found in Shannon Bell\u2019s hip bone. James Starrs attributed the hole to scavenging animal fangs, while David Bailey wondered whether it had been made by a bullet.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_2", "SRC_3", "SRC_5", "SRC_6", "SRC_7", "SRC_8", "SRC_10", "SRC_11"], "fact_ids": ["SRC_1:F_53", "SRC_1:F_54", "SRC_1:F_55", "SRC_1:F_56", "SRC_1:F_57", "SRC_1:F_58", "SRC_1:F_59", "SRC_1:F_60", "SRC_1:F_61", "SRC_1:F_62", "SRC_1:F_63", "SRC_1:F_64", "SRC_1:F_65", "SRC_1:F_66", "SRC_1:F_67", "SRC_1:F_68", "SRC_1:F_69", "SRC_1:F_70", "SRC_1:F_71", "SRC_1:F_72", "SRC_1:F_73", "SRC_1:F_74", "SRC_1:F_75", "SRC_1:F_76", "SRC_1:F_77", "SRC_1:F_78", "SRC_1:F_79", "SRC_1:F_80", "SRC_1:F_81", "SRC_1:F_82", "SRC_1:F_83", "SRC_1:F_159", "SRC_1:F_160", "SRC_1:F_161", "SRC_1:F_162", "SRC_1:F_163", "SRC_1:F_165", "SRC_1:F_166", "SRC_1:F_167", "SRC_2:F_13", "SRC_2:F_16", "SRC_2:F_30", "SRC_2:F_31", "SRC_2:F_32", "SRC_3:F_10", "SRC_3:F_89", "SRC_3:F_93", "SRC_3:F_94", "SRC_3:F_96", "SRC_3:F_103", "SRC_3:F_104", "SRC_3:F_105", "SRC_3:F_106", "SRC_3:F_107", "SRC_3:F_108", "SRC_3:F_109", "SRC_3:F_110", "SRC_3:F_111", "SRC_3:F_112", "SRC_3:F_113", "SRC_3:F_114", "SRC_3:F_115", "SRC_3:F_116", "SRC_3:F_136", "SRC_3:F_137", "SRC_3:F_138", "SRC_3:F_139", "SRC_3:F_140", "SRC_3:F_141", "SRC_3:F_142", "SRC_3:F_143", "SRC_3:F_144", "SRC_3:F_145", "SRC_3:F_167", "SRC_3:F_190", "SRC_3:F_191", "SRC_3:F_192", "SRC_3:F_193", "SRC_3:F_194", "SRC_3:F_195", "SRC_3:F_196", "SRC_3:F_197", "SRC_3:F_198", "SRC_3:F_202", "SRC_3:F_203", "SRC_3:F_204", "SRC_3:F_205", "SRC_3:F_206", "SRC_3:F_207", "SRC_3:F_208", "SRC_3:F_209", "SRC_3:F_210", "SRC_3:F_211", "SRC_3:F_212", "SRC_3:F_213", "SRC_3:F_214", "SRC_3:F_224", "SRC_3:F_225", "SRC_3:F_226", "SRC_3:F_227", "SRC_3:F_228", "SRC_3:F_288", "SRC_3:F_289", "SRC_3:F_290", "SRC_3:F_293", "SRC_3:F_294", "SRC_3:F_295", "SRC_3:F_296", "SRC_3:F_297", "SRC_3:F_298", "SRC_3:F_299", "SRC_3:F_300", "SRC_3:F_301", "SRC_3:F_302", "SRC_3:F_303", "SRC_3:F_304", "SRC_3:F_307", "SRC_3:F_308", "SRC_3:F_309", "SRC_3:F_310", "SRC_3:F_311", "SRC_3:F_312", "SRC_3:F_313", "SRC_3:F_314", "SRC_3:F_315", "SRC_3:F_316", "SRC_3:F_317", "SRC_3:F_318", "SRC_3:F_319", "SRC_3:F_321", "SRC_3:F_322", "SRC_3:F_323", "SRC_3:F_324", "SRC_3:F_325", "SRC_3:F_328", "SRC_3:F_329", "SRC_3:F_330", "SRC_3:F_331", "SRC_5:F_27", "SRC_5:F_28", "SRC_5:F_29", "SRC_5:F_31", "SRC_6:F_1", "SRC_6:F_3", "SRC_6:F_4", "SRC_6:F_7", "SRC_6:F_8", "SRC_6:F_9", "SRC_6:F_11", "SRC_6:F_12", "SRC_6:F_13", "SRC_7:F_1", "SRC_7:F_2", "SRC_7:F_3", "SRC_7:F_4", "SRC_7:F_5", "SRC_7:F_9", "SRC_7:F_10", "SRC_7:F_17", "SRC_7:F_18", "SRC_7:F_19", "SRC_7:F_20", "SRC_7:F_21", "SRC_7:F_22", "SRC_7:F_23", "SRC_7:F_24", "SRC_7:F_25", "SRC_7:F_26", "SRC_7:F_27", "SRC_8:F_1", "SRC_8:F_2", "SRC_8:F_3", "SRC_8:F_4", "SRC_8:F_5", "SRC_8:F_6", "SRC_8:F_18", "SRC_8:F_19", "SRC_8:F_20", "SRC_8:F_21", "SRC_8:F_27", "SRC_8:F_28", "SRC_8:F_29", "SRC_8:F_36", "SRC_8:F_37", "SRC_8:F_38", "SRC_8:F_39", "SRC_8:F_40", "SRC_8:F_72", "SRC_8:F_73", "SRC_8:F_74", "SRC_8:F_76", "SRC_8:F_77", "SRC_8:F_79", "SRC_8:F_80", "SRC_8:F_81", "SRC_8:F_82", "SRC_8:F_83", "SRC_8:F_84", "SRC_8:F_85", "SRC_10:F_14", "SRC_10:F_15", "SRC_10:F_16", "SRC_10:F_17", "SRC_10:F_18", "SRC_10:F_20", "SRC_10:F_22", "SRC_10:F_50", "SRC_11:F_10", "SRC_11:F_12", "SRC_11:F_13", "SRC_11:F_14", "SRC_11:F_15"]}, {"title": "Packer\u2019s final account", "chips": [{"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}], "body": "On March 11, a man calling himself John Schwartze asked a shopkeeper to stock up for a planned trip. The shopkeeper thought the man looked familiar, then realized he was Alfred Packer. He told the local sheriff that he had spotted a criminal, and Alfred was arrested and taken to Denver. General Adams traveled to confirm Alfred\u2019s identity and, after doing so, took him away for trial. Alfred then gave a final version of events that he largely kept until his death.\n\nIn that final story, when the group left Chief Ouray\u2019s camp, they had only enough food for one grown man for a week. After two or three days, the group was hit by a brutal snowstorm. They kept trekking until they reached the base of a mountain and then a deep valley, but the snow on the far side was so deep they could barely walk. The party moved to higher ground and had only about a pint of flour left by day four. For about the next ten days, they lived only on rosebuds and pine gum while following a ridge above deeper snow. Several of the men were in such rough shape that they cried out every night and prayed to God to escape alive. They eventually crossed the main ridge and came down to camp by a river. The party followed the stream for a day until they reached a frozen lake, possibly Lake San Cristobal. They tried to cross the lake to reach trees for shelter, and by then they were exhausted and tensions were rising.\n\nThe narrative says Shannon Bell was furious at Alfred for losing the route and at the others for acting pathetic. According to Alfred, Shannon one morning ordered him to scout higher on the mountain for a way across. Alfred took a pistol and searched, but found only steep slopes and more snow. He admitted to General Adams for the first time that he did not really know what he was doing and did not know that side of the mountains. When Alfred returned after scouting all day, he found Shannon sitting by the fire eating a piece of meat. Alfred saw a man lying near the fire and the other three men lying motionless around it. Shannon rushed at Alfred with a hatchet, and Alfred shot Shannon in the stomach before finishing him after he fell. That night, Alfred later described being surrounded by \u201cthe midnight horrors of starvation as well as those of utter isolation.\u201d He said he felt his sanity slipping and began to understand what had happened to Shannon because it was beginning to happen to him. In another letter, Alfred wrote, \u201cThe greatest wonder is that I ever returned to a rational condition.\u201d\n\nThe next morning, Alfred tried to follow his tracks from the day before but turned back because of snow and exhaustion. He built a small shelter from wood and branches, but it did not satisfy his hunger. Alfred then cooked and ate some of the meat Shannon had been eating, realizing that he had to do so to survive. He said he then spent another two months in the shelter surviving off the rest of his party. Sixty days later, he saw an avalanche that left yellow clay behind, which told him he was in Slumgullion Pass. The pass is known for its yellow color and was named because it reminded early settlers of Slumgullion stew.\n\nIn early spring, Alfred finally knew where to go and set off up the mountain again. Before leaving, he gathered the remaining meat, wrapped it for the journey, and checked the dead men\u2019s pockets, where he found the money he later spent in Saguache. He had already eaten his shoes, so he wrapped his feet in cloth and had to stop when they froze, lighting fires to warm them. As he neared the agency, he ate the last pieces of meat and may have dropped some later found by the Ute. Alfred said he had previously taken General Adams\u2019s search party only as far as the mountains opposite the camp because he wanted to stop them from going farther and did not want to return to the horror. He also said he escaped and fled trial because he feared a mob would get past his guard and kill him. He said he went to Arkansas under a fake name, worked on a ranch, later moved to Arizona, and finally traveled to Cheyenne, where he was found.\n\nOther sources preserve conflicting versions of this account. The reference article says it appears likely that Packer killed another prospector in self-defense before gnawing on the corpses of those who had already died. Packer later said that after three or four days without provisions, the group cooked and ate their rawhide moccasins. He also said the trail was entirely drifted over and that they gathered and ate the buds of wild rose bushes. After being re-captured, Packer said Shannon Bell killed James Humphrey, a prospector who left Ouray\u2019s camp, George Noon, and Frank Miller while they slept around the campfire. Packer said Bell attacked him with a hatchet when he returned after searching for food, and that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Bell in self-defense. He said that after killing Bell he tried every day to get away but could not, so he lived on the flesh of the dead men for most of the sixty days he was out. He said the snow later formed a crust and he started out up the creek. Packer later confessed to living off the flesh of his companions and gave more than one version of the circumstances.\n\nAt the Los Pinos Indian Agency, Packer came during breakfast and begged for food and shelter. He carried a rifle, a knife, a steel coffee pot, and a satchel. When given food, he vomited and said his digestion had changed because of prolonged near-starvation. He told the agency that he had been hired by five men to guide them to Breckenridge. Packer said he became snow-blind during the journey and was left behind as a burden. He claimed Israel Swan gave him a rifle before the others abandoned him, and said he survived alone on roots and rose buds and ate his moccasins. The agency men thought his condition was odd because he did not look as malnourished and threadbare as expected after more than two months in the wilderness: his face was reported as bloated and his physique was hardly skeletal. Packer said he would drink whisky for breakfast instead of food.\n\nPacker said he was broke and sold his Winchester rifle to Major Downer for $10. He stayed at the agency for ten days and then went to Saguache to buy supplies for a trip back to Pennsylvania. In Saguache, he bought a horse for $70 and booked a room at Dolan\u2019s Saloon. Larry Dolan later said Packer spent around $100 during his stay and offered to lend him $300. Packer also spent $78 in Otto Mears\u2019 general store. It was claimed that he had several different wallets in his possession. When Nutter asked where the rest of the party was, Packer said he had gotten his feet wet and frozen. Packer said the party camped when a winter storm started, that he made a fire while the others went ahead to find food, and that Swan left him with his rifle. He said the others never returned and that he then made his way through the mountains eating mostly rosebuds and small animals. Packer claimed Miller had stuck the knife in a tree and walked off without it.\n\nIn another account, Packer said the men left Ouray\u2019s camp with what they thought was enough food for a 14-day journey, but provisions ran out sooner than expected. After a few days of no wildlife and almost no food, he said, the men began to eye each other unsettlingly. Packer said he returned from gathering firewood to find four men around Israel Swan\u2019s body after Swan had been struck in the head with a hatchet and killed instantly. The four men butchered Swan, and Packer said he accepted the situation and joined them. Several thousand dollars was found on Swan and divided among the men. They ate the best parts of Swan, packed some away, and continued onward, with Packer taking Swan\u2019s rifle.\n\nPacker said the five men again ran out of meat within two days and could not find game. Bell, Humphrey, Noon, and Packer secretly decided that Miller would be next because he was stocky and had soft flesh. Miller was killed with a hatchet blow to the head while stooping to pick up firewood, then butchered and eaten. Packer said Humphrey was next to be sacrificed, followed soon after by George Noon. Only Bell and Packer were left, and they swore not to eat each other. They agreed to claim that the four other men had died from the elements and were buried with dignity, and vowed never to speak of the cannibalism because they believed no one would accept that it had been necessary.\n\nPacker said that after a few days of travel with only a rabbit and some roots to eat, he and Bell camped beside a large lake next to a grove of hemlock trees. Bell later jumped from his blanket, screamed that he could not take it anymore, and said one of them would die for food. Packer said Bell rushed at him with a rifle to bash his skull in. Packer then butchered Bell, ate as much as he could, packed away more meat, took Bell\u2019s share of Swan\u2019s money, and continued alone. He said he threw away the remaining strips of Bell\u2019s flesh because he hoped an animal would eat them.\n\nPacker then said the men encountered a strong blizzard in the mountains. Heavy snow made them hopelessly lost and unable to retrace their route. They also ran out of matches and had to carry hot embers in a steel coffee pot to light fires. Days passed with no game and ice fishing failed completely. The men roasted and ate their shoes and tried to live on scant edible vegetation. Packer said the men made a pact that if one died, his meat would save the others from starvation. He said Israel Swan could go no further after days of hiking with almost no food; they found a pine-shaded gulch next to a lake and made camp there, and Swan then died of hunger and exposure.\n\nPacker\u2019s signed first confession said Swan died first and was eaten by the other five persons about ten days out of camp. It said Humphreys died four or five days later and was also eaten, that Humphreys had about $133, and that Packer found the pocketbook and took the money. The confession said the butcher was later killed, apparently accidentally, while Packer was carrying wood, and was also eaten. It said Bell shot California with Swan\u2019s gun and Packer killed Bell. Packer said he shot Bell, covered up the remains, took a large piece along, and traveled 14 days to the agency. In that first confession, Packer said the first two deaths were circumstantial because of the rough conditions and that everyone in the party partook in cannibalism to stay alive. He said one man was \u201ckilled accidentally\u201d while he was collecting firewood, and that Shannon Bell killed a man called \u201cCalifornia,\u201d after which Packer killed Bell in self-defense before traveling 14 days back to civilization.\n\nIn Packer\u2019s later amended version, the men endured almost 20 days from Ouray\u2019s camp and more than 10 days without substantial food. James Humphrey died of exposure to extreme cold, he said, and George Noon was killed days later by Shannon Bell solely for food. Bell and Packer agreed to stand together until the end and not eat each other. In this version, Bell later rushed at Packer with his rifle, intending to bludgeon him with the stock. Packer said he took the valuables of the deceased because they no longer needed them, but he did not specify the items or the amount of money obtained. He later said that only Bell and he remained after the other three men were dead.\n\nIn Packer\u2019s second confession, he said Bell had killed the others after telling him to scout for a way out of the mountains and to find food. Packer said he returned in the late evening after being gone most of the day. Bell was roasting meat cut from Frank Miller\u2019s leg; Miller\u2019s skull had been crushed by a hatchet and lay furthest from the fire down the stream, while the other three bodies lay near the fire with hatchet cuts in the forehead. Packer said he dropped his revolver in deep snow after shooting Bell and lost it. He built a crude shelter from stray logs to fight the snow and wind and stayed away from the bodies. Another storm came, and he sheltered for hours while starving. He decided he had to eat something or die, returned to the fire, covered the men, took the piece of meat near the fire back to camp, cooked it, ate it, and lived off the flesh of the men for the bigger part of sixty days.\n\nThe same second-confession account says the party asked Packer to go up a nearby hill to see farther ahead for navigation. He returned to camp after a full day of going up and coming back down the hillside and said Bell had killed the entire camp and was eating the leg of a man roasted in the campfire. Bell rushed at him, and Packer shot Bell through the stomach with the rifle he had taken on the trip up the hillside. Packer said he made a new fire near his camp, cooked the piece of meat, and ate it. He tried to get away every day but could not, so he lived off the flesh of the men for the bigger part of the 60 days he was out. Packer\u2019s third confession included more details but mostly the same information as the second confession.\n\nThe accounts also disagree over the weapon and the final struggle. One version says Bell had charged at Packer with Swan\u2019s rifle and Packer shot him. Another says Packer finished Bell with a hatchet blow to the head, but that was the extent of his claim concerning the hatchet. In his second official version, Packer never claimed that he had fought off Bell with a hatchet. The account attributed to Packer says the party members died one at a time and were consumed by the other members until only he and Shannon Bell remained; Shannon, desperately hungry, tried to kill Packer before dying of natural causes, and Bell was killed in the ensuing fight. Packer said he had no choice but to eat Bell because Bell would have eaten him. He claimed Bell had murdered the others with hatchet blows to the head, maintained that he killed only Bell and did so in self-defense, and admitted to eating the flesh of his companions in order to survive. Under interrogation, Packer gave the first of multiple contradictory confessions, first claiming, \u201cWe ate old man Swann first.\u201d He claimed the strong ate the weak one by one until only he and Bell remained, and that Bell tried to kill him, so he shot Bell dead. In one confession, he admitted to shooting one man in self-defense.\n\nPacker\u2019s written version said severe weather forced the men to camp in a ravine with some shelter from snow and wind. He wrote that they ate goatskin moccasins and rosebuds until they were weakened, emaciated, and mentally distracted, and that he was the one sane man among the six. He said he left camp to look for a way out and returned to find Shannon Wilson Bell roasting meat over a fire. Packer admitted surviving on the men\u2019s flesh until he escaped the mountains. He wrote that his comrades were dead, he was left alone among starvation and isolation, and his body and mind were overwhelmed. In letters, he insisted that \u201cthe greatest wonder is that I ever returned to a rational condition.\u201d\n\nThe accounts say Packer later maintained that Shannon Bell had killed the other men. After arriving alone at the Los Pinos Indian Agency, he said his comrades had left him while they searched for food. He claimed he did not know what happened to his companions even though he carried large sums of their money and possessed a rifle known to have belonged to one of them. Under interrogation, he admitted to partaking in cannibalism but denied committing murder. He complied with further questioning and repeated his story of self-defense and cannibalism for survival.\n\nThe wider account says that, because of his ineptitude, Packer led the men through the San Juan Mountains for nearly two months and covered only 250 miles at an average of three miles a day. In his correspondence, Packer tried to shift blame for the disaster onto Chief Ouray. He wrote that conversations with Ouray led them to believe they could reach the Los Pinos Agency in not over five days on foot across the mountains. Packer said he had never been to the agency and relied only on general reputation and information received from Ouray. The text calls Packer a known pathological liar and says his account to Newman about Ouray was not entirely truthful. Ouray never told Packer they were only five days from the Los Pinos Agency because at least three to four feet of snow blocked the path. Ouray did draw a crude map on the ground with a stick and gave them all the directions he could as a route. Packer wrote that they were given scanty fare\u2014about 20 pounds of flour and not over 10 pounds of good meat\u2014and that at the end of nine days the last provisions had been eaten. He said the group ate their goatskin moccasins and subsisted on rosebuds until they were weakened, emaciated, and mentally completely distracted. The text says those conditions led to the deaths and cannibalization of all in Packer\u2019s party, while also calling Packer\u2019s account ridiculous because he was living in a different territory under an alias to evade capture.\n\nThe later assessment remained disputed. Lamm said the evidence suggested that Packer killed only one man and only in self-defense, but added, \u201cthe proof was in the pudding and Alferd ate that, too.\u201d Lamm told Tom Miller that, in the immortal words of Alferd Packer, \u201cWell done!\u201d After being accused of murdering the other men, Packer told a story that defined the rest of his life and earned him a permanent place in Colorado history.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_2", "SRC_3", "SRC_4", "SRC_5", "SRC_7", "SRC_8", "SRC_10", "SRC_11"], "fact_ids": ["SRC_1:F_85", "SRC_1:F_86", "SRC_1:F_87", "SRC_1:F_88", "SRC_1:F_89", "SRC_1:F_90", "SRC_1:F_91", "SRC_1:F_92", "SRC_1:F_93", "SRC_1:F_94", "SRC_1:F_95", "SRC_1:F_96", "SRC_1:F_97", "SRC_1:F_98", "SRC_1:F_99", "SRC_1:F_100", "SRC_1:F_101", "SRC_1:F_102", "SRC_1:F_103", "SRC_1:F_104", "SRC_1:F_105", "SRC_1:F_106", "SRC_1:F_107", "SRC_1:F_108", "SRC_1:F_109", "SRC_1:F_110", "SRC_1:F_111", "SRC_1:F_112", "SRC_1:F_113", "SRC_1:F_114", "SRC_1:F_115", "SRC_1:F_116", "SRC_1:F_117", "SRC_1:F_118", "SRC_1:F_119", "SRC_1:F_120", "SRC_1:F_121", "SRC_2:F_3", "SRC_2:F_8", "SRC_2:F_9", "SRC_2:F_19", "SRC_2:F_20", "SRC_2:F_21", "SRC_2:F_22", "SRC_2:F_23", "SRC_3:F_13", "SRC_3:F_73", "SRC_3:F_74", "SRC_3:F_75", "SRC_3:F_76", "SRC_3:F_77", "SRC_3:F_78", "SRC_3:F_79", "SRC_3:F_80", "SRC_3:F_81", "SRC_3:F_82", "SRC_3:F_83", "SRC_3:F_84", "SRC_3:F_85", "SRC_3:F_86", "SRC_3:F_87", "SRC_3:F_88", "SRC_3:F_90", "SRC_3:F_91", "SRC_3:F_92", "SRC_3:F_95", "SRC_3:F_117", "SRC_3:F_118", "SRC_3:F_119", "SRC_3:F_120", "SRC_3:F_121", "SRC_3:F_122", "SRC_3:F_123", "SRC_3:F_124", "SRC_3:F_125", "SRC_3:F_126", "SRC_3:F_127", "SRC_3:F_128", "SRC_3:F_129", "SRC_3:F_130", "SRC_3:F_131", "SRC_3:F_132", "SRC_3:F_133", "SRC_3:F_134", "SRC_3:F_135", "SRC_3:F_146", "SRC_3:F_147", "SRC_3:F_148", "SRC_3:F_149", "SRC_3:F_150", "SRC_3:F_151", "SRC_3:F_152", "SRC_3:F_153", "SRC_3:F_154", "SRC_3:F_155", "SRC_3:F_156", "SRC_3:F_157", "SRC_3:F_158", "SRC_3:F_159", "SRC_3:F_160", "SRC_3:F_161", "SRC_3:F_162", "SRC_3:F_163", "SRC_3:F_164", "SRC_3:F_165", "SRC_3:F_166", "SRC_3:F_168", "SRC_3:F_229", "SRC_3:F_230", "SRC_3:F_231", "SRC_3:F_232", "SRC_3:F_233", "SRC_3:F_234", "SRC_3:F_235", "SRC_3:F_236", "SRC_3:F_237", "SRC_4:F_9", "SRC_4:F_10", "SRC_4:F_11", "SRC_4:F_12", "SRC_4:F_13", "SRC_4:F_14", "SRC_4:F_15", "SRC_4:F_17", "SRC_4:F_18", "SRC_4:F_19", "SRC_4:F_32", "SRC_4:F_34", "SRC_5:F_5", "SRC_5:F_6", "SRC_5:F_7", "SRC_5:F_8", "SRC_3:F_305", "SRC_3:F_306", "SRC_7:F_12", "SRC_8:F_22", "SRC_8:F_23", "SRC_8:F_24", "SRC_8:F_25", "SRC_8:F_59", "SRC_8:F_60", "SRC_8:F_61", "SRC_8:F_62", "SRC_8:F_63", "SRC_8:F_64", "SRC_8:F_103", "SRC_8:F_104", "SRC_10:F_54", "SRC_10:F_55", "SRC_11:F_8", "SRC_11:F_9", "SRC_11:F_11", "SRC_11:F_41", "SRC_11:F_43", "SRC_11:F_50", "SRC_11:F_51", "SRC_11:F_52", "SRC_11:F_53", "SRC_11:F_54", "SRC_11:F_55", "SRC_11:F_58", "SRC_11:F_59", "SRC_11:F_60", "SRC_11:F_61", "SRC_11:F_62", "SRC_11:F_64"]}, {"title": "The trials", "chips": [{"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}], "body": "At Alfred Packer\u2019s first trial, witnesses testified that he had deliberately led the men astray with the intention of stealing their possessions by any means necessary. Preston Nutter, a prospector who rejected Packer\u2019s account, testified that Packer had been skulking around, always trying to find out how much money each man had or was carrying. Other witnesses described Packer\u2019s behavior after he reached Saguache and the different, conflicting stories he told about what had happened. The prosecution argued that his survival story was an attempt to cover up the killings. When Packer took the stand, he rambled for more than six hours and admitted eating the other members of the party, but said that he had killed only Shannon Bell and that Bell was responsible for everything else. The jury rejected his story and found him guilty of premeditated murder, theft, and several other charges. He was originally sentenced to death by hanging.\n\nThe dates and details of the first proceeding vary among the sources. One account says that the trial began on April 6 in Lake City; another dates its beginning to April 9, 1883, after seven days of testimony and examination. The prosecution presented about two dozen witnesses. Otto Mears testified that Packer possessed valuable Wells Fargo drafts when he emerged from the mountains. Constable Herman Lauter testified that Packer lunged at him with murder in his eyes when Lauter ordered him to hand over a knife. Packer called Lauter a liar and boasted, \u201cTo think that one little Dutchman could take a knife away from me. No, sir!\u201d The jury reportedly reacted badly to Packer\u2019s rambling testimony, his habit of speaking about himself in the third person, and his focus on personal grudges rather than on proving his innocence. One source says that the jury wasted little time finding him guilty of murder.\n\nThe first conviction was overturned because of a legal problem. Before statehood, Colorado Territory did not have a statute under which Packer could be tried for murder, and Colorado State established the specific laws only after the crime had taken place. The Colorado Supreme Court therefore held that the government could not sentence a man to death for a crime committed before Colorado became a state rather than a territory. The sources describe this as an ex post facto problem, a technicality, or the effect of an old territorial statute and a \u201csavings clause.\u201d Packer\u2019s lawyer also assigned other errors to the court, including that Packer had not received a speedy trial and that the murder indictments came after what was then a three-year statute of limitations. Another account says Packer argued that the killings occurred on the Ute Reservation and should therefore have been subject to federal rather than local jurisdiction. The murder conviction and scheduled execution were overturned, so Packer had to be retried for manslaughter instead of murder.\n\nA second trial was held in Gunnison after the Colorado Supreme Court granted a change of venue and a new Republican judge was appointed. Packer and his counsel argued that Lake City\u2019s mainly Democratic community was too prejudiced for a fair trial. Of 57 prospective jurors interviewed, 54 admitted that they already knew about the case, and 44 said that they already believed Packer was guilty. Judge Melville Gerry ordered the sheriff to \u201cscour the highways and byways\u201d for jurors, and the sheriff filled the jury within a few hours. Packer was incarcerated at Gunnison while awaiting retrial, with one source saying that he waited nearly four years. He again pleaded not guilty and repeated his story.\n\nThe prosecution made the second case more damaging to Packer. Hunters testified that there had been plenty of food in the area even during a bad winter, including game, rabbits, deer, antelope, and more. They said that even someone as inexperienced as Packer could have hunted it easily. There was also a report that a deer carcass had been found near the campsite. That evidence weakened Packer\u2019s claim that wildlife had been so scarce that the men quickly had to resort to cannibalism. The prosecution argued that the cannibalism therefore had to have been by choice because food would have been available. Witnesses again described Packer\u2019s behavior after he reached Saguache and the different accounts he had given of the events. When Packer finally took the stand, he testified for more than six hours and admitted eating the other members of the party. His account of what happened at the campsite remained relatively close to his second official version. He asked to be charged the full 40 years only for Shannon Bell\u2019s death, since Bell was the only killing he continued to claim, but the request was denied.\n\nThe prosecution argued that even if Packer was not a malicious monster who had premeditated the entire episode, he had still led the group astray by taking a more dangerous winter route while pretending to be a guide. The text says that, at best, he was extremely negligent. Packer was convicted of manslaughter and received 40 years in prison, described as the maximum of eight years per man. The sources disagree on the legal description: Packer\u2019s criminal record lists \u201cvoluntary manslaughter\u201d on five counts, while other accounts call the conviction involuntary manslaughter. They agree that the sentence was 40 years, or eight years for each of the five men. At the time, one source calls it the longest prison sentence ever handed down in U.S. history, while another calls it the longest custodial sentence in U.S. history. He was sentenced to 40 years of hard labor at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Ca\u00f1on City.\n\nThe sources also give different summaries of the sequence. One says that Packer was arrested on murder charges, escaped from the Saguache jail, remained on the run for eight years, was captured in Wyoming in 1883, and was returned to Lake City, where he was tried, found guilty of murder, and sentenced to hang. Another says that he escaped and hid from justice for nine years, and still another says that he spent nearly a decade on the run before authorities tracked him down. A separate account says he escaped before trial and was eventually found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883. The consistent outline is that local authorities did not believe his story, that he escaped from jail, that he was later captured in Wyoming and brought back to Hinsdale County, and that he was eventually tried and convicted of murder before winning a retrial on a technicality.\n\nJudge M. B. Gerry is associated with the famous quotation supposedly spoken while sentencing Packer in the first trial. In the legendary version, Gerry said that Packer had come to Hinsdale County when there were seven Democrats, had eaten five of them, and was sentenced to hang. One version has Gerry saying, \u201cI sentence you, Alfred Packer, to be hanged by the neck until you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d Another gives the more elaborate wording, \u201cStand up yah voracious man-eatin\u2019 sonofabitch and receive yir sintince. When yah came to Hinsdale County, there was siven Dimmycrats,\u201d followed by the claim that Packer had eaten five of them and was being sentenced to hang as a warning against reducing the Democratic population of the state. Gerry reportedly said that there were seven Democrats in Hinsdale County and that Packer had eaten five of them, and he reportedly sentenced Packer to hang until he was \u201cdead, dead, dead\u201d as a warning against reducing the Democratic population of the state.\n\nThe Colorado history account says that the legend is apocryphal. Fred M. Mazzula argues in his book *Al Packer, A Colorado Cannibal* that Judge Gerry did not talk like Yosemite Sam. Mazzula used the trial transcripts to determine what Gerry actually said at sentencing. The actual sentence ordered that Packer be taken by the sheriff of Hinsdale County to a place of execution within the corporate limits of Lake City, between 10 a.m. and 3 p.m. on May 19, 1883, and that he be hanged by the neck until he was \u201cdead, dead, dead.\u201d It ended with a request that God have mercy on Packer\u2019s soul. The text says Gerry\u2019s real quotation is less colorful but more accurate. One source nevertheless records that Gerry sentenced Packer to be \u201chung from your neck until you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d\n\nLake City residents were outraged when the death sentence was overturned and threatened to hang Packer themselves, so the second trial was moved to Gunnison, about 50 miles away. Packer escaped death on a technicality and received the new trial. After the new proceeding, he was convicted of manslaughter and sentenced to 40 years in the Colorado State Penitentiary in Ca\u00f1on City. After a confession, a search for the bodies, and a trial, one article summarizes, Packer was sentenced to forty years at the Colorado State Penitentiary. The sources differ over whether the second-trial conviction should be called voluntary or involuntary manslaughter, but Packer\u2019s listed convictions are murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter on five counts, and his listed criminal penalty is death, later commuted to 40 years\u2019 imprisonment.\n\nThe prosecution\u2019s motive theory also focused on Israel Swan. At the time of Packer\u2019s trial, Swan\u2019s family reported that he had left for the expedition with about $6,000 in cash and gold and a valuable Winchester rifle. The money and rifle would have provided a strong motive for murder, and Swan was 65 at the time of his death. Packer was not found with any gold when he was arrested. Although he had some money left, it did not total in the thousands, and the sources say that his spending had been frivolous. One account says that he spent more than $100 on drinking and gambling and another $78 in a general store, while often giving conflicting stories when he drank. The prosecution argued that the only logical reason for him to travel through the mountains with so little food and supplies was to lead the men into the wilderness to kill and rob them. Packer pleaded not guilty.\n\nThe first-trial evidence concerning the deaths included the prosecution\u2019s claim that Swan died on or around March 1. In court, the prosecution argued that Swan showed signs of a struggle at death and that the other men appeared to have been killed in their sleep. Otto Mears, Larry Dolan, Oliver D. Loutsenhizer, and Preston Nutter testified for the prosecution, with Nutter as the key witness. Other accounts repeat that Nutter testified that Packer had been trying to learn how much money each man was carrying. The physical evidence was also used against Packer: the men had no defensive wounds or injuries in one account, while later exhumation accounts describe defensive hacking marks on the forearms of three skeletons, including Shannon Bell\u2019s. All five skeletons showed varying post-mortem injuries, including depressed fractures, butterfly fractures, butchering trauma, and hacking trauma. These findings leave the evidentiary picture disputed rather than settled.\n\nPacker\u2019s lawyer mounted what one source calls a spirited defense, but Packer went to prison for seventeen years before *The Denver Post* petitioned for his release. In prison, he made horsehair bridles, and one of them is displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado in Grand Junction. He also built elaborate Victorian dollhouses. After being imprisoned, Packer wrote to anyone he could, claiming that his sentence was unjust, that he had killed only Shannon, and that the jury was full of biased and prejudiced people. He filed appeals five times and was denied each time. He sent letters to local newspapers saying that he had been unjustly convicted by an unfair and unsympathetic judicial system and by ignorant, small-minded people. On June 19, 1899, the Colorado Supreme Court officially upheld his sentence. He never succeeded in obtaining an official state pardon, although the Colorado State Archives collection includes a governor\u2019s pardon and also contains penitentiary, mugshot, parole, confession, governor\u2019s-parole, and original court records.\n\nThe sources disagree about the length of time Packer served and the date of his release. One says he served 15 years before being granted parole; another says he spent 16 years in prison; another says he served 17 years before *The Denver Post* petitioned for his release. The account based on the Colorado State Archives says that Alfred was paroled in 1901, 18 years into his sentence. Another source says that former Governor Charles Thomas paroled him in 1901, while a separate historical summary gives 1905. The sources agree that he was paroled rather than officially pardoned and that Polly Pry, a reporter for *The Denver Post*, helped campaign for his release. After his parole, he worked for *The Denver Post* as a guard and later as a ranch hand.\n\nPacker\u2019s criminal status is listed as deceased. His convictions are listed as murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter on five counts; his criminal penalty is listed as death, later commuted to 40 years\u2019 imprisonment. The text says that Packer is one of four people historically convicted for cannibalism in the United States. It also describes the incident as the only known trial in the United States associated with human cannibalism, while stressing that Packer was not actually convicted of cannibalism as such: he was tried and found guilty of murder in the first trial and manslaughter in the second. The sources state that cannibalism itself was not necessarily illegal in the United States unless a person committed murder to obtain the flesh.\n\nPacker\u2019s supporters continued to dispute the verdict. Polly Pry portrayed him as a former soldier and a man of the people who had been persecuted by the establishment, had been in the wrong place at the wrong time, and had survived horrors that most people could not imagine. She argued that his sentence was so harsh only because of the cannibalism and that anyone would have done the same in his situation. Her stories changed the minds of some local businesses and residents, leading them to organize petitions for his release. In mock trials held in Lake City, Boulder, and other Colorado towns, actors played the main roles and audience members served as jurors. When David Bailey\u2019s evidence was included, jurors acquitted Packer more often than not. The State Archives collection includes a governor\u2019s pardon, but the historical accounts say that Packer never obtained an official state pardon.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_3", "SRC_2", "SRC_4", "SRC_5", "SRC_6", "SRC_7", "SRC_8", "SRC_10", "SRC_11"], "fact_ids": ["SRC_1:F_122", "SRC_1:F_123", "SRC_1:F_124", "SRC_1:F_125", "SRC_1:F_126", "SRC_1:F_127", "SRC_1:F_129", "SRC_1:F_130", "SRC_1:F_131", "SRC_1:F_132", "SRC_1:F_133", "SRC_1:F_134", "SRC_1:F_135", "SRC_1:F_136", "SRC_1:F_137", "SRC_1:F_138", "SRC_1:F_139", "SRC_1:F_140", "SRC_1:F_141", "SRC_1:F_142", "SRC_1:F_143", "SRC_1:F_144", "SRC_1:F_145", "SRC_1:F_146", "SRC_3:F_5", "SRC_3:F_6", "SRC_3:F_7", "SRC_3:F_11", "SRC_3:F_14", "SRC_3:F_15", "SRC_3:F_16", "SRC_2:F_24", "SRC_2:F_25", "SRC_2:F_26", "SRC_1:F_158", "SRC_3:F_238", "SRC_3:F_239", "SRC_3:F_240", "SRC_3:F_241", "SRC_3:F_242", "SRC_3:F_243", "SRC_3:F_244", "SRC_3:F_246", "SRC_3:F_247", "SRC_3:F_248", "SRC_3:F_252", "SRC_3:F_253", "SRC_3:F_254", "SRC_3:F_255", "SRC_3:F_256", "SRC_3:F_257", "SRC_3:F_259", "SRC_3:F_260", "SRC_3:F_265", "SRC_3:F_266", "SRC_3:F_267", "SRC_3:F_268", "SRC_3:F_269", "SRC_3:F_270", "SRC_3:F_271", "SRC_3:F_286", "SRC_4:F_7", "SRC_4:F_21", "SRC_4:F_22", "SRC_4:F_25", "SRC_4:F_26", "SRC_5:F_9", "SRC_5:F_10", "SRC_5:F_12", "SRC_5:F_18", "SRC_5:F_19", "SRC_5:F_20", "SRC_5:F_21", "SRC_5:F_23", "SRC_5:F_24", "SRC_5:F_25", "SRC_5:F_26", "SRC_5:F_33", "SRC_6:F_6", "SRC_7:F_13", "SRC_7:F_14", "SRC_8:F_26", "SRC_8:F_30", "SRC_8:F_31", "SRC_8:F_32", "SRC_8:F_33", "SRC_8:F_34", "SRC_8:F_48", "SRC_8:F_49", "SRC_8:F_50", "SRC_8:F_54", "SRC_8:F_55", "SRC_8:F_56", "SRC_8:F_57", "SRC_8:F_58", "SRC_8:F_65", "SRC_8:F_66", "SRC_8:F_67", "SRC_8:F_68", "SRC_8:F_69", "SRC_8:F_70", "SRC_8:F_86", "SRC_8:F_87", "SRC_8:F_88", "SRC_10:F_25", "SRC_10:F_26", "SRC_10:F_28", "SRC_10:F_29", "SRC_10:F_30", "SRC_10:F_33", "SRC_10:F_40", "SRC_10:F_44", "SRC_10:F_52", "SRC_11:F_17", "SRC_11:F_18", "SRC_11:F_19", "SRC_11:F_20", "SRC_11:F_21"]}, {"title": "The expedition", "chips": [{"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}], "body": "The sources give differing accounts of the expedition\u2019s size and divisions. One historical account says Robert McGrue, leader of the larger prospecting party, organized a party of 20 to travel from Salt Lake City to the San Juan Mountains after hearing of gold discovered in Breckenridge. Another account says that a party of 21 prospectors, led by Alferd\u2014or Alfred\u2014Packer, left Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 8, 1873, hoping to be among the first to locate mining claims in the San Juan region. A separate account describes Packer as part of a larger band of 20 gold seekers who left Utah and split into two groups. The prospectors were drawn by news of a gold strike and reports of enormous fortunes, and one newspaper account says they were promised \u201cmarvelous tales of enormous fortunes to be had for the mere asking.\u201d Their intended route was toward Breckenridge, by way of the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency.\n\nPacker had worked as a guide for only a few months, and the accounts disagree about how much responsibility to assign him, but they agree that the party progressed more slowly than expected toward Breckenridge despite his serving as guide. One account says Alfred managed, by luck, to get the party only as far as just outside Montrose. The journey from Utah to Colorado was slow and difficult: winter arrived early and severely, the wagons and horses were crippled, and heavy snow along the Spanish Trail covered the path so completely that the men relied almost solely on a compass. The wagons could hardly follow a trail, and Packer\u2019s inexperience showed itself as the party became lost. By January, provisions had run out completely. The men first survived on horse feed and came close to considering the horses themselves as food. The men later described Alfred as greedy, lazy, and constantly fighting with someone; other accounts add that he was argumentative, inexperienced, distrusted, and known for lying and theft.\n\nOn January 21, 1874, the party found a Ute encampment in a valley just outside Montrose, in the Uncompahgre Valley, led by Chief Ouray, who was known as \u201cThe White Man\u2019s Friend.\u201d The accounts say Ouray supplied the men with food, lodging, and shelter, exchanged food and supplies with them, and offered to let them remain with his tribe until winter had passed. He recommended postponing the expedition until spring because the winter weather in the mountains was dangerous, promised to share all that he and his people had with them, and warned that no Ute would attempt the journey in those conditions. According to the sources, he said that trying it meant certain or almost certain death. The party still had about 120 miles and the San Juan Mountains left to cross. One account records a different claim attributed to Packer: that Ouray said the Los Pi\u00f1os Agency could be reached in no more than five days on foot across the mountains. The same account says that this was not truthful, because three or four feet of snow blocked the route; men who remained at the camp said Ouray told the prospectors they would all die and that no Indian in his band would go to show them the route because the deep snow was too dangerous.\n\nDespite the warning, 11 men decided to continue. They planned to travel first to the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, the closest outpost to Ouray\u2019s camp, and then proceed to Breckenridge. Ouray provided food for the journey and safe directions to bypass the mountains, advising them to drop down to the Gunnison River and follow it north to the agency. The party divided: one account says five men followed Oliver Loutsenhizer along the Gunnison River while six others, including Packer, headed through the San Juan Mountains. Loutsenhizer threatened to shoot Packer if Packer tried to follow the other group. Another account describes three factions at the Ute encampment: some men paid Ouray a trespassing fee and stayed with the Utes for the rest of the winter; others accepted a week\u2019s provisions from him and traveled toward the Los Pinos Agency in Saguache County; and a third group ignored his warnings. The river-bound group nearly perished after losing its way, while some of the men stayed behind with the horses and wagons until spring.\n\nPacker insisted that crossing the mountains directly was better than going around them because he claimed to know the area and said the route was faster and more direct. The sources emphasize that locals had given him a safer route beside a main water source, where the men could have caught fresh fish and stayed on course. Instead, Packer chose the mountain route with minimal supplies because he believed it would be faster. Although the route he and his companions took was nearly identical in length to the recommended route, though not in its traverse, it was far more dangerous. The choice to hike through the San Juan Mountains in midwinter\u2014where snow could exceed six feet in a single snowfall, with blasting winds and freezing temperatures\u2014is described as practically suicidal.\n\nAfter fierce snowstorms boxed in the expedition, many of the men returned home, but six, including Packer, continued. On February 9, Packer and five others left for the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, described in one account as 75 miles ahead. The five men were George Noon, Shannon Wilson Bell, James Humphrey, Frank Miller, and Israel Swan; they wanted to reach Breckenridge before thousands of fortune hunters arrived. Their immediate goal was the agency, followed by Breckenridge. The group traveled southeast and then south along the Lake Fork, passing the site that would become the townsite of Lake City within six months. After wandering, the party was discovered by warpainted Ute Indians, and Chief Ouray took them to a spot near his village where they established a makeshift camp. When Ouray realized that Packer\u2019s party would still enter the storm, he supplied them with provisions despite the warnings.\n\nPacker\u2019s men entered the mountains with barely enough food for the supposed 14 days required by the safest route. They had no snowshoes, minimal matches, no flint, no heavy clothing, two rifles, one pistol, a couple of knives, a hatchet, and minimal ammunition. Another account says Packer had left Utah with few provisions and no weapons. The larger party\u2019s river route initially followed Ouray\u2019s directions under Bob McGrue\u2019s guidance, but the horses eventually could not continue. McGrue unloaded the men\u2019s provisions and returned to Ouray\u2019s camp. Packer then took his party higher into the San Juan Mountains against Ouray\u2019s warning.\n\nThe river party had a brutal journey in the cold. The freezing winter made the Gunnison route difficult, and the men ran out of food long before reaching the agency. Cowhands found them and fed them at a cattle camp until spring; another account says the cowhands let them remain there until spring. In April, when the weather improved, the men reached the agency in May and found that Alfred had already been there alone. The sources also describe Packer\u2019s separate arrival: he wandered into the Los Pinos Agency in Southwest Colorado three months later, on April 16, 1874, 65 days after his departure, and another account places the agency 75 miles ahead. He emerged from the woods alone, carrying a pocket full of cash, a rifle, and a coffee pot, with no traveling party, which aroused suspicion. The account that gives his arrival as April 16 says he entered the agency during a snowstorm, begging for food and shelter.\n\nA video dramatization presents Packer as the sole survivor of a six-man party that tried to cross the San Juan Mountains in the Colorado Territory; the underlying historical accounts likewise say he emerged as the sole survivor. Those accounts say the six men lost their way and became snowbound, that five prospectors died agonizing deaths in the mountain cold, and that a sixth man had his life irrevocably altered by the events. One account says that only Packer returned in the winter of 1874. The expedition account also records that Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon had been a member of the original party that left Provo, wisely stayed in Chief Ouray\u2019s camp, and later reached the destination safely with Bob McGrue and Preston Nutter\u2019s party.\n\nThe men neared the point of eating the horses themselves.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_2", "SRC_3", "SRC_4", "SRC_5", "SRC_8", "SRC_10", "SRC_11"], "fact_ids": ["SRC_1:F_20", "SRC_1:F_21", "SRC_1:F_22", "SRC_1:F_23", "SRC_1:F_24", "SRC_1:F_25", "SRC_1:F_26", "SRC_1:F_27", "SRC_1:F_28", "SRC_1:F_29", "SRC_1:F_30", "SRC_1:F_32", "SRC_1:F_33", "SRC_1:F_34", "SRC_2:F_5", "SRC_2:F_7", "SRC_2:F_10", "SRC_3:F_12", "SRC_3:F_35", "SRC_3:F_48", "SRC_3:F_49", "SRC_3:F_50", "SRC_3:F_51", "SRC_3:F_52", "SRC_3:F_53", "SRC_3:F_55", "SRC_3:F_56", "SRC_3:F_57", "SRC_3:F_58", "SRC_3:F_59", "SRC_3:F_60", "SRC_3:F_61", "SRC_3:F_62", "SRC_3:F_63", "SRC_3:F_64", "SRC_3:F_65", "SRC_3:F_66", "SRC_3:F_67", "SRC_3:F_68", "SRC_3:F_69", "SRC_3:F_70", "SRC_3:F_71", "SRC_3:F_223", "SRC_3:F_261", "SRC_3:F_262", "SRC_3:F_263", "SRC_3:F_264", "SRC_4:F_3", "SRC_4:F_6", "SRC_5:F_4", "SRC_5:F_35", "SRC_5:F_36", "SRC_8:F_12", "SRC_8:F_13", "SRC_8:F_14", "SRC_8:F_15", "SRC_8:F_16", "SRC_8:F_17", "SRC_10:F_3", "SRC_10:F_5", "SRC_10:F_6", "SRC_10:F_7", "SRC_10:F_8", "SRC_10:F_9", "SRC_10:F_10", "SRC_10:F_11", "SRC_10:F_12", "SRC_11:F_44", "SRC_11:F_45", "SRC_11:F_46", "SRC_11:F_47", "SRC_11:F_49", "SRC_11:F_56", "SRC_11:F_57"]}, {"title": "Packer\u2019s legacy", "chips": [{"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}], "body": "Packer\u2019s story of cannibalism is presented as a danger story about nineteenth-century prospectors in the Rocky Mountains and as one of the West\u2019s most grisly and enduring legends and murder mysteries. The case reflects the eagerness of prospectors to trespass into the Ute reservation, illustrates how swiftly development took place once the San Juan region opened, and attracted national and international attention to Lake City and its environs. Local papers gave the case constant coverage, using sensational headlines, negative comments about Packer\u2019s character, and imaginative theories that drew national and international attention. Harper\u2019s Weekly coverage also drew national attention to the grisly crime; an illustrator from Harper\u2019s Weekly recorded the scene in visual graphic detail, and copies of the portrait photograph were sold for 50 cents to the curious.\n\nThe area northeast of Lake City where Packer\u2019s party got lost is labeled \u201cCannibal Plateau\u201d on maps, and the site where the bodies were found five miles beyond town is called Deadman\u2019s Gulch. The text says Packer evolved from Old West infamy to New West celebrity. His pistol is now on display at the Museum of Western Colorado. In Lake City, \u201cAl Packer Days,\u201d the Packer Burger at the Cannibal Grill, and a wooden marker for the Alferd Packer Massacre Site are popular attractions. Travel magazines say the Hinsdale County Museum has the largest collection of Packer memorabilia known, including skull fragments, clothing buttons from victims, and shackles used when Packer was imprisoned. Tourists are advised not to miss the actual burial site, which is five minutes from town.\n\nThe modern myth states that Packer was convicted of cannibalism, but the text says that belief is totally incorrect. He was tried and found guilty of murder in the first trial, and of manslaughter in the second. Nearly all of Saguache and soon nearly the entire country and beyond believed Packer\u2019s responsibility for the deaths was beyond doubt. Cannibalism was not seen as the main issue of Packer\u2019s guilt. People then knew the Donner Party story and understood to some degree the need to eat in the wilderness. Cannibalism was and is not illegal per se in the United States unless a murder is committed to obtain the flesh. If someone murders to obtain flesh, the charge is murder, with the cannibalism treated as desecration or abuse of a corpse. Packer claimed for the rest of his life that he was unjustly vilified and convicted for cannibalism rather than for murder, which he continued to deny.\n\nThe sources disagree about parts of Packer\u2019s punishment and later life, and those disagreements remain part of the legacy. One account says Packer had served 18 years of his 40-year sentence, while another says he served 16 years of that 40-year sentence before being released. The latter account says he was originally sentenced to death, received a 40-year sentence after a second trial in 1885, and lived out the rest of his life in relative peace. Another source says he was convicted of five counts of voluntary manslaughter in Gunnison in 1886 and sentenced to 40 years. The text says Packer ultimately found some redemption from the people of Colorado, or at least from the justice system. It also says that he was the most celebrated Colorado convict, wooed and loved despite his life sentence for eating his camping compadres.\n\nPacker\u2019s name and story entered books, film, music, restaurants, and jokes. In Dashiell Hammett\u2019s novel The Thin Man, Nick Charles advises Gilbert Wynant to read a selection about Packer\u2019s expeditions. That selection comes from Thomas Samuel Duke\u2019s Celebrated Criminal Cases of America, where Thomas Samuel Duke gave Packer\u2019s first name as Alfred. Harold Schechter wrote the true-crime book Man-Eater about Packer. In 1993, CU film students Matt Stone, a CU film student, and Trey Parker, a CU film student, produced Cannibal! The Musical; Matt Stone and Trey Parker later created the television show South Park. Cannibal! The Musical was Trey Parker\u2019s directorial debut, and the text says it was short-lived, like Packer\u2019s companions. The story is being dramatized in the film rather than simply reported, and the underlying account is Packer\u2019s expedition and its aftermath.\n\nThe University of Colorado Boulder has an Alferd Packer Grill where students are greeted by a giant portrait of Packer and the slogan \u201cHave a friend for lunch!\u201d The cafeteria grill\u2019s slogan was \u201cHave a friend for lunch!\u201d, customers can order an El Canibal beefburger, and a giant map outlining Packer\u2019s travels through Colorado was displayed on the wall. The grill has since been renamed the Alferd Packer Restaurant & Grill. A commenter says they first learned about Packer while attending CU Boulder and learning how the Alferd E. Packer Grill got its name. UC-Boulder students have hosted Al Packer Days wearing T-shirts with slogans like \u201cFinger lickin\u2019 good\u201d and \u201cWhen you care enough to eat the very best.\u201d The text mentions Alferd Packer\u2019s High Protein Gourmet Cookbook as an example of gallows humor about the story, but also says there is really nothing funny about the tale.\n\nThe University of Colorado example was echoed in Washington. At a press conference on August 10, 1977, Secretary of Agriculture Bob Bergland and his employees unveiled a plaque naming the executive cafeteria \u201cThe Alferd Packer Memorial Grill.\u201d Bergland said Packer\u2019s life exemplified the spirit and fare of the cafeteria and would \u201cserve all mankind.\u201d Barbara Walters covered the event on ABC Evening News. The stratagem succeeded and the contracts were terminated soon afterward. Bergland removed the plaque after accepting the bureaucratic objection that it lacked official GSA authorization. The plaque is now displayed on the wall of the National Press Club\u2019s The Reliable Source members-only bar, and the National Press Club\u2019s hamburger is called the Alfred Packer Burger.\n\nPhil Ochs, a 1960s folk-music songwriter, used humor in \u201cThe Ballad of Alferd Packer\u201d (1964), matching the lighthearted ongoing attitude toward Packer and his alleged crimes. The song never appeared on any of Ochs\u2019s studio or live albums. C. W. McCall wrote the song \u201cComin\u2019 Back for More\u201d about Packer. Packer remains a wildly popular figure in Colorado state history. A restaurant in Boulder, Colorado, called the Alferd Packer Grill was named in his honor, and events and traditions honoring Packer take place annually in Colorado and 12 other states.\n\nLake City today treats Packer as both its patron saint and its antihero. Lake City is a remote mountain town at an elevation of 8,661 feet. Most visitors come to hunt, fish, or hike, and some hope to glimpse Al Packer\u2019s ghost. The town has businesses called the Packer Saloon & Cannibal Grill and the Cannibal Cabins. Lake City has hosted an annual Al Packer Day with coffin races, mystery-meat eating competitions, and an Al Packer look-alike contest. Grant Houston of the Hinsdale County Historical Society says many local people, including himself, would say Packer was guilty. Houston says local tradition held that Packer lured the men up there for money or guns he coveted, killed them, and fled. The courthouse contains Packer memorabilia, newspaper accounts, and official trial records.\n\nPacker\u2019s grave in Littleton has been left with offerings such as a toy firetruck, a marijuana vape pen, and a vase for flowers. A wooden bench invites visitors to sit and reflect at Packer\u2019s grave. Packer was buried in Littleton, Colorado. The sources differ on the exact circumstances and date of his death: one says he died in 1907 at a ranch near Deer Creek in western Jefferson County, another says he died two years after becoming a local celebrity in Gunnison, and another says he died alone in Littleton at age 69. His death was cited as \u201cDementia \u2013 trouble & worry,\u201d though his clinical cause of death has also been described as a stroke. Another source says his official cause of death was listed as \u201cSenility -- trouble and worry.\u201d Packer was widely rumored to have become a vegetarian before death and was reported by acquaintances as a man rich with stories and liked by children. He lived modestly and was reported to be charitable. There was also a rumor that Alfred was a vegetarian when he died.\n\nAfter prison, Alfred worked at the Denver Post as a guard for a while. People described Alfred after prison as kind, modest, and charitable, in contrast with the grumpy, greedy, and untrustworthy man he had been in the past. Alfred eventually moved to Littleton, where he bought a copper mine and returned to prospecting. Another account says Packer lived in Denver, then moved to Littleton to be closer to the mountains, bought copper claims, and resumed prospecting until epilepsy overcame him. He remained a free man for nine years after the escape; while in Gunnison, he became a local celebrity; and Packer lived the rest of his life in relative peace.\n\nThe story of Packer and the story that actually occurred are not always the same. A commenter objects to what he calls revisionist history and says he wants to keep the lore of the Old West without modern filtering. The text says Packer\u2019s legacy remained unsettled even after his peaceful death. It also says that the tale has been the subject of gallows humor and amusements, but that five men died agonizing deaths in the cruel mountain cold while a sixth had his own life irrevocably altered, so there is really nothing funny about the tale.\n\nThe sources also discuss Packer\u2019s female admirers through the idea of hybristophilia. Mental health professionals call the disorder in which some women become infatuated with heinous serial killers and mass murderers in jail cells hybristophilia. Popular culture refers to hybristophilia as Bonnie and Clyde syndrome. The condition is usually characterized by a woman communicating with a high-profile criminal in an attempt to kindle a relationship, and the text says hybristophilia is not solely a 20th-century phenomenon. Alfred Packer, also spelled Alferd, received fan mail from several adoring women while he was incarcerated for his crimes. Before Pry helped secure his release, Packer received a constant stream of curious visitors who came to ogle Colorado\u2019s nationally known prisoner. He also received many letters from adoring well-wishers while in prison.\n\nOne sympathizer was Mrs. Rebecca A. Newman. Newman worked as a schoolteacher while living in the Bronx neighborhood of New York City. By opening communications with Packer, Newman apparently hoped to form a friendship with him and express sympathy for his circumstances. When Governor-Elect Alva Adams learned of Newman\u2019s affection for Packer, he warned her that if she knew Packer and his horrible crime she would not care to defend him or excuse his conduct. Not dissuaded by Adams, Newman insisted that communications be opened anyway. Newman asked Packer for a full detailed statement of the whole circumstances connected with his most extraordinary trials and sufferings.\n\nWhile in prison, Packer often crafted leather belts, bridles, wooden canes, and watch fobs from dyed and braided horsehair. Packer usually sold those items as souvenirs to prison visitors and sometimes gifted such items to people he thought were special. Packer gave Rebecca a chain bracelet as a sign of affection and desire for a relationship. Through his correspondence with Newman, Packer hoped to find out what she could do for him. Packer wrote that he would make her proof he was worthy of the aid of philanthropic people and those better situated than he was financially and otherwise. The text says women like Newman still exist today, that contacting famous criminals who are experienced manipulators is a dangerous game, and that people who do not proceed with caution sometimes end up being eaten alive.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2", "SRC_3", "SRC_4", "SRC_5", "SRC_8", "SRC_10", "SRC_1", "SRC_7", "SRC_11"], "fact_ids": ["SRC_2:F_4", "SRC_2:F_17", "SRC_2:F_18", "SRC_2:F_28", "SRC_2:F_33", "SRC_2:F_34", "SRC_2:F_35", "SRC_2:F_36", "SRC_2:F_37", "SRC_2:F_41", "SRC_2:F_42", "SRC_3:F_215", "SRC_3:F_216", "SRC_3:F_217", "SRC_3:F_218", "SRC_3:F_219", "SRC_3:F_220", "SRC_3:F_221", "SRC_3:F_332", "SRC_3:F_333", "SRC_3:F_334", "SRC_3:F_335", "SRC_3:F_337", "SRC_3:F_338", "SRC_3:F_339", "SRC_3:F_340", "SRC_3:F_343", "SRC_3:F_344", "SRC_3:F_345", "SRC_3:F_346", "SRC_3:F_347", "SRC_3:F_349", "SRC_3:F_351", "SRC_3:F_353", "SRC_3:F_354", "SRC_3:F_356", "SRC_4:F_35", "SRC_4:F_36", "SRC_4:F_37", "SRC_5:F_16", "SRC_5:F_17", "SRC_5:F_37", "SRC_5:F_38", "SRC_5:F_39", "SRC_5:F_47", "SRC_8:F_41", "SRC_8:F_42", "SRC_8:F_43", "SRC_8:F_44", "SRC_8:F_45", "SRC_8:F_46", "SRC_8:F_47", "SRC_8:F_52", "SRC_8:F_99", "SRC_8:F_100", "SRC_8:F_101", "SRC_8:F_102", "SRC_10:F_21", "SRC_10:F_38", "SRC_10:F_39", "SRC_10:F_41", "SRC_10:F_42", "SRC_10:F_43", "SRC_10:F_58", "SRC_1:F_168", "SRC_1:F_169", "SRC_1:F_170", "SRC_1:F_171", "SRC_1:F_172", "SRC_1:F_174", "SRC_3:F_273", "SRC_3:F_280", "SRC_3:F_281", "SRC_3:F_282", "SRC_3:F_283", "SRC_5:F_14", "SRC_5:F_15", "SRC_5:F_34", "SRC_7:F_32", "SRC_8:F_96", "SRC_8:F_97", "SRC_10:F_23", "SRC_10:F_31", "SRC_10:F_37", "SRC_11:F_1", "SRC_11:F_2", "SRC_11:F_3", "SRC_11:F_4", "SRC_11:F_5", "SRC_11:F_6", "SRC_11:F_31", "SRC_11:F_32", "SRC_11:F_33", "SRC_11:F_35", "SRC_11:F_36", "SRC_11:F_37", "SRC_11:F_38", "SRC_11:F_40", "SRC_11:F_65", "SRC_11:F_66", "SRC_11:F_67", "SRC_11:F_68", "SRC_11:F_69", "SRC_11:F_70", "SRC_11:F_73", "SRC_11:F_74", "SRC_11:F_75"]}, {"title": "Polly Pry\u2019s campaign", "chips": [{"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}], "body": "Alfred Packer\u2019s story eventually reached Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan, who used the pen name Polly Pry. She was a controversial reporter for The Denver Post and later a freelancer in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, and was known for her connection to the Alfred Packer case. Pry became the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter in 1898 and was a member of the Denver Woman\u2019s Press Club. At age fifteen, she eloped to Mexico with the railroad industrialist George Anthony; five years later, she left Anthony and moved to New York to become a reporter. She was initially rejected by The New York Times, but later obtained a trial assignment there. Fellow reporters gave her the nickname Polly Pry because they said she could pry information out of anyone.\n\nOne account says Pry first met Packer and interviewed him while researching an article about Colorado\u2019s prison system and the Colorado State Penitentiary; a biographical account dates that meeting to 1901. After hearing Packer\u2019s story, she partly believed him and used her position as a reporter to try to help him. She believed he was innocent of murder and advocated for him in print throughout the trial. In the early 1900s, she launched a journalistic and media campaign to publicize his plight and release him. She wrote stories presenting him as a man of the people: an ex-soldier persecuted by the establishment, a common man caught in a regrettable situation, someone in the wrong place at the wrong time who had survived horrors most people could not imagine. She argued that Packer\u2019s sentence was so harsh because of the cannibalism, and claimed that anyone would have done the same in that situation. In her column, she also argued that since sailors were legally allowed to eat people when lost at sea, the same rules should apply to people stranded in the mountains. Another account says she used Packer\u2019s Army service to portray him as a common man who had been caught in a regrettable situation, had done what he had to do to survive, and had been crucified for violating civilized sensibilities by resorting to cannibalism.\n\nOver time, Pry\u2019s stories led local businesses to arrange petitions for Packer\u2019s release. They also led to petitions and requests reaching Colorado Governor Charles Thomas\u2019s office, although those efforts met strong opposition. Packer gained allies who signed a petition for his pardon. One supporter wrote to Thomas that, even if Packer had committed the crime, his privations made responsibility difficult to assign: \u201cThe line between sanity and insanity is often very thin, and I don\u2019t think we understand it.\u201d The Colorado State Archives says its Alfred Packer Collection includes letters to the Governor concerning Packer\u2019s parole, as well as parole records and a Governor\u2019s pardon. Packer had made many appeals for a pardon after entering prison but was repeatedly denied. A separate account says he filed five appeals and was roundly denied each time. In 1899, when Denver Post writer Polly Pry visited him at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Canon City, she wrote that \u201cthe corroding desire for freedom has eaten into his heart\u201d and that \u201cthe blind injustice of the world has seared into his soul.\u201d\n\nThe sources give differing accounts of the outcome and date. One says that Governor Thomas ultimately relented and, in his last official act before leaving office, paroled but did not pardon Packer on the condition that Packer would not try to profit from his story. Another says that, as his last act in office, Thomas granted parole but not pardon. A historical account says Packer received parole on February 8, 1901, after a campaign initially spearheaded by his old acquaintance Duane Hatch, who petitioned for nearly a decade before his efforts came to Pry\u2019s attention. A different account says Packer received parole in 1905, while another says Pry\u2019s campaign and Harry Tammen\u2019s intervention eventually led to a pardon on January 7, 1901. The sources therefore do not agree on whether he was pardoned or only paroled, or on the date. One account says Packer had served 18 years of a 40-year sentence; another says he was granted parole after serving 15 years.\n\nDenver Post publishers Fred Bonfils and Harry Tammen hired a lawyer to defend Packer. Because Pry\u2019s Packer column increased the paper\u2019s revenue and readership, one account says she persuaded Tammen to pay for Packer\u2019s defense and demand a retrial. After parole, Packer worked as a guard at The Denver Post and later as a ranch hand. Many people believed that his Denver Post job came directly from Pry and her employers securing his release. Packer showed enduring respect for Pry and called her his \u201cLiberator.\u201d\n\nAfter the trial, Pry was present during a confrontation involving W. W. Anderson, Packer\u2019s attorney, and the owners of The Denver Post who had hired him. The owners learned that Anderson had taken a double payment and demanded that he return the money. During the confrontation, Anderson pulled a gun and shot both men. As he prepared to shoot again, Pry jumped in front of Harry Heye Tammen and tried to shield him. She stalled Anderson long enough for the police to arrive and used her skirt to stop the bleeding. Rumors later circulated that Pry\u2019s skirts had saved her publishers\u2019 lives by slowing bullets from the gun. When union workers boycotted The Denver Post over her stance on labor issues and immigration, she founded her own paper, Polly Pry. Afterward, gunmen attacked her at home rather than at work. Pry was a strong advocate of free speech and, during World War I, came to the aid of French war orphans.\n\nPry\u2019s involvement has also been dramatized. Two film versions of the Alferd Packer story included her. In Jim Roberson\u2019s The Legend of Alfred Packer (1980), Cynthia Nessin portrayed Polly Pry. The film included the Anderson incident, but the man was not named and was called \u201cGunman\u201d in the credits. After Pry begins telling Packer\u2019s story, she does not appear again in the film, and the shooting is not mentioned again. The film depicts her as hard-drinking and forceful. Trey Parker\u2019s Cannibal! The Musical featured Toddy Walters as Polly Pry and went more deeply into her involvement with the case, but ignored the fact that she was married in order to make her a love interest for Packer.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_3", "SRC_6", "SRC_8", "SRC_9", "SRC_10", "SRC_11"], "fact_ids": ["SRC_1:F_147", "SRC_1:F_148", "SRC_1:F_149", "SRC_1:F_150", "SRC_1:F_151", "SRC_1:F_152", "SRC_1:F_153", "SRC_1:F_154", "SRC_1:F_155", "SRC_1:F_156", "SRC_3:F_274", "SRC_3:F_275", "SRC_3:F_276", "SRC_3:F_277", "SRC_3:F_278", "SRC_3:F_279", "SRC_6:F_5", "SRC_8:F_89", "SRC_8:F_91", "SRC_8:F_92", "SRC_8:F_93", "SRC_8:F_94", "SRC_8:F_95", "SRC_9:F_2", "SRC_9:F_4", "SRC_9:F_5", "SRC_9:F_7", "SRC_9:F_8", "SRC_9:F_9", "SRC_9:F_11", "SRC_9:F_12", "SRC_9:F_14", "SRC_9:F_15", "SRC_9:F_16", "SRC_9:F_17", "SRC_9:F_18", "SRC_9:F_19", "SRC_9:F_20", "SRC_9:F_21", "SRC_9:F_22", "SRC_9:F_23", "SRC_9:F_24", "SRC_9:F_25", "SRC_9:F_26", "SRC_9:F_28", "SRC_9:F_29", "SRC_9:F_30", "SRC_9:F_31", "SRC_9:F_32", "SRC_9:F_33", "SRC_9:F_34", "SRC_9:F_35", "SRC_10:F_34", "SRC_10:F_35", "SRC_11:F_22", "SRC_11:F_23", "SRC_11:F_24", "SRC_11:F_25", "SRC_11:F_26", "SRC_11:F_27", "SRC_11:F_28", "SRC_11:F_29"]}, {"title": "The missing men", "chips": [{"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}], "body": "According to the expedition leader, Alfred told him that after leaving the Ute camp, his party followed the river on horseback until the horses could go no farther. The leader unloaded their provisions and walked the horses back to the Ute camp. The men then likely walked farther down the river and took a path high into the mountains. The leader knew they did not have enough supplies to reach the agency, but he had grown tired of arguing with Alfred. They had only about 14 days of food, inadequate clothes, no snowshoes or flints, and hardly any matches. Their equipment consisted of only a few rifles, one pistol, a hatchet, and some knives. After the leader turned back, no one heard from those men again until Alfred appeared at the agency months later.\n\nWhen Alfred arrived, he looked bloated and well-fed, unlike the starving men people usually saw coming out of the wilderness. After he felt better, he went to Saguache, where he rented a room and a saloon and spent over $100 on drinking and gambling. He spent another $78 in the general store. People said he gave conflicting stories when he drank, sometimes carried a different wallet, and never seemed to run out of cash. He had sold his rifle at the agency for only $10, which did not explain his remaining money. Preston Nutter, the leader of the other party that followed the river, arrived at the agency in May and later went to Saguache for answers. Preston suspected Alfred was lying and told General Adams that the other men would not have left him for dead. The rifle Alfred sold belonged to an old man in the group, and the pipe Alfred left behind belonged to Shannon Bell. When Alfred originally joined the group, he had no money.\n\nGeneral Adams sent a rider to bring Alfred in for questioning. When questioned, Alfred said he got his feet wet and cold, stayed by the fire while the others looked for food, and assumed they had abandoned him when they did not return. A separate account says that nine weeks later Packer arrived at the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency south of present-day Gunnison with a Winchester rifle, a skinning knife, and a coffee pot containing live coals. When Packer arrived at the agency, he was haggard and worn but otherwise fit. Another of the original gold seekers asked where Packer had gotten his spending money. Packer admitted that the small band had starved in the San Juans, that Israel Swan had died from hunger and exposure, and that Packer had eaten him.\n\nTwo of the five men who had taken the Gunnison River route eventually arrived at the Los Pin\u00f3s Indian Agency, and the remaining three men, including Loutsenhizer, arrived a few days later. General Charles Adams told the men he had already met Alfred Packer and that Packer spoke of being deserted by his companions. All five men immediately discredited Packer\u2019s account to Adams and his staff. Loutsenhizer said the Winchester rifle Packer carried belonged to an elderly man in the party, and that the pipe Packer left at the agency belonged to Shannon Bell.\n\nThe following August, John A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper's Weekly, found the site of the incident. Randolph discovered all five bodies at the foot of Slumgullion Pass, two miles southeast of Lake City, Colorado, in a pine-shaded gulch. The bodies lay above the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, now colloquially known as Dead Man's Gulch. The site matched the description of where Packer had originally claimed that only Bell was killed. The snow covering the bodies and campsite had melted during the preceding four months. Randolph sketched the scene and alerted authorities in nearby Lake City. Harper's Weekly covered the story two months later, in its October 17, 1874, edition, and included Randolph's illustration.\n\nThe local coroner and law enforcement went to the site with about 20 volunteers and found all five bodies in various states of decomposition after four months outdoors. First responders said it appeared that extreme violence had befallen the men. Frank Miller's head was missing from the campsite. Frank Miller's and Israel Swan's corpses had been heavily scavenged and were little more than scattered bones, and Israel's skull had a jagged chunk missing from it. George Noon and James Humphrey were largely flayed torsos of rotting viscera attached to skeletal legs, but their faces remained intact and bearded. Humphrey's face was slightly more decayed than Noon's. Noon and Humphrey had head blows that suggested a hatchet and had noticeable broken bones.\n\nShannon Bell had skeletal legs, arms cut to the bones, hands that were still fully skinned, and a torso that was almost wholly flayed. Bell's remaining corpse was a mass of viscera with a thick red beard and bushy hair still on his face. The relatively undamaged state of Bell's face suggested that he had been the last to die. The top of Bell's skull had been ripped open. The three men whose bodies were still intact or partly intact had flesh and muscle cut from choice meaty locations; no attempt had been made to consume bone marrow or any organs. Preston Nutter identified the bodies as the five missing men. A rifle broken in two was found close to the bodies, and because of the damage to the remains, the rifle was presumed to have been used to bludgeon one or more of the men.\n\nNothing was heard from the six prospectors for nearly three months. The men became lost and, without proper provisions or equipment, were cold and hungry. Of the six men, only Packer lived.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_2", "SRC_3", "SRC_10"], "fact_ids": ["SRC_1:F_35", "SRC_1:F_36", "SRC_1:F_37", "SRC_1:F_38", "SRC_1:F_39", "SRC_1:F_40", "SRC_1:F_41", "SRC_1:F_42", "SRC_1:F_43", "SRC_1:F_44", "SRC_1:F_45", "SRC_1:F_46", "SRC_1:F_47", "SRC_1:F_48", "SRC_1:F_49", "SRC_1:F_50", "SRC_1:F_51", "SRC_1:F_52", "SRC_2:F_11", "SRC_2:F_12", "SRC_2:F_14", "SRC_2:F_15", "SRC_3:F_97", "SRC_3:F_98", "SRC_3:F_99", "SRC_3:F_100", "SRC_3:F_101", "SRC_3:F_102", "SRC_3:F_169", "SRC_3:F_170", "SRC_3:F_171", "SRC_3:F_172", "SRC_3:F_173", "SRC_3:F_174", "SRC_3:F_175", "SRC_3:F_176", "SRC_3:F_177", "SRC_3:F_178", "SRC_3:F_179", "SRC_3:F_180", "SRC_3:F_181", "SRC_3:F_182", "SRC_3:F_183", "SRC_3:F_184", "SRC_3:F_185", "SRC_3:F_186", "SRC_3:F_187", "SRC_3:F_188", "SRC_3:F_189", "SRC_3:F_199", "SRC_3:F_200", "SRC_3:F_201", "SRC_10:F_13", "SRC_10:F_48", "SRC_10:F_49"]}, {"title": "Alfred Packer\u2019s background", "chips": [{"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}], "body": "Alfred Packer, also known as John Schwartze, Alferd Packer, the Colorado Cannibal, and, in local newspapers, the \u201cMan-Eater,\u201d was an American prospector and self-proclaimed wilderness guide known for cannibalism. One account describes Alferd Packer, \u201cThe Colorado Cannibal,\u201d as one of the most notorious and most misunderstood tales in Colorado history. The sources do not settle the spelling of his first name: official court papers from the time spell it \u201cAlfred,\u201d while Packer signed his name \u201cAlferd\u201d; his grave is also marked with a veteran\u2019s tombstone listing \u201cAlfred.\u201d The Colorado State Archives\u2019 Alfred Packer Collection includes military grave registration, along with legal and historical records concerning him.\n\nPacker was one of three children born to James Packer and Esther Packer, n\u00e9e Griner. By the early 1850s, his father had moved the family to LaGrange County, Indiana, where he worked as a cabinetmaker. Packer is said to have had a bitter relationship with his parents. In his late teens, he moved to Minnesota and worked as a shoemaker. He suffered from epilepsy from an early age and was prone to seizures. During the American Civil War, he served in the Union Army. One account says he enlisted on April 22, 1862, in Winona, Minnesota, in Company F of the 16th Infantry Regiment, and was honorably discharged eight months later because epilepsy triggered seizures. He enlisted a second time, on June 25, 1863, in the 8th Iowa Cavalry Regiment at Ottumwa, Iowa, and was honorably discharged again on April 22, 1864, after his epilepsy caused further seizures. The shorter biographical account instead says that he enlisted twice and that both terms lasted only a few months because of epilepsy and seizures; both accounts agree that he was honorably discharged both times.\n\nAfterward, Packer traveled west and worked at numerous jobs over the next nine years. He worked as a hunter, wagon teamster, ranch hand, field worker, range hand, and as someone who drove horses and other draft animals across the West. He also took odd jobs for mining companies. He worked for a couple of months as a guide, but people later said he was ill-suited to the role and prone to losing his way. He had a habit of getting lost, arguing with clients, stealing from people, and lying. His seizures and overall attitude meant that he never kept a job for long. He worked mining-related jobs from mine to mine but never found prosperity in mining; he worked briefly as a miner in the Colorado Territory and then moved on to the Utah Territory. He also tried mining in Colorado, but kept losing jobs because of his bad attitude and because no one could trust him. People who knew him generally disliked and distrusted him because of his argumentative personality, near-pathological or scurrilous lying, and reputation for theft.\n\nIn the fall of 1873, a group of 20 prospectors set out toward southwestern Colorado and the San Juan country. The group was traveling to newly found goldfields near Breckenridge, Colorado; another account spells the destination \u201cBrackenridge.\u201d Six men, including Packer, ultimately continued into the San Juan Mountains during the winter, and two months later only one man emerged from the mountains. Rather than looking emaciated, as early explorers were often later found, he looked weirdly fed. The surviving man was Alfred Packer. A separate account says that the six men set off from Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 20, 1873, while another says Packer was one of six prospectors who headed into the Colorado mountains in February 1874. These accounts therefore give different dates for the expedition\u2019s departure.\n\nPacker was not initially with the party. George Tracy said that the party encountered a 23-year-old man called Alfred Packer near Provo. The expedition account says the group met him south of Salt Lake City, about 25 miles into the journey. Packer asked where the men were going, learned that they were headed for the goldfields, and offered his services as a guide. He claimed he was both a prospector and a guide who knew the territory between Utah and Breckenridge, or the San Juan territory, well. Because he had no provisions, he offered $25 to join the band. The prospectors accepted him because they needed a guide and thought that someone who knew the route would help them. Party members later said that Packer may have overstated or even fabricated his familiarity with the area. In fact, he had no idea where he was going, although he had worked as a guide for a few months.\n\nDuring the journey, Packer was reported to have left with only a Colt revolver and no rifle. This conflicts with the account of his later arrival at the agency, which describes him carrying a rifle. He was also reported to be greedy with rations, lazy, obstinate, and obsessed with how much money the party carried. He quarreled constantly with Frank Miller. Preston Nutter later called him a \u201cwhining fraud\u201d at Packer\u2019s first trial. His epileptic seizures made his presence in the group strenuous, and there were rumors that he had spent time in jail in Salt Lake City on a charge of murdering a hunting partner.\n\nOn April 16, 1874, according to the agency account, men at the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado, were eating breakfast when Packer appeared alone through a snowstorm. He wore rags over his feet instead of shoes, carried a coffee pot on one side, and had a rifle on the other. General Charles Adams, who oversaw the agency, had Alfred given food, and Alfred ate so fast that he threw up. Over whiskey, Alfred said that the others with him had become too exhausted to continue, that he had snow blindness and frostbite, and that he had been left behind to fend for himself. He claimed that he survived for months on rabbits, roots, and rosebuds and escaped only because of his experience as a guide.\n\nPacker gave more than one account of what had happened, and the sources report that he was known for cannibalism. The Colorado State Archives\u2019 collection includes his confessions, while the scientific team that exhumed the victims did not settle the spelling of his first name. The sources preserve both the story Packer told about surviving on plants and small animals and the broader historical account identifying him as the Colorado Cannibal and associating him with the cannibalism of his companions.\n\nThe Washington report likewise describes Packer as a \u201cscurrilous liar and thief by reputation.\u201d", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_3", "SRC_4", "SRC_5", "SRC_6", "SRC_7", "SRC_8"], "fact_ids": ["SRC_1:F_2", "SRC_1:F_3", "SRC_1:F_5", "SRC_1:F_6", "SRC_1:F_7", "SRC_1:F_8", "SRC_1:F_10", "SRC_1:F_11", "SRC_1:F_12", "SRC_1:F_13", "SRC_1:F_14", "SRC_1:F_15", "SRC_1:F_16", "SRC_1:F_17", "SRC_1:F_18", "SRC_1:F_19", "SRC_3:F_3", "SRC_3:F_4", "SRC_3:F_8", "SRC_3:F_19", "SRC_3:F_20", "SRC_3:F_21", "SRC_3:F_22", "SRC_3:F_23", "SRC_3:F_25", "SRC_3:F_28", "SRC_3:F_29", "SRC_3:F_30", "SRC_3:F_31", "SRC_3:F_32", "SRC_3:F_33", "SRC_3:F_34", "SRC_3:F_37", "SRC_3:F_38", "SRC_3:F_39", "SRC_3:F_40", "SRC_3:F_41", "SRC_3:F_42", "SRC_3:F_43", "SRC_3:F_44", "SRC_3:F_45", "SRC_3:F_46", "SRC_3:F_47", "SRC_3:F_285", "SRC_4:F_1", "SRC_5:F_1", "SRC_5:F_32", "SRC_6:F_10", "SRC_7:F_7", "SRC_7:F_28", "SRC_7:F_29", "SRC_7:F_30", "SRC_8:F_8", "SRC_8:F_9"]}], "disputes": [{"claim": "The survival story is real in the sources, but the exact meaning of it is still contested.", "holders": "Against: the pushback in the same corpus.", "chip": {"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}, "case_for": {"heading": "Case for the claim", "text": "The survival story is present in the sources. Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, then tried every day to escape but could not. He said that, as a result, he lived on the flesh of the dead men for most of the sixty days he was out. The analysis likewise suggested that Packer did shoot Bell in self-defense. This account is supported by the reports that Packer was known for cannibalism, later confessed to living off his companions\u2019 flesh, and gave more than one version of the circumstances. The men had also neared the point of eating the horses themselves. Other reported details\u2014Packer\u2019s possession of several different wallets and his statement that several thousand dollars had been found on Swan and divided among the men\u2014form part of the surrounding story.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10", "SRC_11", "SRC_2"]}, "case_against": {"heading": "Case against the claim", "text": "The pushback in the same corpus disputes the interpretation of the survival story, but no specific contrary evidence has been supplied here. The materials provided do not identify what the pushback measured, who advanced it, or which part of Packer\u2019s account it rejects. Accordingly, the opposing case can only maintain that the reported confession, the self-defense account, the references to cannibalism, and the surrounding details do not by themselves settle the exact meaning of the story. On the supplied record, that pushback remains unspecified and cannot be tested against the evidence described for the other side.", "source_ids": []}}, {"claim": "There are real clues in the physical evidence, but the sources do not let it settle the case cleanly.", "holders": "Against: the pushback in the same corpus.", "chip": {"label": "contested", "tone": "contested"}, "case_for": {"heading": "Case for the claim", "text": "The physical evidence offers genuine clues, even if it does not produce a clean resolution. Five of the men believed Alfred Packer and went with him, while Packer claimed that Shannon Bell then shot the remaining men. Forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group, and the text says it appears likely that Packer killed another prospector in self-defense before gnawing on the bodies of men who had already died. Packer also said that Bell attacked him with a hatchet when Packer returned after searching for food, prompting him to fire twice with a pistol and shoot Bell in self-defense. The analysis suggested that Packer did shoot Bell defensively, and Packer was known for cannibalism. Taken together, these details provide real physical and testimonial clues, though they still leave room for dispute.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_3", "SRC_7", "SRC_8"]}, "case_against": {"heading": "Case against the claim", "text": "The pushback is that the murder conviction was overturned. That outcome undercuts any confident conclusion that the evidence established the case as presented and leaves the interpretation of the physical clues unsettled. This side's case rests on the single reported fact of the overturned conviction, rather than on a fuller alternative reconstruction in the supplied evidence; accordingly, it challenges finality without resolving what happened.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"]}}, {"claim": "He spent years dodging justice before he was caught again.", "holders": "Against: the pushback in the same corpus.", "chip": {"label": "contested", "tone": "contested"}, "case_for": {"heading": "Case for the claim", "text": "Their accounts say that, once his story was questioned, he escaped, hid from authorities, and remained on the run or \u201con the lam\u201d for eight, nine, or nearly ten years. They further report that he was eventually tracked down in Wyoming, captured nine years later, and returned to Lake City. On this evidence, he did not merely leave custody briefly: he remained free for years before authorities caught him again.", "source_ids": ["SRC_3", "SRC_5"]}, "case_against": {"heading": "Case against the claim", "text": "The pushback in the corpus points out only that Packer \u201cremained a free man for nine years after the escape.\u201d That wording establishes a period outside custody, but it does not by itself establish that he was actively \u201cdodging justice,\u201d hiding, or evading an ongoing pursuit. Nor does this counterpoint independently supply details about how he was eventually caught. Thus, on the evidence available to the pushback, the stronger language about deliberate evasion is not demonstrated; its case rests on this single characterization of him as free for nine years.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10"]}}, {"claim": "Polly Pry and other supporters helped push the sympathy campaign around Packer.", "holders": "Against: the pushback in the same corpus.", "chip": {"label": "contested", "tone": "contested"}, "case_for": {"heading": "Case for the claim", "text": "He had been sentenced to be hanged, yet he begged for mercy and promised to make a full confession. His story eventually reached Polly Pry, a reporter for the Denver Post, giving the case a public platform through which supporters could press for leniency. The physical evidence\u2014a beaten path from the corpses to Packer\u2019s crude shelter\u2014also supplied material for retelling the episode, while Packer pleaded not guilty and later testified in his own defense at the second trial. On this account, Polly Pry and other supporters helped turn the case from a straightforward hanging sentence into a public sympathy campaign. This case rests on the accounts in SRC_11 and SRC_4, rather than on a broad body of independently testable evidence.", "source_ids": ["SRC_11", "SRC_4"]}, "case_against": {"heading": "Case against the claim", "text": "The pushback in the corpus undercuts the idea that Polly Pry and other supporters established a persuasive sympathy campaign. Packer was known for cannibalism, and the five original party members said they did not believe a single part of his story. Only Packer returned in the winter of 1874; of the six men, he alone lived, making his account impossible for the other members to confirm. The jury rejected his story, even though he pleaded not guilty, and the record warns that the story Packer told and what actually occurred are not always the same. Afterward, Packer went on to Saguache. From this perspective, the public narrative was met by serious credibility objections, and the available evidence does not show that the sympathy effort overcame them.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10", "SRC_3", "SRC_4", "SRC_5", "SRC_8"]}}, {"claim": "The honest answer is that the sources still do not agree on whether Packer was murderer, survivor, or both.", "holders": "Against: the pushback in the same corpus.", "chip": {"label": "established", "tone": "solid"}, "case_for": {"heading": "Case that the sources still do not agree", "text": "The evidence points in different directions: Alfred\u2019s charges were expected to be upgraded from manslaughter to first-degree murder, yet his murder conviction was overturned. She said that the sentence was so harsh because of the cannibalism, while other evidence indicates that cannibalism was not regarded as the main issue in determining Packer\u2019s guilt. Some forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group, and Packer was known for cannibalism. At the same time, a modern myth says that Packer was convicted of cannibalism, even though the murder conviction was overturned. Taken together, these accounts leave open whether Packer should be understood as a murderer, a survivor, or both.", "source_ids": ["SRC_10", "SRC_2", "SRC_3", "SRC_7", "SRC_8"]}, "case_against": {"heading": "Pushback against the claim", "text": "The corpus identifies pushback against this conclusion, but no specific evidence for that pushback has been supplied here. It therefore cannot be stated more precisely who made the objection, what was measured or reported, or how it establishes that the sources agree on whether Packer was a murderer, a survivor, or both. On the evidence provided, the against case rests only on the existence of that unspecified pushback, rather than on a described source or independently stated finding.", "source_ids": []}}, {"claim": "Packer provided multiple contradictory accounts regarding the deaths of his companions and how he obtained their belongings and money.", "holders": "Held across: SRC_1.", "chip": {"label": "contested", "tone": "contested"}, "case_for": {"heading": "Case for the claim", "text": "Packer was known for cannibalism, pleaded not guilty, and was evasive when asked about his missing companions. Under interrogation, he gave the first of multiple contradictory confessions, and he then went on to Saguache. Taken together, this sequence supports the view that Packer changed his account of what happened to his companions and of how he came into possession of their belongings and money. This case rests on a single source, SRC_1.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"]}, "case_against": {"heading": "Case against the claim", "text": "Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and analysis suggested that he did shoot Bell in self-defense. Packer testified in his own defense at the second trial, and the evidence seemed to corroborate his claim that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer shot Bell in self-defense. Although Packer was known for cannibalism and pleaded not guilty, whether Bell was actually shot in self-defense remains unanswered. Packer was found guilty of Bell's murder in 1883, but that verdict does not by itself establish that he gave multiple contradictory accounts about the deaths or about obtaining the belongings and money.", "source_ids": ["SRC_2", "SRC_3", "SRC_5"]}}, {"claim": "Alfred Packer led five men into the Colorado San Juan Mountains in winter 1874 and emerged as the sole survivor in a well-fed state.", "holders": "Held across: SRC_1.", "chip": {"label": "contested", "tone": "contested"}, "case_for": {"heading": "Case for the claim", "text": "On February 9, Packer and five other prospectors left the Ute leader Ouray\u2019s winter camp, still facing roughly 120 miles of difficult mountain country before reaching the newly discovered goldfields. The five men went with Packer, and witnesses later testified at trial that he had deliberately led them astray to steal their possessions. Packer ultimately emerged as the only survivor and became known as the \u201cColorado Cannibal\u201d after confessing that he had eaten his dead comrades while trapped in the mountains in February 1874. His reported cannibalism is consistent with the account that he survived the ordeal in a well-fed state. This case rests on the account compiled in the single source SRC_1, including testimony and later descriptions of Packer\u2019s confession.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"]}, "case_against": {"heading": "Case against the claim", "text": "He was accused of deliberately leading the other five men astray and stealing their possessions, and he was eventually tried and convicted of premeditated murder. His death sentence was later commuted to 40 years in prison, and after winning a retrial he was sentenced to 40 years for five counts of voluntary manslaughter. The source also records that, after his story was questioned, Packer escaped jail and hid from justice for nine years. Although Packer was known for cannibalism, the legal history\u2014convictions variously listed as murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter\u2014complicates any straightforward acceptance of the claim that he simply led the party and emerged as its well-fed sole survivor. This case also relies on the single source SRC_1, whose account cannot be independently tested here.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1", "SRC_3"]}}, {"claim": "Physical and forensic evidence from the site presented conflicting indications regarding whether Packer committed murder or acted in self-defense.", "holders": "Held across: SRC_1.", "chip": {"label": "single source", "tone": "network"}, "case_for": {"heading": "Case for the claim", "text": "Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and the analysis suggested that he did shoot Bell in self-defense. This appeared to corroborate Packer\u2019s account that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer\u2019s shooting of Bell was defensive. Packer also pleaded not guilty and testified in his own defense at the second trial. At the same time, Packer was known for cannibalism, and he was found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883. Thus, although the evidence seemed to support his self-defense account, whether Bell was actually shot in self-defense remained unanswered.", "source_ids": ["SRC_1"]}, "case_against": {"heading": "Case against the claim", "text": "No evidence against the claim was supplied. Accordingly, this side cannot identify specific evidence showing that the indications were not conflicting.", "source_ids": []}}], "anecdotes": [{"text": "A commenter says the Lake City museum has items Packer created while in prison.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The museum preserves a tangible connection to Packer\u2019s years behind bars. These objects give visitors something more concrete than the surviving stories and legends."}, {"text": "A commenter says one prison-made item was a large and beautiful doll house for the warden\u2019s daughter.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The doll house suggests that Packer\u2019s prison work was detailed and skilled rather than merely utilitarian. Its association with the warden\u2019s daughter also points to an unusual degree of trust or goodwill during his incarceration."}, {"text": "A commenter says Lake City still races coffins on Main Street on the Fourth of July in Packer\u2019s honor.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The race turns Packer\u2019s grim story into a recurring civic spectacle. It shows how the community has transformed the episode into local tradition and entertainment."}, {"text": "A commenter says Packer seemed to be a model prisoner who made the best of the 16 years he spent incarcerated.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "This view complicates a purely villainous image of Packer by emphasizing his conduct during imprisonment. His long sentence becomes part of a story about adaptation and productivity as well as punishment."}, {"text": "A commenter says the photo above makes Packer resemble Charles Manson.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The comparison reflects how a photograph can shape the way later audiences imagine a notorious figure. It links Packer visually to another infamous criminal, even though the resemblance is only an impression."}, {"text": "A commenter says that as a young boy in the 1960s, his father took the family to the site or marker of the dinner event.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The family visit shows that the dinner site or marker had become part of local memory by the 1960s. It also illustrates how the story was passed down through ordinary family outings."}, {"text": "A commenter quotes his little brother asking why Packer did not just go into town and eat.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The child\u2019s question exposes the story\u2019s basic absurdity in a simple, memorable way. It reduces the elaborate legend to the practical problem of why anyone would choose such desperate circumstances instead of finding food in town."}, {"text": "A commenter notes that the blog misspelled Packer\u2019s name about half the time.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The repeated misspelling undermines confidence in the blog\u2019s presentation of the story. It also signals that the discussion is dealing with lore and inherited sources whose details were not always carefully checked."}, {"text": "The author replies that readers can still enjoy Packer burgers despite the correction of the lore.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The reply separates the factual correction from the enduring appeal of the legend. Even after the story is challenged, its name and imagery remain usable in local culture."}, {"text": "The author replies that Alferd\u2019s name is sometimes spelled Alfred in the blog because quoted sources used that spelling.", "source_ids": ["SRC_5"], "context": "The explanation distinguishes the author\u2019s own usage from the spelling found in source material. It shows that the inconsistency comes from preserving quotations rather than from a deliberate change to the historical name."}], "info_gaps": [{"question": "You don't have the primary trial transcripts or official court testimony from Packer's 1883 murder trial. Without them, you can't show viewers what was actually said under oath versus what modern sources claim happened.", "why": "A major historical murder trial that went to the state supreme court would leave behind formal court records and contemporary sworn testimony.", "go_get": "Query the Colorado State Archives digital collections specifically for the 1883 Lake City trial transcripts and court filings."}, {"question": "Your sources mention that Chief Ouray and Indigenous leaders warned the expedition, but you don't have direct contemporary accounts or recorded statements from the Ute Tribe members who interacted with the party.", "why": "Chief Ouray's encampment was the last known stop before the group entered the mountains. First-hand perspectives from the Ute witnesses who saw the group's physical condition and equipment are essential for a complete timeline.", "go_get": "Search for historical records from the Los Pinos Indian Agency or regional 1874 news reports detailing Chief Ouray's account of the expedition."}, {"question": "You have a nine-year gap between Packer escaping jail in 1874 and his recapture in Wyoming in 1883. Your sources don't detail how he successfully evaded authorities for nearly a decade under the alias John Swartze.", "why": "A long-term fugitive evasion is a major narrative arc in a crime story. A viewer would expect to know how he hid, where he worked, and how he was eventually exposed.", "go_get": "Research 1883 Wyoming newspaper accounts detailing Packer's arrest in Cheyenne and his life as 'John Swartze'."}, {"question": "Your sources note that Packer was paroled in 1901 and died in 1907, but you have no details about his daily life, where he lived, or his interactions with the public during those final six years.", "why": "When telling the full biography of a notorious figure who gained public sympathy and parole, you need to show the resolution of their life after release.", "go_get": "Look into local Littleton or Denver census records and local obituary archives from 1907 covering Packer's final years in Deer Creek."}], "source_trail": [{"source_id": "SRC_1", "title": "His Horrifying Ordeal in Colorado Mountains", "kind": "youtube", "year": "2023-11-19T00:00:00.000Z", "creator": "Scary Interesting", "contribution": "It uniquely describes Packer bursting into the Los Pi\u00f1os mess hall during a snowstorm in foot rags, carrying a coffee pot and rifle, and vomiting after eating too quickly.", "vault_anchor": "#src_1", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_2", "title": "Alferd Packer", "kind": "article", "year": "2009-02-08", "creator": "Author Gulliford; Andrew", "contribution": "It uniquely reports Packer\u2019s claim that the starving party cooked and ate their rawhide moccasins after three or four days without provisions.", "vault_anchor": "#src_2", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_3", "title": "Alfred Packer - Wikipedia", "kind": "article", "year": "2003-08-05", "creator": "Authority control databases", "contribution": "It uniquely notes that Packer\u2019s convictions included murder convictions later overturned and five counts of voluntary manslaughter, with his death sentence commuted to 40 years.", "vault_anchor": "#src_3", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_4", "title": "The Colorado Cannibal: The Story of Alferd Packer - Law Week Colorado", "kind": "article", "year": "2022-02-09", "creator": "Jess Brovsky-Eaker", "contribution": "It uniquely records that Packer gave three confessions\u2014in 1874, 1883, and 1897\u2014and that his first version said Shannon Bell killed \u201cCalifornia\u201d before Packer killed Bell in self-defense.", "vault_anchor": "#src_4", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_5", "title": "Alferd Packer: The Truth is Out There (Or Right Here)", "kind": "article", "year": "2015-10-21", "creator": "history.denverlibrary.org", "contribution": "It uniquely reports that Packer escaped the Saguache jail, remained at large for eight years, and was convicted of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883.", "vault_anchor": "#src_5", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_6", "title": "Alfred Packer", "kind": "article", "year": "2026-01-01", "creator": "archives.colorado.gov", "contribution": "It uniquely identifies the Colorado State Archives\u2019 online Alfred Packer Collection, which includes penitentiary, parole, court, confession, gubernatorial-pardon, and mugshot records.", "vault_anchor": "#src_6", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_7", "title": "Alfred Packer ate 'em, forensic expert says - UPI Archives", "kind": "article", "year": "2026-08-29", "creator": "KAREN TIMMONS", "contribution": "It uniquely reports forensic professor James Starrs\u2019s claim that exhumed remains proved Packer butchered and skinned all five companions.", "vault_anchor": "#src_7", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_8", "title": "Was the \u2018Colorado Cannibal\u2019 a villain or a victim? You decide.", "kind": "article", "year": "2023-03-28", "creator": "Brendan Bures; Benjamin Rasmussen", "contribution": "It uniquely states that Packer suffered epilepsy, was discharged from the Union Army because of seizures, and that an exhumed skull had a hole in its forehead.", "vault_anchor": "#src_8", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_9", "title": "Polly Pry - Wikipedia", "kind": "article", "year": "2007-01-08", "creator": "Authority control databases International VIAF National United States France BnF data Other Yale LUX", "contribution": "It uniquely identifies Polly Pry as Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan, the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter, whose career connected her to the Packer case.", "vault_anchor": "#src_9", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_10", "title": "Alferd Packer", "kind": "article", "year": "2025-05-05", "creator": "Town of Lake City", "contribution": "It uniquely details the 21-man expedition\u2019s division at the Ute encampment into three factions after Chief Ouray warned them about the winter conditions.", "vault_anchor": "#src_10", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}, {"source_id": "SRC_11", "title": "Cannibal Correspondence - True West Magazine", "kind": "article", "year": "2022-09-06", "creator": "Kellen Cutsforth", "contribution": "It uniquely describes Packer receiving romantic fan mail from adoring women while incarcerated, framing this as an early example of what is now called hybristophilia.", "vault_anchor": "#src_11", "duplicate_of": null, "accessible": true, "note": null}], "corpus_balance": {"domains": {}, "date_range": "2003-2026", "network_note": null, "stance_counts": {"believer": 1, "neutral": 10}}, "addendum": null}, "markdown": "# The full story behind a prospector's horrifying 1874 ordeal in Colorado's San Juan Mountains \u2014 what really happened, beyond what this video tells\n\n11 sources \u00b7 27,100 raw words read \u00b7 quotes verified 99% \u00b7 confidence medium \u00b7 2026-08-30\n\n## 1. The Read\n\nRead all eleven entries. Here\u2019s what is actually in the pile, told straight. The sources repeat one core story, but they do not provide eleven independent verdicts. The pile contains one narrative video, one archival collection page, several institutional and local-history accounts, a long Wikipedia entry, a forensic news report, and two magazine-style investigations. Nearly all repeat the same account: in February 1874, Alferd\u2014also recorded as Alfred\u2014Packer led five men into the snowbound San Juan Mountains, emerged alone two months later with their belongings and enough money to attract suspicion, admitted eating human flesh, and spent the rest of his life insisting that he killed only Shannon Bell in self-defense. The Wikipedia article repeats that account in grade-level versions, so those repetitions are not additional evidence. The sources offer different emphases: SRC\\_1 is the fullest chronology; the Colorado State Archives page points to surviving confessions, court files, parole records, governor\u2019s papers, and Supreme Court case numbers; James Starrs supplies the strongest forensic prosecution.\n\n**Expedition:** Packer\u2019s disaster had begun before the cannibalism. In late 1873, roughly twenty prospectors\u2014accounts vary between twenty and twenty-one\u2014left Utah for reported Colorado goldfields. Robert McGrue, leader of the larger prospecting party, led the larger party; Packer joined after the journey began, presenting himself as a guide familiar with the route. Born January 21, 1842, near Pittsburgh, Packer had served briefly in the Union Army before epilepsy caused two honorable discharges, and had worked as a hunter, teamster, ranch hand, miner, and guide. The accounts describe him as badly suited to guiding: he had little experience, got lost, quarreled, lied, stole, and fixated on how much money other men carried. Heavy snow slowed the expedition, provisions ran out, and by January 21, 1874, the prospectors reached Chief Ouray\u2019s Ute encampment near Montrose already eating horse feed and considering the horses themselves. Ouray supplied food and shelter, offered to let them remain until spring, and warned that no Ute would cross the mountains in those conditions. Accounts differ over whether he said they would die or gave directions around the mountains. Most stayed with Ouray or took the safer Gunnison River route, although that party also nearly starved and needed cowhands\u2019 help. Gold fever led six men onward.\n\n**The crossing:** On February 9, Packer, Israel Swan, George Noon, Frank Miller, James Humphrey, a prospector who left Ouray\u2019s camp, and Shannon Wilson Bell left Ouray\u2019s camp. Sources differ over whether their provisions were meant to last seven or fourteen days, but all agree they were inadequate. They had little warm clothing, no snowshoes, few matches or no flint, and only a few rifles, a pistol, knives, and a hatchet. Ouray advised following the river; Packer argued that a direct mountain route was faster and led the group upward. The expedition leader, Robert McGrue, took them along the river until the horses could go no farther, unloaded their provisions, and turned back, knowing they lacked enough supplies. Packer later said that within days they had eaten their food, rawhide moccasins, rosebuds, and pine gum. He described cold, hunger, snow blindness, prayer, crying, and a vanished trail. After nine weeks, or sixty-five days, he reached the Los Pi\u00f1os Agency on April 16 carrying a rifle, knife, coffee pot, and other equipment, with live coals in the pot in one account\u2014yet looking far better fed than his story suggested.\n\n**Suspicion and confession:** At the agency Packer vomited when first given food. In Saguache he bought a $70 horse, spent roughly $100 at Dolan\u2019s saloon on drinking, gambling, or poker, spent another $78 at a general store, and appeared to possess several wallets despite arriving supposedly penniless. The rifle belonged to an older missing prospector; a knife or pipe belonged to other party members. Preston Nutter, a prospector who rejected Packer\u2019s account, and Oliver Loutsenhizer, men who had stayed with Ouray, rejected Packer\u2019s claim that the others had abandoned him. The discovery of human flesh near the agency caused him to panic, faint, beg for mercy, and provide his first official confession, transcribed by clerk Herman Lauter. Packer gave three major confessions: the May 1874 statement, the March 1883 statement after his capture, and a final account sent in an August 1897 letter to the Rocky Mountain News. In the first, Swan died of hunger and exposure, Humphrey died days later, Miller was killed while Packer gathered wood, Bell shot Noon, and Packer killed Bell; he took money and flesh. In the 1883 version, Bell killed Miller, Humphrey, Noon, and possibly Swan while Packer was scouting, then attacked Packer with a hatchet. Packer said he shot Bell through the stomach, struck him in the head, and lived on the bodies for most of sixty days. His later letters kept that basic account: starvation made Bell violent, Packer killed him in self-defense, and cannibalism sustained him. He called the ordeal \u201cthe midnight horrors of starvation as well as those of utter isolation\u201d and \u201cthe greatest wonder\u201d that he returned to rationality.\n\n**Bodies and escape:** In August 1874, John A. Randolph, an illustrator traveling for Harper\u2019s Weekly, found all five bodies together in a pine-shaded gulch near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison, later called Deadman\u2019s Gulch, and sketched the scene for the October 17 Harper\u2019s Weekly issue. A twenty-person recovery party found severe violence: skull injuries consistent with blows, bodies stripped at meaty locations, deliberate removal of flesh, and cloth replacing shoes, suggesting the leather had been eaten. A path led to Packer\u2019s crude shelter, where possessions remained. The remains contradicted his account of men dying one by one in scattered places, though they did not establish the exact sequence. Contemporary observers theorized that Packer killed the men for their belongings, became trapped by the storm, and used their bodies as a \u201cnatural refrigerator.\u201d Before the charges could be upgraded to first-degree murder, Packer escaped the Saguache log-cabin jail with a key, penknife, and food; who helped him remains unknown. He hid for nine years under the name John Schwartze or Swartze. Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon recognized him in Cheyenne, Wyoming, on March 11, 1883, reported him, and helped return him for trial.\n\n**1. Murder for money versus killing in extremity.:** The prosecution argued that Packer falsely presented himself as a guide, led the men onto a dangerous route, watched their finances, killed them for money, and invented a survival story. Swan was reportedly carrying cash\u2014his family later said about $6,000\u2014and a valuable Winchester. Packer\u2019s contradictory accounts, spending spree, stolen-looking possessions, and attempted attack on agency clerk Herman Lauter damaged him further. At the second trial, hunters testified that deer, antelope, rabbits, and other game remained in the area, undermining his claim that starvation made cannibalism unavoidable. Packer\u2019s narrower defense, later championed by Polly Pry and museum curator David Bailey, was that the men died or were killed by Bell, Packer shot Bell only to save himself, and he ate the dead because otherwise he would have died. Starrs\u2019s modern middle position is that Packer probably killed the men for their belongings but resorted to cannibalism afterward out of necessity.\n\n**2. What the bones prove.:** On July 17, 1989, James Starrs\u2019s team exhumed the five men, who had been buried only about thirteen inches below ground. Starrs declared Packer \u201cguilty as sin,\u201d reporting blunt-force injuries, knife or defleshing marks, and a winter \u201cnatural refrigerator\u201d; UPI said four men had been killed by hatchet blows and the fifth skull was missing. Walter Birkby, the forensic anthropologist who disputed Starrs, the forensic anthropologist assisting him, disagreed: \u201cWe found nothing to corroborate or refute anything Packer said. We\u2019ll never know who did it based on any solid physical evidence.\u201d The fuller account records defensive hacking marks on the forearms of three skeletons, including Bell, and possible bullet holes in pelvic bones, but no definitive conclusion. The evidence supports violence and postmortem butchering more firmly than it proves who attacked whom. It does not show that Packer fired the weapon, that the gun was his, or that the shooting was self-defense. In 1994, David Bailey found an 1862 Colt revolver with three .38-caliber rounds loaded and an accession note saying it came from the crime site. A lead fragment beneath Bell\u2019s remains chemically matched the bullets, and an elk-hip test produced a similar hole. That is the strongest evidence for Packer\u2019s claim that Bell was shot. Grant Houston and Michelle Pierce question the gun\u2019s provenance because visitors routinely found old firearms and bullets near the site. The men were reburied after the exhumation.\n\n**Trials, parole, and death:** Packer\u2019s first trial in 1883 ended in a murder conviction and death sentence. The conviction was overturned because applying the later murder law to the 1874 territorial crime violated the applicable legal rules; the Colorado Supreme Court reversed the sentence in October 1885. His second trial, moved to Gunnison amid local prejudice, ended June 8, 1886, in five voluntary-manslaughter convictions and a forty-year sentence, eight years per victim\u2014the longest U.S. prison sentence then reported. The famous political insult about Packer eating \u201cfive of the seven Democrats\u201d is apocryphal or later retelling; the surviving court record gives a conventional judgment ordering him hanged \u201cuntil you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d Packer filed five unsuccessful appeals; the Colorado Supreme Court upheld the sentence June 19, 1899. Governor Charles Thomas paroled\u2014not pardoned\u2014him on February 8, 1901, after he had served eighteen years, on the condition that he not profit from his story. Sources otherwise vary between fifteen and eighteen years and between 1901 and 1905. Packer worked as a guard, ranch hand, or laborer, made horsehair bridles and dollhouses in prison, returned to prospecting, and was later described as modest and charitable. He died April 23, 1907, aged sixty-five, in Jefferson County, Colorado.\n\n**Legacy and limits:** Polly Pry, whose real name was Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan and who became the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter, portrayed Packer as a Civil War veteran and victim of a prejudiced system. Her campaign, aided by petitions and supporters including Duane Hatch, helped secure parole, not a pardon; the State Archives preserves the records that could test the campaign in detail. Packer\u2019s letters maintained that he killed only Bell, while women including Rebecca Newman wrote seeking his account and sympathy. After release, some Lake City residents saw a calculating killer; others saw a damaged survivor. Popular culture chose the joke: Alferd Packer restaurants and grills, \u201cHave a friend for lunch,\u201d coffin races, mock trials, a Packer burger, Cannibal! The Musical, and other lore. The strongest sources establish that five men died violently, Packer ate them, he was convicted of manslaughter, and deliberate killing is plausible. They do not definitively reconstruct the killings: no eyewitness survives; the chain of custody, every skull and pelvic injury, and the revolver\u2019s ownership remain unsettled; the Archives page lists confessions and court files without presenting them in full; and no source reconciles Starrs\u2019s certainty with Birkby\u2019s agnosticism. The case therefore answers neither exactly who killed whom nor why.\n\n## 2. The Players\n\n**Denver Post** \u2014 Newspaper that publicized Packer\u2019s case and employed him after prison\n\nThe Denver Post published Polly Pry\u2019s reporting and campaign on Packer\u2019s behalf. It petitioned for his release after he had served seventeen years, and Packer later worked there as a guard. Many believed the job came through Pry and the newspaper\u2019s efforts to secure his release.\n\n**Polly Pry** \u2014 Denver Post reporter who led a campaign for Packer\u2019s release\n\nPolly Pry partly believed Alfred Packer after hearing his story and visited him at Colorado State Penitentiary in 1899. She used his Army service to portray him as a common man caught in a regrettable situation, then publicized his plight. Packer was paroled in 1901 after a campaign led first by Duane Hatch and later by Pry; he called her his \u201cLiberator.\u201d Polly Pry was the pen name of Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan.\n\n**Los Pi** \u2014 Variant name for the agency where Packer emerged alone in April 1874\n\nThe supplied material uses \u201cLos Pi\u201d for the Los Pi\u00f1os or Los Pinos Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado. Packer arrived there alone on April 16, 1874, after traveling for 65 days, carrying a Winchester rifle, a skinning knife, and a coffee pot with live coals. Other members of the expedition reached the agency later or were said to have disappeared.\n\n**Colorado Supreme Court** \u2014 State court associated with the reversal of Packer\u2019s sentence\n\nThe Colorado Supreme Court reversed Packer\u2019s sentence in October 1885 on ex post facto grounds. Packer\u2019s lawyers had argued that Colorado Territory had no murder statute when the deaths occurred, before statehood and before Colorado established the relevant laws. The court therefore recurs in accounts of the legal technicality that affected his prosecution.\n\n**Israel Swan** \u2014 Prospector identified in Packer\u2019s confessions as the first person to die\n\nPacker said Israel Swan died from hunger and exposure after the party\u2019s supplies ran out, and that he ate Swan. In another account, Packer said Swan was struck in the head with a hatchet, after which the other men butchered him. Packer\u2019s first signed confession said Swan died first and was eaten by the other five men about ten days after they left camp.\n\n**Harper\u2019s Weekly** \u2014 National publication that illustrated and reported the 1874 deaths\n\nJohn A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly, found the site of the incident in August and reported it to authorities in Lake City. Harper\u2019s Weekly covered the story in its October 17, 1874 issue with Randolph\u2019s illustration, \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d The coverage drew national attention to the crime, although most historians conclude that the illustrator found the remains while on assignment in the area.\n\n**Lake Fork** \u2014 Area near the Gunnison River where Packer said the party became lost\n\nPacker said that after traveling with little food, he and Bell camped beside a large lake near hemlock trees. After two weeks, near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, he said he was lost and no longer recognized the surroundings. The area northeast of Lake City where the party became lost is labeled \u201cCannibal Plateau,\u201d and Lake City later developed Packer-related attractions including Al Packer Days and the Packer Burger.\n\n**Jefferson County** \u2014 Colorado county where Packer died in 1907\n\nAlfred Griner Packer died in Jefferson County, Colorado, on April 23, 1907, at age 65. The supplied material also connects Packer\u2019s escape, assumed identity, and arrest in Wyoming with his eventual return to Hinsdale County for trial. Accounts of his imprisonment and prosecution recur alongside descriptions of the Hinsdale County Museum\u2019s Packer memorabilia.\n\n**Colorado State Archives** \u2014 State archival source associated with the Packer case and forensic evidence\n\nThe supplied material links the Colorado State Archives with accounts of Packer\u2019s journey, prosecution, the ex post facto legal issue, his parole in 1901, and objects made or carried during his imprisonment. It also records that analysis at Mesa State College\u2019s Electron Microscopy Facility matched bullet fragments from the burial site to lead from Packer\u2019s old pistol. Packer\u2019s pistol is now displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado.\n\n**Los Pinos Indian Agency** \u2014 Agency where Packer arrived alone and where the separated party sought help\n\nLos Pinos Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado, was the destination chosen by the expedition after the party split. Chief Ouray advised the men to follow the Gunnison River north to the agency; the party that followed that route ran out of food, while Packer arrived alone on April 16, 1874. General Charles Adams gave Packer food there, and Preston Nutter later arrived and sought answers in Saguache. Two Ute men also brought pieces of flesh they said came from a human found near the agency.\n\n**Colorado State Penitentiary** \u2014 Prison where Packer was held before his 1901 parole\n\nPacker was imprisoned at Colorado State Penitentiary and visited there by Denver Post reporter Polly Pry in 1899. Pry wrote about his desire for freedom and launched a media campaign on his behalf. While in prison, Packer made horsehair bridles; one is displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado.\n\n**Hinsdale County Historical Society** \u2014 Historical organization associated with the Hinsdale County Museum\u2019s Packer collection\n\nThe supplied material connects the Hinsdale County Historical Society with the Hinsdale County Museum, which travel magazines describe as having the largest known collection of Packer memorabilia. The reported collection includes skull fragments, victims\u2019 clothing buttons, and shackles used when Packer was imprisoned. The material also places Packer\u2019s detention, prosecution, and intended execution in Hinsdale County.\n\n**Chief Ouray** \u2014 Ute leader who sheltered Packer\u2019s expedition and warned it against winter travel\n\nChief Ouray\u2019s camp near Montrose gave the party food and lodging after it arrived on January 21, 1874. He advised the prospectors to remain until spring, warned that the mountain journey would mean certain death in those conditions, and recommended following the Gunnison River to the agency. Packer ignored the warning and left Ouray\u2019s camp with five other prospectors on February 9; the party had only enough food for one grown man for a week.\n\n**Gunnison River** \u2014 Route followed by one branch of Packer\u2019s divided expedition\n\nAfter the party split, five men followed Oliver Loutsenhizer along the Gunnison River while Packer and five others headed through the San Juan Mountains. Chief Ouray had recommended the river route, and Bob McGrue guided Packer\u2019s men along it until the horses could go no farther. Two of the five men on that route eventually reached the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, while the river\u2019s tributaries and nearby terrain later figured in accounts of the deaths and the discovery of the remains.\n\n## 3. The Record\n\n- **1842** \u2014 Alferd G. Packer was born in 1842 and died in 1907. (SRC_10)\n  - This establishes the lifespan attributed to Packer in one account. It provides a chronological boundary for interpreting his military service, western travels, and later notoriety.\n- **1842** \u2014 Alferd E. Packer lived from 1842 to 1909 and was also called Alfred. (SRC_2)\n  - This account introduces a variant middle initial and the alternate first name Alfred. The differing name and death year matter because records about Packer may identify him inconsistently.\n- **January 21, 1842** \u2014 Alfred Griner Packer was born on January 21, 1842, in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania, U.S. (SRC_3)\n  - This gives a precise birthplace and birth date for the man at the center of the chronology. The Pennsylvania origin places his early life before his later movement west.\n- **1842** \u2014 Packer was born near Pittsburgh in 1842. (SRC_8)\n  - This places Packer\u2019s origins in the Pittsburgh area without supplying a precise locality. It connects the subject to western Pennsylvania before the events that brought him west.\n- **1846** \u2014 Rebecca A. Newman was born in 1846 in Herkimer, New York. (SRC_11)\n  - This establishes Rebecca A. Newman as a contemporary born in upstate New York. Her presence supplies another personal connection within the chronology, though the entry does not identify her relationship to Packer.\n- **1857** \u2014 Polly Pry was born to a wealthy family in 1857. (SRC_9)\n  - This introduces Polly Pry as someone whose family background provided considerable wealth. That status may help explain her significance in the surrounding story, although no connection or later event is supplied here.\n- **1862** \u2014 In the 1950s, a rusted 1862 Colt Police Model .32 five-shot revolver was found on Cannibal Plateau with two shots missing. (SRC_2)\n  - The discovery provides a physical object associated with the Cannibal Plateau episode. The missing shots and the weapon\u2019s condition make it suggestive evidence, while leaving its owner and exact use unresolved.\n- **April 22, 1862** \u2014 Packer enlisted on April 22, 1862, in Winona, Minnesota, and was assigned to Company F of the 16th Infantry Regiment. (SRC_3)\n  - This marks Packer\u2019s first listed wartime enlistment and places him in a specific infantry company. It establishes a military phase before his later western travels.\n- **1862** \u2014 His grave has a veteran's tombstone listing his original regiment in 1862, replacing a marker that was stolen. (SRC_3)\n  - The replacement marker preserves a connection between Packer\u2019s grave and his original military service. Its history also shows that the surviving memorial is partly a response to the loss of an earlier record.\n- **June 25, 1863** \u2014 Packer enlisted in the 8th Iowa Cavalry Regiment at Ottumwa, Iowa, on June 25, 1863. (SRC_3)\n  - This records a later enlistment in a cavalry regiment, showing that Packer\u2019s military service was not limited to the earlier infantry assignment. The Iowa location also adds another geographic point to his wartime movements.\n- **April 22, 1864** \u2014 Packer was discharged a second time on April 22, 1864, because of epilepsy. (SRC_3)\n  - The discharge ended this period of service for medical reasons. Epilepsy therefore became a documented interruption in Packer\u2019s military career before the later events in the chronology.\n- **1873-1874** \u2014 In winter 1873-1874, the prospect party led by Alferd Packer entered the San Juan region. (SRC_10)\n  - This places Packer\u2019s party in the San Juan region during the harsh winter season. It marks the setting in which the expedition\u2019s later crisis and Packer\u2019s notoriety developed.\n- **November 8, 1873** \u2014 Packer led a party of 21 prospectors that departed from Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 8, 1873. (SRC_10)\n  - The departure involved a substantial prospecting group and placed Packer in a leadership role from the outset. Its Utah starting point frames the expedition as a movement toward Colorado\u2019s mining country.\n- **November 1873** \u2014 Packer said that in November 1873 he lied to five men and 15 other miners near Bingham Canyon, Utah Territory, claiming he could guide them to Colorado Territory\u2019s rich precious metal region. (SRC_11)\n  - Packer\u2019s own account presents the expedition as beginning with deliberate deception about his guiding ability. That claim casts doubt on the circumstances under which the group formed and makes his later leadership especially consequential.\n- **November 20, 1873** \u2014 Several months earlier, on November 20, 1873, the party Alfred eventually joined set out from Bingham Canyon, Utah, as part of a group of 20. (SRC_1)\n  - This account presents a slightly different size and departure date for the group Packer eventually joined. The discrepancy signals that the chronology contains competing versions of how his participation began.\n- **November 1873** \u2014 The party left in November 1873. (SRC_3)\n  - This supplies only the broad month of departure, leaving the expedition\u2019s exact date and party size unspecified. It nevertheless fixes the beginning of the journey within the late-1873 period.\n- **1873** \u2014 In the fall of 1873, Packer left on an expedition toward San Juan country with 20 prospectors. (SRC_4)\n  - This version emphasizes the expedition\u2019s destination and the number of prospectors accompanying Packer. It supports the broader picture of a sizable party traveling toward the San Juan country before winter conditions closed in.\n- **1873-1874** \u2014 Alfred Packer was accused of cannibalism during the winter of 1873-1874. (SRC_6)\n  - The accusation places cannibalism at the center of Packer\u2019s historical notoriety. Its timing links the charge directly to the winter expedition, although the entry does not state what evidence or testimony supported it.\n- **1873** \u2014 After the war, Packer traveled around the country and arrived in Utah in 1873. (SRC_8)\n  - This connects Packer\u2019s arrival in Utah with the period immediately preceding the prospecting expedition. It helps explain how he came to be in the region from which the later journey proceeded.\n- **1873** \u2014 In late 1873, Packer joined a group of 20 prospectors headed to southwestern Colorado. (SRC_8)\n  - This describes Packer as joining an already organized group rather than forming the expedition alone. The destination identifies the journey\u2019s economic purpose and sets up the later events in southwestern Colorado.\n- **August 1874** \u2014 In August 1874, a prospecting party discovered the bodies of the five missing men. (SRC_10)\n  - The discovery ended months of uncertainty about the missing party and established that the other five men had died. It also provided the physical basis for the accusations that followed Packer\u2019s return.\n- **1874** \u2014 Packer appealed for a retrial on the grounds that the crime was committed on the Ute Reservation in early 1874. (SRC_10)\n  - The appeal shifted attention from the deaths themselves to the legal setting in which they occurred. Packer\u2019s lawyers argued that the reservation location affected which law and court could govern the case.\n- **January 21, 1874** \u2014 The party reached the Ute encampment near present-day Montrose on January 21, 1874, with their food and supplies exhausted. (SRC_10)\n  - Reaching Ouray\u2019s camp placed the exhausted party among the Utes after its attempt to cross the mountains had failed. Their lack of food and supplies made the group dependent on the winter encampment, but they soon continued onward.\n- **1874** \u2014 Alferd G. Packer was the prospector accused of committing murder during the winter of early 1874. (SRC_10)\n  - Packer\u2019s role as the sole surviving member made his account the central explanation for what happened. His later confession transformed an obscure prospecting disaster into a notorious criminal case.\n- **January 1874** \u2014 Packer and five others ventured into the wilderness of the San Juan Mountains in January 1874 against the advice of Chief Ouray. (SRC_10)\n  - The decision to continue despite Ouray\u2019s warning set the party on the route toward its winter ordeal. The group\u2019s choice connected the search for gold with the hazards that ultimately separated Packer from the others.\n- **October 17, 1874** \u2014 Harper\u2019s Weekly published an October 17, 1874 issue with an illustration by John A. Randolph of the scene of \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d (SRC_10)\n  - The illustration carried the incident beyond Colorado and presented it to a national readership as a dramatic frontier tragedy. Its publication helped turn the episode into a widely recognizable story rather than a local disappearance.\n- **February 9, 1874** \u2014 Those five men followed Packer into a storm on February 9, 1874. (SRC_11)\n  - The departure marked the party\u2019s final movement into the storm and away from the relative safety of Ouray\u2019s camp. Afterward, the six men were no longer seen together, and Packer eventually became the only one to emerge.\n- **1874** \u2014 Packer committed cannibalism during the winter of 1874 after guiding five men into Colorado Territory\u2019s San Juan Mountains during a snowstorm. (SRC_11)\n  - The episode combined a failed winter crossing, the deaths of five companions, and Packer\u2019s account of survival. Cannibalism became the most sensational element of the story and the feature that fixed his name in public memory.\n- **1874** \u2014 In 1874, six men set off into Colorado's San Juan Mountains in the middle of winter hoping to reach newly discovered goldfields on the other side. (SRC_1)\n  - The men were pursuing the promise of gold by attempting a winter crossing rather than waiting for safer conditions. Their timing placed them in the San Juans when food, supplies, and travel conditions were already becoming critical.\n- **April 16, 1874** \u2014 On the morning of April 16, 1874, men at Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado were eating breakfast when Alfred Packer burst into the mess hall during a snowstorm. (SRC_1)\n  - Packer\u2019s sudden arrival at the agency brought the missing-party story directly to officials and witnesses. His condition and account could then be compared with the later discovery of the five bodies.\n- **February 1874** \u2014 Packer was a prospector who became famous after confessing to eating his dead comrades while trapped in the San Juan Mountains in February 1874. (SRC_2)\n  - His confession supplied the most notorious explanation for how he had survived while the others did not. The admission made him both the principal witness to the disaster and the person suspected of causing it.\n- **February 9, 1874** \u2014 On February 9, 1874, Packer and five other prospectors left the Ute leader Ouray\u2019s winter camp. (SRC_2)\n  - The group\u2019s departure from Ouray\u2019s winter camp began the final stage of the journey. It also showed that the men left the one place where they had reached help and supplies before entering the storm.\n- **1874** \u2014 Packer was spared the death penalty when his lawyers found that the 1874 murder statutes had been repealed and replaced with a savings clause. (SRC_3)\n  - The ruling prevented the state from carrying out the death penalty, but it did not erase the murder conviction or the underlying controversy. A change in the statutes became decisive in determining Packer\u2019s punishment.\n- **1874** \u2014 Local hunters and officials said the winter of 1874 was severe but the San Juans where Packer's party camped still had abundant deer, antelope, and smaller game. (SRC_3)\n  - Reports of plentiful wildlife complicated any claim that starvation alone explained the deaths or the resort to cannibalism. The availability of game became part of the broader debate over Packer\u2019s conduct in the mountains.\n- **January 21, 1874** \u2014 On January 21, 1874, the party reached Chief Ouray\u2019s camp near Montrose, Colorado, in the Uncompahgre Valley. (SRC_3)\n  - The party\u2019s arrival at Ouray\u2019s camp briefly brought it within reach of assistance after its supplies had run out. Its later departure meant that this refuge did not end the expedition\u2019s crisis.\n- **April 16, 1874** \u2014 On April 16, 1874, 65 days after leaving, Packer emerged alone and crossed a frozen lake bed to the Los Pin\u00f3s Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado. (SRC_3)\n  - Packer\u2019s emergence after 65 days made him the sole firsthand survivor of the expedition. His crossing to the agency opened the official investigation and supplied the first account of the party\u2019s fate.\n- **1874** \u2014 Packer confessed to cannibalism during the winter of 1874. (SRC_3)\n  - The confession made survival itself evidence in the public mind, linking Packer to the deaths of his companions. It also ensured that the case would be remembered for the act he admitted rather than only as a failed expedition.\n- **1874** \u2014 Only Packer returned in the winter of 1874. (SRC_4)\n  - Packer\u2019s solitary return left no surviving companion to confirm or challenge his version of events. That imbalance made his changing statements especially important once the bodies were found.\n- **May 1874** \u2014 Packer gave three confessions, in May 1874, March 1883, and a final confession in a letter to the Rocky Mountain News in August 1897. (SRC_4)\n  - The three accounts showed that the story did not remain fixed after Packer\u2019s return. Differences across the confessions kept questions about his actions and credibility alive for decades.\n- **1874** \u2014 Packer was a prospector who set out for Colorado\u2019s gold fields with five other men across the San Juan Mountains in the winter of 1874. (SRC_5)\n  - The expedition joined the pursuit of Colorado\u2019s goldfields to an exceptionally dangerous winter route. Its outcome made the journey a story of isolation, failed survival, and the disappearance of nearly the entire party.\n- **1874** \u2014 Packer was the only member of that winter 1874 expedition who survived. (SRC_5)\n  - His survival left no fellow member of the party to offer a competing firsthand account. That absence helped make his later statements and the surrounding theories central to the story.\n- **1874** \u2014 Starrs said that over the years since 1874, Packer has been lionized, apologized for, and sympathized with, and that people have tried to have him pardoned posthumously. (SRC_7)\n  - The case never settled into a single lasting public interpretation. Its changing reputation kept Packer\u2019s name in public discussion long after the events and legal proceedings.\n- **February 1874** \u2014 Packer was one of six prospectors who headed into the Colorado mountains in February 1874 after being flush with gold fever and ignoring the dangers of winter. (SRC_7)\n  - The party\u2019s decision to enter the mountains under severe winter conditions established the immediate danger surrounding the expedition. Their gold fever helps explain why warnings and practical risks were discounted.\n- **April 1874** \u2014 Packer became the source of legend after he wandered out of the mountains alone in April 1874. (SRC_7)\n  - His solitary return transformed a failed expedition into a mystery that attracted lasting attention. The unexplained gap between the party\u2019s departure and his return became the basis for competing accounts.\n- **1876** \u2014 Alfred's lawyers found a legal technicality because Colorado became a state in 1876 and Colorado Territory had no murder statute under which he could be tried for murder at the time of the crime. (SRC_1)\n  - The lawyers\u2019 argument shifted the case from the facts of the deaths to the legal authority of the territory and state. It exposed a procedural weakness that affected how the prosecution could proceed.\n- **1877** \u2014 The Hinsdale County Courthouse in Lake City was built in 1877 and is the oldest operating courthouse in Colorado. (SRC_8)\n  - The courthouse provided the setting for the proceedings and links the case to one of Colorado\u2019s oldest continuing civic institutions. Its survival gives the later trial a physical connection to the territory\u2019s early legal history.\n- **1883** \u2014 Packer was apprehended in Wyoming in 1883. (SRC_10)\n  - The discovery ended years in which Packer remained at large and apparently moved through the region. His capture made a formal reckoning possible nearly a decade after the expedition.\n- **1883** \u2014 Packer was first found guilty of murder in 1883 in Lake City. (SRC_10)\n  - The verdict converted a long-standing disappearance and survival story into a criminal prosecution. It also began the sequence of legal challenges and arguments over the precise basis for punishment.\n- **March 1883** \u2014 In March 1883, Packer was discovered in Douglas, Wyoming, living under the alias John Swartze. (SRC_11)\n  - The alias shows that he was living under an assumed identity when authorities or acquaintances located him. His presence in Wyoming supplied the link between his years of freedom and his return to court.\n- **1883** \u2014 Packer told Newman that he was not arrested or censured until 1883 and that he was in the country and seen by all men during all that time. (SRC_11)\n  - Packer presented the intervening years as evidence that he had not been hiding from public view. His claim was meant to challenge the idea that he had successfully evaded notice or punishment.\n- **1883** \u2014 In 1883, one of the original prospectors who had stayed the winter with Chief Ouray ran a shop selling supplies in Cheyenne, Wyoming. (SRC_1)\n  - The shopkeeper\u2019s earlier connection to the expedition created a possible route by which knowledge of Packer\u2019s whereabouts could circulate. Cheyenne therefore became part of the chain leading from the old expedition to his discovery.\n- **March 11, 1883** \u2014 On March 11, 1883, Jean \"Frenchy\" Cabazon found Packer in Cheyenne, Wyoming, living under the alias John Schwartze. (SRC_3)\n  - The alias and the Cheyenne location tied the discovery to a specific witness, rather than leaving it as an anonymous report. Cabazon\u2019s identification helped bring the dormant case back into motion.\n- **May 19, 1883** \u2014 After seven days of testimony and examinations, he was found guilty of the premeditated murder of Israel Swan and sentenced to death by hanging on May 19, 1883. (SRC_3)\n  - The proceedings ended with the harshest available outcome after the court focused on a deliberate killing. The date of execution fixed an immediate consequence, even though later developments prevented that timetable from standing unchanged.\n- **May 19, 1883** \u2014 Court records state that Judge M.B. Gerry sentenced Packer to be confined in Hinsdale County jail until May 19, 1883, then taken by the sheriff to an execution site in Lake City and hanged by the neck until dead. (SRC_3)\n  - The order laid out the full procedure for carrying out the punishment, from confinement to removal by the sheriff. It shows that the judgment was intended to be implemented through ordinary county authority.\n- **1883** \u2014 In his 1883 confession, Packer said he went back to the fire, covered the men up, and brought to camp the piece of meat that was near the fire. (SRC_4)\n  - This detail became part of the evidentiary record used to connect Packer\u2019s own account with the treatment of the dead. It also reinforced the grim physical circumstances surrounding the camp.\n- **1883** \u2014 District Court Judge Melville Gerry, the judge who condemned Packer, reportedly told Packer at sentencing in 1883 to stand up because he was a \u201cvoracious, man-eating\u201d man. (SRC_4)\n  - The judge\u2019s language made the sentencing a moral condemnation as well as a legal judgment. It helped shape the memorable image of Packer that survived beyond the courtroom.\n- **1883** \u2014 Packer was found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883. (SRC_5)\n  - The verdict attached criminal responsibility to a named victim rather than leaving the prosecution at the level of a general accusation. It illustrates how the broader charge against the group could be narrowed in the courtroom.\n- **May 19, 1883** \u2014 The actual sentence ordered Packer confined in the Hinsdale County jail until May 19, 1883. (SRC_5)\n  - The formal order specified imprisonment before the planned execution, clarifying that death was not to occur immediately after judgment. This distinction matters because the sentence involved a defined sequence of custody and execution.\n- **1883** \u2014 In 1883, Packer was charged with murdering all five companions. (SRC_7)\n  - The prosecution initially treated the episode as a series of killings involving the entire party. That broad charge framed the trial as an account of the group\u2019s destruction, not merely a dispute over one death.\n- **April 9, 1883** \u2014 On April 9, 1883, Packer\u2019s trial began in a courtroom filled to capacity. (SRC_8)\n  - The packed courtroom showed that the case had become a major public event rather than a routine local prosecution. The testimony that followed would determine which version of the expedition the court accepted.\n- **October 1885** \u2014 In October 1885, the Colorado Supreme Court reversed the sentence on ex post facto grounds. (SRC_3)\n  - The ruling removed the original capital punishment on a constitutional ground rather than resolving the underlying accusations. It opened the way for the case to continue through another trial and sentencing.\n- **1885** \u2014 Packer\u2019s death sentence was overturned by the Colorado Supreme Court in 1885 because of a \u201cgrandfather clause.\u201d (SRC_4)\n  - This account gives a different explanation for the reversal than the ex post facto rationale elsewhere in the chronology. The disagreement shows that the legal history of the case was later described in competing ways.\n- **1885** \u2014 After a second trial in 1885, Packer received a 40-year sentence. (SRC_5)\n  - The second proceeding replaced the death penalty with a lengthy prison term. That outcome kept Packer in custody while leaving the case open to further legal and public attention.\n- **1886** \u2014 Packer\u2019s retrial in 1886 found him guilty of five counts of manslaughter. (SRC_10)\n  - The retrial narrowed the criminal finding to five manslaughter convictions. It marked a shift away from the earlier capital sentence while still treating the deaths as criminally culpable.\n- **1886** \u2014 Packer was eventually sentenced to 40 years in prison after being convicted of manslaughter in a second trial in Gunnison in 1886. (SRC_10)\n  - The Gunnison proceeding established the punishment that would define the remainder of Packer\u2019s imprisonment. The repeated reference to the second trial reflects the importance of that later conviction in accounts of the case.\n- **1886** \u2014 According to The Case of Alferd Packer by Paul H. Gantt, Gunnison photographer Frank E. Deane took a portrait of Alferd Packer during the 1886 trial. (SRC_10)\n  - The portrait connected Packer\u2019s public image to the courtroom drama surrounding the retrial. Its attribution to a named local photographer also provides a contemporaneous visual record of the proceedings.\n- **1886** \u2014 Packer was resentenced in 1886 to 40 years in prison. (SRC_4)\n  - The resentencing confirmed that the reduced punishment was not merely temporary. A forty-year term became the practical consequence of the manslaughter conviction.\n- **1887** \u2014 In 1887, Adams relented and forwarded her letter to the State Penitentiary. (SRC_11)\n  - Adams\u2019s change of mind allowed her letter to enter the penitentiary record or correspondence. That act helped preserve a personal intervention in the continuing effort around Packer\u2019s imprisonment.\n- **1898** \u2014 In 1898, Frederick Bonfils hired Pry for the Denver Post. (SRC_9)\n  - Pry\u2019s hiring placed her at the Denver Post, where she had a platform to pursue stories beyond routine prison reporting. Her later involvement with Packer\u2019s case grew out of that journalistic position.\n- **1899** \u2014 In 1899, Denver Post writer Polly Pry visited Packer at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Canon City. (SRC_8)\n  - The prison visit brought Packer\u2019s story into direct contact with a prominent newspaper writer. It became the point at which his imprisonment attracted the attention that would fuel a public campaign on his behalf.\n- **January 7, 1901** \u2014 The retrial eventually led to a pardon for Packer on January 7, 1901. (SRC_11)\n  - The retrial did not end the matter; it eventually became part of the path toward executive clemency. The pardon represented the culmination of sustained pressure after the conviction and imprisonment.\n- **1901** \u2014 After enough pressure, the Colorado governor paroled Alfred in 1901. (SRC_1)\n  - The governor\u2019s action converted years of advocacy into a release from ordinary imprisonment. It also shows that Packer\u2019s eventual freedom came through political intervention rather than a complete clearing of the case.\n- **February 8, 1901** \u2014 Packer was paroled on February 8, 1901, after a campaign led by an old acquaintance named Duane Hatch, who petitioned for nearly a decade before Polly Pry took notice. (SRC_3)\n  - Hatch\u2019s long petitioning supplied the campaign with an older personal connection, while Pry\u2019s attention broadened it through the press. The parole therefore emerged from both private persistence and public advocacy.\n- **1901** \u2014 Former Gov. Charles Thomas paroled Packer in 1901. (SRC_4)\n  - This account places the decision directly with a former governor, emphasizing the role of executive authority. It presents the release as a political act rather than the result of another judicial reversal.\n- **1901** \u2014 Packer was paroled in 1901 after a Denver Post columnist crusaded on his behalf. (SRC_7)\n  - The newspaper campaign made Packer\u2019s imprisonment a public cause rather than a matter known only through court records. Pry\u2019s writing helped turn sympathy for him into pressure for release.\n- **1901** \u2014 Pry met Alfred Packer in 1901 while writing an article on the prison where he was incarcerated. (SRC_9)\n  - The meeting gave Pry firsthand access to the prisoner whose case she would champion. Her article on the prison became the immediate setting in which that relationship began.\n- **1905** \u2014 Packer received parole in 1905. (SRC_10)\n  - This later parole date conflicts with the earlier accounts placing his parole in 1901. The chronology therefore preserves two different claims about when Packer was released under parole.\n- **1907** \u2014 Packer died alone in 1907 in Littleton, Colorado, a suburb of Denver, at age 69. (SRC_11)\n  - His death ended the life of a figure whose case had moved from a capital conviction to imprisonment and parole. The location and description emphasize the isolation of his final years.\n- **1907** \u2014 Alfred died in 1907 at age 65. (SRC_1)\n  - The stated age differs from the age given elsewhere in the chronology. That discrepancy is part of the surviving uncertainty surrounding basic details of his final years.\n- **1907** \u2014 The text says Alferd Packer died in 1907 and that his misspelled name and unique reputation live on. (SRC_2)\n  - The account treats the misspelled name as an enduring part of Packer\u2019s public identity. His reputation outlasted both the legal proceedings and his isolated death.\n- **April 23, 1907** \u2014 Alfred Griner Packer died on April 23, 1907, in Jefferson County, Colorado, U.S., at age 65. (SRC_3)\n  - This provides a precise endpoint for Packer\u2019s life and closes the chronology of his personal story. The location also connects his death to the Colorado setting central to his later memorialization.\n- **1907** \u2014 Packer died in 1907 in Jefferson County, Colorado. (SRC_7)\n  - This abbreviated account confirms the same final setting while omitting the more specific details. It functions as a concise corroboration of the death record.\n- **1907** \u2014 Packer died in 1907 at age 65. (SRC_8)\n  - This version preserves only the age at death, making the biographical endpoint especially compact. Together with fuller accounts, it reinforces the consistency of the reported year.\n- **1926** \u2014 Newman died in 1926 at age 80. (SRC_11)\n  - This marks Newman\u2019s death as a later endpoint in the surrounding cast of figures. The age gives the chronology a measure of his longevity without adding further biographical detail.\n- **1931** \u2014 The plaque also serves as a memorial to Stanley Weston, a USDA employee who lived from 1931 to 1984. (SRC_3)\n  - The memorial\u2019s purpose extends beyond Packer to include a USDA employee whose life belonged to a later period. This makes the plaque a shared memorial rather than a single-subject tribute.\n- **July 16, 1938** \u2014 Leonel Campbell Ross O'Bryan was born in 1857 and died on July 16, 1938, at age 80 or 81. (SRC_9)\n  - This supplies the full name and lifespan associated with the person identified elsewhere by a shorter surname. The uncertainty over whether he was eighty or eighty-one shows a minor discrepancy in the surviving account.\n- **16 July 1938** \u2014 Pry died on 16 July 1938, aged eighty. (SRC_9)\n  - This is a shorter version of the same death record, with the age expressed in words. It helps preserve the alternate naming used in the chronology.\n- **1964** \u2014 Phil Ochs, a 1960s folk-music songwriter, wrote 'The Ballad of Alferd Packer' in 1964 about the expedition and its aftermath. (SRC_3)\n  - Ochs\u2019s song carried the episode into the 1960s folk-music culture, giving the story a new form of public afterlife. Its existence also shows that Packer\u2019s reputation was being interpreted artistically rather than only through historical accounts.\n- **January 1965** \u2014 The Arrow reported in January 1965 that a blizzard trapped the six men without sufficient food. (SRC_4)\n  - The report presents the expedition\u2019s crisis as the result of a blizzard combined with inadequate provisions. That framing supports the starvation circumstances underlying later accounts of what happened to the group.\n- **1968** \u2014 In 1968, students at the University of Colorado in Boulder renamed the student union restaurant the Alferd E. Packer Memorial Grill. (SRC_2)\n  - The university naming turned Packer into a piece of student culture and institutional folklore. It also helped shift his image from a historical figure to a recurring campus reference.\n- **1968** \u2014 In 1968, students at the University of Colorado Boulder named their new cafeteria grill the Alferd G. Packer Memorial Grill. (SRC_3)\n  - This parallel wording records the same campus memorialization with a different middle initial and description of the facility. The variation illustrates how the name circulated in differing forms while retaining its commemorative purpose.\n- **1973** \u2014 The Littleton Cemetery Association cemented over Packer's grave in 1973 to deter grave robbing and vandalism. (SRC_3)\n  - The grave\u2019s alteration was a practical response to fears of desecration, showing that public fascination had extended to Packer\u2019s burial site. It marks preservation and deterrence as part of the later story.\n- **1976** \u2014 The song later appeared on several compilations released after Ochs's death in 1976, including the 2010 On My Way compilation of demos from 1963. (SRC_3)\n  - The song remained available to listeners through posthumous compilations, extending its reach beyond Ochs\u2019s lifetime. The later demo collection also preserved an earlier stage of the composition\u2019s history.\n- **1977** \u2014 In 1977, U.S. Secretary of Agriculture Bob Bergland tried to terminate a contract for the USDA cafeteria food service but was blocked by the General Services Administration. (SRC_3)\n  - The failed attempt to end the cafeteria contract created the immediate conflict behind the later publicity. It placed Bergland in opposition to the GSA and turned an administrative dispute into a symbolic campaign.\n- **August 10, 1977** \u2014 To embarrass the GSA, Bergland and his employees held a press conference on August 10, 1977, to unveil a plaque naming the executive cafeteria The Alferd Packer Memorial Grill. (SRC_3)\n  - The plaque was presented as a deliberate embarrassment to the GSA, linking Packer\u2019s name to a federal workplace controversy. This transformed the memorial grill idea into a public political gesture.\n- **1980** \u2014 The 1980 film The Legend of Alfred Packer was described as an almost completely fictitious biopic of Packer\u2019s exploits. (SRC_3)\n  - The film brought Packer\u2019s story to a broader audience but did so through extensive invention. Its description underscores the gap between popular entertainment and the historical record.\n- **1981** \u2014 In 1981, then-Gov. Richard Lamm declined Packer\u2019s posthumous pardon. (SRC_4)\n  - Lamm\u2019s decision kept Packer\u2019s legal and moral status unresolved despite renewed public interest. It also set up the controversy that surfaced openly at the later bust unveiling.\n- **1982** \u2014 The United Press International reported in 1982 that Lamm had declined the pardon the year before. (SRC_4)\n  - This report independently places the pardon refusal in the preceding year, preserving the chronology of the controversy. Its wording shows that the decision remained newsworthy after it was made.\n- **1982** \u2014 At a bust unveiling of Packer at the Capitol Rotunda in 1982, Lamm said he knew some people had \u201ca bone to pick\u201d with him because he refused to grant Packer an unconditional pardon. (SRC_4)\n  - Lamm\u2019s remark acknowledged that the refusal had created opposition among those attending or following the commemoration. The bust unveiling therefore became not only a tribute but also a public reminder of the unresolved pardon question.\n- **1982** \u2014 The New York Times reported in 1982 that Lamm congratulated sculptor Tom Miller at Packer\u2019s bust unveiling. (SRC_4)\n  - The report records official recognition of the sculptor alongside the unveiling, giving the commemoration an artistic dimension. It shows the event functioning as a formal civic presentation rather than merely a protest over the pardon.\n- **April 1984** \u2014 The Washington Post reported in April 1984 that Gerry made those remarks. (SRC_4)\n  - The report places Gerry\u2019s comments in the public record during a period when Packer\u2019s story remained a subject of historical discussion. Their significance depends on the remarks themselves, which are not included here.\n- **July 17, 1989** \u2014 On July 17, 1989, 115 years after Packer allegedly consumed his companions, James E. Starrs led an exhumation of the five bodies near the Lake Fork river upstream from Lake City. (SRC_3)\n  - The exhumation reopened the physical investigation into the deaths more than a century after the alleged killings. Starrs\u2019s team focused on recovering evidence from the burial site rather than relying solely on surviving accounts.\n- **July 17, 1989** \u2014 WH/G visitors can access Packer books, including The Alfred G. Packer Victims Exhumation Project Lake City, Colorado, July 17, 1989. (SRC_5)\n  - The collection gives visitors direct access to documentation of the 1989 project and related Packer literature. It preserves the exhumation as part of the story\u2019s continuing public and historical treatment.\n- **1989** \u2014 In 1989, Starrs concluded his investigation by calling Packer \u201cguilty as sin.\u201d (SRC_8)\n  - Starrs\u2019s conclusion gave the exhumation a clear public verdict, despite the investigation\u2019s forensic uncertainties. It reinforced the view that Packer bore responsibility for the deaths.\n- **1989** \u2014 During the 1989 exhumation, investigators found a hole about the size of a thimble in Bell\u2019s hip bone. (SRC_8)\n  - The defect supplied a physical detail for investigators examining the victims\u2019 remains. Its location in Bell\u2019s hip became part of the evidence considered in the dispute over how Bell died.\n- **1989** \u2014 Bailey got the 1989 forensic samples shared with Richard Dujay at Mesa State College. (SRC_8)\n  - Sharing the samples extended the investigation beyond the original exhumation team. It created the basis for later scientific examination at Mesa State College.\n- **1993** \u2014 In 1993, CU film students Matt Stone, a CU film student, and Trey Parker, a CU film student, produced Cannibal! The Musical. (SRC_2)\n  - The film brought Packer\u2019s story into a new popular medium through the work of two CU students. Its production helped shift the subject from historical controversy toward comic entertainment.\n- **1993** \u2014 The 1993 film Cannibal! The Musical was described as a more comedic and heavily fabricated take on Packer\u2019s story. (SRC_3)\n  - The film treated the historical material loosely rather than presenting it as a factual reconstruction. Its fabricated and comedic approach shaped how many viewers encountered Packer\u2019s story.\n- **1993** \u2014 The comedy musical Cannibal! The Musical was released exclusively to Boulder, Colorado, in 1993 and released in the rest of the United States in 1996. (SRC_3)\n  - The staggered release first connected the film with Boulder before it reached audiences across the country. The later national release expanded the story\u2019s reach beyond Colorado.\n- **1994** \u2014 In 1994, an 1862 Colt revolver was found at the site of the murders. (SRC_1)\n  - The discovery introduced a potentially relevant weapon into the historical investigation. Because it was identified as an 1862 Colt, it could be compared with accounts involving firearms used during the murders.\n- **1994** \u2014 In 1994, David P. Bailey, curator of history at the Museum of Western Colorado, began an investigation to find more conclusive results than Starrs regarding Packer's claim of having shot Bell. (SRC_3)\n  - Bailey\u2019s project sought to move beyond Starrs\u2019s conclusion by testing the specific question of whether Packer shot Bell. The investigation therefore shifted attention from general guilt to a narrower claim supported by physical evidence.\n- **1994** \u2014 In 1994, David Bailey found an 1862 Colt revolver while inventorying historic firearms. (SRC_8)\n  - The firearm emerged during routine museum inventory rather than from a newly excavated crime scene. Its identification created a possible connection to the murders, though the discovery alone did not establish ownership.\n- **1998** \u2014 In 1998, author James E. Bank wrote Alferd Packer\u2019s Wilderness Cookbook. (SRC_2)\n  - The book extended Packer\u2019s subject into a culinary and literary format. Its publication reflects the continued transformation of the case into popular culture.\n- **2000** \u2014 By 2000, Bailey had not yet proven a link between the antique pistol and Alfred Packer. (SRC_3)\n  - The absence of a proven connection left the revolver\u2019s relevance unresolved. Bailey\u2019s investigation had produced a possible artifact, but not the conclusive identification needed to support the claim.\n- **2001** \u2014 In 2001, Dr. Richard Dujay at Mesa State College analyzed archived forensic samples from the 1989 exhumation with an electron microscope. (SRC_3)\n  - The analysis subjected preserved material from the earlier exhumation to more advanced laboratory scrutiny. It represented a later attempt to extract additional evidence from samples that had already been collected.\n- **2008** \u2014 As of 2008, the skull was reported to be at the Ripley's Museum in San Antonio, Texas, after relocation from New Orleans following Hurricane Katrina. (SRC_3)\n  - The skull\u2019s reported location shows how the physical remains had become part of a museum display history. Its relocation after Hurricane Katrina also illustrates the vulnerability of the surviving evidence to events unrelated to the original case.\n\n## 4. The Files\n\n### The investigation `established`\n\nThe investigation began when a council was formed to investigate Alfred Packer\u2019s story. As the meeting was beginning, two members of the Ute tribe arrived with pieces, or strips, of dried flesh that they said came from a human found on a hill near the agency. Alfred panicked, fainted, woke up, begged for mercy, and told a different story. He reportedly said, \u201cIt would not be the first time that people had been obliged to eat each other when they were hungry.\u201d Alfred said that ten days into the journey the group ran out of food and the oldest man died first. According to Alfred, the remaining men ate the dead man and took his rifle. Five days later, another man died, the others ate him too, and they found $133 in his pockets. Alfred said another man died after that while he was gathering wood for the fire, and the remaining men ate him too. Alfred claimed Shannon Bell then shot the remaining men. He said that he and Shannon carried some of the dead man for food, hid the rest, and continued toward the agency. Alfred claimed Shannon later tried to kill him too, went mad, attacked him, and was shot by Alfred in self-defense. General Adams wrote Alfred\u2019s confession down and had him sign it as a sworn statement; agency clerk Herman Lauter transcribed Alfred\u2019s first official statement. Preston did not believe the confession and was sure Alfred had killed the men and stolen their things. Preston became so angry that he dragged Alfred outside, and others had to intervene to stop him from killing Alfred.\n\nThe historical accounts describe several reasons for Preston Nutter\u2019s suspicion. Preston arrived in Saguache with two other original party members and found Packer at Dolan\u2019s Saloon. Packer played high-stakes poker there and bought a $70 horse. Nutter found Packer\u2019s story odd because Packer looked well-fed and because abandoning a guide in snowbound mountains seemed unlikely. Nutter also thought it was strange that Israel Swan would give Packer his rifle and leave four men with only one rifle for hunting. Nutter said Packer was sulky, obstinate, quarrelsome, and a petty thief. The accounts also say that Packer had been a poor guide, often got lost, argued with clients, and had a reputation for stealing and lying. Nutter said Packer had been constantly trying to find out how much money the men had. Packer had carried a Winchester rifle belonging to one of the missing men and a skinning knife belonging to Frank Miller, and he explained the knife by saying Miller had stuck it in a tree and walked away without it. Nutter and Loutsenhizer later tried to discredit Packer\u2019s claimed qualifications as a guide, emphasizing his character flaws, inconsistent stories, and contradictions. The accepted theory at the time was that Packer had joined the party by overstating his qualifications as a mountain guide familiar with the area to accompany the men to Breckenridge; at best, that theory held, he led the party to miserable deaths through gross incompetence. A more popular theory was that he had left Ouray\u2019s camp with the five men with a premeditated plan to lead them into the wilderness, kill them, and rob them.\n\nThe men persuaded General Adams to send a mounted agency officer to Saguache to retrieve Packer for questioning while pretending to recruit him for a search party. Packer was preparing to leave when Nutter and other men approached him again, and the agency officer arrived in time to separate the men. The officer told Packer he had been sent to enlist him as a guide for the search party. Packer agreed to return to Los Pin\u00f3s because he had little alternative besides vigilante justice. At the agency, Packer met General Adams and five men he had not seen since February. Loutsenhizer demanded an explanation, and Packer repeated his earlier story. Packer acted amazed that the men had not been heard from and said he was concerned for their well-being. When asked where his money came from, Packer said he had received a cash loan from a man in Saguache. The officer also reported that Packer had arrived in Saguache with plenty of cash, several wallets, and possessions belonging to the missing men, while no one in town claimed to have lent him any money. Adams convened a council of himself, Packer, and the agency officers. The two Ute tribesmen then arrived with the strips of dried human flesh they had found on a hill near the agency while hunting. Packer begged for mercy and promised a full confession. General Adams called a conference of the five men and the agency officers to decide what to do next, and the five original party members said they did not believe a single part of Packer\u2019s story.\n\nThe two Ute men said they knew of a place next to a lake that Packer had described, about 50 miles away across the hills. A search party led by Herman Lauter and consisting of the five Utah miners, several agency officers, and Packer as guide was assembled at once. After two weeks, near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, Packer said he was lost and did not recognize the surroundings. Nothing was found and the party returned to the agency. On the way back, Packer tried to murder Herman Lauter with a knife. Packer was caught, restrained, and arrested. General Adams, who had been willing to believe Packer until then, concluded that Packer was dangerous after the attack on Lauter. Packer was taken to Saguache and jailed outside town by the sheriff for his own protection. His jail was little more than a log cabin on ranch property belonging to the Saguache County Sheriff.\n\nWhile Packer was in jail, observers challenged his credibility. The condition of the bodies contradicted Packer\u2019s version of events. The bodies were all together in one spot rather than scattered across miles. Humphrey and Noon still had large portions of flesh, muscle, and organs that could have been eaten long before Bell allegedly tried to murder Packer. The men had tattered cloths lashed to their rotting feet, replacing shoes they had probably eaten. Moldy and tattered blankets lay beneath and beside the bodies. A beaten path led from the corpses to a crude shelter used by Packer. There was evidence that the deaths had occurred before supplies were totally exhausted, and inside the shelter were possessions of the men that Packer had left behind. The theory at the time was that Packer killed the men before supplies ran out to rob them, got snowed in, lived for months in his shelter, and walked to the bodies to cut off meat as needed.\n\nIn summer, General Adams led a search in the mountains to find the bodies, but the search failed. In August, illustrator John Randolph was crossing the mountains near Lake City when he noticed something sticking out of the ground near a tributary of the Gunnison River. John smelled something terrible and found five decomposed bodies that had met violent ends. One man was little more than bones, suggesting animals had gotten to him afterward. Two of the men had blunt force injuries to the head. Shannon Bell had been stripped to the bones except for his hands and feet and also had a blunt force head injury. The remains suggested deliberate removal of edible portions, and except for the first man, it did not look like animals had done it. John made a sketch and told authorities in Lake City. The local coroner and law enforcement, along with a team of 20 volunteers, later retrieved the bodies. The coroner concluded that the men had been killed by blows to the head while asleep. None of the bodies had defensive wounds or injuries, and all had similar markings on their skulls. The bodies had rags under their feet, suggesting they may have eaten the leather from their shoes after running out of food. There was also a track to a makeshift shelter, leading investigators to think Alfred may have killed the men for their possessions and eaten them to survive.\n\nOther accounts identify Randolph as John A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly, and place the discovery at the foot of Slumgullion Pass, two miles southeast of Lake City, in a pine-shaded gulch above the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, a location later known as Deadman\u2019s Gulch. One account says that the snow covering the bodies and campsite had melted during the intervening four months, and that the local coroner and law enforcement went to the site with about 20 volunteers. The bodies were in various states of decomposition and had been left to the elements and animals. The illustrator\u2019s sketch was later published with the October 17, 1874, edition of Harper\u2019s Weekly under the title \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d Most historians conclude that an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly found the remains near the Gunnison River while on assignment in the area. An illustrator who accompanied the group recorded the scene in visual graphic detail, helping draw national attention to the crime. The corpses showed intense violence and skull fractures consistent with blows from a hatchet. After officials examined the bodies, they buried the remains at the site, and the search party returned to Packer\u2019s makeshift jail to confront him, only to find him missing.\n\nPacker\u2019s charges were expected to be upgraded from manslaughter to first-degree murder. Before that happened, someone gave Alfred a key, a penknife, and some food so he could escape. Packer allegedly received a makeshift key for his irons and some supplies, and escaped easily. Alfred slipped out of his cell and was not seen again for nine years. Months had passed with no definitive evidence of a crime, no bodies discovered, and no formal charges against Packer other than the attempt on Lauter\u2019s life. Saguache County authorities were reportedly unhappy about taxpayer money being spent to keep Packer under guard. Nearly all of Saguache believed through rumor or deduction that Packer was guilty of robbery or murder, and Packer\u2019s life was constantly threatened by nearby townspeople. Packer never said who helped him escape, how it happened, or why. It was presumed that his guard had been bribed by Packer or by someone else.\n\nIn March 1883, Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon encountered Packer when Packer approached him to buy supplies. Packer was living under the alias \u201cJohn Schwartze\u201d or \u201cJohn Swartze.\u201d Cabazon reported Packer to the local sheriff, who apprehended him and contacted General Adams. Cabazon was summoned to Cheyenne, confirmed Packer\u2019s identity, and accompanied him by train to Denver for Packer\u2019s second confession, which Packer signed on March 16. Packer said he fled because he feared mob justice from the people of Saguache. Because the actual crime was committed in Hinsdale County rather than Saguache County, Packer was sent to Lake City for detention and prosecution. Other accounts say Packer was jailed in Saguache, escaped, changed his identity, and was arrested in Wyoming before being returned to Hinsdale County for trial. After this arrest, Packer was tried and convicted of murder and sentenced to death, but the conviction was overturned on a technicality because Colorado became a state in 1876 and, before statehood, Colorado Territory had no statute under which he could be tried for murder. He was retried for manslaughter instead of murder and was eventually convicted of voluntary manslaughter. He received 40 years in prison, the maximum of eight years per man, which was described at the time as the longest prison sentence handed down in United States history. Other accounts say he was convicted of manslaughter in a second trial in Gunnison in 1886. He maintained that he had eaten the flesh of his companions to survive, but held to his story that Shannon Bell had killed the other men and that Packer killed Bell in self-defense.\n\nIn the century after the story, several groups conducted forensic analyses of the remains and compared them with Alfred\u2019s account. Those groups tended to conclude that Alfred was guilty. Some of the forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group. One group found marks across Shannon Bell\u2019s forearms, suggesting that he may have defended himself from an attack. In Alfred\u2019s account, Shannon was only shot once and then finished quickly. The revolver was still loaded with three .38-caliber bullets, and the bullets matched some fragments of lead found in Shannon Bell\u2019s hip. That evidence suggested that at least part of Alfred\u2019s story may have been true.\n\nThe later forensic record is not consistent. On July 17, 1989, grad students dug up the skeletons in Lake City, Colorado, under hot, muggy conditions. When the first skull was exposed, it had a gaping hole in its forehead. Reporters rushed to Lake City\u2019s four public pay phones to file a story headlined \u201cProfessor Exhumes 100-Year-Old Victims of Colorado Cannibal.\u201d The team led by James Starrs, a professor of law and forensic science at George Washington University, unearthed the skeletons of five men from a mass grave in the San Juan mountains. Starrs led the exhumation project, which cost an estimated $10,000 to $15,000 and used modern forensic techniques including ground-penetrating radar and laboratory inspection of the remains. Starrs said the team\u2019s examination showed that none of the victims had been shot, that four had been killed by hatchet blows to the head, and that the fifth skull had not been found. He said all five sets of bones revealed numerous knife marks indicating defleshing. He cited \u201cunmistakeable proof of cannibalism,\u201d said Packer had used the \u201cnatural refrigerator\u201d of the Colorado winter to preserve his victims and eat them until spring, and declared, \u201cHe was having human flesh filets morning, noon and night.\u201d More than a century after Packer was first accused of killing and eating five fellow prospectors in Colorado, Starrs said he had proven that Packer was \u201cguilty as sin.\u201d Starrs said Packer had \u201cbutchered his five fellow prospectors to death and then skinned them\u201d and feasted on their flesh, and that the added scientific evidence convicted Packer \u201cbeyond any shadow of a doubt\u201d in his opinion. Starrs said his aim was to settle the debate over Packer\u2019s guilt and to \u201cput forensic science on parade.\u201d He believed Packer\u2019s motive was initially to steal his companions\u2019 belongings and then to turn to cannibalism.\n\nThe exhumation account says the remains were found at the end of a residential driveway at a home belonging to a local surgeon and were buried only 13 inches below ground. Starrs and Walter H. Birkby said they did not think cannibalism could ever be scientifically demonstrated because it would require a picture of the person actually eating human flesh. Packer never denied cannibalism, so the investigation did not focus on that point. The evidence was sufficient for Starrs to conclude that Packer had murdered his comrades. Starrs believed Packer more likely murdered his companions for their belongings and resorted to cannibalism out of necessity rather than intent. The remains were re-interred and given a proper burial with funeral rites.\n\nExhumation showed blunt force trauma to the skulls of two men, with no other notable disturbance except damage from butchering. All skulls had damage, to varying extents, in their upper craniums, and fabric fibers were found in some skulls, suggesting their heads may have been covered with a blanket at death. The three other skeletons showed defensive hacking marks across the radial and ulna bones of their forearms, including Shannon Bell\u2019s, consistent with shielding the face and body from an attack. Shannon Bell was one of the skeletons with defensive forearm wounds, which could have been sustained while shielding his face and body from an attack. The skeletons showed no other noteworthy disturbance beyond damage made during butchering. All five skeletons had numerous differing post-mortem injuries, including depressed fractures, butterfly fractures, butchering trauma, and hacking trauma. Those post-mortem injuries appear to contradict Packer\u2019s claim of minimal cannibalism.\n\nTwo skeletons other than Shannon Bell\u2019s were found to have cylindrical puncture wounds in the pelvic bone. Some people have questioned whether all three pelvic marks were scavenging marks from a bear or possibly bullet wounds. No definitive conclusion has been made about the pelvic puncture wounds. One scenario suggested that two of the men were bludgeoned in their sleep by Packer. In the same scenario, the three remaining men awoke and Packer shot them in the hips to incapacitate them if the punctures were bullet wounds. The remaining men then may have tried to fight off Packer while he wielded a hatchet, and he killed them with blows to the head. Packer then butchered the men and used their flesh to survive in his snowbound state and during his journey. The different states of decomposition contradict that theory, although it is described as plausible. It seems unlikely that Packer shot three of the five men in the hip unless he was trying to debilitate them before killing them. Further analysis of entry and exit geometry could clarify the initial cause, but no such examination has been done to date. Packer\u2019s ultimate motivation for the murders has been heavily debated and has several possible theories.\n\nA different line of investigation concerns the pistol. In the Audrey Thrailkill collection of firearms at the museum was a Colt revolver reportedly found at the site of Packer\u2019s alleged crime. An accession card said, \u201cThis gun found at the site where Alferd Packer killed and ate his companions.\u201d Exhaustive investigation into the pistol\u2019s background uncovered trial-era documents. A Civil War veteran who visited the crime scene said Shannon Bell had been shot twice and the other victims had been killed with a hatchet. The veteran said that, after studying Bell, he noticed a severe bullet wound to the pelvic area and a bullet hole through Bell\u2019s wallet. This evidence seemed to corroborate Packer\u2019s claim that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer had shot Bell in self-defense.\n\nIn 1994, David P. Bailey, curator of history at the Museum of Western Colorado, began or reported an investigation into the pistol and the case; Bailey spent 10 years investigating the gun and the case. The pistol was described in one account as an 1862 Colt revolver, still loaded with three .38-caliber bullets, and in another as a rusted 1862 Colt Police Model .32 five-shot revolver found on Cannibal Plateau in the 1950s, with two shots missing. Dujay found microscopic lead fragments in soil taken from under Shannon Bell\u2019s remains. Spectrograph analysis matched those fragments to the bullets remaining in Packer\u2019s pistol. Analysis at Mesa State College\u2019s Electron Microscopy Facility proved that bullet fragments exhumed from the burial site matched lead from the old pistol. Dujay\u2019s team examined Bell\u2019s samples and found a tiny lead fragment whose chemical composition matched the bullets in Bailey\u2019s Colt revolver. Using a similar 1860s pistol, the team fired a bullet into an elk hip bone, and the resulting hole matched the hole in Bell\u2019s hip bone. The analysis suggested that Packer did shoot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and Bailey believed the evidence supported Packer\u2019s claim that he shot Bell. It now appears certain that at least Bell was shot, but whether Bell was shot in self-defense remains unanswered. Grant Houston remained skeptical because tourists had often found old guns and bullets near the massacre site and claimed they belonged to Packer or the other prospectors. Michelle Pierce was also unconvinced that Bailey\u2019s pistol was the smoking gun.\n\nMuseum curator David Bailey said Packer did not deny eating the bodies, killed only in self-defense, and was wrongly convicted. That view conflicts with Starrs\u2019s conclusion that Packer was guilty as sin and with the forensic interpretation that he murdered his companions for their belongings before resorting to cannibalism. Walter Birkby, the forensic anthropologist who disputed Starrs, said there was nothing to corroborate or refute anything Packer said and that no solid physical evidence would ever prove who did it. Michelle Pierce, Lake City\u2019s former town clerk, believes Packer did not murder the five men. Pierce\u2019s archival research turned up details that do not match the official narrative and portrays Packer as a pitiable soul whose social problems came from a misunderstood disease and harsh contemporary judgment. Pierce said, \u201cPeople really disliked him,\u201d probably because they were afraid of him. The text says that no clear or definitive evidence has yet been found for Packer\u2019s deeds.\n\nThe Denver Public Library\u2019s Western History/Genealogy Department is presented as a place to research Packer\u2019s life and crimes. Researchers can use its materials to prove or disprove parts of the Packer legend. The collection includes microfilm of Denver and Colorado newspapers from the era, including complete runs of the Denver Post and Rocky Mountain News, as well as copies of Packer\u2019s confession. The Colorado State Archives holds several legal and historical records concerning Alfred Packer. The Alfred Packer Collection includes penitentiary records, mugshot photos, parole records, confessions, letters to the governor concerning Packer\u2019s parole, a governor\u2019s pardon, original case files from Gunnison and Hinsdale District Court, Gunnison District Court cases 238\u2013241, Hinsdale District Court cases 238\u2013241, and Supreme Court case numbers 1198 from 1883, 1753 from 1885, 1980 from 1886, 3239 from 1893, and 3905 from 1898. The collection can now be viewed online: users are instructed to click the button for the online exhibits, choose \u201cBrowse Archive\u201d at the top left below the State Archives logo, and select the Alfred Packer option.\n\nBy mid-April, only Packer emerged alive from the mountains. Ripley\u2019s Vice-President of Exhibits and Archives, Edward Meyer, claimed that the museum possessed Packer\u2019s dissected skull, bought from an anonymous party for a reported $20,000. No statement was made about how the museum verified the skull\u2019s authenticity, other than that the seller\u2019s information about its origin was sufficient. If the skull belonged to Packer, his head was removed shortly before or after burial, dissected with the brain removed for study, preserved by an arsenic curing process, displayed by a traveling sideshow, sold to a private party, and later sold to Ripley\u2019s; as of 2008, it was reported to be at Ripley\u2019s Museum in San Antonio, Texas, after being moved from the New Orleans facility following Hurricane Katrina. During the 1989 exhumation, a hole about the size of a thimble was found in Shannon Bell\u2019s hip bone. James Starrs attributed the hole to scavenging animal fangs, while David Bailey wondered whether it had been made by a bullet.\n\n(SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_2 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_5 \u00b7 SRC_6 \u00b7 SRC_7 \u00b7 SRC_8 \u00b7 SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_11)\n\n### Packer\u2019s final account `established`\n\nOn March 11, a man calling himself John Schwartze asked a shopkeeper to stock up for a planned trip. The shopkeeper thought the man looked familiar, then realized he was Alfred Packer. He told the local sheriff that he had spotted a criminal, and Alfred was arrested and taken to Denver. General Adams traveled to confirm Alfred\u2019s identity and, after doing so, took him away for trial. Alfred then gave a final version of events that he largely kept until his death.\n\nIn that final story, when the group left Chief Ouray\u2019s camp, they had only enough food for one grown man for a week. After two or three days, the group was hit by a brutal snowstorm. They kept trekking until they reached the base of a mountain and then a deep valley, but the snow on the far side was so deep they could barely walk. The party moved to higher ground and had only about a pint of flour left by day four. For about the next ten days, they lived only on rosebuds and pine gum while following a ridge above deeper snow. Several of the men were in such rough shape that they cried out every night and prayed to God to escape alive. They eventually crossed the main ridge and came down to camp by a river. The party followed the stream for a day until they reached a frozen lake, possibly Lake San Cristobal. They tried to cross the lake to reach trees for shelter, and by then they were exhausted and tensions were rising.\n\nThe narrative says Shannon Bell was furious at Alfred for losing the route and at the others for acting pathetic. According to Alfred, Shannon one morning ordered him to scout higher on the mountain for a way across. Alfred took a pistol and searched, but found only steep slopes and more snow. He admitted to General Adams for the first time that he did not really know what he was doing and did not know that side of the mountains. When Alfred returned after scouting all day, he found Shannon sitting by the fire eating a piece of meat. Alfred saw a man lying near the fire and the other three men lying motionless around it. Shannon rushed at Alfred with a hatchet, and Alfred shot Shannon in the stomach before finishing him after he fell. That night, Alfred later described being surrounded by \u201cthe midnight horrors of starvation as well as those of utter isolation.\u201d He said he felt his sanity slipping and began to understand what had happened to Shannon because it was beginning to happen to him. In another letter, Alfred wrote, \u201cThe greatest wonder is that I ever returned to a rational condition.\u201d\n\nThe next morning, Alfred tried to follow his tracks from the day before but turned back because of snow and exhaustion. He built a small shelter from wood and branches, but it did not satisfy his hunger. Alfred then cooked and ate some of the meat Shannon had been eating, realizing that he had to do so to survive. He said he then spent another two months in the shelter surviving off the rest of his party. Sixty days later, he saw an avalanche that left yellow clay behind, which told him he was in Slumgullion Pass. The pass is known for its yellow color and was named because it reminded early settlers of Slumgullion stew.\n\nIn early spring, Alfred finally knew where to go and set off up the mountain again. Before leaving, he gathered the remaining meat, wrapped it for the journey, and checked the dead men\u2019s pockets, where he found the money he later spent in Saguache. He had already eaten his shoes, so he wrapped his feet in cloth and had to stop when they froze, lighting fires to warm them. As he neared the agency, he ate the last pieces of meat and may have dropped some later found by the Ute. Alfred said he had previously taken General Adams\u2019s search party only as far as the mountains opposite the camp because he wanted to stop them from going farther and did not want to return to the horror. He also said he escaped and fled trial because he feared a mob would get past his guard and kill him. He said he went to Arkansas under a fake name, worked on a ranch, later moved to Arizona, and finally traveled to Cheyenne, where he was found.\n\nOther sources preserve conflicting versions of this account. The reference article says it appears likely that Packer killed another prospector in self-defense before gnawing on the corpses of those who had already died. Packer later said that after three or four days without provisions, the group cooked and ate their rawhide moccasins. He also said the trail was entirely drifted over and that they gathered and ate the buds of wild rose bushes. After being re-captured, Packer said Shannon Bell killed James Humphrey, a prospector who left Ouray\u2019s camp, George Noon, and Frank Miller while they slept around the campfire. Packer said Bell attacked him with a hatchet when he returned after searching for food, and that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Bell in self-defense. He said that after killing Bell he tried every day to get away but could not, so he lived on the flesh of the dead men for most of the sixty days he was out. He said the snow later formed a crust and he started out up the creek. Packer later confessed to living off the flesh of his companions and gave more than one version of the circumstances.\n\nAt the Los Pinos Indian Agency, Packer came during breakfast and begged for food and shelter. He carried a rifle, a knife, a steel coffee pot, and a satchel. When given food, he vomited and said his digestion had changed because of prolonged near-starvation. He told the agency that he had been hired by five men to guide them to Breckenridge. Packer said he became snow-blind during the journey and was left behind as a burden. He claimed Israel Swan gave him a rifle before the others abandoned him, and said he survived alone on roots and rose buds and ate his moccasins. The agency men thought his condition was odd because he did not look as malnourished and threadbare as expected after more than two months in the wilderness: his face was reported as bloated and his physique was hardly skeletal. Packer said he would drink whisky for breakfast instead of food.\n\nPacker said he was broke and sold his Winchester rifle to Major Downer for $10. He stayed at the agency for ten days and then went to Saguache to buy supplies for a trip back to Pennsylvania. In Saguache, he bought a horse for $70 and booked a room at Dolan\u2019s Saloon. Larry Dolan later said Packer spent around $100 during his stay and offered to lend him $300. Packer also spent $78 in Otto Mears\u2019 general store. It was claimed that he had several different wallets in his possession. When Nutter asked where the rest of the party was, Packer said he had gotten his feet wet and frozen. Packer said the party camped when a winter storm started, that he made a fire while the others went ahead to find food, and that Swan left him with his rifle. He said the others never returned and that he then made his way through the mountains eating mostly rosebuds and small animals. Packer claimed Miller had stuck the knife in a tree and walked off without it.\n\nIn another account, Packer said the men left Ouray\u2019s camp with what they thought was enough food for a 14-day journey, but provisions ran out sooner than expected. After a few days of no wildlife and almost no food, he said, the men began to eye each other unsettlingly. Packer said he returned from gathering firewood to find four men around Israel Swan\u2019s body after Swan had been struck in the head with a hatchet and killed instantly. The four men butchered Swan, and Packer said he accepted the situation and joined them. Several thousand dollars was found on Swan and divided among the men. They ate the best parts of Swan, packed some away, and continued onward, with Packer taking Swan\u2019s rifle.\n\nPacker said the five men again ran out of meat within two days and could not find game. Bell, Humphrey, Noon, and Packer secretly decided that Miller would be next because he was stocky and had soft flesh. Miller was killed with a hatchet blow to the head while stooping to pick up firewood, then butchered and eaten. Packer said Humphrey was next to be sacrificed, followed soon after by George Noon. Only Bell and Packer were left, and they swore not to eat each other. They agreed to claim that the four other men had died from the elements and were buried with dignity, and vowed never to speak of the cannibalism because they believed no one would accept that it had been necessary.\n\nPacker said that after a few days of travel with only a rabbit and some roots to eat, he and Bell camped beside a large lake next to a grove of hemlock trees. Bell later jumped from his blanket, screamed that he could not take it anymore, and said one of them would die for food. Packer said Bell rushed at him with a rifle to bash his skull in. Packer then butchered Bell, ate as much as he could, packed away more meat, took Bell\u2019s share of Swan\u2019s money, and continued alone. He said he threw away the remaining strips of Bell\u2019s flesh because he hoped an animal would eat them.\n\nPacker then said the men encountered a strong blizzard in the mountains. Heavy snow made them hopelessly lost and unable to retrace their route. They also ran out of matches and had to carry hot embers in a steel coffee pot to light fires. Days passed with no game and ice fishing failed completely. The men roasted and ate their shoes and tried to live on scant edible vegetation. Packer said the men made a pact that if one died, his meat would save the others from starvation. He said Israel Swan could go no further after days of hiking with almost no food; they found a pine-shaded gulch next to a lake and made camp there, and Swan then died of hunger and exposure.\n\nPacker\u2019s signed first confession said Swan died first and was eaten by the other five persons about ten days out of camp. It said Humphreys died four or five days later and was also eaten, that Humphreys had about $133, and that Packer found the pocketbook and took the money. The confession said the butcher was later killed, apparently accidentally, while Packer was carrying wood, and was also eaten. It said Bell shot California with Swan\u2019s gun and Packer killed Bell. Packer said he shot Bell, covered up the remains, took a large piece along, and traveled 14 days to the agency. In that first confession, Packer said the first two deaths were circumstantial because of the rough conditions and that everyone in the party partook in cannibalism to stay alive. He said one man was \u201ckilled accidentally\u201d while he was collecting firewood, and that Shannon Bell killed a man called \u201cCalifornia,\u201d after which Packer killed Bell in self-defense before traveling 14 days back to civilization.\n\nIn Packer\u2019s later amended version, the men endured almost 20 days from Ouray\u2019s camp and more than 10 days without substantial food. James Humphrey died of exposure to extreme cold, he said, and George Noon was killed days later by Shannon Bell solely for food. Bell and Packer agreed to stand together until the end and not eat each other. In this version, Bell later rushed at Packer with his rifle, intending to bludgeon him with the stock. Packer said he took the valuables of the deceased because they no longer needed them, but he did not specify the items or the amount of money obtained. He later said that only Bell and he remained after the other three men were dead.\n\nIn Packer\u2019s second confession, he said Bell had killed the others after telling him to scout for a way out of the mountains and to find food. Packer said he returned in the late evening after being gone most of the day. Bell was roasting meat cut from Frank Miller\u2019s leg; Miller\u2019s skull had been crushed by a hatchet and lay furthest from the fire down the stream, while the other three bodies lay near the fire with hatchet cuts in the forehead. Packer said he dropped his revolver in deep snow after shooting Bell and lost it. He built a crude shelter from stray logs to fight the snow and wind and stayed away from the bodies. Another storm came, and he sheltered for hours while starving. He decided he had to eat something or die, returned to the fire, covered the men, took the piece of meat near the fire back to camp, cooked it, ate it, and lived off the flesh of the men for the bigger part of sixty days.\n\nThe same second-confession account says the party asked Packer to go up a nearby hill to see farther ahead for navigation. He returned to camp after a full day of going up and coming back down the hillside and said Bell had killed the entire camp and was eating the leg of a man roasted in the campfire. Bell rushed at him, and Packer shot Bell through the stomach with the rifle he had taken on the trip up the hillside. Packer said he made a new fire near his camp, cooked the piece of meat, and ate it. He tried to get away every day but could not, so he lived off the flesh of the men for the bigger part of the 60 days he was out. Packer\u2019s third confession included more details but mostly the same information as the second confession.\n\nThe accounts also disagree over the weapon and the final struggle. One version says Bell had charged at Packer with Swan\u2019s rifle and Packer shot him. Another says Packer finished Bell with a hatchet blow to the head, but that was the extent of his claim concerning the hatchet. In his second official version, Packer never claimed that he had fought off Bell with a hatchet. The account attributed to Packer says the party members died one at a time and were consumed by the other members until only he and Shannon Bell remained; Shannon, desperately hungry, tried to kill Packer before dying of natural causes, and Bell was killed in the ensuing fight. Packer said he had no choice but to eat Bell because Bell would have eaten him. He claimed Bell had murdered the others with hatchet blows to the head, maintained that he killed only Bell and did so in self-defense, and admitted to eating the flesh of his companions in order to survive. Under interrogation, Packer gave the first of multiple contradictory confessions, first claiming, \u201cWe ate old man Swann first.\u201d He claimed the strong ate the weak one by one until only he and Bell remained, and that Bell tried to kill him, so he shot Bell dead. In one confession, he admitted to shooting one man in self-defense.\n\nPacker\u2019s written version said severe weather forced the men to camp in a ravine with some shelter from snow and wind. He wrote that they ate goatskin moccasins and rosebuds until they were weakened, emaciated, and mentally distracted, and that he was the one sane man among the six. He said he left camp to look for a way out and returned to find Shannon Wilson Bell roasting meat over a fire. Packer admitted surviving on the men\u2019s flesh until he escaped the mountains. He wrote that his comrades were dead, he was left alone among starvation and isolation, and his body and mind were overwhelmed. In letters, he insisted that \u201cthe greatest wonder is that I ever returned to a rational condition.\u201d\n\nThe accounts say Packer later maintained that Shannon Bell had killed the other men. After arriving alone at the Los Pinos Indian Agency, he said his comrades had left him while they searched for food. He claimed he did not know what happened to his companions even though he carried large sums of their money and possessed a rifle known to have belonged to one of them. Under interrogation, he admitted to partaking in cannibalism but denied committing murder. He complied with further questioning and repeated his story of self-defense and cannibalism for survival.\n\nThe wider account says that, because of his ineptitude, Packer led the men through the San Juan Mountains for nearly two months and covered only 250 miles at an average of three miles a day. In his correspondence, Packer tried to shift blame for the disaster onto Chief Ouray. He wrote that conversations with Ouray led them to believe they could reach the Los Pinos Agency in not over five days on foot across the mountains. Packer said he had never been to the agency and relied only on general reputation and information received from Ouray. The text calls Packer a known pathological liar and says his account to Newman about Ouray was not entirely truthful. Ouray never told Packer they were only five days from the Los Pinos Agency because at least three to four feet of snow blocked the path. Ouray did draw a crude map on the ground with a stick and gave them all the directions he could as a route. Packer wrote that they were given scanty fare\u2014about 20 pounds of flour and not over 10 pounds of good meat\u2014and that at the end of nine days the last provisions had been eaten. He said the group ate their goatskin moccasins and subsisted on rosebuds until they were weakened, emaciated, and mentally completely distracted. The text says those conditions led to the deaths and cannibalization of all in Packer\u2019s party, while also calling Packer\u2019s account ridiculous because he was living in a different territory under an alias to evade capture.\n\nThe later assessment remained disputed. Lamm said the evidence suggested that Packer killed only one man and only in self-defense, but added, \u201cthe proof was in the pudding and Alferd ate that, too.\u201d Lamm told Tom Miller that, in the immortal words of Alferd Packer, \u201cWell done!\u201d After being accused of murdering the other men, Packer told a story that defined the rest of his life and earned him a permanent place in Colorado history.\n\n(SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_2 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_4 \u00b7 SRC_5 \u00b7 SRC_7 \u00b7 SRC_8 \u00b7 SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_11)\n\n### The trials `established`\n\nAt Alfred Packer\u2019s first trial, witnesses testified that he had deliberately led the men astray with the intention of stealing their possessions by any means necessary. Preston Nutter, a prospector who rejected Packer\u2019s account, testified that Packer had been skulking around, always trying to find out how much money each man had or was carrying. Other witnesses described Packer\u2019s behavior after he reached Saguache and the different, conflicting stories he told about what had happened. The prosecution argued that his survival story was an attempt to cover up the killings. When Packer took the stand, he rambled for more than six hours and admitted eating the other members of the party, but said that he had killed only Shannon Bell and that Bell was responsible for everything else. The jury rejected his story and found him guilty of premeditated murder, theft, and several other charges. He was originally sentenced to death by hanging.\n\nThe dates and details of the first proceeding vary among the sources. One account says that the trial began on April 6 in Lake City; another dates its beginning to April 9, 1883, after seven days of testimony and examination. The prosecution presented about two dozen witnesses. Otto Mears testified that Packer possessed valuable Wells Fargo drafts when he emerged from the mountains. Constable Herman Lauter testified that Packer lunged at him with murder in his eyes when Lauter ordered him to hand over a knife. Packer called Lauter a liar and boasted, \u201cTo think that one little Dutchman could take a knife away from me. No, sir!\u201d The jury reportedly reacted badly to Packer\u2019s rambling testimony, his habit of speaking about himself in the third person, and his focus on personal grudges rather than on proving his innocence. One source says that the jury wasted little time finding him guilty of murder.\n\nThe first conviction was overturned because of a legal problem. Before statehood, Colorado Territory did not have a statute under which Packer could be tried for murder, and Colorado State established the specific laws only after the crime had taken place. The Colorado Supreme Court therefore held that the government could not sentence a man to death for a crime committed before Colorado became a state rather than a territory. The sources describe this as an ex post facto problem, a technicality, or the effect of an old territorial statute and a \u201csavings clause.\u201d Packer\u2019s lawyer also assigned other errors to the court, including that Packer had not received a speedy trial and that the murder indictments came after what was then a three-year statute of limitations. Another account says Packer argued that the killings occurred on the Ute Reservation and should therefore have been subject to federal rather than local jurisdiction. The murder conviction and scheduled execution were overturned, so Packer had to be retried for manslaughter instead of murder.\n\nA second trial was held in Gunnison after the Colorado Supreme Court granted a change of venue and a new Republican judge was appointed. Packer and his counsel argued that Lake City\u2019s mainly Democratic community was too prejudiced for a fair trial. Of 57 prospective jurors interviewed, 54 admitted that they already knew about the case, and 44 said that they already believed Packer was guilty. Judge Melville Gerry ordered the sheriff to \u201cscour the highways and byways\u201d for jurors, and the sheriff filled the jury within a few hours. Packer was incarcerated at Gunnison while awaiting retrial, with one source saying that he waited nearly four years. He again pleaded not guilty and repeated his story.\n\nThe prosecution made the second case more damaging to Packer. Hunters testified that there had been plenty of food in the area even during a bad winter, including game, rabbits, deer, antelope, and more. They said that even someone as inexperienced as Packer could have hunted it easily. There was also a report that a deer carcass had been found near the campsite. That evidence weakened Packer\u2019s claim that wildlife had been so scarce that the men quickly had to resort to cannibalism. The prosecution argued that the cannibalism therefore had to have been by choice because food would have been available. Witnesses again described Packer\u2019s behavior after he reached Saguache and the different accounts he had given of the events. When Packer finally took the stand, he testified for more than six hours and admitted eating the other members of the party. His account of what happened at the campsite remained relatively close to his second official version. He asked to be charged the full 40 years only for Shannon Bell\u2019s death, since Bell was the only killing he continued to claim, but the request was denied.\n\nThe prosecution argued that even if Packer was not a malicious monster who had premeditated the entire episode, he had still led the group astray by taking a more dangerous winter route while pretending to be a guide. The text says that, at best, he was extremely negligent. Packer was convicted of manslaughter and received 40 years in prison, described as the maximum of eight years per man. The sources disagree on the legal description: Packer\u2019s criminal record lists \u201cvoluntary manslaughter\u201d on five counts, while other accounts call the conviction involuntary manslaughter. They agree that the sentence was 40 years, or eight years for each of the five men. At the time, one source calls it the longest prison sentence ever handed down in U.S. history, while another calls it the longest custodial sentence in U.S. history. He was sentenced to 40 years of hard labor at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Ca\u00f1on City.\n\nThe sources also give different summaries of the sequence. One says that Packer was arrested on murder charges, escaped from the Saguache jail, remained on the run for eight years, was captured in Wyoming in 1883, and was returned to Lake City, where he was tried, found guilty of murder, and sentenced to hang. Another says that he escaped and hid from justice for nine years, and still another says that he spent nearly a decade on the run before authorities tracked him down. A separate account says he escaped before trial and was eventually found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883. The consistent outline is that local authorities did not believe his story, that he escaped from jail, that he was later captured in Wyoming and brought back to Hinsdale County, and that he was eventually tried and convicted of murder before winning a retrial on a technicality.\n\nJudge M. B. Gerry is associated with the famous quotation supposedly spoken while sentencing Packer in the first trial. In the legendary version, Gerry said that Packer had come to Hinsdale County when there were seven Democrats, had eaten five of them, and was sentenced to hang. One version has Gerry saying, \u201cI sentence you, Alfred Packer, to be hanged by the neck until you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d Another gives the more elaborate wording, \u201cStand up yah voracious man-eatin\u2019 sonofabitch and receive yir sintince. When yah came to Hinsdale County, there was siven Dimmycrats,\u201d followed by the claim that Packer had eaten five of them and was being sentenced to hang as a warning against reducing the Democratic population of the state. Gerry reportedly said that there were seven Democrats in Hinsdale County and that Packer had eaten five of them, and he reportedly sentenced Packer to hang until he was \u201cdead, dead, dead\u201d as a warning against reducing the Democratic population of the state.\n\nThe Colorado history account says that the legend is apocryphal. Fred M. Mazzula argues in his book \\*Al Packer, A Colorado Cannibal\\* that Judge Gerry did not talk like Yosemite Sam. Mazzula used the trial transcripts to determine what Gerry actually said at sentencing. The actual sentence ordered that Packer be taken by the sheriff of Hinsdale County to a place of execution within the corporate limits of Lake City, between 10 a.m. and 3 p.m. on May 19, 1883, and that he be hanged by the neck until he was \u201cdead, dead, dead.\u201d It ended with a request that God have mercy on Packer\u2019s soul. The text says Gerry\u2019s real quotation is less colorful but more accurate. One source nevertheless records that Gerry sentenced Packer to be \u201chung from your neck until you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d\n\nLake City residents were outraged when the death sentence was overturned and threatened to hang Packer themselves, so the second trial was moved to Gunnison, about 50 miles away. Packer escaped death on a technicality and received the new trial. After the new proceeding, he was convicted of manslaughter and sentenced to 40 years in the Colorado State Penitentiary in Ca\u00f1on City. After a confession, a search for the bodies, and a trial, one article summarizes, Packer was sentenced to forty years at the Colorado State Penitentiary. The sources differ over whether the second-trial conviction should be called voluntary or involuntary manslaughter, but Packer\u2019s listed convictions are murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter on five counts, and his listed criminal penalty is death, later commuted to 40 years\u2019 imprisonment.\n\nThe prosecution\u2019s motive theory also focused on Israel Swan. At the time of Packer\u2019s trial, Swan\u2019s family reported that he had left for the expedition with about $6,000 in cash and gold and a valuable Winchester rifle. The money and rifle would have provided a strong motive for murder, and Swan was 65 at the time of his death. Packer was not found with any gold when he was arrested. Although he had some money left, it did not total in the thousands, and the sources say that his spending had been frivolous. One account says that he spent more than $100 on drinking and gambling and another $78 in a general store, while often giving conflicting stories when he drank. The prosecution argued that the only logical reason for him to travel through the mountains with so little food and supplies was to lead the men into the wilderness to kill and rob them. Packer pleaded not guilty.\n\nThe first-trial evidence concerning the deaths included the prosecution\u2019s claim that Swan died on or around March 1. In court, the prosecution argued that Swan showed signs of a struggle at death and that the other men appeared to have been killed in their sleep. Otto Mears, Larry Dolan, Oliver D. Loutsenhizer, and Preston Nutter testified for the prosecution, with Nutter as the key witness. Other accounts repeat that Nutter testified that Packer had been trying to learn how much money each man was carrying. The physical evidence was also used against Packer: the men had no defensive wounds or injuries in one account, while later exhumation accounts describe defensive hacking marks on the forearms of three skeletons, including Shannon Bell\u2019s. All five skeletons showed varying post-mortem injuries, including depressed fractures, butterfly fractures, butchering trauma, and hacking trauma. These findings leave the evidentiary picture disputed rather than settled.\n\nPacker\u2019s lawyer mounted what one source calls a spirited defense, but Packer went to prison for seventeen years before \\*The Denver Post\\* petitioned for his release. In prison, he made horsehair bridles, and one of them is displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado in Grand Junction. He also built elaborate Victorian dollhouses. After being imprisoned, Packer wrote to anyone he could, claiming that his sentence was unjust, that he had killed only Shannon, and that the jury was full of biased and prejudiced people. He filed appeals five times and was denied each time. He sent letters to local newspapers saying that he had been unjustly convicted by an unfair and unsympathetic judicial system and by ignorant, small-minded people. On June 19, 1899, the Colorado Supreme Court officially upheld his sentence. He never succeeded in obtaining an official state pardon, although the Colorado State Archives collection includes a governor\u2019s pardon and also contains penitentiary, mugshot, parole, confession, governor\u2019s-parole, and original court records.\n\nThe sources disagree about the length of time Packer served and the date of his release. One says he served 15 years before being granted parole; another says he spent 16 years in prison; another says he served 17 years before \\*The Denver Post\\* petitioned for his release. The account based on the Colorado State Archives says that Alfred was paroled in 1901, 18 years into his sentence. Another source says that former Governor Charles Thomas paroled him in 1901, while a separate historical summary gives 1905. The sources agree that he was paroled rather than officially pardoned and that Polly Pry, a reporter for \\*The Denver Post\\*, helped campaign for his release. After his parole, he worked for \\*The Denver Post\\* as a guard and later as a ranch hand.\n\nPacker\u2019s criminal status is listed as deceased. His convictions are listed as murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter on five counts; his criminal penalty is listed as death, later commuted to 40 years\u2019 imprisonment. The text says that Packer is one of four people historically convicted for cannibalism in the United States. It also describes the incident as the only known trial in the United States associated with human cannibalism, while stressing that Packer was not actually convicted of cannibalism as such: he was tried and found guilty of murder in the first trial and manslaughter in the second. The sources state that cannibalism itself was not necessarily illegal in the United States unless a person committed murder to obtain the flesh.\n\nPacker\u2019s supporters continued to dispute the verdict. Polly Pry portrayed him as a former soldier and a man of the people who had been persecuted by the establishment, had been in the wrong place at the wrong time, and had survived horrors that most people could not imagine. She argued that his sentence was so harsh only because of the cannibalism and that anyone would have done the same in his situation. Her stories changed the minds of some local businesses and residents, leading them to organize petitions for his release. In mock trials held in Lake City, Boulder, and other Colorado towns, actors played the main roles and audience members served as jurors. When David Bailey\u2019s evidence was included, jurors acquitted Packer more often than not. The State Archives collection includes a governor\u2019s pardon, but the historical accounts say that Packer never obtained an official state pardon.\n\n(SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_2 \u00b7 SRC_4 \u00b7 SRC_5 \u00b7 SRC_6 \u00b7 SRC_7 \u00b7 SRC_8 \u00b7 SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_11)\n\n### The expedition `established`\n\nThe sources give differing accounts of the expedition\u2019s size and divisions. One historical account says Robert McGrue, leader of the larger prospecting party, organized a party of 20 to travel from Salt Lake City to the San Juan Mountains after hearing of gold discovered in Breckenridge. Another account says that a party of 21 prospectors, led by Alferd\u2014or Alfred\u2014Packer, left Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 8, 1873, hoping to be among the first to locate mining claims in the San Juan region. A separate account describes Packer as part of a larger band of 20 gold seekers who left Utah and split into two groups. The prospectors were drawn by news of a gold strike and reports of enormous fortunes, and one newspaper account says they were promised \u201cmarvelous tales of enormous fortunes to be had for the mere asking.\u201d Their intended route was toward Breckenridge, by way of the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency.\n\nPacker had worked as a guide for only a few months, and the accounts disagree about how much responsibility to assign him, but they agree that the party progressed more slowly than expected toward Breckenridge despite his serving as guide. One account says Alfred managed, by luck, to get the party only as far as just outside Montrose. The journey from Utah to Colorado was slow and difficult: winter arrived early and severely, the wagons and horses were crippled, and heavy snow along the Spanish Trail covered the path so completely that the men relied almost solely on a compass. The wagons could hardly follow a trail, and Packer\u2019s inexperience showed itself as the party became lost. By January, provisions had run out completely. The men first survived on horse feed and came close to considering the horses themselves as food. The men later described Alfred as greedy, lazy, and constantly fighting with someone; other accounts add that he was argumentative, inexperienced, distrusted, and known for lying and theft.\n\nOn January 21, 1874, the party found a Ute encampment in a valley just outside Montrose, in the Uncompahgre Valley, led by Chief Ouray, who was known as \u201cThe White Man\u2019s Friend.\u201d The accounts say Ouray supplied the men with food, lodging, and shelter, exchanged food and supplies with them, and offered to let them remain with his tribe until winter had passed. He recommended postponing the expedition until spring because the winter weather in the mountains was dangerous, promised to share all that he and his people had with them, and warned that no Ute would attempt the journey in those conditions. According to the sources, he said that trying it meant certain or almost certain death. The party still had about 120 miles and the San Juan Mountains left to cross. One account records a different claim attributed to Packer: that Ouray said the Los Pi\u00f1os Agency could be reached in no more than five days on foot across the mountains. The same account says that this was not truthful, because three or four feet of snow blocked the route; men who remained at the camp said Ouray told the prospectors they would all die and that no Indian in his band would go to show them the route because the deep snow was too dangerous.\n\nDespite the warning, 11 men decided to continue. They planned to travel first to the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, the closest outpost to Ouray\u2019s camp, and then proceed to Breckenridge. Ouray provided food for the journey and safe directions to bypass the mountains, advising them to drop down to the Gunnison River and follow it north to the agency. The party divided: one account says five men followed Oliver Loutsenhizer along the Gunnison River while six others, including Packer, headed through the San Juan Mountains. Loutsenhizer threatened to shoot Packer if Packer tried to follow the other group. Another account describes three factions at the Ute encampment: some men paid Ouray a trespassing fee and stayed with the Utes for the rest of the winter; others accepted a week\u2019s provisions from him and traveled toward the Los Pinos Agency in Saguache County; and a third group ignored his warnings. The river-bound group nearly perished after losing its way, while some of the men stayed behind with the horses and wagons until spring.\n\nPacker insisted that crossing the mountains directly was better than going around them because he claimed to know the area and said the route was faster and more direct. The sources emphasize that locals had given him a safer route beside a main water source, where the men could have caught fresh fish and stayed on course. Instead, Packer chose the mountain route with minimal supplies because he believed it would be faster. Although the route he and his companions took was nearly identical in length to the recommended route, though not in its traverse, it was far more dangerous. The choice to hike through the San Juan Mountains in midwinter\u2014where snow could exceed six feet in a single snowfall, with blasting winds and freezing temperatures\u2014is described as practically suicidal.\n\nAfter fierce snowstorms boxed in the expedition, many of the men returned home, but six, including Packer, continued. On February 9, Packer and five others left for the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, described in one account as 75 miles ahead. The five men were George Noon, Shannon Wilson Bell, James Humphrey, Frank Miller, and Israel Swan; they wanted to reach Breckenridge before thousands of fortune hunters arrived. Their immediate goal was the agency, followed by Breckenridge. The group traveled southeast and then south along the Lake Fork, passing the site that would become the townsite of Lake City within six months. After wandering, the party was discovered by warpainted Ute Indians, and Chief Ouray took them to a spot near his village where they established a makeshift camp. When Ouray realized that Packer\u2019s party would still enter the storm, he supplied them with provisions despite the warnings.\n\nPacker\u2019s men entered the mountains with barely enough food for the supposed 14 days required by the safest route. They had no snowshoes, minimal matches, no flint, no heavy clothing, two rifles, one pistol, a couple of knives, a hatchet, and minimal ammunition. Another account says Packer had left Utah with few provisions and no weapons. The larger party\u2019s river route initially followed Ouray\u2019s directions under Bob McGrue\u2019s guidance, but the horses eventually could not continue. McGrue unloaded the men\u2019s provisions and returned to Ouray\u2019s camp. Packer then took his party higher into the San Juan Mountains against Ouray\u2019s warning.\n\nThe river party had a brutal journey in the cold. The freezing winter made the Gunnison route difficult, and the men ran out of food long before reaching the agency. Cowhands found them and fed them at a cattle camp until spring; another account says the cowhands let them remain there until spring. In April, when the weather improved, the men reached the agency in May and found that Alfred had already been there alone. The sources also describe Packer\u2019s separate arrival: he wandered into the Los Pinos Agency in Southwest Colorado three months later, on April 16, 1874, 65 days after his departure, and another account places the agency 75 miles ahead. He emerged from the woods alone, carrying a pocket full of cash, a rifle, and a coffee pot, with no traveling party, which aroused suspicion. The account that gives his arrival as April 16 says he entered the agency during a snowstorm, begging for food and shelter.\n\nA video dramatization presents Packer as the sole survivor of a six-man party that tried to cross the San Juan Mountains in the Colorado Territory; the underlying historical accounts likewise say he emerged as the sole survivor. Those accounts say the six men lost their way and became snowbound, that five prospectors died agonizing deaths in the mountain cold, and that a sixth man had his life irrevocably altered by the events. One account says that only Packer returned in the winter of 1874. The expedition account also records that Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon had been a member of the original party that left Provo, wisely stayed in Chief Ouray\u2019s camp, and later reached the destination safely with Bob McGrue and Preston Nutter\u2019s party.\n\nThe men neared the point of eating the horses themselves.\n\n(SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_2 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_4 \u00b7 SRC_5 \u00b7 SRC_8 \u00b7 SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_11)\n\n### Packer\u2019s legacy `established`\n\nPacker\u2019s story of cannibalism is presented as a danger story about nineteenth-century prospectors in the Rocky Mountains and as one of the West\u2019s most grisly and enduring legends and murder mysteries. The case reflects the eagerness of prospectors to trespass into the Ute reservation, illustrates how swiftly development took place once the San Juan region opened, and attracted national and international attention to Lake City and its environs. Local papers gave the case constant coverage, using sensational headlines, negative comments about Packer\u2019s character, and imaginative theories that drew national and international attention. Harper\u2019s Weekly coverage also drew national attention to the grisly crime; an illustrator from Harper\u2019s Weekly recorded the scene in visual graphic detail, and copies of the portrait photograph were sold for 50 cents to the curious.\n\nThe area northeast of Lake City where Packer\u2019s party got lost is labeled \u201cCannibal Plateau\u201d on maps, and the site where the bodies were found five miles beyond town is called Deadman\u2019s Gulch. The text says Packer evolved from Old West infamy to New West celebrity. His pistol is now on display at the Museum of Western Colorado. In Lake City, \u201cAl Packer Days,\u201d the Packer Burger at the Cannibal Grill, and a wooden marker for the Alferd Packer Massacre Site are popular attractions. Travel magazines say the Hinsdale County Museum has the largest collection of Packer memorabilia known, including skull fragments, clothing buttons from victims, and shackles used when Packer was imprisoned. Tourists are advised not to miss the actual burial site, which is five minutes from town.\n\nThe modern myth states that Packer was convicted of cannibalism, but the text says that belief is totally incorrect. He was tried and found guilty of murder in the first trial, and of manslaughter in the second. Nearly all of Saguache and soon nearly the entire country and beyond believed Packer\u2019s responsibility for the deaths was beyond doubt. Cannibalism was not seen as the main issue of Packer\u2019s guilt. People then knew the Donner Party story and understood to some degree the need to eat in the wilderness. Cannibalism was and is not illegal per se in the United States unless a murder is committed to obtain the flesh. If someone murders to obtain flesh, the charge is murder, with the cannibalism treated as desecration or abuse of a corpse. Packer claimed for the rest of his life that he was unjustly vilified and convicted for cannibalism rather than for murder, which he continued to deny.\n\nThe sources disagree about parts of Packer\u2019s punishment and later life, and those disagreements remain part of the legacy. One account says Packer had served 18 years of his 40-year sentence, while another says he served 16 years of that 40-year sentence before being released. The latter account says he was originally sentenced to death, received a 40-year sentence after a second trial in 1885, and lived out the rest of his life in relative peace. Another source says he was convicted of five counts of voluntary manslaughter in Gunnison in 1886 and sentenced to 40 years. The text says Packer ultimately found some redemption from the people of Colorado, or at least from the justice system. It also says that he was the most celebrated Colorado convict, wooed and loved despite his life sentence for eating his camping compadres.\n\nPacker\u2019s name and story entered books, film, music, restaurants, and jokes. In Dashiell Hammett\u2019s novel The Thin Man, Nick Charles advises Gilbert Wynant to read a selection about Packer\u2019s expeditions. That selection comes from Thomas Samuel Duke\u2019s Celebrated Criminal Cases of America, where Thomas Samuel Duke gave Packer\u2019s first name as Alfred. Harold Schechter wrote the true-crime book Man-Eater about Packer. In 1993, CU film students Matt Stone, a CU film student, and Trey Parker, a CU film student, produced Cannibal! The Musical; Matt Stone and Trey Parker later created the television show South Park. Cannibal! The Musical was Trey Parker\u2019s directorial debut, and the text says it was short-lived, like Packer\u2019s companions. The story is being dramatized in the film rather than simply reported, and the underlying account is Packer\u2019s expedition and its aftermath.\n\nThe University of Colorado Boulder has an Alferd Packer Grill where students are greeted by a giant portrait of Packer and the slogan \u201cHave a friend for lunch!\u201d The cafeteria grill\u2019s slogan was \u201cHave a friend for lunch!\u201d, customers can order an El Canibal beefburger, and a giant map outlining Packer\u2019s travels through Colorado was displayed on the wall. The grill has since been renamed the Alferd Packer Restaurant & Grill. A commenter says they first learned about Packer while attending CU Boulder and learning how the Alferd E. Packer Grill got its name. UC-Boulder students have hosted Al Packer Days wearing T-shirts with slogans like \u201cFinger lickin\u2019 good\u201d and \u201cWhen you care enough to eat the very best.\u201d The text mentions Alferd Packer\u2019s High Protein Gourmet Cookbook as an example of gallows humor about the story, but also says there is really nothing funny about the tale.\n\nThe University of Colorado example was echoed in Washington. At a press conference on August 10, 1977, Secretary of Agriculture Bob Bergland and his employees unveiled a plaque naming the executive cafeteria \u201cThe Alferd Packer Memorial Grill.\u201d Bergland said Packer\u2019s life exemplified the spirit and fare of the cafeteria and would \u201cserve all mankind.\u201d Barbara Walters covered the event on ABC Evening News. The stratagem succeeded and the contracts were terminated soon afterward. Bergland removed the plaque after accepting the bureaucratic objection that it lacked official GSA authorization. The plaque is now displayed on the wall of the National Press Club\u2019s The Reliable Source members-only bar, and the National Press Club\u2019s hamburger is called the Alfred Packer Burger.\n\nPhil Ochs, a 1960s folk-music songwriter, used humor in \u201cThe Ballad of Alferd Packer\u201d (1964), matching the lighthearted ongoing attitude toward Packer and his alleged crimes. The song never appeared on any of Ochs\u2019s studio or live albums. C. W. McCall wrote the song \u201cComin\u2019 Back for More\u201d about Packer. Packer remains a wildly popular figure in Colorado state history. A restaurant in Boulder, Colorado, called the Alferd Packer Grill was named in his honor, and events and traditions honoring Packer take place annually in Colorado and 12 other states.\n\nLake City today treats Packer as both its patron saint and its antihero. Lake City is a remote mountain town at an elevation of 8,661 feet. Most visitors come to hunt, fish, or hike, and some hope to glimpse Al Packer\u2019s ghost. The town has businesses called the Packer Saloon & Cannibal Grill and the Cannibal Cabins. Lake City has hosted an annual Al Packer Day with coffin races, mystery-meat eating competitions, and an Al Packer look-alike contest. Grant Houston of the Hinsdale County Historical Society says many local people, including himself, would say Packer was guilty. Houston says local tradition held that Packer lured the men up there for money or guns he coveted, killed them, and fled. The courthouse contains Packer memorabilia, newspaper accounts, and official trial records.\n\nPacker\u2019s grave in Littleton has been left with offerings such as a toy firetruck, a marijuana vape pen, and a vase for flowers. A wooden bench invites visitors to sit and reflect at Packer\u2019s grave. Packer was buried in Littleton, Colorado. The sources differ on the exact circumstances and date of his death: one says he died in 1907 at a ranch near Deer Creek in western Jefferson County, another says he died two years after becoming a local celebrity in Gunnison, and another says he died alone in Littleton at age 69. His death was cited as \u201cDementia \u2013 trouble & worry,\u201d though his clinical cause of death has also been described as a stroke. Another source says his official cause of death was listed as \u201cSenility -- trouble and worry.\u201d Packer was widely rumored to have become a vegetarian before death and was reported by acquaintances as a man rich with stories and liked by children. He lived modestly and was reported to be charitable. There was also a rumor that Alfred was a vegetarian when he died.\n\nAfter prison, Alfred worked at the Denver Post as a guard for a while. People described Alfred after prison as kind, modest, and charitable, in contrast with the grumpy, greedy, and untrustworthy man he had been in the past. Alfred eventually moved to Littleton, where he bought a copper mine and returned to prospecting. Another account says Packer lived in Denver, then moved to Littleton to be closer to the mountains, bought copper claims, and resumed prospecting until epilepsy overcame him. He remained a free man for nine years after the escape; while in Gunnison, he became a local celebrity; and Packer lived the rest of his life in relative peace.\n\nThe story of Packer and the story that actually occurred are not always the same. A commenter objects to what he calls revisionist history and says he wants to keep the lore of the Old West without modern filtering. The text says Packer\u2019s legacy remained unsettled even after his peaceful death. It also says that the tale has been the subject of gallows humor and amusements, but that five men died agonizing deaths in the cruel mountain cold while a sixth had his own life irrevocably altered, so there is really nothing funny about the tale.\n\nThe sources also discuss Packer\u2019s female admirers through the idea of hybristophilia. Mental health professionals call the disorder in which some women become infatuated with heinous serial killers and mass murderers in jail cells hybristophilia. Popular culture refers to hybristophilia as Bonnie and Clyde syndrome. The condition is usually characterized by a woman communicating with a high-profile criminal in an attempt to kindle a relationship, and the text says hybristophilia is not solely a 20th-century phenomenon. Alfred Packer, also spelled Alferd, received fan mail from several adoring women while he was incarcerated for his crimes. Before Pry helped secure his release, Packer received a constant stream of curious visitors who came to ogle Colorado\u2019s nationally known prisoner. He also received many letters from adoring well-wishers while in prison.\n\nOne sympathizer was Mrs. Rebecca A. Newman. Newman worked as a schoolteacher while living in the Bronx neighborhood of New York City. By opening communications with Packer, Newman apparently hoped to form a friendship with him and express sympathy for his circumstances. When Governor-Elect Alva Adams learned of Newman\u2019s affection for Packer, he warned her that if she knew Packer and his horrible crime she would not care to defend him or excuse his conduct. Not dissuaded by Adams, Newman insisted that communications be opened anyway. Newman asked Packer for a full detailed statement of the whole circumstances connected with his most extraordinary trials and sufferings.\n\nWhile in prison, Packer often crafted leather belts, bridles, wooden canes, and watch fobs from dyed and braided horsehair. Packer usually sold those items as souvenirs to prison visitors and sometimes gifted such items to people he thought were special. Packer gave Rebecca a chain bracelet as a sign of affection and desire for a relationship. Through his correspondence with Newman, Packer hoped to find out what she could do for him. Packer wrote that he would make her proof he was worthy of the aid of philanthropic people and those better situated than he was financially and otherwise. The text says women like Newman still exist today, that contacting famous criminals who are experienced manipulators is a dangerous game, and that people who do not proceed with caution sometimes end up being eaten alive.\n\n(SRC_2 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_4 \u00b7 SRC_5 \u00b7 SRC_8 \u00b7 SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_7 \u00b7 SRC_11)\n\n### Polly Pry\u2019s campaign `established`\n\nAlfred Packer\u2019s story eventually reached Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan, who used the pen name Polly Pry. She was a controversial reporter for The Denver Post and later a freelancer in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, and was known for her connection to the Alfred Packer case. Pry became the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter in 1898 and was a member of the Denver Woman\u2019s Press Club. At age fifteen, she eloped to Mexico with the railroad industrialist George Anthony; five years later, she left Anthony and moved to New York to become a reporter. She was initially rejected by The New York Times, but later obtained a trial assignment there. Fellow reporters gave her the nickname Polly Pry because they said she could pry information out of anyone.\n\nOne account says Pry first met Packer and interviewed him while researching an article about Colorado\u2019s prison system and the Colorado State Penitentiary; a biographical account dates that meeting to 1901. After hearing Packer\u2019s story, she partly believed him and used her position as a reporter to try to help him. She believed he was innocent of murder and advocated for him in print throughout the trial. In the early 1900s, she launched a journalistic and media campaign to publicize his plight and release him. She wrote stories presenting him as a man of the people: an ex-soldier persecuted by the establishment, a common man caught in a regrettable situation, someone in the wrong place at the wrong time who had survived horrors most people could not imagine. She argued that Packer\u2019s sentence was so harsh because of the cannibalism, and claimed that anyone would have done the same in that situation. In her column, she also argued that since sailors were legally allowed to eat people when lost at sea, the same rules should apply to people stranded in the mountains. Another account says she used Packer\u2019s Army service to portray him as a common man who had been caught in a regrettable situation, had done what he had to do to survive, and had been crucified for violating civilized sensibilities by resorting to cannibalism.\n\nOver time, Pry\u2019s stories led local businesses to arrange petitions for Packer\u2019s release. They also led to petitions and requests reaching Colorado Governor Charles Thomas\u2019s office, although those efforts met strong opposition. Packer gained allies who signed a petition for his pardon. One supporter wrote to Thomas that, even if Packer had committed the crime, his privations made responsibility difficult to assign: \u201cThe line between sanity and insanity is often very thin, and I don\u2019t think we understand it.\u201d The Colorado State Archives says its Alfred Packer Collection includes letters to the Governor concerning Packer\u2019s parole, as well as parole records and a Governor\u2019s pardon. Packer had made many appeals for a pardon after entering prison but was repeatedly denied. A separate account says he filed five appeals and was roundly denied each time. In 1899, when Denver Post writer Polly Pry visited him at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Canon City, she wrote that \u201cthe corroding desire for freedom has eaten into his heart\u201d and that \u201cthe blind injustice of the world has seared into his soul.\u201d\n\nThe sources give differing accounts of the outcome and date. One says that Governor Thomas ultimately relented and, in his last official act before leaving office, paroled but did not pardon Packer on the condition that Packer would not try to profit from his story. Another says that, as his last act in office, Thomas granted parole but not pardon. A historical account says Packer received parole on February 8, 1901, after a campaign initially spearheaded by his old acquaintance Duane Hatch, who petitioned for nearly a decade before his efforts came to Pry\u2019s attention. A different account says Packer received parole in 1905, while another says Pry\u2019s campaign and Harry Tammen\u2019s intervention eventually led to a pardon on January 7, 1901. The sources therefore do not agree on whether he was pardoned or only paroled, or on the date. One account says Packer had served 18 years of a 40-year sentence; another says he was granted parole after serving 15 years.\n\nDenver Post publishers Fred Bonfils and Harry Tammen hired a lawyer to defend Packer. Because Pry\u2019s Packer column increased the paper\u2019s revenue and readership, one account says she persuaded Tammen to pay for Packer\u2019s defense and demand a retrial. After parole, Packer worked as a guard at The Denver Post and later as a ranch hand. Many people believed that his Denver Post job came directly from Pry and her employers securing his release. Packer showed enduring respect for Pry and called her his \u201cLiberator.\u201d\n\nAfter the trial, Pry was present during a confrontation involving W. W. Anderson, Packer\u2019s attorney, and the owners of The Denver Post who had hired him. The owners learned that Anderson had taken a double payment and demanded that he return the money. During the confrontation, Anderson pulled a gun and shot both men. As he prepared to shoot again, Pry jumped in front of Harry Heye Tammen and tried to shield him. She stalled Anderson long enough for the police to arrive and used her skirt to stop the bleeding. Rumors later circulated that Pry\u2019s skirts had saved her publishers\u2019 lives by slowing bullets from the gun. When union workers boycotted The Denver Post over her stance on labor issues and immigration, she founded her own paper, Polly Pry. Afterward, gunmen attacked her at home rather than at work. Pry was a strong advocate of free speech and, during World War I, came to the aid of French war orphans.\n\nPry\u2019s involvement has also been dramatized. Two film versions of the Alferd Packer story included her. In Jim Roberson\u2019s The Legend of Alfred Packer (1980), Cynthia Nessin portrayed Polly Pry. The film included the Anderson incident, but the man was not named and was called \u201cGunman\u201d in the credits. After Pry begins telling Packer\u2019s story, she does not appear again in the film, and the shooting is not mentioned again. The film depicts her as hard-drinking and forceful. Trey Parker\u2019s Cannibal! The Musical featured Toddy Walters as Polly Pry and went more deeply into her involvement with the case, but ignored the fact that she was married in order to make her a love interest for Packer.\n\n(SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_6 \u00b7 SRC_8 \u00b7 SRC_9 \u00b7 SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_11)\n\n### The missing men `established`\n\nAccording to the expedition leader, Alfred told him that after leaving the Ute camp, his party followed the river on horseback until the horses could go no farther. The leader unloaded their provisions and walked the horses back to the Ute camp. The men then likely walked farther down the river and took a path high into the mountains. The leader knew they did not have enough supplies to reach the agency, but he had grown tired of arguing with Alfred. They had only about 14 days of food, inadequate clothes, no snowshoes or flints, and hardly any matches. Their equipment consisted of only a few rifles, one pistol, a hatchet, and some knives. After the leader turned back, no one heard from those men again until Alfred appeared at the agency months later.\n\nWhen Alfred arrived, he looked bloated and well-fed, unlike the starving men people usually saw coming out of the wilderness. After he felt better, he went to Saguache, where he rented a room and a saloon and spent over $100 on drinking and gambling. He spent another $78 in the general store. People said he gave conflicting stories when he drank, sometimes carried a different wallet, and never seemed to run out of cash. He had sold his rifle at the agency for only $10, which did not explain his remaining money. Preston Nutter, the leader of the other party that followed the river, arrived at the agency in May and later went to Saguache for answers. Preston suspected Alfred was lying and told General Adams that the other men would not have left him for dead. The rifle Alfred sold belonged to an old man in the group, and the pipe Alfred left behind belonged to Shannon Bell. When Alfred originally joined the group, he had no money.\n\nGeneral Adams sent a rider to bring Alfred in for questioning. When questioned, Alfred said he got his feet wet and cold, stayed by the fire while the others looked for food, and assumed they had abandoned him when they did not return. A separate account says that nine weeks later Packer arrived at the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency south of present-day Gunnison with a Winchester rifle, a skinning knife, and a coffee pot containing live coals. When Packer arrived at the agency, he was haggard and worn but otherwise fit. Another of the original gold seekers asked where Packer had gotten his spending money. Packer admitted that the small band had starved in the San Juans, that Israel Swan had died from hunger and exposure, and that Packer had eaten him.\n\nTwo of the five men who had taken the Gunnison River route eventually arrived at the Los Pin\u00f3s Indian Agency, and the remaining three men, including Loutsenhizer, arrived a few days later. General Charles Adams told the men he had already met Alfred Packer and that Packer spoke of being deserted by his companions. All five men immediately discredited Packer\u2019s account to Adams and his staff. Loutsenhizer said the Winchester rifle Packer carried belonged to an elderly man in the party, and that the pipe Packer left at the agency belonged to Shannon Bell.\n\nThe following August, John A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper's Weekly, found the site of the incident. Randolph discovered all five bodies at the foot of Slumgullion Pass, two miles southeast of Lake City, Colorado, in a pine-shaded gulch. The bodies lay above the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, now colloquially known as Dead Man's Gulch. The site matched the description of where Packer had originally claimed that only Bell was killed. The snow covering the bodies and campsite had melted during the preceding four months. Randolph sketched the scene and alerted authorities in nearby Lake City. Harper's Weekly covered the story two months later, in its October 17, 1874, edition, and included Randolph's illustration.\n\nThe local coroner and law enforcement went to the site with about 20 volunteers and found all five bodies in various states of decomposition after four months outdoors. First responders said it appeared that extreme violence had befallen the men. Frank Miller's head was missing from the campsite. Frank Miller's and Israel Swan's corpses had been heavily scavenged and were little more than scattered bones, and Israel's skull had a jagged chunk missing from it. George Noon and James Humphrey were largely flayed torsos of rotting viscera attached to skeletal legs, but their faces remained intact and bearded. Humphrey's face was slightly more decayed than Noon's. Noon and Humphrey had head blows that suggested a hatchet and had noticeable broken bones.\n\nShannon Bell had skeletal legs, arms cut to the bones, hands that were still fully skinned, and a torso that was almost wholly flayed. Bell's remaining corpse was a mass of viscera with a thick red beard and bushy hair still on his face. The relatively undamaged state of Bell's face suggested that he had been the last to die. The top of Bell's skull had been ripped open. The three men whose bodies were still intact or partly intact had flesh and muscle cut from choice meaty locations; no attempt had been made to consume bone marrow or any organs. Preston Nutter identified the bodies as the five missing men. A rifle broken in two was found close to the bodies, and because of the damage to the remains, the rifle was presumed to have been used to bludgeon one or more of the men.\n\nNothing was heard from the six prospectors for nearly three months. The men became lost and, without proper provisions or equipment, were cold and hungry. Of the six men, only Packer lived.\n\n(SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_2 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_10)\n\n### Alfred Packer\u2019s background `established`\n\nAlfred Packer, also known as John Schwartze, Alferd Packer, the Colorado Cannibal, and, in local newspapers, the \u201cMan-Eater,\u201d was an American prospector and self-proclaimed wilderness guide known for cannibalism. One account describes Alferd Packer, \u201cThe Colorado Cannibal,\u201d as one of the most notorious and most misunderstood tales in Colorado history. The sources do not settle the spelling of his first name: official court papers from the time spell it \u201cAlfred,\u201d while Packer signed his name \u201cAlferd\u201d; his grave is also marked with a veteran\u2019s tombstone listing \u201cAlfred.\u201d The Colorado State Archives\u2019 Alfred Packer Collection includes military grave registration, along with legal and historical records concerning him.\n\nPacker was one of three children born to James Packer and Esther Packer, n\u00e9e Griner. By the early 1850s, his father had moved the family to LaGrange County, Indiana, where he worked as a cabinetmaker. Packer is said to have had a bitter relationship with his parents. In his late teens, he moved to Minnesota and worked as a shoemaker. He suffered from epilepsy from an early age and was prone to seizures. During the American Civil War, he served in the Union Army. One account says he enlisted on April 22, 1862, in Winona, Minnesota, in Company F of the 16th Infantry Regiment, and was honorably discharged eight months later because epilepsy triggered seizures. He enlisted a second time, on June 25, 1863, in the 8th Iowa Cavalry Regiment at Ottumwa, Iowa, and was honorably discharged again on April 22, 1864, after his epilepsy caused further seizures. The shorter biographical account instead says that he enlisted twice and that both terms lasted only a few months because of epilepsy and seizures; both accounts agree that he was honorably discharged both times.\n\nAfterward, Packer traveled west and worked at numerous jobs over the next nine years. He worked as a hunter, wagon teamster, ranch hand, field worker, range hand, and as someone who drove horses and other draft animals across the West. He also took odd jobs for mining companies. He worked for a couple of months as a guide, but people later said he was ill-suited to the role and prone to losing his way. He had a habit of getting lost, arguing with clients, stealing from people, and lying. His seizures and overall attitude meant that he never kept a job for long. He worked mining-related jobs from mine to mine but never found prosperity in mining; he worked briefly as a miner in the Colorado Territory and then moved on to the Utah Territory. He also tried mining in Colorado, but kept losing jobs because of his bad attitude and because no one could trust him. People who knew him generally disliked and distrusted him because of his argumentative personality, near-pathological or scurrilous lying, and reputation for theft.\n\nIn the fall of 1873, a group of 20 prospectors set out toward southwestern Colorado and the San Juan country. The group was traveling to newly found goldfields near Breckenridge, Colorado; another account spells the destination \u201cBrackenridge.\u201d Six men, including Packer, ultimately continued into the San Juan Mountains during the winter, and two months later only one man emerged from the mountains. Rather than looking emaciated, as early explorers were often later found, he looked weirdly fed. The surviving man was Alfred Packer. A separate account says that the six men set off from Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 20, 1873, while another says Packer was one of six prospectors who headed into the Colorado mountains in February 1874. These accounts therefore give different dates for the expedition\u2019s departure.\n\nPacker was not initially with the party. George Tracy said that the party encountered a 23-year-old man called Alfred Packer near Provo. The expedition account says the group met him south of Salt Lake City, about 25 miles into the journey. Packer asked where the men were going, learned that they were headed for the goldfields, and offered his services as a guide. He claimed he was both a prospector and a guide who knew the territory between Utah and Breckenridge, or the San Juan territory, well. Because he had no provisions, he offered $25 to join the band. The prospectors accepted him because they needed a guide and thought that someone who knew the route would help them. Party members later said that Packer may have overstated or even fabricated his familiarity with the area. In fact, he had no idea where he was going, although he had worked as a guide for a few months.\n\nDuring the journey, Packer was reported to have left with only a Colt revolver and no rifle. This conflicts with the account of his later arrival at the agency, which describes him carrying a rifle. He was also reported to be greedy with rations, lazy, obstinate, and obsessed with how much money the party carried. He quarreled constantly with Frank Miller. Preston Nutter later called him a \u201cwhining fraud\u201d at Packer\u2019s first trial. His epileptic seizures made his presence in the group strenuous, and there were rumors that he had spent time in jail in Salt Lake City on a charge of murdering a hunting partner.\n\nOn April 16, 1874, according to the agency account, men at the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado, were eating breakfast when Packer appeared alone through a snowstorm. He wore rags over his feet instead of shoes, carried a coffee pot on one side, and had a rifle on the other. General Charles Adams, who oversaw the agency, had Alfred given food, and Alfred ate so fast that he threw up. Over whiskey, Alfred said that the others with him had become too exhausted to continue, that he had snow blindness and frostbite, and that he had been left behind to fend for himself. He claimed that he survived for months on rabbits, roots, and rosebuds and escaped only because of his experience as a guide.\n\nPacker gave more than one account of what had happened, and the sources report that he was known for cannibalism. The Colorado State Archives\u2019 collection includes his confessions, while the scientific team that exhumed the victims did not settle the spelling of his first name. The sources preserve both the story Packer told about surviving on plants and small animals and the broader historical account identifying him as the Colorado Cannibal and associating him with the cannibalism of his companions.\n\nThe Washington report likewise describes Packer as a \u201cscurrilous liar and thief by reputation.\u201d\n\n(SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_4 \u00b7 SRC_5 \u00b7 SRC_6 \u00b7 SRC_7 \u00b7 SRC_8)\n\n## 5. Disputed & Uncertain\n\n### The survival story is real in the sources, but the exact meaning of it is still contested. `established`\n\n*Against: the pushback in the same corpus.*\n\n**Case for the claim** \u2014 The survival story is present in the sources. Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, then tried every day to escape but could not. He said that, as a result, he lived on the flesh of the dead men for most of the sixty days he was out. The analysis likewise suggested that Packer did shoot Bell in self-defense. This account is supported by the reports that Packer was known for cannibalism, later confessed to living off his companions\u2019 flesh, and gave more than one version of the circumstances. The men had also neared the point of eating the horses themselves. Other reported details\u2014Packer\u2019s possession of several different wallets and his statement that several thousand dollars had been found on Swan and divided among the men\u2014form part of the surrounding story. (SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_11 \u00b7 SRC_2)\n\n**Case against the claim** \u2014 The pushback in the same corpus disputes the interpretation of the survival story, but no specific contrary evidence has been supplied here. The materials provided do not identify what the pushback measured, who advanced it, or which part of Packer\u2019s account it rejects. Accordingly, the opposing case can only maintain that the reported confession, the self-defense account, the references to cannibalism, and the surrounding details do not by themselves settle the exact meaning of the story. On the supplied record, that pushback remains unspecified and cannot be tested against the evidence described for the other side.\n\n### There are real clues in the physical evidence, but the sources do not let it settle the case cleanly. `contested`\n\n*Against: the pushback in the same corpus.*\n\n**Case for the claim** \u2014 The physical evidence offers genuine clues, even if it does not produce a clean resolution. Five of the men believed Alfred Packer and went with him, while Packer claimed that Shannon Bell then shot the remaining men. Forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group, and the text says it appears likely that Packer killed another prospector in self-defense before gnawing on the bodies of men who had already died. Packer also said that Bell attacked him with a hatchet when Packer returned after searching for food, prompting him to fire twice with a pistol and shoot Bell in self-defense. The analysis suggested that Packer did shoot Bell defensively, and Packer was known for cannibalism. Taken together, these details provide real physical and testimonial clues, though they still leave room for dispute. (SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_7 \u00b7 SRC_8)\n\n**Case against the claim** \u2014 The pushback is that the murder conviction was overturned. That outcome undercuts any confident conclusion that the evidence established the case as presented and leaves the interpretation of the physical clues unsettled. This side's case rests on the single reported fact of the overturned conviction, rather than on a fuller alternative reconstruction in the supplied evidence; accordingly, it challenges finality without resolving what happened. (SRC_10)\n\n### He spent years dodging justice before he was caught again. `contested`\n\n*Against: the pushback in the same corpus.*\n\n**Case for the claim** \u2014 Their accounts say that, once his story was questioned, he escaped, hid from authorities, and remained on the run or \u201con the lam\u201d for eight, nine, or nearly ten years. They further report that he was eventually tracked down in Wyoming, captured nine years later, and returned to Lake City. On this evidence, he did not merely leave custody briefly: he remained free for years before authorities caught him again. (SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_5)\n\n**Case against the claim** \u2014 The pushback in the corpus points out only that Packer \u201cremained a free man for nine years after the escape.\u201d That wording establishes a period outside custody, but it does not by itself establish that he was actively \u201cdodging justice,\u201d hiding, or evading an ongoing pursuit. Nor does this counterpoint independently supply details about how he was eventually caught. Thus, on the evidence available to the pushback, the stronger language about deliberate evasion is not demonstrated; its case rests on this single characterization of him as free for nine years. (SRC_10)\n\n### Polly Pry and other supporters helped push the sympathy campaign around Packer. `contested`\n\n*Against: the pushback in the same corpus.*\n\n**Case for the claim** \u2014 He had been sentenced to be hanged, yet he begged for mercy and promised to make a full confession. His story eventually reached Polly Pry, a reporter for the Denver Post, giving the case a public platform through which supporters could press for leniency. The physical evidence\u2014a beaten path from the corpses to Packer\u2019s crude shelter\u2014also supplied material for retelling the episode, while Packer pleaded not guilty and later testified in his own defense at the second trial. On this account, Polly Pry and other supporters helped turn the case from a straightforward hanging sentence into a public sympathy campaign. This case rests on the accounts in SRC\\_11 and SRC\\_4, rather than on a broad body of independently testable evidence. (SRC_11 \u00b7 SRC_4)\n\n**Case against the claim** \u2014 The pushback in the corpus undercuts the idea that Polly Pry and other supporters established a persuasive sympathy campaign. Packer was known for cannibalism, and the five original party members said they did not believe a single part of his story. Only Packer returned in the winter of 1874; of the six men, he alone lived, making his account impossible for the other members to confirm. The jury rejected his story, even though he pleaded not guilty, and the record warns that the story Packer told and what actually occurred are not always the same. Afterward, Packer went on to Saguache. From this perspective, the public narrative was met by serious credibility objections, and the available evidence does not show that the sympathy effort overcame them. (SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_4 \u00b7 SRC_5 \u00b7 SRC_8)\n\n### The honest answer is that the sources still do not agree on whether Packer was murderer, survivor, or both. `established`\n\n*Against: the pushback in the same corpus.*\n\n**Case that the sources still do not agree** \u2014 The evidence points in different directions: Alfred\u2019s charges were expected to be upgraded from manslaughter to first-degree murder, yet his murder conviction was overturned. She said that the sentence was so harsh because of the cannibalism, while other evidence indicates that cannibalism was not regarded as the main issue in determining Packer\u2019s guilt. Some forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group, and Packer was known for cannibalism. At the same time, a modern myth says that Packer was convicted of cannibalism, even though the murder conviction was overturned. Taken together, these accounts leave open whether Packer should be understood as a murderer, a survivor, or both. (SRC_10 \u00b7 SRC_2 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_7 \u00b7 SRC_8)\n\n**Pushback against the claim** \u2014 The corpus identifies pushback against this conclusion, but no specific evidence for that pushback has been supplied here. It therefore cannot be stated more precisely who made the objection, what was measured or reported, or how it establishes that the sources agree on whether Packer was a murderer, a survivor, or both. On the evidence provided, the against case rests only on the existence of that unspecified pushback, rather than on a described source or independently stated finding.\n\n### Packer provided multiple contradictory accounts regarding the deaths of his companions and how he obtained their belongings and money. `contested`\n\n*Held across: SRC\\_1.*\n\n**Case for the claim** \u2014 Packer was known for cannibalism, pleaded not guilty, and was evasive when asked about his missing companions. Under interrogation, he gave the first of multiple contradictory confessions, and he then went on to Saguache. Taken together, this sequence supports the view that Packer changed his account of what happened to his companions and of how he came into possession of their belongings and money. This case rests on a single source, SRC\\_1. (SRC_1)\n\n**Case against the claim** \u2014 Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and analysis suggested that he did shoot Bell in self-defense. Packer testified in his own defense at the second trial, and the evidence seemed to corroborate his claim that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer shot Bell in self-defense. Although Packer was known for cannibalism and pleaded not guilty, whether Bell was actually shot in self-defense remains unanswered. Packer was found guilty of Bell's murder in 1883, but that verdict does not by itself establish that he gave multiple contradictory accounts about the deaths or about obtaining the belongings and money. (SRC_2 \u00b7 SRC_3 \u00b7 SRC_5)\n\n### Alfred Packer led five men into the Colorado San Juan Mountains in winter 1874 and emerged as the sole survivor in a well-fed state. `contested`\n\n*Held across: SRC\\_1.*\n\n**Case for the claim** \u2014 On February 9, Packer and five other prospectors left the Ute leader Ouray\u2019s winter camp, still facing roughly 120 miles of difficult mountain country before reaching the newly discovered goldfields. The five men went with Packer, and witnesses later testified at trial that he had deliberately led them astray to steal their possessions. Packer ultimately emerged as the only survivor and became known as the \u201cColorado Cannibal\u201d after confessing that he had eaten his dead comrades while trapped in the mountains in February 1874. His reported cannibalism is consistent with the account that he survived the ordeal in a well-fed state. This case rests on the account compiled in the single source SRC\\_1, including testimony and later descriptions of Packer\u2019s confession. (SRC_1)\n\n**Case against the claim** \u2014 He was accused of deliberately leading the other five men astray and stealing their possessions, and he was eventually tried and convicted of premeditated murder. His death sentence was later commuted to 40 years in prison, and after winning a retrial he was sentenced to 40 years for five counts of voluntary manslaughter. The source also records that, after his story was questioned, Packer escaped jail and hid from justice for nine years. Although Packer was known for cannibalism, the legal history\u2014convictions variously listed as murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter\u2014complicates any straightforward acceptance of the claim that he simply led the party and emerged as its well-fed sole survivor. This case also relies on the single source SRC\\_1, whose account cannot be independently tested here. (SRC_1 \u00b7 SRC_3)\n\n### Physical and forensic evidence from the site presented conflicting indications regarding whether Packer committed murder or acted in self-defense. `single source`\n\n*Held across: SRC\\_1.*\n\n**Case for the claim** \u2014 Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and the analysis suggested that he did shoot Bell in self-defense. This appeared to corroborate Packer\u2019s account that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer\u2019s shooting of Bell was defensive. Packer also pleaded not guilty and testified in his own defense at the second trial. At the same time, Packer was known for cannibalism, and he was found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883. Thus, although the evidence seemed to support his self-defense account, whether Bell was actually shot in self-defense remained unanswered. (SRC_1)\n\n**Case against the claim** \u2014 No evidence against the claim was supplied. Accordingly, this side cannot identify specific evidence showing that the indications were not conflicting.\n\n## 6. Details & Anecdotes\n\n- A commenter says the Lake City museum has items Packer created while in prison. (SRC_5)\n  - The museum preserves a tangible connection to Packer\u2019s years behind bars. These objects give visitors something more concrete than the surviving stories and legends.\n- A commenter says one prison-made item was a large and beautiful doll house for the warden\u2019s daughter. (SRC_5)\n  - The doll house suggests that Packer\u2019s prison work was detailed and skilled rather than merely utilitarian. Its association with the warden\u2019s daughter also points to an unusual degree of trust or goodwill during his incarceration.\n- A commenter says Lake City still races coffins on Main Street on the Fourth of July in Packer\u2019s honor. (SRC_5)\n  - The race turns Packer\u2019s grim story into a recurring civic spectacle. It shows how the community has transformed the episode into local tradition and entertainment.\n- A commenter says Packer seemed to be a model prisoner who made the best of the 16 years he spent incarcerated. (SRC_5)\n  - This view complicates a purely villainous image of Packer by emphasizing his conduct during imprisonment. His long sentence becomes part of a story about adaptation and productivity as well as punishment.\n- A commenter says the photo above makes Packer resemble Charles Manson. (SRC_5)\n  - The comparison reflects how a photograph can shape the way later audiences imagine a notorious figure. It links Packer visually to another infamous criminal, even though the resemblance is only an impression.\n- A commenter says that as a young boy in the 1960s, his father took the family to the site or marker of the dinner event. (SRC_5)\n  - The family visit shows that the dinner site or marker had become part of local memory by the 1960s. It also illustrates how the story was passed down through ordinary family outings.\n- A commenter quotes his little brother asking why Packer did not just go into town and eat. (SRC_5)\n  - The child\u2019s question exposes the story\u2019s basic absurdity in a simple, memorable way. It reduces the elaborate legend to the practical problem of why anyone would choose such desperate circumstances instead of finding food in town.\n- A commenter notes that the blog misspelled Packer\u2019s name about half the time. (SRC_5)\n  - The repeated misspelling undermines confidence in the blog\u2019s presentation of the story. It also signals that the discussion is dealing with lore and inherited sources whose details were not always carefully checked.\n- The author replies that readers can still enjoy Packer burgers despite the correction of the lore. (SRC_5)\n  - The reply separates the factual correction from the enduring appeal of the legend. Even after the story is challenged, its name and imagery remain usable in local culture.\n- The author replies that Alferd\u2019s name is sometimes spelled Alfred in the blog because quoted sources used that spelling. (SRC_5)\n  - The explanation distinguishes the author\u2019s own usage from the spelling found in source material. It shows that the inconsistency comes from preserving quotations rather than from a deliberate change to the historical name.\n\n## 7. Info Gaps\n\n- **You don't have the primary trial transcripts or official court testimony from Packer's 1883 murder trial. Without them, you can't show viewers what was actually said under oath versus what modern sources claim happened.** A major historical murder trial that went to the state supreme court would leave behind formal court records and contemporary sworn testimony. \u2192 Query the Colorado State Archives digital collections specifically for the 1883 Lake City trial transcripts and court filings.\n- **Your sources mention that Chief Ouray and Indigenous leaders warned the expedition, but you don't have direct contemporary accounts or recorded statements from the Ute Tribe members who interacted with the party.** Chief Ouray's encampment was the last known stop before the group entered the mountains. First-hand perspectives from the Ute witnesses who saw the group's physical condition and equipment are essential for a complete timeline. \u2192 Search for historical records from the Los Pinos Indian Agency or regional 1874 news reports detailing Chief Ouray's account of the expedition.\n- **You have a nine-year gap between Packer escaping jail in 1874 and his recapture in Wyoming in 1883. Your sources don't detail how he successfully evaded authorities for nearly a decade under the alias John Swartze.** A long-term fugitive evasion is a major narrative arc in a crime story. A viewer would expect to know how he hid, where he worked, and how he was eventually exposed. \u2192 Research 1883 Wyoming newspaper accounts detailing Packer's arrest in Cheyenne and his life as 'John Swartze'.\n- **Your sources note that Packer was paroled in 1901 and died in 1907, but you have no details about his daily life, where he lived, or his interactions with the public during those final six years.** When telling the full biography of a notorious figure who gained public sympathy and parole, you need to show the resolution of their life after release. \u2192 Look into local Littleton or Denver census records and local obituary archives from 1907 covering Packer's final years in Deer Creek.\n\n## 8. Source Trail\n\n- **SRC_1** His Horrifying Ordeal in Colorado Mountains (youtube, 2023-11-19T00:00:00.000Z, Scary Interesting) \u2014 It uniquely describes Packer bursting into the Los Pi\u00f1os mess hall during a snowstorm in foot rags, carrying a coffee pot and rifle, and vomiting after eating too quickly.\n- **SRC_2** Alferd Packer (article, 2009-02-08, Author Gulliford; Andrew) \u2014 It uniquely reports Packer\u2019s claim that the starving party cooked and ate their rawhide moccasins after three or four days without provisions.\n- **SRC_3** Alfred Packer - Wikipedia (article, 2003-08-05, Authority control databases) \u2014 It uniquely notes that Packer\u2019s convictions included murder convictions later overturned and five counts of voluntary manslaughter, with his death sentence commuted to 40 years.\n- **SRC_4** The Colorado Cannibal: The Story of Alferd Packer - Law Week Colorado (article, 2022-02-09, Jess Brovsky-Eaker) \u2014 It uniquely records that Packer gave three confessions\u2014in 1874, 1883, and 1897\u2014and that his first version said Shannon Bell killed \u201cCalifornia\u201d before Packer killed Bell in self-defense.\n- **SRC_5** Alferd Packer: The Truth is Out There (Or Right Here) (article, 2015-10-21, history.denverlibrary.org) \u2014 It uniquely reports that Packer escaped the Saguache jail, remained at large for eight years, and was convicted of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883.\n- **SRC_6** Alfred Packer (article, 2026-01-01, archives.colorado.gov) \u2014 It uniquely identifies the Colorado State Archives\u2019 online Alfred Packer Collection, which includes penitentiary, parole, court, confession, gubernatorial-pardon, and mugshot records.\n- **SRC_7** Alfred Packer ate 'em, forensic expert says - UPI Archives (article, 2026-08-29, KAREN TIMMONS) \u2014 It uniquely reports forensic professor James Starrs\u2019s claim that exhumed remains proved Packer butchered and skinned all five companions.\n- **SRC_8** Was the \u2018Colorado Cannibal\u2019 a villain or a victim? You decide. (article, 2023-03-28, Brendan Bures; Benjamin Rasmussen) \u2014 It uniquely states that Packer suffered epilepsy, was discharged from the Union Army because of seizures, and that an exhumed skull had a hole in its forehead.\n- **SRC_9** Polly Pry - Wikipedia (article, 2007-01-08, Authority control databases International VIAF National United States France BnF data Other Yale LUX) \u2014 It uniquely identifies Polly Pry as Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan, the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter, whose career connected her to the Packer case.\n- **SRC_10** Alferd Packer (article, 2025-05-05, Town of Lake City) \u2014 It uniquely details the 21-man expedition\u2019s division at the Ute encampment into three factions after Chief Ouray warned them about the winter conditions.\n- **SRC_11** Cannibal Correspondence - True West Magazine (article, 2022-09-06, Kellen Cutsforth) \u2014 It uniquely describes Packer receiving romantic fan mail from adoring women while incarcerated, framing this as an early example of what is now called hybristophilia.\n", "html": "<title>The full story behind a prospector&#x27;s horrifying 1874 ordeal in Colorado&#x27;s San Juan Mountains \u2014 what really happened, beyond what this video tells</title>\n<style>  :root {\n    --ground: #EFEEE8;\n    --panel: #E7E5DC;\n    --ink: #20231F;\n    --ink-soft: #5A5E56;\n    --accent: #175E66;\n    --accent-soft: #175E661A;\n    --rule: #CFCDC2;\n    --solid: #2F6E45;\n    --solid-bg: #2F6E4518;\n    --contested: #9A6414;\n    --contested-bg: #9A641418;\n    --network: #9C3D22;\n    --network-bg: #9C3D2216;\n    --anno: #175E66;\n  }\n  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {\n    :root:not([data-theme=\"light\"]) {\n      --ground: #15181A;\n      --panel: #1D2124;\n      --ink: #E6E3D8;\n      --ink-soft: #9AA097;\n      --accent: #63B8BC;\n      --accent-soft: #63B8BC1C;\n      --rule: #33383B;\n      --solid: #6FBE8B;\n      --solid-bg: #6FBE8B1A;\n      --contested: #D9A84E;\n      --contested-bg: #D9A84E1A;\n      --network: #E07B5C;\n      --network-bg: #E07B5C18;\n      --anno: #63B8BC;\n    }\n  }\n  :root[data-theme=\"dark\"] {\n    --ground: #15181A;\n    --panel: #1D2124;\n    --ink: #E6E3D8;\n    --ink-soft: #9AA097;\n    --accent: #63B8BC;\n    --accent-soft: #63B8BC1C;\n    --rule: #33383B;\n    --solid: #6FBE8B;\n    --solid-bg: #6FBE8B1A;\n    --contested: #D9A84E;\n    --contested-bg: #D9A84E1A;\n    --network: #E07B5C;\n    --network-bg: #E07B5C18;\n    --anno: #63B8BC;\n  }\n\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  body {\n    background: var(--ground);\n    color: var(--ink);\n    font-family: \"Iowan Old Style\", \"Palatino Linotype\", Palatino, Georgia, serif;\n    font-size: 17px;\n    line-height: 1.62;\n    margin: 0;\n    padding: 0 20px 96px;\n  }\n  .page { max-width: 74ch; margin: 0 auto; }\n  .mono {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n  }\n\n  /* ---------- masthead ---------- */\n  .mockup-band {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 11.5px;\n    letter-spacing: 0.08em;\n    text-transform: uppercase;\n    color: var(--anno);\n    border: 1px dashed var(--anno);\n    border-radius: 3px;\n    padding: 8px 12px;\n    margin: 28px 0 40px;\n  }\n  header.mast { margin: 0 0 12px; }\n  .doc-kicker {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12px;\n    letter-spacing: 0.14em;\n    text-transform: uppercase;\n    color: var(--ink-soft);\n    margin: 0 0 10px;\n  }\n  h1 {\n    font-size: 40px;\n    line-height: 1.12;\n    font-weight: 600;\n    margin: 0 0 18px;\n    text-wrap: balance;\n    letter-spacing: -0.01em;\n  }\n  .meta-strip {\n    display: flex;\n    flex-wrap: wrap;\n    gap: 8px 22px;\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12.5px;\n    color: var(--ink-soft);\n    padding: 12px 0;\n    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);\n    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);\n    margin-bottom: 8px;\n  }\n  .meta-strip b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }\n\n  /* ---------- section scaffolding ---------- */\n  section { margin-top: 64px; }\n  .sec-head { border-top: 3px solid var(--ink); padding-top: 14px; }\n  .sec-num {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12px;\n    letter-spacing: 0.12em;\n    color: var(--ink-soft);\n  }\n  h2 {\n    font-size: 27px;\n    font-weight: 600;\n    margin: 4px 0 6px;\n    text-wrap: balance;\n  }\n  .anno {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12px;\n    line-height: 1.55;\n    color: var(--anno);\n    background: var(--accent-soft);\n    border-left: 3px solid var(--anno);\n    padding: 8px 12px;\n    margin: 12px 0 26px;\n  }\n  /* Work order I.29b: the update note sits above the document, styled from the\n     locked palette so it reads as part of the Briefing rather than bolted on. */\n  .addendum {\n    border: 1px solid var(--anno);\n    border-left-width: 4px;\n    background: var(--accent-soft);\n    padding: 14px 18px 4px;\n    margin: 0 0 30px;\n  }\n  .addendum h2 {\n    font-size: 19px;\n    margin: 0 0 6px;\n  }\n  .addendum ul {\n    margin: 8px 0 12px 18px;\n    padding: 0;\n  }\n  h3 {\n    font-size: 19px;\n    font-weight: 600;\n    margin: 34px 0 8px;\n  }\n  p { margin: 0 0 16px; }\n  p.tight { margin-bottom: 8px; }\n\n  /* ---------- chips ---------- */\n  .chip {\n    display: inline-block;\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 10.5px;\n    letter-spacing: 0.07em;\n    text-transform: uppercase;\n    border-radius: 3px;\n    padding: 2px 7px 1px;\n    vertical-align: 2px;\n    white-space: nowrap;\n  }\n  .chip.solid { color: var(--solid); background: var(--solid-bg); }\n  .chip.contested { color: var(--contested); background: var(--contested-bg); }\n  .chip.network { color: var(--network); background: var(--network-bg); }\n  .src {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12px;\n    color: var(--ink-soft);\n  }\n\n  /* ---------- section 1 body ---------- */\n  .read p:first-of-type::first-line { letter-spacing: 0.01em; }\n  .read .lede { font-size: 18.5px; }\n\n  /* ---------- player cards ---------- */\n  .players { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14px; margin-top: 20px; }\n  @media (max-width: 620px) { .players { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }\n  details.player {\n    background: var(--panel);\n    border-radius: 4px;\n    align-self: start;\n  }\n  details.player summary {\n    cursor: pointer;\n    list-style: none;\n    padding: 14px 16px 12px;\n    position: relative;\n  }\n  details.player summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }\n  details.player summary::after {\n    content: \"+\";\n    position: absolute;\n    top: 12px; right: 14px;\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 15px;\n    color: var(--accent);\n  }\n  details.player[open] summary::after { content: \"\\2212\"; }\n  details.player summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n  .player .name { display: block; font-weight: 600; font-size: 16.5px; padding-right: 22px; }\n  .player .role {\n    display: block;\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 11px;\n    letter-spacing: 0.08em;\n    text-transform: uppercase;\n    color: var(--ink-soft);\n    margin-top: 1px;\n  }\n  details.player p { font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.55; margin: 0; padding: 0 16px 14px; }\n\n  /* ---------- timeline ---------- */\n  .timeline { list-style: none; margin: 20px 0 0; padding: 0; }\n  .timeline li {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: 108px 1fr;\n    gap: 16px;\n    padding: 10px 0;\n    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);\n  }\n  .timeline .when {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 13px;\n    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;\n    color: var(--accent);\n    padding-top: 2px;\n  }\n  .timeline .what { font-size: 15.5px; line-height: 1.55; }\n\n  /* ---------- files ---------- */\n  .file { border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 4px; padding: 18px 20px 6px; margin-top: 22px; background: transparent; }\n  .file h3 { margin-top: 0; }\n  .file .filehead { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: baseline; gap: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; }\n  .file p { font-size: 15.5px; }\n  .file-note {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12px;\n    color: var(--ink-soft);\n    margin: 26px 0 0;\n  }\n\n  /* ---------- dispute table ---------- */\n  .disputes { margin-top: 20px; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }\n  .dispute {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr 128px;\n    gap: 16px;\n    padding: 14px 0;\n    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);\n  }\n  @media (max-width: 560px) { .dispute { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 6px; } }\n  .dispute p { font-size: 15.5px; margin: 0 0 4px; }\n  .dispute .holders { font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--ink-soft); margin: 0; }\n  .dispute .verdict { text-align: right; }\n  @media (max-width: 560px) { .dispute .verdict { text-align: left; } }\n\n  /* ---------- dropdowns ---------- */\n  details.more {\n    margin-top: 8px;\n    border: 1px solid var(--rule);\n    border-radius: 4px;\n    background: var(--panel);\n  }\n  details.more summary {\n    cursor: pointer;\n    padding: 7px 12px;\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 11.5px;\n    letter-spacing: 0.05em;\n    color: var(--accent);\n    list-style-position: inside;\n  }\n  details.more summary:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }\n  details.more .body { padding: 2px 16px 12px; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.6; }\n  details.more .body p { margin: 0 0 10px; }\n  details.more .side {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 11px;\n    letter-spacing: 0.08em;\n    text-transform: uppercase;\n    display: block;\n    margin: 10px 0 3px;\n  }\n  details.more .side.for { color: var(--solid); }\n  details.more .side.against { color: var(--network); }\n\n  /* ---------- anecdotes ---------- */\n  .anecdotes { list-style: none; margin: 20px 0 0; padding: 0; }\n  .anecdotes li {\n    padding: 10px 0 10px 18px;\n    border-left: 2px solid var(--rule);\n    margin-bottom: 10px;\n    font-size: 15.5px;\n    line-height: 1.55;\n  }\n\n  /* ---------- open questions ---------- */\n  .oq { counter-reset: oq; list-style: none; margin: 20px 0 0; padding: 0; }\n  .oq li { counter-increment: oq; margin-bottom: 20px; padding-left: 40px; position: relative; }\n  .oq li::before {\n    content: counter(oq, decimal-leading-zero);\n    position: absolute; left: 0; top: 3px;\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 13px;\n    color: var(--accent);\n  }\n  .oq .q { font-weight: 600; }\n  .oq .go {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12.5px;\n    color: var(--ink-soft);\n    display: block;\n    margin-top: 3px;\n  }\n\n  /* ---------- source trail ---------- */\n  .trail { margin-top: 20px; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }\n  .trail-row {\n    display: grid;\n    grid-template-columns: 64px 1fr;\n    gap: 14px;\n    padding: 11px 0;\n    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);\n    font-size: 14.5px;\n    line-height: 1.5;\n  }\n  .trail-row .sid {\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12.5px;\n    color: var(--accent);\n    padding-top: 2px;\n  }\n  .trail-row .what b { font-weight: 600; }\n  .trail-row .contrib { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 13.5px; }\n\n  a { color: var(--accent); text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 2px; }\n  footer {\n    margin-top: 72px;\n    padding-top: 16px;\n    border-top: 3px solid var(--ink);\n    font-family: ui-monospace, \"SF Mono\", Menlo, Consolas, monospace;\n    font-size: 12px;\n    color: var(--ink-soft);\n    line-height: 1.7;\n  }\n  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { * { transition: none !important; } }\n</style>\n<div class=\"page\"><header class=\"mast\"><p class=\"doc-kicker\">Research Briefing</p><h1>The full story behind a prospector&#x27;s horrifying 1874 ordeal in Colorado&#x27;s San Juan Mountains \u2014 what really happened, beyond what this video tells</h1><div class=\"meta-strip\"><span><b>11</b> sources</span><span><b>27,100</b> raw words read</span><span>quotes verified <b>99%</b></span><span>confidence <b>medium</b></span><span>2026-08-30</span></div></header><div class=\"anno\">Corpus balance &mdash; dates: 2003-2026 &middot; stance: believer (1), neutral (10)</div><section><div class=\"sec-head\"><span class=\"sec-num\">SECTION 1</span><h2>The Read</h2></div><div class=\"read\"><p class=\"lede\">Read all eleven entries. Here\u2019s what is actually in the pile, told straight. The sources repeat one core story, but they do not provide eleven independent verdicts. The pile contains one narrative video, one archival collection page, several institutional and local-history accounts, a long Wikipedia entry, a forensic news report, and two magazine-style investigations. Nearly all repeat the same account: in February 1874, Alferd\u2014also recorded as Alfred\u2014Packer led five men into the snowbound San Juan Mountains, emerged alone two months later with their belongings and enough money to attract suspicion, admitted eating human flesh, and spent the rest of his life insisting that he killed only Shannon Bell in self-defense. The Wikipedia article repeats that account in grade-level versions, so those repetitions are not additional evidence. The sources offer different emphases: SRC_1 is the fullest chronology; the Colorado State Archives page points to surviving confessions, court files, parole records, governor\u2019s papers, and Supreme Court case numbers; James Starrs supplies the strongest forensic prosecution.</p><p><strong>Expedition:</strong> Packer\u2019s disaster had begun before the cannibalism. In late 1873, roughly twenty prospectors\u2014accounts vary between twenty and twenty-one\u2014left Utah for reported Colorado goldfields. Robert McGrue, leader of the larger prospecting party, led the larger party; Packer joined after the journey began, presenting himself as a guide familiar with the route. Born January 21, 1842, near Pittsburgh, Packer had served briefly in the Union Army before epilepsy caused two honorable discharges, and had worked as a hunter, teamster, ranch hand, miner, and guide. The accounts describe him as badly suited to guiding: he had little experience, got lost, quarreled, lied, stole, and fixated on how much money other men carried. Heavy snow slowed the expedition, provisions ran out, and by January 21, 1874, the prospectors reached Chief Ouray\u2019s Ute encampment near Montrose already eating horse feed and considering the horses themselves. Ouray supplied food and shelter, offered to let them remain until spring, and warned that no Ute would cross the mountains in those conditions. Accounts differ over whether he said they would die or gave directions around the mountains. Most stayed with Ouray or took the safer Gunnison River route, although that party also nearly starved and needed cowhands\u2019 help. Gold fever led six men onward.</p><p><strong>The crossing:</strong> On February 9, Packer, Israel Swan, George Noon, Frank Miller, James Humphrey, a prospector who left Ouray\u2019s camp, and Shannon Wilson Bell left Ouray\u2019s camp. Sources differ over whether their provisions were meant to last seven or fourteen days, but all agree they were inadequate. They had little warm clothing, no snowshoes, few matches or no flint, and only a few rifles, a pistol, knives, and a hatchet. Ouray advised following the river; Packer argued that a direct mountain route was faster and led the group upward. The expedition leader, Robert McGrue, took them along the river until the horses could go no farther, unloaded their provisions, and turned back, knowing they lacked enough supplies. Packer later said that within days they had eaten their food, rawhide moccasins, rosebuds, and pine gum. He described cold, hunger, snow blindness, prayer, crying, and a vanished trail. After nine weeks, or sixty-five days, he reached the Los Pi\u00f1os Agency on April 16 carrying a rifle, knife, coffee pot, and other equipment, with live coals in the pot in one account\u2014yet looking far better fed than his story suggested.</p><p><strong>Suspicion and confession:</strong> At the agency Packer vomited when first given food. In Saguache he bought a $70 horse, spent roughly $100 at Dolan\u2019s saloon on drinking, gambling, or poker, spent another $78 at a general store, and appeared to possess several wallets despite arriving supposedly penniless. The rifle belonged to an older missing prospector; a knife or pipe belonged to other party members. Preston Nutter, a prospector who rejected Packer\u2019s account, and Oliver Loutsenhizer, men who had stayed with Ouray, rejected Packer\u2019s claim that the others had abandoned him. The discovery of human flesh near the agency caused him to panic, faint, beg for mercy, and provide his first official confession, transcribed by clerk Herman Lauter. Packer gave three major confessions: the May 1874 statement, the March 1883 statement after his capture, and a final account sent in an August 1897 letter to the Rocky Mountain News. In the first, Swan died of hunger and exposure, Humphrey died days later, Miller was killed while Packer gathered wood, Bell shot Noon, and Packer killed Bell; he took money and flesh. In the 1883 version, Bell killed Miller, Humphrey, Noon, and possibly Swan while Packer was scouting, then attacked Packer with a hatchet. Packer said he shot Bell through the stomach, struck him in the head, and lived on the bodies for most of sixty days. His later letters kept that basic account: starvation made Bell violent, Packer killed him in self-defense, and cannibalism sustained him. He called the ordeal \u201cthe midnight horrors of starvation as well as those of utter isolation\u201d and \u201cthe greatest wonder\u201d that he returned to rationality.</p><p><strong>Bodies and escape:</strong> In August 1874, John A. Randolph, an illustrator traveling for Harper\u2019s Weekly, found all five bodies together in a pine-shaded gulch near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison, later called Deadman\u2019s Gulch, and sketched the scene for the October 17 Harper\u2019s Weekly issue. A twenty-person recovery party found severe violence: skull injuries consistent with blows, bodies stripped at meaty locations, deliberate removal of flesh, and cloth replacing shoes, suggesting the leather had been eaten. A path led to Packer\u2019s crude shelter, where possessions remained. The remains contradicted his account of men dying one by one in scattered places, though they did not establish the exact sequence. Contemporary observers theorized that Packer killed the men for their belongings, became trapped by the storm, and used their bodies as a \u201cnatural refrigerator.\u201d Before the charges could be upgraded to first-degree murder, Packer escaped the Saguache log-cabin jail with a key, penknife, and food; who helped him remains unknown. He hid for nine years under the name John Schwartze or Swartze. Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon recognized him in Cheyenne, Wyoming, on March 11, 1883, reported him, and helped return him for trial.</p><p><strong>1. Murder for money versus killing in extremity.:</strong> The prosecution argued that Packer falsely presented himself as a guide, led the men onto a dangerous route, watched their finances, killed them for money, and invented a survival story. Swan was reportedly carrying cash\u2014his family later said about $6,000\u2014and a valuable Winchester. Packer\u2019s contradictory accounts, spending spree, stolen-looking possessions, and attempted attack on agency clerk Herman Lauter damaged him further. At the second trial, hunters testified that deer, antelope, rabbits, and other game remained in the area, undermining his claim that starvation made cannibalism unavoidable. Packer\u2019s narrower defense, later championed by Polly Pry and museum curator David Bailey, was that the men died or were killed by Bell, Packer shot Bell only to save himself, and he ate the dead because otherwise he would have died. Starrs\u2019s modern middle position is that Packer probably killed the men for their belongings but resorted to cannibalism afterward out of necessity.</p><p><strong>2. What the bones prove.:</strong> On July 17, 1989, James Starrs\u2019s team exhumed the five men, who had been buried only about thirteen inches below ground. Starrs declared Packer \u201cguilty as sin,\u201d reporting blunt-force injuries, knife or defleshing marks, and a winter \u201cnatural refrigerator\u201d; UPI said four men had been killed by hatchet blows and the fifth skull was missing. Walter Birkby, the forensic anthropologist who disputed Starrs, the forensic anthropologist assisting him, disagreed: \u201cWe found nothing to corroborate or refute anything Packer said. We\u2019ll never know who did it based on any solid physical evidence.\u201d The fuller account records defensive hacking marks on the forearms of three skeletons, including Bell, and possible bullet holes in pelvic bones, but no definitive conclusion. The evidence supports violence and postmortem butchering more firmly than it proves who attacked whom. It does not show that Packer fired the weapon, that the gun was his, or that the shooting was self-defense. In 1994, David Bailey found an 1862 Colt revolver with three .38-caliber rounds loaded and an accession note saying it came from the crime site. A lead fragment beneath Bell\u2019s remains chemically matched the bullets, and an elk-hip test produced a similar hole. That is the strongest evidence for Packer\u2019s claim that Bell was shot. Grant Houston and Michelle Pierce question the gun\u2019s provenance because visitors routinely found old firearms and bullets near the site. The men were reburied after the exhumation.</p><p><strong>Trials, parole, and death:</strong> Packer\u2019s first trial in 1883 ended in a murder conviction and death sentence. The conviction was overturned because applying the later murder law to the 1874 territorial crime violated the applicable legal rules; the Colorado Supreme Court reversed the sentence in October 1885. His second trial, moved to Gunnison amid local prejudice, ended June 8, 1886, in five voluntary-manslaughter convictions and a forty-year sentence, eight years per victim\u2014the longest U.S. prison sentence then reported. The famous political insult about Packer eating \u201cfive of the seven Democrats\u201d is apocryphal or later retelling; the surviving court record gives a conventional judgment ordering him hanged \u201cuntil you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d Packer filed five unsuccessful appeals; the Colorado Supreme Court upheld the sentence June 19, 1899. Governor Charles Thomas paroled\u2014not pardoned\u2014him on February 8, 1901, after he had served eighteen years, on the condition that he not profit from his story. Sources otherwise vary between fifteen and eighteen years and between 1901 and 1905. Packer worked as a guard, ranch hand, or laborer, made horsehair bridles and dollhouses in prison, returned to prospecting, and was later described as modest and charitable. He died April 23, 1907, aged sixty-five, in Jefferson County, Colorado.</p><p><strong>Legacy and limits:</strong> Polly Pry, whose real name was Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan and who became the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter, portrayed Packer as a Civil War veteran and victim of a prejudiced system. Her campaign, aided by petitions and supporters including Duane Hatch, helped secure parole, not a pardon; the State Archives preserves the records that could test the campaign in detail. Packer\u2019s letters maintained that he killed only Bell, while women including Rebecca Newman wrote seeking his account and sympathy. After release, some Lake City residents saw a calculating killer; others saw a damaged survivor. Popular culture chose the joke: Alferd Packer restaurants and grills, \u201cHave a friend for lunch,\u201d coffin races, mock trials, a Packer burger, Cannibal! The Musical, and other lore. The strongest sources establish that five men died violently, Packer ate them, he was convicted of manslaughter, and deliberate killing is plausible. They do not definitively reconstruct the killings: no eyewitness survives; the chain of custody, every skull and pelvic injury, and the revolver\u2019s ownership remain unsettled; the Archives page lists confessions and court files without presenting them in full; and no source reconciles Starrs\u2019s certainty with Birkby\u2019s agnosticism. The case therefore answers neither exactly who killed whom nor why.</p></div></section><section><div class=\"sec-head\"><span class=\"sec-num\">SECTION 2</span><h2>The Players</h2></div><div class=\"players\"><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Denver Post</span><span class=\"role\">Newspaper that publicized Packer\u2019s case and employed him after prison</span></summary><p>The Denver Post published Polly Pry\u2019s reporting and campaign on Packer\u2019s behalf. It petitioned for his release after he had served seventeen years, and Packer later worked there as a guard. Many believed the job came through Pry and the newspaper\u2019s efforts to secure his release. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Polly Pry</span><span class=\"role\">Denver Post reporter who led a campaign for Packer\u2019s release</span></summary><p>Polly Pry partly believed Alfred Packer after hearing his story and visited him at Colorado State Penitentiary in 1899. She used his Army service to portray him as a common man caught in a regrettable situation, then publicized his plight. Packer was paroled in 1901 after a campaign led first by Duane Hatch and later by Pry; he called her his \u201cLiberator.\u201d Polly Pry was the pen name of Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Los Pi</span><span class=\"role\">Variant name for the agency where Packer emerged alone in April 1874</span></summary><p>The supplied material uses \u201cLos Pi\u201d for the Los Pi\u00f1os or Los Pinos Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado. Packer arrived there alone on April 16, 1874, after traveling for 65 days, carrying a Winchester rifle, a skinning knife, and a coffee pot with live coals. Other members of the expedition reached the agency later or were said to have disappeared. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Colorado Supreme Court</span><span class=\"role\">State court associated with the reversal of Packer\u2019s sentence</span></summary><p>The Colorado Supreme Court reversed Packer\u2019s sentence in October 1885 on ex post facto grounds. Packer\u2019s lawyers had argued that Colorado Territory had no murder statute when the deaths occurred, before statehood and before Colorado established the relevant laws. The court therefore recurs in accounts of the legal technicality that affected his prosecution. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Israel Swan</span><span class=\"role\">Prospector identified in Packer\u2019s confessions as the first person to die</span></summary><p>Packer said Israel Swan died from hunger and exposure after the party\u2019s supplies ran out, and that he ate Swan. In another account, Packer said Swan was struck in the head with a hatchet, after which the other men butchered him. Packer\u2019s first signed confession said Swan died first and was eaten by the other five men about ten days after they left camp. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Harper\u2019s Weekly</span><span class=\"role\">National publication that illustrated and reported the 1874 deaths</span></summary><p>John A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly, found the site of the incident in August and reported it to authorities in Lake City. Harper\u2019s Weekly covered the story in its October 17, 1874 issue with Randolph\u2019s illustration, \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d The coverage drew national attention to the crime, although most historians conclude that the illustrator found the remains while on assignment in the area. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Lake Fork</span><span class=\"role\">Area near the Gunnison River where Packer said the party became lost</span></summary><p>Packer said that after traveling with little food, he and Bell camped beside a large lake near hemlock trees. After two weeks, near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, he said he was lost and no longer recognized the surroundings. The area northeast of Lake City where the party became lost is labeled \u201cCannibal Plateau,\u201d and Lake City later developed Packer-related attractions including Al Packer Days and the Packer Burger. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Jefferson County</span><span class=\"role\">Colorado county where Packer died in 1907</span></summary><p>Alfred Griner Packer died in Jefferson County, Colorado, on April 23, 1907, at age 65. The supplied material also connects Packer\u2019s escape, assumed identity, and arrest in Wyoming with his eventual return to Hinsdale County for trial. Accounts of his imprisonment and prosecution recur alongside descriptions of the Hinsdale County Museum\u2019s Packer memorabilia. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Colorado State Archives</span><span class=\"role\">State archival source associated with the Packer case and forensic evidence</span></summary><p>The supplied material links the Colorado State Archives with accounts of Packer\u2019s journey, prosecution, the ex post facto legal issue, his parole in 1901, and objects made or carried during his imprisonment. It also records that analysis at Mesa State College\u2019s Electron Microscopy Facility matched bullet fragments from the burial site to lead from Packer\u2019s old pistol. Packer\u2019s pistol is now displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Los Pinos Indian Agency</span><span class=\"role\">Agency where Packer arrived alone and where the separated party sought help</span></summary><p>Los Pinos Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado, was the destination chosen by the expedition after the party split. Chief Ouray advised the men to follow the Gunnison River north to the agency; the party that followed that route ran out of food, while Packer arrived alone on April 16, 1874. General Charles Adams gave Packer food there, and Preston Nutter later arrived and sought answers in Saguache. Two Ute men also brought pieces of flesh they said came from a human found near the agency. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Colorado State Penitentiary</span><span class=\"role\">Prison where Packer was held before his 1901 parole</span></summary><p>Packer was imprisoned at Colorado State Penitentiary and visited there by Denver Post reporter Polly Pry in 1899. Pry wrote about his desire for freedom and launched a media campaign on his behalf. While in prison, Packer made horsehair bridles; one is displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Hinsdale County Historical Society</span><span class=\"role\">Historical organization associated with the Hinsdale County Museum\u2019s Packer collection</span></summary><p>The supplied material connects the Hinsdale County Historical Society with the Hinsdale County Museum, which travel magazines describe as having the largest known collection of Packer memorabilia. The reported collection includes skull fragments, victims\u2019 clothing buttons, and shackles used when Packer was imprisoned. The material also places Packer\u2019s detention, prosecution, and intended execution in Hinsdale County. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Chief Ouray</span><span class=\"role\">Ute leader who sheltered Packer\u2019s expedition and warned it against winter travel</span></summary><p>Chief Ouray\u2019s camp near Montrose gave the party food and lodging after it arrived on January 21, 1874. He advised the prospectors to remain until spring, warned that the mountain journey would mean certain death in those conditions, and recommended following the Gunnison River to the agency. Packer ignored the warning and left Ouray\u2019s camp with five other prospectors on February 9; the party had only enough food for one grown man for a week. </p></details><details class=\"player\"><summary><span class=\"name\">Gunnison River</span><span class=\"role\">Route followed by one branch of Packer\u2019s divided expedition</span></summary><p>After the party split, five men followed Oliver Loutsenhizer along the Gunnison River while Packer and five others headed through the San Juan Mountains. Chief Ouray had recommended the river route, and Bob McGrue guided Packer\u2019s men along it until the horses could go no farther. Two of the five men on that route eventually reached the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, while the river\u2019s tributaries and nearby terrain later figured in accounts of the deaths and the discovery of the remains. </p></details></div></section><section><div class=\"sec-head\"><span class=\"sec-num\">SECTION 3</span><h2>The Record</h2></div><ul class=\"timeline\"><li><span class=\"when\">1842</span><span class=\"what\">Alferd G. Packer was born in 1842 and died in 1907. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This establishes the lifespan attributed to Packer in one account. It provides a chronological boundary for interpreting his military service, western travels, and later notoriety.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1842</span><span class=\"what\">Alferd E. Packer lived from 1842 to 1909 and was also called Alfred. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This account introduces a variant middle initial and the alternate first name Alfred. The differing name and death year matter because records about Packer may identify him inconsistently.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">January 21, 1842</span><span class=\"what\">Alfred Griner Packer was born on January 21, 1842, in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania, U.S. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This gives a precise birthplace and birth date for the man at the center of the chronology. The Pennsylvania origin places his early life before his later movement west.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1842</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was born near Pittsburgh in 1842. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This places Packer\u2019s origins in the Pittsburgh area without supplying a precise locality. It connects the subject to western Pennsylvania before the events that brought him west.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1846</span><span class=\"what\">Rebecca A. Newman was born in 1846 in Herkimer, New York. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This establishes Rebecca A. Newman as a contemporary born in upstate New York. Her presence supplies another personal connection within the chronology, though the entry does not identify her relationship to Packer.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1857</span><span class=\"what\">Polly Pry was born to a wealthy family in 1857. <span class=\"src\">SRC_9</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This introduces Polly Pry as someone whose family background provided considerable wealth. That status may help explain her significance in the surrounding story, although no connection or later event is supplied here.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1862</span><span class=\"what\">In the 1950s, a rusted 1862 Colt Police Model .32 five-shot revolver was found on Cannibal Plateau with two shots missing. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The discovery provides a physical object associated with the Cannibal Plateau episode. The missing shots and the weapon\u2019s condition make it suggestive evidence, while leaving its owner and exact use unresolved.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">April 22, 1862</span><span class=\"what\">Packer enlisted on April 22, 1862, in Winona, Minnesota, and was assigned to Company F of the 16th Infantry Regiment. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This marks Packer\u2019s first listed wartime enlistment and places him in a specific infantry company. It establishes a military phase before his later western travels.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1862</span><span class=\"what\">His grave has a veteran&#x27;s tombstone listing his original regiment in 1862, replacing a marker that was stolen. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The replacement marker preserves a connection between Packer\u2019s grave and his original military service. Its history also shows that the surviving memorial is partly a response to the loss of an earlier record.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">June 25, 1863</span><span class=\"what\">Packer enlisted in the 8th Iowa Cavalry Regiment at Ottumwa, Iowa, on June 25, 1863. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This records a later enlistment in a cavalry regiment, showing that Packer\u2019s military service was not limited to the earlier infantry assignment. The Iowa location also adds another geographic point to his wartime movements.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">April 22, 1864</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was discharged a second time on April 22, 1864, because of epilepsy. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The discharge ended this period of service for medical reasons. Epilepsy therefore became a documented interruption in Packer\u2019s military career before the later events in the chronology.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1873-1874</span><span class=\"what\">In winter 1873-1874, the prospect party led by Alferd Packer entered the San Juan region. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This places Packer\u2019s party in the San Juan region during the harsh winter season. It marks the setting in which the expedition\u2019s later crisis and Packer\u2019s notoriety developed.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">November 8, 1873</span><span class=\"what\">Packer led a party of 21 prospectors that departed from Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 8, 1873. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The departure involved a substantial prospecting group and placed Packer in a leadership role from the outset. Its Utah starting point frames the expedition as a movement toward Colorado\u2019s mining country.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">November 1873</span><span class=\"what\">Packer said that in November 1873 he lied to five men and 15 other miners near Bingham Canyon, Utah Territory, claiming he could guide them to Colorado Territory\u2019s rich precious metal region. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Packer\u2019s own account presents the expedition as beginning with deliberate deception about his guiding ability. That claim casts doubt on the circumstances under which the group formed and makes his later leadership especially consequential.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">November 20, 1873</span><span class=\"what\">Several months earlier, on November 20, 1873, the party Alfred eventually joined set out from Bingham Canyon, Utah, as part of a group of 20. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This account presents a slightly different size and departure date for the group Packer eventually joined. The discrepancy signals that the chronology contains competing versions of how his participation began.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">November 1873</span><span class=\"what\">The party left in November 1873. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This supplies only the broad month of departure, leaving the expedition\u2019s exact date and party size unspecified. It nevertheless fixes the beginning of the journey within the late-1873 period.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1873</span><span class=\"what\">In the fall of 1873, Packer left on an expedition toward San Juan country with 20 prospectors. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This version emphasizes the expedition\u2019s destination and the number of prospectors accompanying Packer. It supports the broader picture of a sizable party traveling toward the San Juan country before winter conditions closed in.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1873-1874</span><span class=\"what\">Alfred Packer was accused of cannibalism during the winter of 1873-1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_6</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The accusation places cannibalism at the center of Packer\u2019s historical notoriety. Its timing links the charge directly to the winter expedition, although the entry does not state what evidence or testimony supported it.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1873</span><span class=\"what\">After the war, Packer traveled around the country and arrived in Utah in 1873. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This connects Packer\u2019s arrival in Utah with the period immediately preceding the prospecting expedition. It helps explain how he came to be in the region from which the later journey proceeded.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1873</span><span class=\"what\">In late 1873, Packer joined a group of 20 prospectors headed to southwestern Colorado. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This describes Packer as joining an already organized group rather than forming the expedition alone. The destination identifies the journey\u2019s economic purpose and sets up the later events in southwestern Colorado.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">August 1874</span><span class=\"what\">In August 1874, a prospecting party discovered the bodies of the five missing men. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The discovery ended months of uncertainty about the missing party and established that the other five men had died. It also provided the physical basis for the accusations that followed Packer\u2019s return.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer appealed for a retrial on the grounds that the crime was committed on the Ute Reservation in early 1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The appeal shifted attention from the deaths themselves to the legal setting in which they occurred. Packer\u2019s lawyers argued that the reservation location affected which law and court could govern the case.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">January 21, 1874</span><span class=\"what\">The party reached the Ute encampment near present-day Montrose on January 21, 1874, with their food and supplies exhausted. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Reaching Ouray\u2019s camp placed the exhausted party among the Utes after its attempt to cross the mountains had failed. Their lack of food and supplies made the group dependent on the winter encampment, but they soon continued onward.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Alferd G. Packer was the prospector accused of committing murder during the winter of early 1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Packer\u2019s role as the sole surviving member made his account the central explanation for what happened. His later confession transformed an obscure prospecting disaster into a notorious criminal case.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">January 1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer and five others ventured into the wilderness of the San Juan Mountains in January 1874 against the advice of Chief Ouray. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The decision to continue despite Ouray\u2019s warning set the party on the route toward its winter ordeal. The group\u2019s choice connected the search for gold with the hazards that ultimately separated Packer from the others.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">October 17, 1874</span><span class=\"what\">Harper\u2019s Weekly published an October 17, 1874 issue with an illustration by John A. Randolph of the scene of \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The illustration carried the incident beyond Colorado and presented it to a national readership as a dramatic frontier tragedy. Its publication helped turn the episode into a widely recognizable story rather than a local disappearance.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">February 9, 1874</span><span class=\"what\">Those five men followed Packer into a storm on February 9, 1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The departure marked the party\u2019s final movement into the storm and away from the relative safety of Ouray\u2019s camp. Afterward, the six men were no longer seen together, and Packer eventually became the only one to emerge.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer committed cannibalism during the winter of 1874 after guiding five men into Colorado Territory\u2019s San Juan Mountains during a snowstorm. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The episode combined a failed winter crossing, the deaths of five companions, and Packer\u2019s account of survival. Cannibalism became the most sensational element of the story and the feature that fixed his name in public memory.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">In 1874, six men set off into Colorado&#x27;s San Juan Mountains in the middle of winter hoping to reach newly discovered goldfields on the other side. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The men were pursuing the promise of gold by attempting a winter crossing rather than waiting for safer conditions. Their timing placed them in the San Juans when food, supplies, and travel conditions were already becoming critical.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">April 16, 1874</span><span class=\"what\">On the morning of April 16, 1874, men at Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado were eating breakfast when Alfred Packer burst into the mess hall during a snowstorm. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Packer\u2019s sudden arrival at the agency brought the missing-party story directly to officials and witnesses. His condition and account could then be compared with the later discovery of the five bodies.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">February 1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was a prospector who became famous after confessing to eating his dead comrades while trapped in the San Juan Mountains in February 1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>His confession supplied the most notorious explanation for how he had survived while the others did not. The admission made him both the principal witness to the disaster and the person suspected of causing it.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">February 9, 1874</span><span class=\"what\">On February 9, 1874, Packer and five other prospectors left the Ute leader Ouray\u2019s winter camp. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The group\u2019s departure from Ouray\u2019s winter camp began the final stage of the journey. It also showed that the men left the one place where they had reached help and supplies before entering the storm.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was spared the death penalty when his lawyers found that the 1874 murder statutes had been repealed and replaced with a savings clause. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The ruling prevented the state from carrying out the death penalty, but it did not erase the murder conviction or the underlying controversy. A change in the statutes became decisive in determining Packer\u2019s punishment.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Local hunters and officials said the winter of 1874 was severe but the San Juans where Packer&#x27;s party camped still had abundant deer, antelope, and smaller game. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Reports of plentiful wildlife complicated any claim that starvation alone explained the deaths or the resort to cannibalism. The availability of game became part of the broader debate over Packer\u2019s conduct in the mountains.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">January 21, 1874</span><span class=\"what\">On January 21, 1874, the party reached Chief Ouray\u2019s camp near Montrose, Colorado, in the Uncompahgre Valley. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The party\u2019s arrival at Ouray\u2019s camp briefly brought it within reach of assistance after its supplies had run out. Its later departure meant that this refuge did not end the expedition\u2019s crisis.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">April 16, 1874</span><span class=\"what\">On April 16, 1874, 65 days after leaving, Packer emerged alone and crossed a frozen lake bed to the Los Pin\u00f3s Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Packer\u2019s emergence after 65 days made him the sole firsthand survivor of the expedition. His crossing to the agency opened the official investigation and supplied the first account of the party\u2019s fate.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer confessed to cannibalism during the winter of 1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The confession made survival itself evidence in the public mind, linking Packer to the deaths of his companions. It also ensured that the case would be remembered for the act he admitted rather than only as a failed expedition.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Only Packer returned in the winter of 1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Packer\u2019s solitary return left no surviving companion to confirm or challenge his version of events. That imbalance made his changing statements especially important once the bodies were found.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">May 1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer gave three confessions, in May 1874, March 1883, and a final confession in a letter to the Rocky Mountain News in August 1897. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The three accounts showed that the story did not remain fixed after Packer\u2019s return. Differences across the confessions kept questions about his actions and credibility alive for decades.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was a prospector who set out for Colorado\u2019s gold fields with five other men across the San Juan Mountains in the winter of 1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The expedition joined the pursuit of Colorado\u2019s goldfields to an exceptionally dangerous winter route. Its outcome made the journey a story of isolation, failed survival, and the disappearance of nearly the entire party.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was the only member of that winter 1874 expedition who survived. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>His survival left no fellow member of the party to offer a competing firsthand account. That absence helped make his later statements and the surrounding theories central to the story.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1874</span><span class=\"what\">Starrs said that over the years since 1874, Packer has been lionized, apologized for, and sympathized with, and that people have tried to have him pardoned posthumously. <span class=\"src\">SRC_7</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The case never settled into a single lasting public interpretation. Its changing reputation kept Packer\u2019s name in public discussion long after the events and legal proceedings.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">February 1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was one of six prospectors who headed into the Colorado mountains in February 1874 after being flush with gold fever and ignoring the dangers of winter. <span class=\"src\">SRC_7</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The party\u2019s decision to enter the mountains under severe winter conditions established the immediate danger surrounding the expedition. Their gold fever helps explain why warnings and practical risks were discounted.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">April 1874</span><span class=\"what\">Packer became the source of legend after he wandered out of the mountains alone in April 1874. <span class=\"src\">SRC_7</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>His solitary return transformed a failed expedition into a mystery that attracted lasting attention. The unexplained gap between the party\u2019s departure and his return became the basis for competing accounts.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1876</span><span class=\"what\">Alfred&#x27;s lawyers found a legal technicality because Colorado became a state in 1876 and Colorado Territory had no murder statute under which he could be tried for murder at the time of the crime. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The lawyers\u2019 argument shifted the case from the facts of the deaths to the legal authority of the territory and state. It exposed a procedural weakness that affected how the prosecution could proceed.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1877</span><span class=\"what\">The Hinsdale County Courthouse in Lake City was built in 1877 and is the oldest operating courthouse in Colorado. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The courthouse provided the setting for the proceedings and links the case to one of Colorado\u2019s oldest continuing civic institutions. Its survival gives the later trial a physical connection to the territory\u2019s early legal history.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1883</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was apprehended in Wyoming in 1883. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The discovery ended years in which Packer remained at large and apparently moved through the region. His capture made a formal reckoning possible nearly a decade after the expedition.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1883</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was first found guilty of murder in 1883 in Lake City. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The verdict converted a long-standing disappearance and survival story into a criminal prosecution. It also began the sequence of legal challenges and arguments over the precise basis for punishment.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">March 1883</span><span class=\"what\">In March 1883, Packer was discovered in Douglas, Wyoming, living under the alias John Swartze. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The alias shows that he was living under an assumed identity when authorities or acquaintances located him. His presence in Wyoming supplied the link between his years of freedom and his return to court.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1883</span><span class=\"what\">Packer told Newman that he was not arrested or censured until 1883 and that he was in the country and seen by all men during all that time. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Packer presented the intervening years as evidence that he had not been hiding from public view. His claim was meant to challenge the idea that he had successfully evaded notice or punishment.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1883</span><span class=\"what\">In 1883, one of the original prospectors who had stayed the winter with Chief Ouray ran a shop selling supplies in Cheyenne, Wyoming. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The shopkeeper\u2019s earlier connection to the expedition created a possible route by which knowledge of Packer\u2019s whereabouts could circulate. Cheyenne therefore became part of the chain leading from the old expedition to his discovery.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">March 11, 1883</span><span class=\"what\">On March 11, 1883, Jean &quot;Frenchy&quot; Cabazon found Packer in Cheyenne, Wyoming, living under the alias John Schwartze. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The alias and the Cheyenne location tied the discovery to a specific witness, rather than leaving it as an anonymous report. Cabazon\u2019s identification helped bring the dormant case back into motion.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">May 19, 1883</span><span class=\"what\">After seven days of testimony and examinations, he was found guilty of the premeditated murder of Israel Swan and sentenced to death by hanging on May 19, 1883. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The proceedings ended with the harshest available outcome after the court focused on a deliberate killing. The date of execution fixed an immediate consequence, even though later developments prevented that timetable from standing unchanged.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">May 19, 1883</span><span class=\"what\">Court records state that Judge M.B. Gerry sentenced Packer to be confined in Hinsdale County jail until May 19, 1883, then taken by the sheriff to an execution site in Lake City and hanged by the neck until dead. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The order laid out the full procedure for carrying out the punishment, from confinement to removal by the sheriff. It shows that the judgment was intended to be implemented through ordinary county authority.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1883</span><span class=\"what\">In his 1883 confession, Packer said he went back to the fire, covered the men up, and brought to camp the piece of meat that was near the fire. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This detail became part of the evidentiary record used to connect Packer\u2019s own account with the treatment of the dead. It also reinforced the grim physical circumstances surrounding the camp.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1883</span><span class=\"what\">District Court Judge Melville Gerry, the judge who condemned Packer, reportedly told Packer at sentencing in 1883 to stand up because he was a \u201cvoracious, man-eating\u201d man. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The judge\u2019s language made the sentencing a moral condemnation as well as a legal judgment. It helped shape the memorable image of Packer that survived beyond the courtroom.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1883</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The verdict attached criminal responsibility to a named victim rather than leaving the prosecution at the level of a general accusation. It illustrates how the broader charge against the group could be narrowed in the courtroom.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">May 19, 1883</span><span class=\"what\">The actual sentence ordered Packer confined in the Hinsdale County jail until May 19, 1883. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The formal order specified imprisonment before the planned execution, clarifying that death was not to occur immediately after judgment. This distinction matters because the sentence involved a defined sequence of custody and execution.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1883</span><span class=\"what\">In 1883, Packer was charged with murdering all five companions. <span class=\"src\">SRC_7</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The prosecution initially treated the episode as a series of killings involving the entire party. That broad charge framed the trial as an account of the group\u2019s destruction, not merely a dispute over one death.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">April 9, 1883</span><span class=\"what\">On April 9, 1883, Packer\u2019s trial began in a courtroom filled to capacity. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The packed courtroom showed that the case had become a major public event rather than a routine local prosecution. The testimony that followed would determine which version of the expedition the court accepted.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">October 1885</span><span class=\"what\">In October 1885, the Colorado Supreme Court reversed the sentence on ex post facto grounds. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The ruling removed the original capital punishment on a constitutional ground rather than resolving the underlying accusations. It opened the way for the case to continue through another trial and sentencing.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1885</span><span class=\"what\">Packer\u2019s death sentence was overturned by the Colorado Supreme Court in 1885 because of a \u201cgrandfather clause.\u201d <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This account gives a different explanation for the reversal than the ex post facto rationale elsewhere in the chronology. The disagreement shows that the legal history of the case was later described in competing ways.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1885</span><span class=\"what\">After a second trial in 1885, Packer received a 40-year sentence. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The second proceeding replaced the death penalty with a lengthy prison term. That outcome kept Packer in custody while leaving the case open to further legal and public attention.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1886</span><span class=\"what\">Packer\u2019s retrial in 1886 found him guilty of five counts of manslaughter. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The retrial narrowed the criminal finding to five manslaughter convictions. It marked a shift away from the earlier capital sentence while still treating the deaths as criminally culpable.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1886</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was eventually sentenced to 40 years in prison after being convicted of manslaughter in a second trial in Gunnison in 1886. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The Gunnison proceeding established the punishment that would define the remainder of Packer\u2019s imprisonment. The repeated reference to the second trial reflects the importance of that later conviction in accounts of the case.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1886</span><span class=\"what\">According to The Case of Alferd Packer by Paul H. Gantt, Gunnison photographer Frank E. Deane took a portrait of Alferd Packer during the 1886 trial. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The portrait connected Packer\u2019s public image to the courtroom drama surrounding the retrial. Its attribution to a named local photographer also provides a contemporaneous visual record of the proceedings.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1886</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was resentenced in 1886 to 40 years in prison. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The resentencing confirmed that the reduced punishment was not merely temporary. A forty-year term became the practical consequence of the manslaughter conviction.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1887</span><span class=\"what\">In 1887, Adams relented and forwarded her letter to the State Penitentiary. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Adams\u2019s change of mind allowed her letter to enter the penitentiary record or correspondence. That act helped preserve a personal intervention in the continuing effort around Packer\u2019s imprisonment.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1898</span><span class=\"what\">In 1898, Frederick Bonfils hired Pry for the Denver Post. <span class=\"src\">SRC_9</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Pry\u2019s hiring placed her at the Denver Post, where she had a platform to pursue stories beyond routine prison reporting. Her later involvement with Packer\u2019s case grew out of that journalistic position.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1899</span><span class=\"what\">In 1899, Denver Post writer Polly Pry visited Packer at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Canon City. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The prison visit brought Packer\u2019s story into direct contact with a prominent newspaper writer. It became the point at which his imprisonment attracted the attention that would fuel a public campaign on his behalf.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">January 7, 1901</span><span class=\"what\">The retrial eventually led to a pardon for Packer on January 7, 1901. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The retrial did not end the matter; it eventually became part of the path toward executive clemency. The pardon represented the culmination of sustained pressure after the conviction and imprisonment.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1901</span><span class=\"what\">After enough pressure, the Colorado governor paroled Alfred in 1901. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The governor\u2019s action converted years of advocacy into a release from ordinary imprisonment. It also shows that Packer\u2019s eventual freedom came through political intervention rather than a complete clearing of the case.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">February 8, 1901</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was paroled on February 8, 1901, after a campaign led by an old acquaintance named Duane Hatch, who petitioned for nearly a decade before Polly Pry took notice. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Hatch\u2019s long petitioning supplied the campaign with an older personal connection, while Pry\u2019s attention broadened it through the press. The parole therefore emerged from both private persistence and public advocacy.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1901</span><span class=\"what\">Former Gov. Charles Thomas paroled Packer in 1901. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This account places the decision directly with a former governor, emphasizing the role of executive authority. It presents the release as a political act rather than the result of another judicial reversal.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1901</span><span class=\"what\">Packer was paroled in 1901 after a Denver Post columnist crusaded on his behalf. <span class=\"src\">SRC_7</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The newspaper campaign made Packer\u2019s imprisonment a public cause rather than a matter known only through court records. Pry\u2019s writing helped turn sympathy for him into pressure for release.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1901</span><span class=\"what\">Pry met Alfred Packer in 1901 while writing an article on the prison where he was incarcerated. <span class=\"src\">SRC_9</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The meeting gave Pry firsthand access to the prisoner whose case she would champion. Her article on the prison became the immediate setting in which that relationship began.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1905</span><span class=\"what\">Packer received parole in 1905. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This later parole date conflicts with the earlier accounts placing his parole in 1901. The chronology therefore preserves two different claims about when Packer was released under parole.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1907</span><span class=\"what\">Packer died alone in 1907 in Littleton, Colorado, a suburb of Denver, at age 69. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>His death ended the life of a figure whose case had moved from a capital conviction to imprisonment and parole. The location and description emphasize the isolation of his final years.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1907</span><span class=\"what\">Alfred died in 1907 at age 65. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The stated age differs from the age given elsewhere in the chronology. That discrepancy is part of the surviving uncertainty surrounding basic details of his final years.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1907</span><span class=\"what\">The text says Alferd Packer died in 1907 and that his misspelled name and unique reputation live on. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The account treats the misspelled name as an enduring part of Packer\u2019s public identity. His reputation outlasted both the legal proceedings and his isolated death.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">April 23, 1907</span><span class=\"what\">Alfred Griner Packer died on April 23, 1907, in Jefferson County, Colorado, U.S., at age 65. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This provides a precise endpoint for Packer\u2019s life and closes the chronology of his personal story. The location also connects his death to the Colorado setting central to his later memorialization.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1907</span><span class=\"what\">Packer died in 1907 in Jefferson County, Colorado. <span class=\"src\">SRC_7</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This abbreviated account confirms the same final setting while omitting the more specific details. It functions as a concise corroboration of the death record.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1907</span><span class=\"what\">Packer died in 1907 at age 65. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This version preserves only the age at death, making the biographical endpoint especially compact. Together with fuller accounts, it reinforces the consistency of the reported year.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1926</span><span class=\"what\">Newman died in 1926 at age 80. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This marks Newman\u2019s death as a later endpoint in the surrounding cast of figures. The age gives the chronology a measure of his longevity without adding further biographical detail.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1931</span><span class=\"what\">The plaque also serves as a memorial to Stanley Weston, a USDA employee who lived from 1931 to 1984. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The memorial\u2019s purpose extends beyond Packer to include a USDA employee whose life belonged to a later period. This makes the plaque a shared memorial rather than a single-subject tribute.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">July 16, 1938</span><span class=\"what\">Leonel Campbell Ross O&#x27;Bryan was born in 1857 and died on July 16, 1938, at age 80 or 81. <span class=\"src\">SRC_9</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This supplies the full name and lifespan associated with the person identified elsewhere by a shorter surname. The uncertainty over whether he was eighty or eighty-one shows a minor discrepancy in the surviving account.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">16 July 1938</span><span class=\"what\">Pry died on 16 July 1938, aged eighty. <span class=\"src\">SRC_9</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This is a shorter version of the same death record, with the age expressed in words. It helps preserve the alternate naming used in the chronology.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1964</span><span class=\"what\">Phil Ochs, a 1960s folk-music songwriter, wrote &#x27;The Ballad of Alferd Packer&#x27; in 1964 about the expedition and its aftermath. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Ochs\u2019s song carried the episode into the 1960s folk-music culture, giving the story a new form of public afterlife. Its existence also shows that Packer\u2019s reputation was being interpreted artistically rather than only through historical accounts.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">January 1965</span><span class=\"what\">The Arrow reported in January 1965 that a blizzard trapped the six men without sufficient food. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The report presents the expedition\u2019s crisis as the result of a blizzard combined with inadequate provisions. That framing supports the starvation circumstances underlying later accounts of what happened to the group.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1968</span><span class=\"what\">In 1968, students at the University of Colorado in Boulder renamed the student union restaurant the Alferd E. Packer Memorial Grill. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The university naming turned Packer into a piece of student culture and institutional folklore. It also helped shift his image from a historical figure to a recurring campus reference.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1968</span><span class=\"what\">In 1968, students at the University of Colorado Boulder named their new cafeteria grill the Alferd G. Packer Memorial Grill. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This parallel wording records the same campus memorialization with a different middle initial and description of the facility. The variation illustrates how the name circulated in differing forms while retaining its commemorative purpose.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1973</span><span class=\"what\">The Littleton Cemetery Association cemented over Packer&#x27;s grave in 1973 to deter grave robbing and vandalism. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The grave\u2019s alteration was a practical response to fears of desecration, showing that public fascination had extended to Packer\u2019s burial site. It marks preservation and deterrence as part of the later story.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1976</span><span class=\"what\">The song later appeared on several compilations released after Ochs&#x27;s death in 1976, including the 2010 On My Way compilation of demos from 1963. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The song remained available to listeners through posthumous compilations, extending its reach beyond Ochs\u2019s lifetime. The later demo collection also preserved an earlier stage of the composition\u2019s history.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1977</span><span class=\"what\">In 1977, U.S. Secretary of Agriculture Bob Bergland tried to terminate a contract for the USDA cafeteria food service but was blocked by the General Services Administration. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The failed attempt to end the cafeteria contract created the immediate conflict behind the later publicity. It placed Bergland in opposition to the GSA and turned an administrative dispute into a symbolic campaign.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">August 10, 1977</span><span class=\"what\">To embarrass the GSA, Bergland and his employees held a press conference on August 10, 1977, to unveil a plaque naming the executive cafeteria The Alferd Packer Memorial Grill. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The plaque was presented as a deliberate embarrassment to the GSA, linking Packer\u2019s name to a federal workplace controversy. This transformed the memorial grill idea into a public political gesture.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1980</span><span class=\"what\">The 1980 film The Legend of Alfred Packer was described as an almost completely fictitious biopic of Packer\u2019s exploits. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The film brought Packer\u2019s story to a broader audience but did so through extensive invention. Its description underscores the gap between popular entertainment and the historical record.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1981</span><span class=\"what\">In 1981, then-Gov. Richard Lamm declined Packer\u2019s posthumous pardon. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Lamm\u2019s decision kept Packer\u2019s legal and moral status unresolved despite renewed public interest. It also set up the controversy that surfaced openly at the later bust unveiling.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1982</span><span class=\"what\">The United Press International reported in 1982 that Lamm had declined the pardon the year before. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This report independently places the pardon refusal in the preceding year, preserving the chronology of the controversy. Its wording shows that the decision remained newsworthy after it was made.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1982</span><span class=\"what\">At a bust unveiling of Packer at the Capitol Rotunda in 1982, Lamm said he knew some people had \u201ca bone to pick\u201d with him because he refused to grant Packer an unconditional pardon. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Lamm\u2019s remark acknowledged that the refusal had created opposition among those attending or following the commemoration. The bust unveiling therefore became not only a tribute but also a public reminder of the unresolved pardon question.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1982</span><span class=\"what\">The New York Times reported in 1982 that Lamm congratulated sculptor Tom Miller at Packer\u2019s bust unveiling. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The report records official recognition of the sculptor alongside the unveiling, giving the commemoration an artistic dimension. It shows the event functioning as a formal civic presentation rather than merely a protest over the pardon.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">April 1984</span><span class=\"what\">The Washington Post reported in April 1984 that Gerry made those remarks. <span class=\"src\">SRC_4</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The report places Gerry\u2019s comments in the public record during a period when Packer\u2019s story remained a subject of historical discussion. Their significance depends on the remarks themselves, which are not included here.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">July 17, 1989</span><span class=\"what\">On July 17, 1989, 115 years after Packer allegedly consumed his companions, James E. Starrs led an exhumation of the five bodies near the Lake Fork river upstream from Lake City. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The exhumation reopened the physical investigation into the deaths more than a century after the alleged killings. Starrs\u2019s team focused on recovering evidence from the burial site rather than relying solely on surviving accounts.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">July 17, 1989</span><span class=\"what\">WH/G visitors can access Packer books, including The Alfred G. Packer Victims Exhumation Project Lake City, Colorado, July 17, 1989. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The collection gives visitors direct access to documentation of the 1989 project and related Packer literature. It preserves the exhumation as part of the story\u2019s continuing public and historical treatment.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1989</span><span class=\"what\">In 1989, Starrs concluded his investigation by calling Packer \u201cguilty as sin.\u201d <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Starrs\u2019s conclusion gave the exhumation a clear public verdict, despite the investigation\u2019s forensic uncertainties. It reinforced the view that Packer bore responsibility for the deaths.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1989</span><span class=\"what\">During the 1989 exhumation, investigators found a hole about the size of a thimble in Bell\u2019s hip bone. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The defect supplied a physical detail for investigators examining the victims\u2019 remains. Its location in Bell\u2019s hip became part of the evidence considered in the dispute over how Bell died.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1989</span><span class=\"what\">Bailey got the 1989 forensic samples shared with Richard Dujay at Mesa State College. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Sharing the samples extended the investigation beyond the original exhumation team. It created the basis for later scientific examination at Mesa State College.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1993</span><span class=\"what\">In 1993, CU film students Matt Stone, a CU film student, and Trey Parker, a CU film student, produced Cannibal! The Musical. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The film brought Packer\u2019s story into a new popular medium through the work of two CU students. Its production helped shift the subject from historical controversy toward comic entertainment.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1993</span><span class=\"what\">The 1993 film Cannibal! The Musical was described as a more comedic and heavily fabricated take on Packer\u2019s story. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The film treated the historical material loosely rather than presenting it as a factual reconstruction. Its fabricated and comedic approach shaped how many viewers encountered Packer\u2019s story.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1993</span><span class=\"what\">The comedy musical Cannibal! The Musical was released exclusively to Boulder, Colorado, in 1993 and released in the rest of the United States in 1996. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The staggered release first connected the film with Boulder before it reached audiences across the country. The later national release expanded the story\u2019s reach beyond Colorado.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1994</span><span class=\"what\">In 1994, an 1862 Colt revolver was found at the site of the murders. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The discovery introduced a potentially relevant weapon into the historical investigation. Because it was identified as an 1862 Colt, it could be compared with accounts involving firearms used during the murders.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1994</span><span class=\"what\">In 1994, David P. Bailey, curator of history at the Museum of Western Colorado, began an investigation to find more conclusive results than Starrs regarding Packer&#x27;s claim of having shot Bell. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>Bailey\u2019s project sought to move beyond Starrs\u2019s conclusion by testing the specific question of whether Packer shot Bell. The investigation therefore shifted attention from general guilt to a narrower claim supported by physical evidence.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1994</span><span class=\"what\">In 1994, David Bailey found an 1862 Colt revolver while inventorying historic firearms. <span class=\"src\">SRC_8</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The firearm emerged during routine museum inventory rather than from a newly excavated crime scene. Its identification created a possible connection to the murders, though the discovery alone did not establish ownership.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">1998</span><span class=\"what\">In 1998, author James E. Bank wrote Alferd Packer\u2019s Wilderness Cookbook. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The book extended Packer\u2019s subject into a culinary and literary format. Its publication reflects the continued transformation of the case into popular culture.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">2000</span><span class=\"what\">By 2000, Bailey had not yet proven a link between the antique pistol and Alfred Packer. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The absence of a proven connection left the revolver\u2019s relevance unresolved. Bailey\u2019s investigation had produced a possible artifact, but not the conclusive identification needed to support the claim.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">2001</span><span class=\"what\">In 2001, Dr. Richard Dujay at Mesa State College analyzed archived forensic samples from the 1989 exhumation with an electron microscope. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The analysis subjected preserved material from the earlier exhumation to more advanced laboratory scrutiny. It represented a later attempt to extract additional evidence from samples that had already been collected.</p></div></details></span></li><li><span class=\"when\">2008</span><span class=\"what\">As of 2008, the skull was reported to be at the Ripley&#x27;s Museum in San Antonio, Texas, after relocation from New Orleans following Hurricane Katrina. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>More</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The skull\u2019s reported location shows how the physical remains had become part of a museum display history. Its relocation after Hurricane Katrina also illustrates the vulnerability of the surviving evidence to events unrelated to the original case.</p></div></details></span></li></ul></section><section><div class=\"sec-head\"><span class=\"sec-num\">SECTION 4</span><h2>The Files</h2></div><div class=\"file\"><div class=\"filehead\"><h3>File: The investigation</h3> <span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div><p>The investigation began when a council was formed to investigate Alfred Packer\u2019s story. As the meeting was beginning, two members of the Ute tribe arrived with pieces, or strips, of dried flesh that they said came from a human found on a hill near the agency. Alfred panicked, fainted, woke up, begged for mercy, and told a different story. He reportedly said, \u201cIt would not be the first time that people had been obliged to eat each other when they were hungry.\u201d Alfred said that ten days into the journey the group ran out of food and the oldest man died first. According to Alfred, the remaining men ate the dead man and took his rifle. Five days later, another man died, the others ate him too, and they found $133 in his pockets. Alfred said another man died after that while he was gathering wood for the fire, and the remaining men ate him too. Alfred claimed Shannon Bell then shot the remaining men. He said that he and Shannon carried some of the dead man for food, hid the rest, and continued toward the agency. Alfred claimed Shannon later tried to kill him too, went mad, attacked him, and was shot by Alfred in self-defense. General Adams wrote Alfred\u2019s confession down and had him sign it as a sworn statement; agency clerk Herman Lauter transcribed Alfred\u2019s first official statement. Preston did not believe the confession and was sure Alfred had killed the men and stolen their things. Preston became so angry that he dragged Alfred outside, and others had to intervene to stop him from killing Alfred.</p><p>The historical accounts describe several reasons for Preston Nutter\u2019s suspicion. Preston arrived in Saguache with two other original party members and found Packer at Dolan\u2019s Saloon. Packer played high-stakes poker there and bought a $70 horse. Nutter found Packer\u2019s story odd because Packer looked well-fed and because abandoning a guide in snowbound mountains seemed unlikely. Nutter also thought it was strange that Israel Swan would give Packer his rifle and leave four men with only one rifle for hunting. Nutter said Packer was sulky, obstinate, quarrelsome, and a petty thief. The accounts also say that Packer had been a poor guide, often got lost, argued with clients, and had a reputation for stealing and lying. Nutter said Packer had been constantly trying to find out how much money the men had. Packer had carried a Winchester rifle belonging to one of the missing men and a skinning knife belonging to Frank Miller, and he explained the knife by saying Miller had stuck it in a tree and walked away without it. Nutter and Loutsenhizer later tried to discredit Packer\u2019s claimed qualifications as a guide, emphasizing his character flaws, inconsistent stories, and contradictions. The accepted theory at the time was that Packer had joined the party by overstating his qualifications as a mountain guide familiar with the area to accompany the men to Breckenridge; at best, that theory held, he led the party to miserable deaths through gross incompetence. A more popular theory was that he had left Ouray\u2019s camp with the five men with a premeditated plan to lead them into the wilderness, kill them, and rob them.</p><p>The men persuaded General Adams to send a mounted agency officer to Saguache to retrieve Packer for questioning while pretending to recruit him for a search party. Packer was preparing to leave when Nutter and other men approached him again, and the agency officer arrived in time to separate the men. The officer told Packer he had been sent to enlist him as a guide for the search party. Packer agreed to return to Los Pin\u00f3s because he had little alternative besides vigilante justice. At the agency, Packer met General Adams and five men he had not seen since February. Loutsenhizer demanded an explanation, and Packer repeated his earlier story. Packer acted amazed that the men had not been heard from and said he was concerned for their well-being. When asked where his money came from, Packer said he had received a cash loan from a man in Saguache. The officer also reported that Packer had arrived in Saguache with plenty of cash, several wallets, and possessions belonging to the missing men, while no one in town claimed to have lent him any money. Adams convened a council of himself, Packer, and the agency officers. The two Ute tribesmen then arrived with the strips of dried human flesh they had found on a hill near the agency while hunting. Packer begged for mercy and promised a full confession. General Adams called a conference of the five men and the agency officers to decide what to do next, and the five original party members said they did not believe a single part of Packer\u2019s story.</p><p>The two Ute men said they knew of a place next to a lake that Packer had described, about 50 miles away across the hills. A search party led by Herman Lauter and consisting of the five Utah miners, several agency officers, and Packer as guide was assembled at once. After two weeks, near the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, Packer said he was lost and did not recognize the surroundings. Nothing was found and the party returned to the agency. On the way back, Packer tried to murder Herman Lauter with a knife. Packer was caught, restrained, and arrested. General Adams, who had been willing to believe Packer until then, concluded that Packer was dangerous after the attack on Lauter. Packer was taken to Saguache and jailed outside town by the sheriff for his own protection. His jail was little more than a log cabin on ranch property belonging to the Saguache County Sheriff.</p><p>While Packer was in jail, observers challenged his credibility. The condition of the bodies contradicted Packer\u2019s version of events. The bodies were all together in one spot rather than scattered across miles. Humphrey and Noon still had large portions of flesh, muscle, and organs that could have been eaten long before Bell allegedly tried to murder Packer. The men had tattered cloths lashed to their rotting feet, replacing shoes they had probably eaten. Moldy and tattered blankets lay beneath and beside the bodies. A beaten path led from the corpses to a crude shelter used by Packer. There was evidence that the deaths had occurred before supplies were totally exhausted, and inside the shelter were possessions of the men that Packer had left behind. The theory at the time was that Packer killed the men before supplies ran out to rob them, got snowed in, lived for months in his shelter, and walked to the bodies to cut off meat as needed.</p><p>In summer, General Adams led a search in the mountains to find the bodies, but the search failed. In August, illustrator John Randolph was crossing the mountains near Lake City when he noticed something sticking out of the ground near a tributary of the Gunnison River. John smelled something terrible and found five decomposed bodies that had met violent ends. One man was little more than bones, suggesting animals had gotten to him afterward. Two of the men had blunt force injuries to the head. Shannon Bell had been stripped to the bones except for his hands and feet and also had a blunt force head injury. The remains suggested deliberate removal of edible portions, and except for the first man, it did not look like animals had done it. John made a sketch and told authorities in Lake City. The local coroner and law enforcement, along with a team of 20 volunteers, later retrieved the bodies. The coroner concluded that the men had been killed by blows to the head while asleep. None of the bodies had defensive wounds or injuries, and all had similar markings on their skulls. The bodies had rags under their feet, suggesting they may have eaten the leather from their shoes after running out of food. There was also a track to a makeshift shelter, leading investigators to think Alfred may have killed the men for their possessions and eaten them to survive.</p><p>Other accounts identify Randolph as John A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly, and place the discovery at the foot of Slumgullion Pass, two miles southeast of Lake City, in a pine-shaded gulch above the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, a location later known as Deadman\u2019s Gulch. One account says that the snow covering the bodies and campsite had melted during the intervening four months, and that the local coroner and law enforcement went to the site with about 20 volunteers. The bodies were in various states of decomposition and had been left to the elements and animals. The illustrator\u2019s sketch was later published with the October 17, 1874, edition of Harper\u2019s Weekly under the title \u201cA Colorado Tragedy.\u201d Most historians conclude that an illustrator for Harper\u2019s Weekly found the remains near the Gunnison River while on assignment in the area. An illustrator who accompanied the group recorded the scene in visual graphic detail, helping draw national attention to the crime. The corpses showed intense violence and skull fractures consistent with blows from a hatchet. After officials examined the bodies, they buried the remains at the site, and the search party returned to Packer\u2019s makeshift jail to confront him, only to find him missing.</p><p>Packer\u2019s charges were expected to be upgraded from manslaughter to first-degree murder. Before that happened, someone gave Alfred a key, a penknife, and some food so he could escape. Packer allegedly received a makeshift key for his irons and some supplies, and escaped easily. Alfred slipped out of his cell and was not seen again for nine years. Months had passed with no definitive evidence of a crime, no bodies discovered, and no formal charges against Packer other than the attempt on Lauter\u2019s life. Saguache County authorities were reportedly unhappy about taxpayer money being spent to keep Packer under guard. Nearly all of Saguache believed through rumor or deduction that Packer was guilty of robbery or murder, and Packer\u2019s life was constantly threatened by nearby townspeople. Packer never said who helped him escape, how it happened, or why. It was presumed that his guard had been bribed by Packer or by someone else.</p><p>In March 1883, Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon encountered Packer when Packer approached him to buy supplies. Packer was living under the alias \u201cJohn Schwartze\u201d or \u201cJohn Swartze.\u201d Cabazon reported Packer to the local sheriff, who apprehended him and contacted General Adams. Cabazon was summoned to Cheyenne, confirmed Packer\u2019s identity, and accompanied him by train to Denver for Packer\u2019s second confession, which Packer signed on March 16. Packer said he fled because he feared mob justice from the people of Saguache. Because the actual crime was committed in Hinsdale County rather than Saguache County, Packer was sent to Lake City for detention and prosecution. Other accounts say Packer was jailed in Saguache, escaped, changed his identity, and was arrested in Wyoming before being returned to Hinsdale County for trial. After this arrest, Packer was tried and convicted of murder and sentenced to death, but the conviction was overturned on a technicality because Colorado became a state in 1876 and, before statehood, Colorado Territory had no statute under which he could be tried for murder. He was retried for manslaughter instead of murder and was eventually convicted of voluntary manslaughter. He received 40 years in prison, the maximum of eight years per man, which was described at the time as the longest prison sentence handed down in United States history. Other accounts say he was convicted of manslaughter in a second trial in Gunnison in 1886. He maintained that he had eaten the flesh of his companions to survive, but held to his story that Shannon Bell had killed the other men and that Packer killed Bell in self-defense.</p><p>In the century after the story, several groups conducted forensic analyses of the remains and compared them with Alfred\u2019s account. Those groups tended to conclude that Alfred was guilty. Some of the forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group. One group found marks across Shannon Bell\u2019s forearms, suggesting that he may have defended himself from an attack. In Alfred\u2019s account, Shannon was only shot once and then finished quickly. The revolver was still loaded with three .38-caliber bullets, and the bullets matched some fragments of lead found in Shannon Bell\u2019s hip. That evidence suggested that at least part of Alfred\u2019s story may have been true.</p><p>The later forensic record is not consistent. On July 17, 1989, grad students dug up the skeletons in Lake City, Colorado, under hot, muggy conditions. When the first skull was exposed, it had a gaping hole in its forehead. Reporters rushed to Lake City\u2019s four public pay phones to file a story headlined \u201cProfessor Exhumes 100-Year-Old Victims of Colorado Cannibal.\u201d The team led by James Starrs, a professor of law and forensic science at George Washington University, unearthed the skeletons of five men from a mass grave in the San Juan mountains. Starrs led the exhumation project, which cost an estimated $10,000 to $15,000 and used modern forensic techniques including ground-penetrating radar and laboratory inspection of the remains. Starrs said the team\u2019s examination showed that none of the victims had been shot, that four had been killed by hatchet blows to the head, and that the fifth skull had not been found. He said all five sets of bones revealed numerous knife marks indicating defleshing. He cited \u201cunmistakeable proof of cannibalism,\u201d said Packer had used the \u201cnatural refrigerator\u201d of the Colorado winter to preserve his victims and eat them until spring, and declared, \u201cHe was having human flesh filets morning, noon and night.\u201d More than a century after Packer was first accused of killing and eating five fellow prospectors in Colorado, Starrs said he had proven that Packer was \u201cguilty as sin.\u201d Starrs said Packer had \u201cbutchered his five fellow prospectors to death and then skinned them\u201d and feasted on their flesh, and that the added scientific evidence convicted Packer \u201cbeyond any shadow of a doubt\u201d in his opinion. Starrs said his aim was to settle the debate over Packer\u2019s guilt and to \u201cput forensic science on parade.\u201d He believed Packer\u2019s motive was initially to steal his companions\u2019 belongings and then to turn to cannibalism.</p><p>The exhumation account says the remains were found at the end of a residential driveway at a home belonging to a local surgeon and were buried only 13 inches below ground. Starrs and Walter H. Birkby said they did not think cannibalism could ever be scientifically demonstrated because it would require a picture of the person actually eating human flesh. Packer never denied cannibalism, so the investigation did not focus on that point. The evidence was sufficient for Starrs to conclude that Packer had murdered his comrades. Starrs believed Packer more likely murdered his companions for their belongings and resorted to cannibalism out of necessity rather than intent. The remains were re-interred and given a proper burial with funeral rites.</p><p>Exhumation showed blunt force trauma to the skulls of two men, with no other notable disturbance except damage from butchering. All skulls had damage, to varying extents, in their upper craniums, and fabric fibers were found in some skulls, suggesting their heads may have been covered with a blanket at death. The three other skeletons showed defensive hacking marks across the radial and ulna bones of their forearms, including Shannon Bell\u2019s, consistent with shielding the face and body from an attack. Shannon Bell was one of the skeletons with defensive forearm wounds, which could have been sustained while shielding his face and body from an attack. The skeletons showed no other noteworthy disturbance beyond damage made during butchering. All five skeletons had numerous differing post-mortem injuries, including depressed fractures, butterfly fractures, butchering trauma, and hacking trauma. Those post-mortem injuries appear to contradict Packer\u2019s claim of minimal cannibalism.</p><p>Two skeletons other than Shannon Bell\u2019s were found to have cylindrical puncture wounds in the pelvic bone. Some people have questioned whether all three pelvic marks were scavenging marks from a bear or possibly bullet wounds. No definitive conclusion has been made about the pelvic puncture wounds. One scenario suggested that two of the men were bludgeoned in their sleep by Packer. In the same scenario, the three remaining men awoke and Packer shot them in the hips to incapacitate them if the punctures were bullet wounds. The remaining men then may have tried to fight off Packer while he wielded a hatchet, and he killed them with blows to the head. Packer then butchered the men and used their flesh to survive in his snowbound state and during his journey. The different states of decomposition contradict that theory, although it is described as plausible. It seems unlikely that Packer shot three of the five men in the hip unless he was trying to debilitate them before killing them. Further analysis of entry and exit geometry could clarify the initial cause, but no such examination has been done to date. Packer\u2019s ultimate motivation for the murders has been heavily debated and has several possible theories.</p><p>A different line of investigation concerns the pistol. In the Audrey Thrailkill collection of firearms at the museum was a Colt revolver reportedly found at the site of Packer\u2019s alleged crime. An accession card said, \u201cThis gun found at the site where Alferd Packer killed and ate his companions.\u201d Exhaustive investigation into the pistol\u2019s background uncovered trial-era documents. A Civil War veteran who visited the crime scene said Shannon Bell had been shot twice and the other victims had been killed with a hatchet. The veteran said that, after studying Bell, he noticed a severe bullet wound to the pelvic area and a bullet hole through Bell\u2019s wallet. This evidence seemed to corroborate Packer\u2019s claim that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer had shot Bell in self-defense.</p><p>In 1994, David P. Bailey, curator of history at the Museum of Western Colorado, began or reported an investigation into the pistol and the case; Bailey spent 10 years investigating the gun and the case. The pistol was described in one account as an 1862 Colt revolver, still loaded with three .38-caliber bullets, and in another as a rusted 1862 Colt Police Model .32 five-shot revolver found on Cannibal Plateau in the 1950s, with two shots missing. Dujay found microscopic lead fragments in soil taken from under Shannon Bell\u2019s remains. Spectrograph analysis matched those fragments to the bullets remaining in Packer\u2019s pistol. Analysis at Mesa State College\u2019s Electron Microscopy Facility proved that bullet fragments exhumed from the burial site matched lead from the old pistol. Dujay\u2019s team examined Bell\u2019s samples and found a tiny lead fragment whose chemical composition matched the bullets in Bailey\u2019s Colt revolver. Using a similar 1860s pistol, the team fired a bullet into an elk hip bone, and the resulting hole matched the hole in Bell\u2019s hip bone. The analysis suggested that Packer did shoot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and Bailey believed the evidence supported Packer\u2019s claim that he shot Bell. It now appears certain that at least Bell was shot, but whether Bell was shot in self-defense remains unanswered. Grant Houston remained skeptical because tourists had often found old guns and bullets near the massacre site and claimed they belonged to Packer or the other prospectors. Michelle Pierce was also unconvinced that Bailey\u2019s pistol was the smoking gun.</p><p>Museum curator David Bailey said Packer did not deny eating the bodies, killed only in self-defense, and was wrongly convicted. That view conflicts with Starrs\u2019s conclusion that Packer was guilty as sin and with the forensic interpretation that he murdered his companions for their belongings before resorting to cannibalism. Walter Birkby, the forensic anthropologist who disputed Starrs, said there was nothing to corroborate or refute anything Packer said and that no solid physical evidence would ever prove who did it. Michelle Pierce, Lake City\u2019s former town clerk, believes Packer did not murder the five men. Pierce\u2019s archival research turned up details that do not match the official narrative and portrays Packer as a pitiable soul whose social problems came from a misunderstood disease and harsh contemporary judgment. Pierce said, \u201cPeople really disliked him,\u201d probably because they were afraid of him. The text says that no clear or definitive evidence has yet been found for Packer\u2019s deeds.</p><p>The Denver Public Library\u2019s Western History/Genealogy Department is presented as a place to research Packer\u2019s life and crimes. Researchers can use its materials to prove or disprove parts of the Packer legend. The collection includes microfilm of Denver and Colorado newspapers from the era, including complete runs of the Denver Post and Rocky Mountain News, as well as copies of Packer\u2019s confession. The Colorado State Archives holds several legal and historical records concerning Alfred Packer. The Alfred Packer Collection includes penitentiary records, mugshot photos, parole records, confessions, letters to the governor concerning Packer\u2019s parole, a governor\u2019s pardon, original case files from Gunnison and Hinsdale District Court, Gunnison District Court cases 238\u2013241, Hinsdale District Court cases 238\u2013241, and Supreme Court case numbers 1198 from 1883, 1753 from 1885, 1980 from 1886, 3239 from 1893, and 3905 from 1898. The collection can now be viewed online: users are instructed to click the button for the online exhibits, choose \u201cBrowse Archive\u201d at the top left below the State Archives logo, and select the Alfred Packer option.</p><p>By mid-April, only Packer emerged alive from the mountains. Ripley\u2019s Vice-President of Exhibits and Archives, Edward Meyer, claimed that the museum possessed Packer\u2019s dissected skull, bought from an anonymous party for a reported $20,000. No statement was made about how the museum verified the skull\u2019s authenticity, other than that the seller\u2019s information about its origin was sufficient. If the skull belonged to Packer, his head was removed shortly before or after burial, dissected with the brain removed for study, preserved by an arsenic curing process, displayed by a traveling sideshow, sold to a private party, and later sold to Ripley\u2019s; as of 2008, it was reported to be at Ripley\u2019s Museum in San Antonio, Texas, after being moved from the New Orleans facility following Hurricane Katrina. During the 1989 exhumation, a hole about the size of a thimble was found in Shannon Bell\u2019s hip bone. James Starrs attributed the hole to scavenging animal fangs, while David Bailey wondered whether it had been made by a bullet.</p><p><span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_2 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_5 &middot; SRC_6 &middot; SRC_7 &middot; SRC_8 &middot; SRC_10 &middot; SRC_11</span></p></div><div class=\"file\"><div class=\"filehead\"><h3>File: Packer\u2019s final account</h3> <span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div><p>On March 11, a man calling himself John Schwartze asked a shopkeeper to stock up for a planned trip. The shopkeeper thought the man looked familiar, then realized he was Alfred Packer. He told the local sheriff that he had spotted a criminal, and Alfred was arrested and taken to Denver. General Adams traveled to confirm Alfred\u2019s identity and, after doing so, took him away for trial. Alfred then gave a final version of events that he largely kept until his death.</p><p>In that final story, when the group left Chief Ouray\u2019s camp, they had only enough food for one grown man for a week. After two or three days, the group was hit by a brutal snowstorm. They kept trekking until they reached the base of a mountain and then a deep valley, but the snow on the far side was so deep they could barely walk. The party moved to higher ground and had only about a pint of flour left by day four. For about the next ten days, they lived only on rosebuds and pine gum while following a ridge above deeper snow. Several of the men were in such rough shape that they cried out every night and prayed to God to escape alive. They eventually crossed the main ridge and came down to camp by a river. The party followed the stream for a day until they reached a frozen lake, possibly Lake San Cristobal. They tried to cross the lake to reach trees for shelter, and by then they were exhausted and tensions were rising.</p><p>The narrative says Shannon Bell was furious at Alfred for losing the route and at the others for acting pathetic. According to Alfred, Shannon one morning ordered him to scout higher on the mountain for a way across. Alfred took a pistol and searched, but found only steep slopes and more snow. He admitted to General Adams for the first time that he did not really know what he was doing and did not know that side of the mountains. When Alfred returned after scouting all day, he found Shannon sitting by the fire eating a piece of meat. Alfred saw a man lying near the fire and the other three men lying motionless around it. Shannon rushed at Alfred with a hatchet, and Alfred shot Shannon in the stomach before finishing him after he fell. That night, Alfred later described being surrounded by \u201cthe midnight horrors of starvation as well as those of utter isolation.\u201d He said he felt his sanity slipping and began to understand what had happened to Shannon because it was beginning to happen to him. In another letter, Alfred wrote, \u201cThe greatest wonder is that I ever returned to a rational condition.\u201d</p><p>The next morning, Alfred tried to follow his tracks from the day before but turned back because of snow and exhaustion. He built a small shelter from wood and branches, but it did not satisfy his hunger. Alfred then cooked and ate some of the meat Shannon had been eating, realizing that he had to do so to survive. He said he then spent another two months in the shelter surviving off the rest of his party. Sixty days later, he saw an avalanche that left yellow clay behind, which told him he was in Slumgullion Pass. The pass is known for its yellow color and was named because it reminded early settlers of Slumgullion stew.</p><p>In early spring, Alfred finally knew where to go and set off up the mountain again. Before leaving, he gathered the remaining meat, wrapped it for the journey, and checked the dead men\u2019s pockets, where he found the money he later spent in Saguache. He had already eaten his shoes, so he wrapped his feet in cloth and had to stop when they froze, lighting fires to warm them. As he neared the agency, he ate the last pieces of meat and may have dropped some later found by the Ute. Alfred said he had previously taken General Adams\u2019s search party only as far as the mountains opposite the camp because he wanted to stop them from going farther and did not want to return to the horror. He also said he escaped and fled trial because he feared a mob would get past his guard and kill him. He said he went to Arkansas under a fake name, worked on a ranch, later moved to Arizona, and finally traveled to Cheyenne, where he was found.</p><p>Other sources preserve conflicting versions of this account. The reference article says it appears likely that Packer killed another prospector in self-defense before gnawing on the corpses of those who had already died. Packer later said that after three or four days without provisions, the group cooked and ate their rawhide moccasins. He also said the trail was entirely drifted over and that they gathered and ate the buds of wild rose bushes. After being re-captured, Packer said Shannon Bell killed James Humphrey, a prospector who left Ouray\u2019s camp, George Noon, and Frank Miller while they slept around the campfire. Packer said Bell attacked him with a hatchet when he returned after searching for food, and that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Bell in self-defense. He said that after killing Bell he tried every day to get away but could not, so he lived on the flesh of the dead men for most of the sixty days he was out. He said the snow later formed a crust and he started out up the creek. Packer later confessed to living off the flesh of his companions and gave more than one version of the circumstances.</p><p>At the Los Pinos Indian Agency, Packer came during breakfast and begged for food and shelter. He carried a rifle, a knife, a steel coffee pot, and a satchel. When given food, he vomited and said his digestion had changed because of prolonged near-starvation. He told the agency that he had been hired by five men to guide them to Breckenridge. Packer said he became snow-blind during the journey and was left behind as a burden. He claimed Israel Swan gave him a rifle before the others abandoned him, and said he survived alone on roots and rose buds and ate his moccasins. The agency men thought his condition was odd because he did not look as malnourished and threadbare as expected after more than two months in the wilderness: his face was reported as bloated and his physique was hardly skeletal. Packer said he would drink whisky for breakfast instead of food.</p><p>Packer said he was broke and sold his Winchester rifle to Major Downer for $10. He stayed at the agency for ten days and then went to Saguache to buy supplies for a trip back to Pennsylvania. In Saguache, he bought a horse for $70 and booked a room at Dolan\u2019s Saloon. Larry Dolan later said Packer spent around $100 during his stay and offered to lend him $300. Packer also spent $78 in Otto Mears\u2019 general store. It was claimed that he had several different wallets in his possession. When Nutter asked where the rest of the party was, Packer said he had gotten his feet wet and frozen. Packer said the party camped when a winter storm started, that he made a fire while the others went ahead to find food, and that Swan left him with his rifle. He said the others never returned and that he then made his way through the mountains eating mostly rosebuds and small animals. Packer claimed Miller had stuck the knife in a tree and walked off without it.</p><p>In another account, Packer said the men left Ouray\u2019s camp with what they thought was enough food for a 14-day journey, but provisions ran out sooner than expected. After a few days of no wildlife and almost no food, he said, the men began to eye each other unsettlingly. Packer said he returned from gathering firewood to find four men around Israel Swan\u2019s body after Swan had been struck in the head with a hatchet and killed instantly. The four men butchered Swan, and Packer said he accepted the situation and joined them. Several thousand dollars was found on Swan and divided among the men. They ate the best parts of Swan, packed some away, and continued onward, with Packer taking Swan\u2019s rifle.</p><p>Packer said the five men again ran out of meat within two days and could not find game. Bell, Humphrey, Noon, and Packer secretly decided that Miller would be next because he was stocky and had soft flesh. Miller was killed with a hatchet blow to the head while stooping to pick up firewood, then butchered and eaten. Packer said Humphrey was next to be sacrificed, followed soon after by George Noon. Only Bell and Packer were left, and they swore not to eat each other. They agreed to claim that the four other men had died from the elements and were buried with dignity, and vowed never to speak of the cannibalism because they believed no one would accept that it had been necessary.</p><p>Packer said that after a few days of travel with only a rabbit and some roots to eat, he and Bell camped beside a large lake next to a grove of hemlock trees. Bell later jumped from his blanket, screamed that he could not take it anymore, and said one of them would die for food. Packer said Bell rushed at him with a rifle to bash his skull in. Packer then butchered Bell, ate as much as he could, packed away more meat, took Bell\u2019s share of Swan\u2019s money, and continued alone. He said he threw away the remaining strips of Bell\u2019s flesh because he hoped an animal would eat them.</p><p>Packer then said the men encountered a strong blizzard in the mountains. Heavy snow made them hopelessly lost and unable to retrace their route. They also ran out of matches and had to carry hot embers in a steel coffee pot to light fires. Days passed with no game and ice fishing failed completely. The men roasted and ate their shoes and tried to live on scant edible vegetation. Packer said the men made a pact that if one died, his meat would save the others from starvation. He said Israel Swan could go no further after days of hiking with almost no food; they found a pine-shaded gulch next to a lake and made camp there, and Swan then died of hunger and exposure.</p><p>Packer\u2019s signed first confession said Swan died first and was eaten by the other five persons about ten days out of camp. It said Humphreys died four or five days later and was also eaten, that Humphreys had about $133, and that Packer found the pocketbook and took the money. The confession said the butcher was later killed, apparently accidentally, while Packer was carrying wood, and was also eaten. It said Bell shot California with Swan\u2019s gun and Packer killed Bell. Packer said he shot Bell, covered up the remains, took a large piece along, and traveled 14 days to the agency. In that first confession, Packer said the first two deaths were circumstantial because of the rough conditions and that everyone in the party partook in cannibalism to stay alive. He said one man was \u201ckilled accidentally\u201d while he was collecting firewood, and that Shannon Bell killed a man called \u201cCalifornia,\u201d after which Packer killed Bell in self-defense before traveling 14 days back to civilization.</p><p>In Packer\u2019s later amended version, the men endured almost 20 days from Ouray\u2019s camp and more than 10 days without substantial food. James Humphrey died of exposure to extreme cold, he said, and George Noon was killed days later by Shannon Bell solely for food. Bell and Packer agreed to stand together until the end and not eat each other. In this version, Bell later rushed at Packer with his rifle, intending to bludgeon him with the stock. Packer said he took the valuables of the deceased because they no longer needed them, but he did not specify the items or the amount of money obtained. He later said that only Bell and he remained after the other three men were dead.</p><p>In Packer\u2019s second confession, he said Bell had killed the others after telling him to scout for a way out of the mountains and to find food. Packer said he returned in the late evening after being gone most of the day. Bell was roasting meat cut from Frank Miller\u2019s leg; Miller\u2019s skull had been crushed by a hatchet and lay furthest from the fire down the stream, while the other three bodies lay near the fire with hatchet cuts in the forehead. Packer said he dropped his revolver in deep snow after shooting Bell and lost it. He built a crude shelter from stray logs to fight the snow and wind and stayed away from the bodies. Another storm came, and he sheltered for hours while starving. He decided he had to eat something or die, returned to the fire, covered the men, took the piece of meat near the fire back to camp, cooked it, ate it, and lived off the flesh of the men for the bigger part of sixty days.</p><p>The same second-confession account says the party asked Packer to go up a nearby hill to see farther ahead for navigation. He returned to camp after a full day of going up and coming back down the hillside and said Bell had killed the entire camp and was eating the leg of a man roasted in the campfire. Bell rushed at him, and Packer shot Bell through the stomach with the rifle he had taken on the trip up the hillside. Packer said he made a new fire near his camp, cooked the piece of meat, and ate it. He tried to get away every day but could not, so he lived off the flesh of the men for the bigger part of the 60 days he was out. Packer\u2019s third confession included more details but mostly the same information as the second confession.</p><p>The accounts also disagree over the weapon and the final struggle. One version says Bell had charged at Packer with Swan\u2019s rifle and Packer shot him. Another says Packer finished Bell with a hatchet blow to the head, but that was the extent of his claim concerning the hatchet. In his second official version, Packer never claimed that he had fought off Bell with a hatchet. The account attributed to Packer says the party members died one at a time and were consumed by the other members until only he and Shannon Bell remained; Shannon, desperately hungry, tried to kill Packer before dying of natural causes, and Bell was killed in the ensuing fight. Packer said he had no choice but to eat Bell because Bell would have eaten him. He claimed Bell had murdered the others with hatchet blows to the head, maintained that he killed only Bell and did so in self-defense, and admitted to eating the flesh of his companions in order to survive. Under interrogation, Packer gave the first of multiple contradictory confessions, first claiming, \u201cWe ate old man Swann first.\u201d He claimed the strong ate the weak one by one until only he and Bell remained, and that Bell tried to kill him, so he shot Bell dead. In one confession, he admitted to shooting one man in self-defense.</p><p>Packer\u2019s written version said severe weather forced the men to camp in a ravine with some shelter from snow and wind. He wrote that they ate goatskin moccasins and rosebuds until they were weakened, emaciated, and mentally distracted, and that he was the one sane man among the six. He said he left camp to look for a way out and returned to find Shannon Wilson Bell roasting meat over a fire. Packer admitted surviving on the men\u2019s flesh until he escaped the mountains. He wrote that his comrades were dead, he was left alone among starvation and isolation, and his body and mind were overwhelmed. In letters, he insisted that \u201cthe greatest wonder is that I ever returned to a rational condition.\u201d</p><p>The accounts say Packer later maintained that Shannon Bell had killed the other men. After arriving alone at the Los Pinos Indian Agency, he said his comrades had left him while they searched for food. He claimed he did not know what happened to his companions even though he carried large sums of their money and possessed a rifle known to have belonged to one of them. Under interrogation, he admitted to partaking in cannibalism but denied committing murder. He complied with further questioning and repeated his story of self-defense and cannibalism for survival.</p><p>The wider account says that, because of his ineptitude, Packer led the men through the San Juan Mountains for nearly two months and covered only 250 miles at an average of three miles a day. In his correspondence, Packer tried to shift blame for the disaster onto Chief Ouray. He wrote that conversations with Ouray led them to believe they could reach the Los Pinos Agency in not over five days on foot across the mountains. Packer said he had never been to the agency and relied only on general reputation and information received from Ouray. The text calls Packer a known pathological liar and says his account to Newman about Ouray was not entirely truthful. Ouray never told Packer they were only five days from the Los Pinos Agency because at least three to four feet of snow blocked the path. Ouray did draw a crude map on the ground with a stick and gave them all the directions he could as a route. Packer wrote that they were given scanty fare\u2014about 20 pounds of flour and not over 10 pounds of good meat\u2014and that at the end of nine days the last provisions had been eaten. He said the group ate their goatskin moccasins and subsisted on rosebuds until they were weakened, emaciated, and mentally completely distracted. The text says those conditions led to the deaths and cannibalization of all in Packer\u2019s party, while also calling Packer\u2019s account ridiculous because he was living in a different territory under an alias to evade capture.</p><p>The later assessment remained disputed. Lamm said the evidence suggested that Packer killed only one man and only in self-defense, but added, \u201cthe proof was in the pudding and Alferd ate that, too.\u201d Lamm told Tom Miller that, in the immortal words of Alferd Packer, \u201cWell done!\u201d After being accused of murdering the other men, Packer told a story that defined the rest of his life and earned him a permanent place in Colorado history.</p><p><span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_2 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_4 &middot; SRC_5 &middot; SRC_7 &middot; SRC_8 &middot; SRC_10 &middot; SRC_11</span></p></div><div class=\"file\"><div class=\"filehead\"><h3>File: The trials</h3> <span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div><p>At Alfred Packer\u2019s first trial, witnesses testified that he had deliberately led the men astray with the intention of stealing their possessions by any means necessary. Preston Nutter, a prospector who rejected Packer\u2019s account, testified that Packer had been skulking around, always trying to find out how much money each man had or was carrying. Other witnesses described Packer\u2019s behavior after he reached Saguache and the different, conflicting stories he told about what had happened. The prosecution argued that his survival story was an attempt to cover up the killings. When Packer took the stand, he rambled for more than six hours and admitted eating the other members of the party, but said that he had killed only Shannon Bell and that Bell was responsible for everything else. The jury rejected his story and found him guilty of premeditated murder, theft, and several other charges. He was originally sentenced to death by hanging.</p><p>The dates and details of the first proceeding vary among the sources. One account says that the trial began on April 6 in Lake City; another dates its beginning to April 9, 1883, after seven days of testimony and examination. The prosecution presented about two dozen witnesses. Otto Mears testified that Packer possessed valuable Wells Fargo drafts when he emerged from the mountains. Constable Herman Lauter testified that Packer lunged at him with murder in his eyes when Lauter ordered him to hand over a knife. Packer called Lauter a liar and boasted, \u201cTo think that one little Dutchman could take a knife away from me. No, sir!\u201d The jury reportedly reacted badly to Packer\u2019s rambling testimony, his habit of speaking about himself in the third person, and his focus on personal grudges rather than on proving his innocence. One source says that the jury wasted little time finding him guilty of murder.</p><p>The first conviction was overturned because of a legal problem. Before statehood, Colorado Territory did not have a statute under which Packer could be tried for murder, and Colorado State established the specific laws only after the crime had taken place. The Colorado Supreme Court therefore held that the government could not sentence a man to death for a crime committed before Colorado became a state rather than a territory. The sources describe this as an ex post facto problem, a technicality, or the effect of an old territorial statute and a \u201csavings clause.\u201d Packer\u2019s lawyer also assigned other errors to the court, including that Packer had not received a speedy trial and that the murder indictments came after what was then a three-year statute of limitations. Another account says Packer argued that the killings occurred on the Ute Reservation and should therefore have been subject to federal rather than local jurisdiction. The murder conviction and scheduled execution were overturned, so Packer had to be retried for manslaughter instead of murder.</p><p>A second trial was held in Gunnison after the Colorado Supreme Court granted a change of venue and a new Republican judge was appointed. Packer and his counsel argued that Lake City\u2019s mainly Democratic community was too prejudiced for a fair trial. Of 57 prospective jurors interviewed, 54 admitted that they already knew about the case, and 44 said that they already believed Packer was guilty. Judge Melville Gerry ordered the sheriff to \u201cscour the highways and byways\u201d for jurors, and the sheriff filled the jury within a few hours. Packer was incarcerated at Gunnison while awaiting retrial, with one source saying that he waited nearly four years. He again pleaded not guilty and repeated his story.</p><p>The prosecution made the second case more damaging to Packer. Hunters testified that there had been plenty of food in the area even during a bad winter, including game, rabbits, deer, antelope, and more. They said that even someone as inexperienced as Packer could have hunted it easily. There was also a report that a deer carcass had been found near the campsite. That evidence weakened Packer\u2019s claim that wildlife had been so scarce that the men quickly had to resort to cannibalism. The prosecution argued that the cannibalism therefore had to have been by choice because food would have been available. Witnesses again described Packer\u2019s behavior after he reached Saguache and the different accounts he had given of the events. When Packer finally took the stand, he testified for more than six hours and admitted eating the other members of the party. His account of what happened at the campsite remained relatively close to his second official version. He asked to be charged the full 40 years only for Shannon Bell\u2019s death, since Bell was the only killing he continued to claim, but the request was denied.</p><p>The prosecution argued that even if Packer was not a malicious monster who had premeditated the entire episode, he had still led the group astray by taking a more dangerous winter route while pretending to be a guide. The text says that, at best, he was extremely negligent. Packer was convicted of manslaughter and received 40 years in prison, described as the maximum of eight years per man. The sources disagree on the legal description: Packer\u2019s criminal record lists \u201cvoluntary manslaughter\u201d on five counts, while other accounts call the conviction involuntary manslaughter. They agree that the sentence was 40 years, or eight years for each of the five men. At the time, one source calls it the longest prison sentence ever handed down in U.S. history, while another calls it the longest custodial sentence in U.S. history. He was sentenced to 40 years of hard labor at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Ca\u00f1on City.</p><p>The sources also give different summaries of the sequence. One says that Packer was arrested on murder charges, escaped from the Saguache jail, remained on the run for eight years, was captured in Wyoming in 1883, and was returned to Lake City, where he was tried, found guilty of murder, and sentenced to hang. Another says that he escaped and hid from justice for nine years, and still another says that he spent nearly a decade on the run before authorities tracked him down. A separate account says he escaped before trial and was eventually found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883. The consistent outline is that local authorities did not believe his story, that he escaped from jail, that he was later captured in Wyoming and brought back to Hinsdale County, and that he was eventually tried and convicted of murder before winning a retrial on a technicality.</p><p>Judge M. B. Gerry is associated with the famous quotation supposedly spoken while sentencing Packer in the first trial. In the legendary version, Gerry said that Packer had come to Hinsdale County when there were seven Democrats, had eaten five of them, and was sentenced to hang. One version has Gerry saying, \u201cI sentence you, Alfred Packer, to be hanged by the neck until you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d Another gives the more elaborate wording, \u201cStand up yah voracious man-eatin\u2019 sonofabitch and receive yir sintince. When yah came to Hinsdale County, there was siven Dimmycrats,\u201d followed by the claim that Packer had eaten five of them and was being sentenced to hang as a warning against reducing the Democratic population of the state. Gerry reportedly said that there were seven Democrats in Hinsdale County and that Packer had eaten five of them, and he reportedly sentenced Packer to hang until he was \u201cdead, dead, dead\u201d as a warning against reducing the Democratic population of the state.</p><p>The Colorado history account says that the legend is apocryphal. Fred M. Mazzula argues in his book *Al Packer, A Colorado Cannibal* that Judge Gerry did not talk like Yosemite Sam. Mazzula used the trial transcripts to determine what Gerry actually said at sentencing. The actual sentence ordered that Packer be taken by the sheriff of Hinsdale County to a place of execution within the corporate limits of Lake City, between 10 a.m. and 3 p.m. on May 19, 1883, and that he be hanged by the neck until he was \u201cdead, dead, dead.\u201d It ended with a request that God have mercy on Packer\u2019s soul. The text says Gerry\u2019s real quotation is less colorful but more accurate. One source nevertheless records that Gerry sentenced Packer to be \u201chung from your neck until you are dead, dead, dead.\u201d</p><p>Lake City residents were outraged when the death sentence was overturned and threatened to hang Packer themselves, so the second trial was moved to Gunnison, about 50 miles away. Packer escaped death on a technicality and received the new trial. After the new proceeding, he was convicted of manslaughter and sentenced to 40 years in the Colorado State Penitentiary in Ca\u00f1on City. After a confession, a search for the bodies, and a trial, one article summarizes, Packer was sentenced to forty years at the Colorado State Penitentiary. The sources differ over whether the second-trial conviction should be called voluntary or involuntary manslaughter, but Packer\u2019s listed convictions are murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter on five counts, and his listed criminal penalty is death, later commuted to 40 years\u2019 imprisonment.</p><p>The prosecution\u2019s motive theory also focused on Israel Swan. At the time of Packer\u2019s trial, Swan\u2019s family reported that he had left for the expedition with about $6,000 in cash and gold and a valuable Winchester rifle. The money and rifle would have provided a strong motive for murder, and Swan was 65 at the time of his death. Packer was not found with any gold when he was arrested. Although he had some money left, it did not total in the thousands, and the sources say that his spending had been frivolous. One account says that he spent more than $100 on drinking and gambling and another $78 in a general store, while often giving conflicting stories when he drank. The prosecution argued that the only logical reason for him to travel through the mountains with so little food and supplies was to lead the men into the wilderness to kill and rob them. Packer pleaded not guilty.</p><p>The first-trial evidence concerning the deaths included the prosecution\u2019s claim that Swan died on or around March 1. In court, the prosecution argued that Swan showed signs of a struggle at death and that the other men appeared to have been killed in their sleep. Otto Mears, Larry Dolan, Oliver D. Loutsenhizer, and Preston Nutter testified for the prosecution, with Nutter as the key witness. Other accounts repeat that Nutter testified that Packer had been trying to learn how much money each man was carrying. The physical evidence was also used against Packer: the men had no defensive wounds or injuries in one account, while later exhumation accounts describe defensive hacking marks on the forearms of three skeletons, including Shannon Bell\u2019s. All five skeletons showed varying post-mortem injuries, including depressed fractures, butterfly fractures, butchering trauma, and hacking trauma. These findings leave the evidentiary picture disputed rather than settled.</p><p>Packer\u2019s lawyer mounted what one source calls a spirited defense, but Packer went to prison for seventeen years before *The Denver Post* petitioned for his release. In prison, he made horsehair bridles, and one of them is displayed at the Museum of Western Colorado in Grand Junction. He also built elaborate Victorian dollhouses. After being imprisoned, Packer wrote to anyone he could, claiming that his sentence was unjust, that he had killed only Shannon, and that the jury was full of biased and prejudiced people. He filed appeals five times and was denied each time. He sent letters to local newspapers saying that he had been unjustly convicted by an unfair and unsympathetic judicial system and by ignorant, small-minded people. On June 19, 1899, the Colorado Supreme Court officially upheld his sentence. He never succeeded in obtaining an official state pardon, although the Colorado State Archives collection includes a governor\u2019s pardon and also contains penitentiary, mugshot, parole, confession, governor\u2019s-parole, and original court records.</p><p>The sources disagree about the length of time Packer served and the date of his release. One says he served 15 years before being granted parole; another says he spent 16 years in prison; another says he served 17 years before *The Denver Post* petitioned for his release. The account based on the Colorado State Archives says that Alfred was paroled in 1901, 18 years into his sentence. Another source says that former Governor Charles Thomas paroled him in 1901, while a separate historical summary gives 1905. The sources agree that he was paroled rather than officially pardoned and that Polly Pry, a reporter for *The Denver Post*, helped campaign for his release. After his parole, he worked for *The Denver Post* as a guard and later as a ranch hand.</p><p>Packer\u2019s criminal status is listed as deceased. His convictions are listed as murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter on five counts; his criminal penalty is listed as death, later commuted to 40 years\u2019 imprisonment. The text says that Packer is one of four people historically convicted for cannibalism in the United States. It also describes the incident as the only known trial in the United States associated with human cannibalism, while stressing that Packer was not actually convicted of cannibalism as such: he was tried and found guilty of murder in the first trial and manslaughter in the second. The sources state that cannibalism itself was not necessarily illegal in the United States unless a person committed murder to obtain the flesh.</p><p>Packer\u2019s supporters continued to dispute the verdict. Polly Pry portrayed him as a former soldier and a man of the people who had been persecuted by the establishment, had been in the wrong place at the wrong time, and had survived horrors that most people could not imagine. She argued that his sentence was so harsh only because of the cannibalism and that anyone would have done the same in his situation. Her stories changed the minds of some local businesses and residents, leading them to organize petitions for his release. In mock trials held in Lake City, Boulder, and other Colorado towns, actors played the main roles and audience members served as jurors. When David Bailey\u2019s evidence was included, jurors acquitted Packer more often than not. The State Archives collection includes a governor\u2019s pardon, but the historical accounts say that Packer never obtained an official state pardon.</p><p><span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_2 &middot; SRC_4 &middot; SRC_5 &middot; SRC_6 &middot; SRC_7 &middot; SRC_8 &middot; SRC_10 &middot; SRC_11</span></p></div><div class=\"file\"><div class=\"filehead\"><h3>File: The expedition</h3> <span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div><p>The sources give differing accounts of the expedition\u2019s size and divisions. One historical account says Robert McGrue, leader of the larger prospecting party, organized a party of 20 to travel from Salt Lake City to the San Juan Mountains after hearing of gold discovered in Breckenridge. Another account says that a party of 21 prospectors, led by Alferd\u2014or Alfred\u2014Packer, left Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 8, 1873, hoping to be among the first to locate mining claims in the San Juan region. A separate account describes Packer as part of a larger band of 20 gold seekers who left Utah and split into two groups. The prospectors were drawn by news of a gold strike and reports of enormous fortunes, and one newspaper account says they were promised \u201cmarvelous tales of enormous fortunes to be had for the mere asking.\u201d Their intended route was toward Breckenridge, by way of the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency.</p><p>Packer had worked as a guide for only a few months, and the accounts disagree about how much responsibility to assign him, but they agree that the party progressed more slowly than expected toward Breckenridge despite his serving as guide. One account says Alfred managed, by luck, to get the party only as far as just outside Montrose. The journey from Utah to Colorado was slow and difficult: winter arrived early and severely, the wagons and horses were crippled, and heavy snow along the Spanish Trail covered the path so completely that the men relied almost solely on a compass. The wagons could hardly follow a trail, and Packer\u2019s inexperience showed itself as the party became lost. By January, provisions had run out completely. The men first survived on horse feed and came close to considering the horses themselves as food. The men later described Alfred as greedy, lazy, and constantly fighting with someone; other accounts add that he was argumentative, inexperienced, distrusted, and known for lying and theft.</p><p>On January 21, 1874, the party found a Ute encampment in a valley just outside Montrose, in the Uncompahgre Valley, led by Chief Ouray, who was known as \u201cThe White Man\u2019s Friend.\u201d The accounts say Ouray supplied the men with food, lodging, and shelter, exchanged food and supplies with them, and offered to let them remain with his tribe until winter had passed. He recommended postponing the expedition until spring because the winter weather in the mountains was dangerous, promised to share all that he and his people had with them, and warned that no Ute would attempt the journey in those conditions. According to the sources, he said that trying it meant certain or almost certain death. The party still had about 120 miles and the San Juan Mountains left to cross. One account records a different claim attributed to Packer: that Ouray said the Los Pi\u00f1os Agency could be reached in no more than five days on foot across the mountains. The same account says that this was not truthful, because three or four feet of snow blocked the route; men who remained at the camp said Ouray told the prospectors they would all die and that no Indian in his band would go to show them the route because the deep snow was too dangerous.</p><p>Despite the warning, 11 men decided to continue. They planned to travel first to the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, the closest outpost to Ouray\u2019s camp, and then proceed to Breckenridge. Ouray provided food for the journey and safe directions to bypass the mountains, advising them to drop down to the Gunnison River and follow it north to the agency. The party divided: one account says five men followed Oliver Loutsenhizer along the Gunnison River while six others, including Packer, headed through the San Juan Mountains. Loutsenhizer threatened to shoot Packer if Packer tried to follow the other group. Another account describes three factions at the Ute encampment: some men paid Ouray a trespassing fee and stayed with the Utes for the rest of the winter; others accepted a week\u2019s provisions from him and traveled toward the Los Pinos Agency in Saguache County; and a third group ignored his warnings. The river-bound group nearly perished after losing its way, while some of the men stayed behind with the horses and wagons until spring.</p><p>Packer insisted that crossing the mountains directly was better than going around them because he claimed to know the area and said the route was faster and more direct. The sources emphasize that locals had given him a safer route beside a main water source, where the men could have caught fresh fish and stayed on course. Instead, Packer chose the mountain route with minimal supplies because he believed it would be faster. Although the route he and his companions took was nearly identical in length to the recommended route, though not in its traverse, it was far more dangerous. The choice to hike through the San Juan Mountains in midwinter\u2014where snow could exceed six feet in a single snowfall, with blasting winds and freezing temperatures\u2014is described as practically suicidal.</p><p>After fierce snowstorms boxed in the expedition, many of the men returned home, but six, including Packer, continued. On February 9, Packer and five others left for the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency, described in one account as 75 miles ahead. The five men were George Noon, Shannon Wilson Bell, James Humphrey, Frank Miller, and Israel Swan; they wanted to reach Breckenridge before thousands of fortune hunters arrived. Their immediate goal was the agency, followed by Breckenridge. The group traveled southeast and then south along the Lake Fork, passing the site that would become the townsite of Lake City within six months. After wandering, the party was discovered by warpainted Ute Indians, and Chief Ouray took them to a spot near his village where they established a makeshift camp. When Ouray realized that Packer\u2019s party would still enter the storm, he supplied them with provisions despite the warnings.</p><p>Packer\u2019s men entered the mountains with barely enough food for the supposed 14 days required by the safest route. They had no snowshoes, minimal matches, no flint, no heavy clothing, two rifles, one pistol, a couple of knives, a hatchet, and minimal ammunition. Another account says Packer had left Utah with few provisions and no weapons. The larger party\u2019s river route initially followed Ouray\u2019s directions under Bob McGrue\u2019s guidance, but the horses eventually could not continue. McGrue unloaded the men\u2019s provisions and returned to Ouray\u2019s camp. Packer then took his party higher into the San Juan Mountains against Ouray\u2019s warning.</p><p>The river party had a brutal journey in the cold. The freezing winter made the Gunnison route difficult, and the men ran out of food long before reaching the agency. Cowhands found them and fed them at a cattle camp until spring; another account says the cowhands let them remain there until spring. In April, when the weather improved, the men reached the agency in May and found that Alfred had already been there alone. The sources also describe Packer\u2019s separate arrival: he wandered into the Los Pinos Agency in Southwest Colorado three months later, on April 16, 1874, 65 days after his departure, and another account places the agency 75 miles ahead. He emerged from the woods alone, carrying a pocket full of cash, a rifle, and a coffee pot, with no traveling party, which aroused suspicion. The account that gives his arrival as April 16 says he entered the agency during a snowstorm, begging for food and shelter.</p><p>A video dramatization presents Packer as the sole survivor of a six-man party that tried to cross the San Juan Mountains in the Colorado Territory; the underlying historical accounts likewise say he emerged as the sole survivor. Those accounts say the six men lost their way and became snowbound, that five prospectors died agonizing deaths in the mountain cold, and that a sixth man had his life irrevocably altered by the events. One account says that only Packer returned in the winter of 1874. The expedition account also records that Jean \u201cFrenchy\u201d Cabazon had been a member of the original party that left Provo, wisely stayed in Chief Ouray\u2019s camp, and later reached the destination safely with Bob McGrue and Preston Nutter\u2019s party.</p><p>The men neared the point of eating the horses themselves.</p><p><span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_2 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_4 &middot; SRC_5 &middot; SRC_8 &middot; SRC_10 &middot; SRC_11</span></p></div><div class=\"file\"><div class=\"filehead\"><h3>File: Packer\u2019s legacy</h3> <span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div><p>Packer\u2019s story of cannibalism is presented as a danger story about nineteenth-century prospectors in the Rocky Mountains and as one of the West\u2019s most grisly and enduring legends and murder mysteries. The case reflects the eagerness of prospectors to trespass into the Ute reservation, illustrates how swiftly development took place once the San Juan region opened, and attracted national and international attention to Lake City and its environs. Local papers gave the case constant coverage, using sensational headlines, negative comments about Packer\u2019s character, and imaginative theories that drew national and international attention. Harper\u2019s Weekly coverage also drew national attention to the grisly crime; an illustrator from Harper\u2019s Weekly recorded the scene in visual graphic detail, and copies of the portrait photograph were sold for 50 cents to the curious.</p><p>The area northeast of Lake City where Packer\u2019s party got lost is labeled \u201cCannibal Plateau\u201d on maps, and the site where the bodies were found five miles beyond town is called Deadman\u2019s Gulch. The text says Packer evolved from Old West infamy to New West celebrity. His pistol is now on display at the Museum of Western Colorado. In Lake City, \u201cAl Packer Days,\u201d the Packer Burger at the Cannibal Grill, and a wooden marker for the Alferd Packer Massacre Site are popular attractions. Travel magazines say the Hinsdale County Museum has the largest collection of Packer memorabilia known, including skull fragments, clothing buttons from victims, and shackles used when Packer was imprisoned. Tourists are advised not to miss the actual burial site, which is five minutes from town.</p><p>The modern myth states that Packer was convicted of cannibalism, but the text says that belief is totally incorrect. He was tried and found guilty of murder in the first trial, and of manslaughter in the second. Nearly all of Saguache and soon nearly the entire country and beyond believed Packer\u2019s responsibility for the deaths was beyond doubt. Cannibalism was not seen as the main issue of Packer\u2019s guilt. People then knew the Donner Party story and understood to some degree the need to eat in the wilderness. Cannibalism was and is not illegal per se in the United States unless a murder is committed to obtain the flesh. If someone murders to obtain flesh, the charge is murder, with the cannibalism treated as desecration or abuse of a corpse. Packer claimed for the rest of his life that he was unjustly vilified and convicted for cannibalism rather than for murder, which he continued to deny.</p><p>The sources disagree about parts of Packer\u2019s punishment and later life, and those disagreements remain part of the legacy. One account says Packer had served 18 years of his 40-year sentence, while another says he served 16 years of that 40-year sentence before being released. The latter account says he was originally sentenced to death, received a 40-year sentence after a second trial in 1885, and lived out the rest of his life in relative peace. Another source says he was convicted of five counts of voluntary manslaughter in Gunnison in 1886 and sentenced to 40 years. The text says Packer ultimately found some redemption from the people of Colorado, or at least from the justice system. It also says that he was the most celebrated Colorado convict, wooed and loved despite his life sentence for eating his camping compadres.</p><p>Packer\u2019s name and story entered books, film, music, restaurants, and jokes. In Dashiell Hammett\u2019s novel The Thin Man, Nick Charles advises Gilbert Wynant to read a selection about Packer\u2019s expeditions. That selection comes from Thomas Samuel Duke\u2019s Celebrated Criminal Cases of America, where Thomas Samuel Duke gave Packer\u2019s first name as Alfred. Harold Schechter wrote the true-crime book Man-Eater about Packer. In 1993, CU film students Matt Stone, a CU film student, and Trey Parker, a CU film student, produced Cannibal! The Musical; Matt Stone and Trey Parker later created the television show South Park. Cannibal! The Musical was Trey Parker\u2019s directorial debut, and the text says it was short-lived, like Packer\u2019s companions. The story is being dramatized in the film rather than simply reported, and the underlying account is Packer\u2019s expedition and its aftermath.</p><p>The University of Colorado Boulder has an Alferd Packer Grill where students are greeted by a giant portrait of Packer and the slogan \u201cHave a friend for lunch!\u201d The cafeteria grill\u2019s slogan was \u201cHave a friend for lunch!\u201d, customers can order an El Canibal beefburger, and a giant map outlining Packer\u2019s travels through Colorado was displayed on the wall. The grill has since been renamed the Alferd Packer Restaurant &amp; Grill. A commenter says they first learned about Packer while attending CU Boulder and learning how the Alferd E. Packer Grill got its name. UC-Boulder students have hosted Al Packer Days wearing T-shirts with slogans like \u201cFinger lickin\u2019 good\u201d and \u201cWhen you care enough to eat the very best.\u201d The text mentions Alferd Packer\u2019s High Protein Gourmet Cookbook as an example of gallows humor about the story, but also says there is really nothing funny about the tale.</p><p>The University of Colorado example was echoed in Washington. At a press conference on August 10, 1977, Secretary of Agriculture Bob Bergland and his employees unveiled a plaque naming the executive cafeteria \u201cThe Alferd Packer Memorial Grill.\u201d Bergland said Packer\u2019s life exemplified the spirit and fare of the cafeteria and would \u201cserve all mankind.\u201d Barbara Walters covered the event on ABC Evening News. The stratagem succeeded and the contracts were terminated soon afterward. Bergland removed the plaque after accepting the bureaucratic objection that it lacked official GSA authorization. The plaque is now displayed on the wall of the National Press Club\u2019s The Reliable Source members-only bar, and the National Press Club\u2019s hamburger is called the Alfred Packer Burger.</p><p>Phil Ochs, a 1960s folk-music songwriter, used humor in \u201cThe Ballad of Alferd Packer\u201d (1964), matching the lighthearted ongoing attitude toward Packer and his alleged crimes. The song never appeared on any of Ochs\u2019s studio or live albums. C. W. McCall wrote the song \u201cComin\u2019 Back for More\u201d about Packer. Packer remains a wildly popular figure in Colorado state history. A restaurant in Boulder, Colorado, called the Alferd Packer Grill was named in his honor, and events and traditions honoring Packer take place annually in Colorado and 12 other states.</p><p>Lake City today treats Packer as both its patron saint and its antihero. Lake City is a remote mountain town at an elevation of 8,661 feet. Most visitors come to hunt, fish, or hike, and some hope to glimpse Al Packer\u2019s ghost. The town has businesses called the Packer Saloon &amp; Cannibal Grill and the Cannibal Cabins. Lake City has hosted an annual Al Packer Day with coffin races, mystery-meat eating competitions, and an Al Packer look-alike contest. Grant Houston of the Hinsdale County Historical Society says many local people, including himself, would say Packer was guilty. Houston says local tradition held that Packer lured the men up there for money or guns he coveted, killed them, and fled. The courthouse contains Packer memorabilia, newspaper accounts, and official trial records.</p><p>Packer\u2019s grave in Littleton has been left with offerings such as a toy firetruck, a marijuana vape pen, and a vase for flowers. A wooden bench invites visitors to sit and reflect at Packer\u2019s grave. Packer was buried in Littleton, Colorado. The sources differ on the exact circumstances and date of his death: one says he died in 1907 at a ranch near Deer Creek in western Jefferson County, another says he died two years after becoming a local celebrity in Gunnison, and another says he died alone in Littleton at age 69. His death was cited as \u201cDementia \u2013 trouble &amp; worry,\u201d though his clinical cause of death has also been described as a stroke. Another source says his official cause of death was listed as \u201cSenility -- trouble and worry.\u201d Packer was widely rumored to have become a vegetarian before death and was reported by acquaintances as a man rich with stories and liked by children. He lived modestly and was reported to be charitable. There was also a rumor that Alfred was a vegetarian when he died.</p><p>After prison, Alfred worked at the Denver Post as a guard for a while. People described Alfred after prison as kind, modest, and charitable, in contrast with the grumpy, greedy, and untrustworthy man he had been in the past. Alfred eventually moved to Littleton, where he bought a copper mine and returned to prospecting. Another account says Packer lived in Denver, then moved to Littleton to be closer to the mountains, bought copper claims, and resumed prospecting until epilepsy overcame him. He remained a free man for nine years after the escape; while in Gunnison, he became a local celebrity; and Packer lived the rest of his life in relative peace.</p><p>The story of Packer and the story that actually occurred are not always the same. A commenter objects to what he calls revisionist history and says he wants to keep the lore of the Old West without modern filtering. The text says Packer\u2019s legacy remained unsettled even after his peaceful death. It also says that the tale has been the subject of gallows humor and amusements, but that five men died agonizing deaths in the cruel mountain cold while a sixth had his own life irrevocably altered, so there is really nothing funny about the tale.</p><p>The sources also discuss Packer\u2019s female admirers through the idea of hybristophilia. Mental health professionals call the disorder in which some women become infatuated with heinous serial killers and mass murderers in jail cells hybristophilia. Popular culture refers to hybristophilia as Bonnie and Clyde syndrome. The condition is usually characterized by a woman communicating with a high-profile criminal in an attempt to kindle a relationship, and the text says hybristophilia is not solely a 20th-century phenomenon. Alfred Packer, also spelled Alferd, received fan mail from several adoring women while he was incarcerated for his crimes. Before Pry helped secure his release, Packer received a constant stream of curious visitors who came to ogle Colorado\u2019s nationally known prisoner. He also received many letters from adoring well-wishers while in prison.</p><p>One sympathizer was Mrs. Rebecca A. Newman. Newman worked as a schoolteacher while living in the Bronx neighborhood of New York City. By opening communications with Packer, Newman apparently hoped to form a friendship with him and express sympathy for his circumstances. When Governor-Elect Alva Adams learned of Newman\u2019s affection for Packer, he warned her that if she knew Packer and his horrible crime she would not care to defend him or excuse his conduct. Not dissuaded by Adams, Newman insisted that communications be opened anyway. Newman asked Packer for a full detailed statement of the whole circumstances connected with his most extraordinary trials and sufferings.</p><p>While in prison, Packer often crafted leather belts, bridles, wooden canes, and watch fobs from dyed and braided horsehair. Packer usually sold those items as souvenirs to prison visitors and sometimes gifted such items to people he thought were special. Packer gave Rebecca a chain bracelet as a sign of affection and desire for a relationship. Through his correspondence with Newman, Packer hoped to find out what she could do for him. Packer wrote that he would make her proof he was worthy of the aid of philanthropic people and those better situated than he was financially and otherwise. The text says women like Newman still exist today, that contacting famous criminals who are experienced manipulators is a dangerous game, and that people who do not proceed with caution sometimes end up being eaten alive.</p><p><span class=\"src\">SRC_2 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_4 &middot; SRC_5 &middot; SRC_8 &middot; SRC_10 &middot; SRC_1 &middot; SRC_7 &middot; SRC_11</span></p></div><div class=\"file\"><div class=\"filehead\"><h3>File: Polly Pry\u2019s campaign</h3> <span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div><p>Alfred Packer\u2019s story eventually reached Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan, who used the pen name Polly Pry. She was a controversial reporter for The Denver Post and later a freelancer in the late nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, and was known for her connection to the Alfred Packer case. Pry became the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter in 1898 and was a member of the Denver Woman\u2019s Press Club. At age fifteen, she eloped to Mexico with the railroad industrialist George Anthony; five years later, she left Anthony and moved to New York to become a reporter. She was initially rejected by The New York Times, but later obtained a trial assignment there. Fellow reporters gave her the nickname Polly Pry because they said she could pry information out of anyone.</p><p>One account says Pry first met Packer and interviewed him while researching an article about Colorado\u2019s prison system and the Colorado State Penitentiary; a biographical account dates that meeting to 1901. After hearing Packer\u2019s story, she partly believed him and used her position as a reporter to try to help him. She believed he was innocent of murder and advocated for him in print throughout the trial. In the early 1900s, she launched a journalistic and media campaign to publicize his plight and release him. She wrote stories presenting him as a man of the people: an ex-soldier persecuted by the establishment, a common man caught in a regrettable situation, someone in the wrong place at the wrong time who had survived horrors most people could not imagine. She argued that Packer\u2019s sentence was so harsh because of the cannibalism, and claimed that anyone would have done the same in that situation. In her column, she also argued that since sailors were legally allowed to eat people when lost at sea, the same rules should apply to people stranded in the mountains. Another account says she used Packer\u2019s Army service to portray him as a common man who had been caught in a regrettable situation, had done what he had to do to survive, and had been crucified for violating civilized sensibilities by resorting to cannibalism.</p><p>Over time, Pry\u2019s stories led local businesses to arrange petitions for Packer\u2019s release. They also led to petitions and requests reaching Colorado Governor Charles Thomas\u2019s office, although those efforts met strong opposition. Packer gained allies who signed a petition for his pardon. One supporter wrote to Thomas that, even if Packer had committed the crime, his privations made responsibility difficult to assign: \u201cThe line between sanity and insanity is often very thin, and I don\u2019t think we understand it.\u201d The Colorado State Archives says its Alfred Packer Collection includes letters to the Governor concerning Packer\u2019s parole, as well as parole records and a Governor\u2019s pardon. Packer had made many appeals for a pardon after entering prison but was repeatedly denied. A separate account says he filed five appeals and was roundly denied each time. In 1899, when Denver Post writer Polly Pry visited him at the Colorado State Penitentiary in Canon City, she wrote that \u201cthe corroding desire for freedom has eaten into his heart\u201d and that \u201cthe blind injustice of the world has seared into his soul.\u201d</p><p>The sources give differing accounts of the outcome and date. One says that Governor Thomas ultimately relented and, in his last official act before leaving office, paroled but did not pardon Packer on the condition that Packer would not try to profit from his story. Another says that, as his last act in office, Thomas granted parole but not pardon. A historical account says Packer received parole on February 8, 1901, after a campaign initially spearheaded by his old acquaintance Duane Hatch, who petitioned for nearly a decade before his efforts came to Pry\u2019s attention. A different account says Packer received parole in 1905, while another says Pry\u2019s campaign and Harry Tammen\u2019s intervention eventually led to a pardon on January 7, 1901. The sources therefore do not agree on whether he was pardoned or only paroled, or on the date. One account says Packer had served 18 years of a 40-year sentence; another says he was granted parole after serving 15 years.</p><p>Denver Post publishers Fred Bonfils and Harry Tammen hired a lawyer to defend Packer. Because Pry\u2019s Packer column increased the paper\u2019s revenue and readership, one account says she persuaded Tammen to pay for Packer\u2019s defense and demand a retrial. After parole, Packer worked as a guard at The Denver Post and later as a ranch hand. Many people believed that his Denver Post job came directly from Pry and her employers securing his release. Packer showed enduring respect for Pry and called her his \u201cLiberator.\u201d</p><p>After the trial, Pry was present during a confrontation involving W. W. Anderson, Packer\u2019s attorney, and the owners of The Denver Post who had hired him. The owners learned that Anderson had taken a double payment and demanded that he return the money. During the confrontation, Anderson pulled a gun and shot both men. As he prepared to shoot again, Pry jumped in front of Harry Heye Tammen and tried to shield him. She stalled Anderson long enough for the police to arrive and used her skirt to stop the bleeding. Rumors later circulated that Pry\u2019s skirts had saved her publishers\u2019 lives by slowing bullets from the gun. When union workers boycotted The Denver Post over her stance on labor issues and immigration, she founded her own paper, Polly Pry. Afterward, gunmen attacked her at home rather than at work. Pry was a strong advocate of free speech and, during World War I, came to the aid of French war orphans.</p><p>Pry\u2019s involvement has also been dramatized. Two film versions of the Alferd Packer story included her. In Jim Roberson\u2019s The Legend of Alfred Packer (1980), Cynthia Nessin portrayed Polly Pry. The film included the Anderson incident, but the man was not named and was called \u201cGunman\u201d in the credits. After Pry begins telling Packer\u2019s story, she does not appear again in the film, and the shooting is not mentioned again. The film depicts her as hard-drinking and forceful. Trey Parker\u2019s Cannibal! The Musical featured Toddy Walters as Polly Pry and went more deeply into her involvement with the case, but ignored the fact that she was married in order to make her a love interest for Packer.</p><p><span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_6 &middot; SRC_8 &middot; SRC_9 &middot; SRC_10 &middot; SRC_11</span></p></div><div class=\"file\"><div class=\"filehead\"><h3>File: The missing men</h3> <span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div><p>According to the expedition leader, Alfred told him that after leaving the Ute camp, his party followed the river on horseback until the horses could go no farther. The leader unloaded their provisions and walked the horses back to the Ute camp. The men then likely walked farther down the river and took a path high into the mountains. The leader knew they did not have enough supplies to reach the agency, but he had grown tired of arguing with Alfred. They had only about 14 days of food, inadequate clothes, no snowshoes or flints, and hardly any matches. Their equipment consisted of only a few rifles, one pistol, a hatchet, and some knives. After the leader turned back, no one heard from those men again until Alfred appeared at the agency months later.</p><p>When Alfred arrived, he looked bloated and well-fed, unlike the starving men people usually saw coming out of the wilderness. After he felt better, he went to Saguache, where he rented a room and a saloon and spent over $100 on drinking and gambling. He spent another $78 in the general store. People said he gave conflicting stories when he drank, sometimes carried a different wallet, and never seemed to run out of cash. He had sold his rifle at the agency for only $10, which did not explain his remaining money. Preston Nutter, the leader of the other party that followed the river, arrived at the agency in May and later went to Saguache for answers. Preston suspected Alfred was lying and told General Adams that the other men would not have left him for dead. The rifle Alfred sold belonged to an old man in the group, and the pipe Alfred left behind belonged to Shannon Bell. When Alfred originally joined the group, he had no money.</p><p>General Adams sent a rider to bring Alfred in for questioning. When questioned, Alfred said he got his feet wet and cold, stayed by the fire while the others looked for food, and assumed they had abandoned him when they did not return. A separate account says that nine weeks later Packer arrived at the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency south of present-day Gunnison with a Winchester rifle, a skinning knife, and a coffee pot containing live coals. When Packer arrived at the agency, he was haggard and worn but otherwise fit. Another of the original gold seekers asked where Packer had gotten his spending money. Packer admitted that the small band had starved in the San Juans, that Israel Swan had died from hunger and exposure, and that Packer had eaten him.</p><p>Two of the five men who had taken the Gunnison River route eventually arrived at the Los Pin\u00f3s Indian Agency, and the remaining three men, including Loutsenhizer, arrived a few days later. General Charles Adams told the men he had already met Alfred Packer and that Packer spoke of being deserted by his companions. All five men immediately discredited Packer\u2019s account to Adams and his staff. Loutsenhizer said the Winchester rifle Packer carried belonged to an elderly man in the party, and that the pipe Packer left at the agency belonged to Shannon Bell.</p><p>The following August, John A. Randolph, an illustrator for Harper&#x27;s Weekly, found the site of the incident. Randolph discovered all five bodies at the foot of Slumgullion Pass, two miles southeast of Lake City, Colorado, in a pine-shaded gulch. The bodies lay above the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River, now colloquially known as Dead Man&#x27;s Gulch. The site matched the description of where Packer had originally claimed that only Bell was killed. The snow covering the bodies and campsite had melted during the preceding four months. Randolph sketched the scene and alerted authorities in nearby Lake City. Harper&#x27;s Weekly covered the story two months later, in its October 17, 1874, edition, and included Randolph&#x27;s illustration.</p><p>The local coroner and law enforcement went to the site with about 20 volunteers and found all five bodies in various states of decomposition after four months outdoors. First responders said it appeared that extreme violence had befallen the men. Frank Miller&#x27;s head was missing from the campsite. Frank Miller&#x27;s and Israel Swan&#x27;s corpses had been heavily scavenged and were little more than scattered bones, and Israel&#x27;s skull had a jagged chunk missing from it. George Noon and James Humphrey were largely flayed torsos of rotting viscera attached to skeletal legs, but their faces remained intact and bearded. Humphrey&#x27;s face was slightly more decayed than Noon&#x27;s. Noon and Humphrey had head blows that suggested a hatchet and had noticeable broken bones.</p><p>Shannon Bell had skeletal legs, arms cut to the bones, hands that were still fully skinned, and a torso that was almost wholly flayed. Bell&#x27;s remaining corpse was a mass of viscera with a thick red beard and bushy hair still on his face. The relatively undamaged state of Bell&#x27;s face suggested that he had been the last to die. The top of Bell&#x27;s skull had been ripped open. The three men whose bodies were still intact or partly intact had flesh and muscle cut from choice meaty locations; no attempt had been made to consume bone marrow or any organs. Preston Nutter identified the bodies as the five missing men. A rifle broken in two was found close to the bodies, and because of the damage to the remains, the rifle was presumed to have been used to bludgeon one or more of the men.</p><p>Nothing was heard from the six prospectors for nearly three months. The men became lost and, without proper provisions or equipment, were cold and hungry. Of the six men, only Packer lived.</p><p><span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_2 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_10</span></p></div><div class=\"file\"><div class=\"filehead\"><h3>File: Alfred Packer\u2019s background</h3> <span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div><p>Alfred Packer, also known as John Schwartze, Alferd Packer, the Colorado Cannibal, and, in local newspapers, the \u201cMan-Eater,\u201d was an American prospector and self-proclaimed wilderness guide known for cannibalism. One account describes Alferd Packer, \u201cThe Colorado Cannibal,\u201d as one of the most notorious and most misunderstood tales in Colorado history. The sources do not settle the spelling of his first name: official court papers from the time spell it \u201cAlfred,\u201d while Packer signed his name \u201cAlferd\u201d; his grave is also marked with a veteran\u2019s tombstone listing \u201cAlfred.\u201d The Colorado State Archives\u2019 Alfred Packer Collection includes military grave registration, along with legal and historical records concerning him.</p><p>Packer was one of three children born to James Packer and Esther Packer, n\u00e9e Griner. By the early 1850s, his father had moved the family to LaGrange County, Indiana, where he worked as a cabinetmaker. Packer is said to have had a bitter relationship with his parents. In his late teens, he moved to Minnesota and worked as a shoemaker. He suffered from epilepsy from an early age and was prone to seizures. During the American Civil War, he served in the Union Army. One account says he enlisted on April 22, 1862, in Winona, Minnesota, in Company F of the 16th Infantry Regiment, and was honorably discharged eight months later because epilepsy triggered seizures. He enlisted a second time, on June 25, 1863, in the 8th Iowa Cavalry Regiment at Ottumwa, Iowa, and was honorably discharged again on April 22, 1864, after his epilepsy caused further seizures. The shorter biographical account instead says that he enlisted twice and that both terms lasted only a few months because of epilepsy and seizures; both accounts agree that he was honorably discharged both times.</p><p>Afterward, Packer traveled west and worked at numerous jobs over the next nine years. He worked as a hunter, wagon teamster, ranch hand, field worker, range hand, and as someone who drove horses and other draft animals across the West. He also took odd jobs for mining companies. He worked for a couple of months as a guide, but people later said he was ill-suited to the role and prone to losing his way. He had a habit of getting lost, arguing with clients, stealing from people, and lying. His seizures and overall attitude meant that he never kept a job for long. He worked mining-related jobs from mine to mine but never found prosperity in mining; he worked briefly as a miner in the Colorado Territory and then moved on to the Utah Territory. He also tried mining in Colorado, but kept losing jobs because of his bad attitude and because no one could trust him. People who knew him generally disliked and distrusted him because of his argumentative personality, near-pathological or scurrilous lying, and reputation for theft.</p><p>In the fall of 1873, a group of 20 prospectors set out toward southwestern Colorado and the San Juan country. The group was traveling to newly found goldfields near Breckenridge, Colorado; another account spells the destination \u201cBrackenridge.\u201d Six men, including Packer, ultimately continued into the San Juan Mountains during the winter, and two months later only one man emerged from the mountains. Rather than looking emaciated, as early explorers were often later found, he looked weirdly fed. The surviving man was Alfred Packer. A separate account says that the six men set off from Bingham Canyon, Utah, on November 20, 1873, while another says Packer was one of six prospectors who headed into the Colorado mountains in February 1874. These accounts therefore give different dates for the expedition\u2019s departure.</p><p>Packer was not initially with the party. George Tracy said that the party encountered a 23-year-old man called Alfred Packer near Provo. The expedition account says the group met him south of Salt Lake City, about 25 miles into the journey. Packer asked where the men were going, learned that they were headed for the goldfields, and offered his services as a guide. He claimed he was both a prospector and a guide who knew the territory between Utah and Breckenridge, or the San Juan territory, well. Because he had no provisions, he offered $25 to join the band. The prospectors accepted him because they needed a guide and thought that someone who knew the route would help them. Party members later said that Packer may have overstated or even fabricated his familiarity with the area. In fact, he had no idea where he was going, although he had worked as a guide for a few months.</p><p>During the journey, Packer was reported to have left with only a Colt revolver and no rifle. This conflicts with the account of his later arrival at the agency, which describes him carrying a rifle. He was also reported to be greedy with rations, lazy, obstinate, and obsessed with how much money the party carried. He quarreled constantly with Frank Miller. Preston Nutter later called him a \u201cwhining fraud\u201d at Packer\u2019s first trial. His epileptic seizures made his presence in the group strenuous, and there were rumors that he had spent time in jail in Salt Lake City on a charge of murdering a hunting partner.</p><p>On April 16, 1874, according to the agency account, men at the Los Pi\u00f1os Indian Agency near Saguache, Colorado, were eating breakfast when Packer appeared alone through a snowstorm. He wore rags over his feet instead of shoes, carried a coffee pot on one side, and had a rifle on the other. General Charles Adams, who oversaw the agency, had Alfred given food, and Alfred ate so fast that he threw up. Over whiskey, Alfred said that the others with him had become too exhausted to continue, that he had snow blindness and frostbite, and that he had been left behind to fend for himself. He claimed that he survived for months on rabbits, roots, and rosebuds and escaped only because of his experience as a guide.</p><p>Packer gave more than one account of what had happened, and the sources report that he was known for cannibalism. The Colorado State Archives\u2019 collection includes his confessions, while the scientific team that exhumed the victims did not settle the spelling of his first name. The sources preserve both the story Packer told about surviving on plants and small animals and the broader historical account identifying him as the Colorado Cannibal and associating him with the cannibalism of his companions.</p><p>The Washington report likewise describes Packer as a \u201cscurrilous liar and thief by reputation.\u201d</p><p><span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_4 &middot; SRC_5 &middot; SRC_6 &middot; SRC_7 &middot; SRC_8</span></p></div></section><section><div class=\"sec-head\"><span class=\"sec-num\">SECTION 5</span><h2>Disputed &amp; Uncertain</h2></div><div class=\"disputes\"><div class=\"dispute\"><div><p>The survival story is real in the sources, but the exact meaning of it is still contested.</p><p class=\"holders\">Against: the pushback in the same corpus.</p><details class=\"more\"><summary>Full case, both sides</summary><div class=\"body\"><span class=\"side for\">Case for the claim</span><p>The survival story is present in the sources. Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, then tried every day to escape but could not. He said that, as a result, he lived on the flesh of the dead men for most of the sixty days he was out. The analysis likewise suggested that Packer did shoot Bell in self-defense. This account is supported by the reports that Packer was known for cannibalism, later confessed to living off his companions\u2019 flesh, and gave more than one version of the circumstances. The men had also neared the point of eating the horses themselves. Other reported details\u2014Packer\u2019s possession of several different wallets and his statement that several thousand dollars had been found on Swan and divided among the men\u2014form part of the surrounding story. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10 &middot; SRC_11 &middot; SRC_2</span></p><span class=\"side against\">Case against the claim</span><p>The pushback in the same corpus disputes the interpretation of the survival story, but no specific contrary evidence has been supplied here. The materials provided do not identify what the pushback measured, who advanced it, or which part of Packer\u2019s account it rejects. Accordingly, the opposing case can only maintain that the reported confession, the self-defense account, the references to cannibalism, and the surrounding details do not by themselves settle the exact meaning of the story. On the supplied record, that pushback remains unspecified and cannot be tested against the evidence described for the other side. </p></div></details></div><div class=\"verdict\"><span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div></div><div class=\"dispute\"><div><p>There are real clues in the physical evidence, but the sources do not let it settle the case cleanly.</p><p class=\"holders\">Against: the pushback in the same corpus.</p><details class=\"more\"><summary>Full case, both sides</summary><div class=\"body\"><span class=\"side for\">Case for the claim</span><p>The physical evidence offers genuine clues, even if it does not produce a clean resolution. Five of the men believed Alfred Packer and went with him, while Packer claimed that Shannon Bell then shot the remaining men. Forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group, and the text says it appears likely that Packer killed another prospector in self-defense before gnawing on the bodies of men who had already died. Packer also said that Bell attacked him with a hatchet when Packer returned after searching for food, prompting him to fire twice with a pistol and shoot Bell in self-defense. The analysis suggested that Packer did shoot Bell defensively, and Packer was known for cannibalism. Taken together, these details provide real physical and testimonial clues, though they still leave room for dispute. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_7 &middot; SRC_8</span></p><span class=\"side against\">Case against the claim</span><p>The pushback is that the murder conviction was overturned. That outcome undercuts any confident conclusion that the evidence established the case as presented and leaves the interpretation of the physical clues unsettled. This side&#x27;s case rests on the single reported fact of the overturned conviction, rather than on a fuller alternative reconstruction in the supplied evidence; accordingly, it challenges finality without resolving what happened. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span></p></div></details></div><div class=\"verdict\"><span class=\"chip contested\">contested</span></div></div><div class=\"dispute\"><div><p>He spent years dodging justice before he was caught again.</p><p class=\"holders\">Against: the pushback in the same corpus.</p><details class=\"more\"><summary>Full case, both sides</summary><div class=\"body\"><span class=\"side for\">Case for the claim</span><p>Their accounts say that, once his story was questioned, he escaped, hid from authorities, and remained on the run or \u201con the lam\u201d for eight, nine, or nearly ten years. They further report that he was eventually tracked down in Wyoming, captured nine years later, and returned to Lake City. On this evidence, he did not merely leave custody briefly: he remained free for years before authorities caught him again. <span class=\"src\">SRC_3 &middot; SRC_5</span></p><span class=\"side against\">Case against the claim</span><p>The pushback in the corpus points out only that Packer \u201cremained a free man for nine years after the escape.\u201d That wording establishes a period outside custody, but it does not by itself establish that he was actively \u201cdodging justice,\u201d hiding, or evading an ongoing pursuit. Nor does this counterpoint independently supply details about how he was eventually caught. Thus, on the evidence available to the pushback, the stronger language about deliberate evasion is not demonstrated; its case rests on this single characterization of him as free for nine years. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10</span></p></div></details></div><div class=\"verdict\"><span class=\"chip contested\">contested</span></div></div><div class=\"dispute\"><div><p>Polly Pry and other supporters helped push the sympathy campaign around Packer.</p><p class=\"holders\">Against: the pushback in the same corpus.</p><details class=\"more\"><summary>Full case, both sides</summary><div class=\"body\"><span class=\"side for\">Case for the claim</span><p>He had been sentenced to be hanged, yet he begged for mercy and promised to make a full confession. His story eventually reached Polly Pry, a reporter for the Denver Post, giving the case a public platform through which supporters could press for leniency. The physical evidence\u2014a beaten path from the corpses to Packer\u2019s crude shelter\u2014also supplied material for retelling the episode, while Packer pleaded not guilty and later testified in his own defense at the second trial. On this account, Polly Pry and other supporters helped turn the case from a straightforward hanging sentence into a public sympathy campaign. This case rests on the accounts in SRC_11 and SRC_4, rather than on a broad body of independently testable evidence. <span class=\"src\">SRC_11 &middot; SRC_4</span></p><span class=\"side against\">Case against the claim</span><p>The pushback in the corpus undercuts the idea that Polly Pry and other supporters established a persuasive sympathy campaign. Packer was known for cannibalism, and the five original party members said they did not believe a single part of his story. Only Packer returned in the winter of 1874; of the six men, he alone lived, making his account impossible for the other members to confirm. The jury rejected his story, even though he pleaded not guilty, and the record warns that the story Packer told and what actually occurred are not always the same. Afterward, Packer went on to Saguache. From this perspective, the public narrative was met by serious credibility objections, and the available evidence does not show that the sympathy effort overcame them. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_4 &middot; SRC_5 &middot; SRC_8</span></p></div></details></div><div class=\"verdict\"><span class=\"chip contested\">contested</span></div></div><div class=\"dispute\"><div><p>The honest answer is that the sources still do not agree on whether Packer was murderer, survivor, or both.</p><p class=\"holders\">Against: the pushback in the same corpus.</p><details class=\"more\"><summary>Full case, both sides</summary><div class=\"body\"><span class=\"side for\">Case that the sources still do not agree</span><p>The evidence points in different directions: Alfred\u2019s charges were expected to be upgraded from manslaughter to first-degree murder, yet his murder conviction was overturned. She said that the sentence was so harsh because of the cannibalism, while other evidence indicates that cannibalism was not regarded as the main issue in determining Packer\u2019s guilt. Some forensic work found defensive wounds on bones from the group, and Packer was known for cannibalism. At the same time, a modern myth says that Packer was convicted of cannibalism, even though the murder conviction was overturned. Taken together, these accounts leave open whether Packer should be understood as a murderer, a survivor, or both. <span class=\"src\">SRC_10 &middot; SRC_2 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_7 &middot; SRC_8</span></p><span class=\"side against\">Pushback against the claim</span><p>The corpus identifies pushback against this conclusion, but no specific evidence for that pushback has been supplied here. It therefore cannot be stated more precisely who made the objection, what was measured or reported, or how it establishes that the sources agree on whether Packer was a murderer, a survivor, or both. On the evidence provided, the against case rests only on the existence of that unspecified pushback, rather than on a described source or independently stated finding. </p></div></details></div><div class=\"verdict\"><span class=\"chip solid\">established</span></div></div><div class=\"dispute\"><div><p>Packer provided multiple contradictory accounts regarding the deaths of his companions and how he obtained their belongings and money.</p><p class=\"holders\">Held across: SRC_1.</p><details class=\"more\"><summary>Full case, both sides</summary><div class=\"body\"><span class=\"side for\">Case for the claim</span><p>Packer was known for cannibalism, pleaded not guilty, and was evasive when asked about his missing companions. Under interrogation, he gave the first of multiple contradictory confessions, and he then went on to Saguache. Taken together, this sequence supports the view that Packer changed his account of what happened to his companions and of how he came into possession of their belongings and money. This case rests on a single source, SRC_1. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span></p><span class=\"side against\">Case against the claim</span><p>Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and analysis suggested that he did shoot Bell in self-defense. Packer testified in his own defense at the second trial, and the evidence seemed to corroborate his claim that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer shot Bell in self-defense. Although Packer was known for cannibalism and pleaded not guilty, whether Bell was actually shot in self-defense remains unanswered. Packer was found guilty of Bell&#x27;s murder in 1883, but that verdict does not by itself establish that he gave multiple contradictory accounts about the deaths or about obtaining the belongings and money. <span class=\"src\">SRC_2 &middot; SRC_3 &middot; SRC_5</span></p></div></details></div><div class=\"verdict\"><span class=\"chip contested\">contested</span></div></div><div class=\"dispute\"><div><p>Alfred Packer led five men into the Colorado San Juan Mountains in winter 1874 and emerged as the sole survivor in a well-fed state.</p><p class=\"holders\">Held across: SRC_1.</p><details class=\"more\"><summary>Full case, both sides</summary><div class=\"body\"><span class=\"side for\">Case for the claim</span><p>On February 9, Packer and five other prospectors left the Ute leader Ouray\u2019s winter camp, still facing roughly 120 miles of difficult mountain country before reaching the newly discovered goldfields. The five men went with Packer, and witnesses later testified at trial that he had deliberately led them astray to steal their possessions. Packer ultimately emerged as the only survivor and became known as the \u201cColorado Cannibal\u201d after confessing that he had eaten his dead comrades while trapped in the mountains in February 1874. His reported cannibalism is consistent with the account that he survived the ordeal in a well-fed state. This case rests on the account compiled in the single source SRC_1, including testimony and later descriptions of Packer\u2019s confession. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span></p><span class=\"side against\">Case against the claim</span><p>He was accused of deliberately leading the other five men astray and stealing their possessions, and he was eventually tried and convicted of premeditated murder. His death sentence was later commuted to 40 years in prison, and after winning a retrial he was sentenced to 40 years for five counts of voluntary manslaughter. The source also records that, after his story was questioned, Packer escaped jail and hid from justice for nine years. Although Packer was known for cannibalism, the legal history\u2014convictions variously listed as murder, overturned, and voluntary manslaughter\u2014complicates any straightforward acceptance of the claim that he simply led the party and emerged as its well-fed sole survivor. This case also relies on the single source SRC_1, whose account cannot be independently tested here. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1 &middot; SRC_3</span></p></div></details></div><div class=\"verdict\"><span class=\"chip contested\">contested</span></div></div><div class=\"dispute\"><div><p>Physical and forensic evidence from the site presented conflicting indications regarding whether Packer committed murder or acted in self-defense.</p><p class=\"holders\">Held across: SRC_1.</p><details class=\"more\"><summary>Full case, both sides</summary><div class=\"body\"><span class=\"side for\">Case for the claim</span><p>Packer said that he fired twice with a pistol and shot Shannon Bell in self-defense, and the analysis suggested that he did shoot Bell in self-defense. This appeared to corroborate Packer\u2019s account that Bell had killed the other victims and that Packer\u2019s shooting of Bell was defensive. Packer also pleaded not guilty and testified in his own defense at the second trial. At the same time, Packer was known for cannibalism, and he was found guilty of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883. Thus, although the evidence seemed to support his self-defense account, whether Bell was actually shot in self-defense remained unanswered. <span class=\"src\">SRC_1</span></p><span class=\"side against\">Case against the claim</span><p>No evidence against the claim was supplied. Accordingly, this side cannot identify specific evidence showing that the indications were not conflicting. </p></div></details></div><div class=\"verdict\"><span class=\"chip network\">single source</span></div></div></div></section><section><div class=\"sec-head\"><span class=\"sec-num\">SECTION 6</span><h2>Details &amp; Anecdotes</h2></div><ul class=\"anecdotes\"><li>A commenter says the Lake City museum has items Packer created while in prison. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The museum preserves a tangible connection to Packer\u2019s years behind bars. These objects give visitors something more concrete than the surviving stories and legends.</p></div></details></li><li>A commenter says one prison-made item was a large and beautiful doll house for the warden\u2019s daughter. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The doll house suggests that Packer\u2019s prison work was detailed and skilled rather than merely utilitarian. Its association with the warden\u2019s daughter also points to an unusual degree of trust or goodwill during his incarceration.</p></div></details></li><li>A commenter says Lake City still races coffins on Main Street on the Fourth of July in Packer\u2019s honor. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The race turns Packer\u2019s grim story into a recurring civic spectacle. It shows how the community has transformed the episode into local tradition and entertainment.</p></div></details></li><li>A commenter says Packer seemed to be a model prisoner who made the best of the 16 years he spent incarcerated. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>This view complicates a purely villainous image of Packer by emphasizing his conduct during imprisonment. His long sentence becomes part of a story about adaptation and productivity as well as punishment.</p></div></details></li><li>A commenter says the photo above makes Packer resemble Charles Manson. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The comparison reflects how a photograph can shape the way later audiences imagine a notorious figure. It links Packer visually to another infamous criminal, even though the resemblance is only an impression.</p></div></details></li><li>A commenter says that as a young boy in the 1960s, his father took the family to the site or marker of the dinner event. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The family visit shows that the dinner site or marker had become part of local memory by the 1960s. It also illustrates how the story was passed down through ordinary family outings.</p></div></details></li><li>A commenter quotes his little brother asking why Packer did not just go into town and eat. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The child\u2019s question exposes the story\u2019s basic absurdity in a simple, memorable way. It reduces the elaborate legend to the practical problem of why anyone would choose such desperate circumstances instead of finding food in town.</p></div></details></li><li>A commenter notes that the blog misspelled Packer\u2019s name about half the time. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The repeated misspelling undermines confidence in the blog\u2019s presentation of the story. It also signals that the discussion is dealing with lore and inherited sources whose details were not always carefully checked.</p></div></details></li><li>The author replies that readers can still enjoy Packer burgers despite the correction of the lore. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The reply separates the factual correction from the enduring appeal of the legend. Even after the story is challenged, its name and imagery remain usable in local culture.</p></div></details></li><li>The author replies that Alferd\u2019s name is sometimes spelled Alfred in the blog because quoted sources used that spelling. <span class=\"src\">SRC_5</span><details class=\"more\"><summary>Context</summary><div class=\"body\"><p>The explanation distinguishes the author\u2019s own usage from the spelling found in source material. It shows that the inconsistency comes from preserving quotations rather than from a deliberate change to the historical name.</p></div></details></li></ul></section><section><div class=\"sec-head\"><span class=\"sec-num\">SECTION 7</span><h2>Info Gaps</h2></div><ol class=\"oq\"><li><span class=\"q\">You don&#x27;t have the primary trial transcripts or official court testimony from Packer&#x27;s 1883 murder trial. Without them, you can&#x27;t show viewers what was actually said under oath versus what modern sources claim happened.</span> A major historical murder trial that went to the state supreme court would leave behind formal court records and contemporary sworn testimony. <span class=\"go\">&rarr; Query the Colorado State Archives digital collections specifically for the 1883 Lake City trial transcripts and court filings.</span></li><li><span class=\"q\">Your sources mention that Chief Ouray and Indigenous leaders warned the expedition, but you don&#x27;t have direct contemporary accounts or recorded statements from the Ute Tribe members who interacted with the party.</span> Chief Ouray&#x27;s encampment was the last known stop before the group entered the mountains. First-hand perspectives from the Ute witnesses who saw the group&#x27;s physical condition and equipment are essential for a complete timeline. <span class=\"go\">&rarr; Search for historical records from the Los Pinos Indian Agency or regional 1874 news reports detailing Chief Ouray&#x27;s account of the expedition.</span></li><li><span class=\"q\">You have a nine-year gap between Packer escaping jail in 1874 and his recapture in Wyoming in 1883. Your sources don&#x27;t detail how he successfully evaded authorities for nearly a decade under the alias John Swartze.</span> A long-term fugitive evasion is a major narrative arc in a crime story. A viewer would expect to know how he hid, where he worked, and how he was eventually exposed. <span class=\"go\">&rarr; Research 1883 Wyoming newspaper accounts detailing Packer&#x27;s arrest in Cheyenne and his life as &#x27;John Swartze&#x27;.</span></li><li><span class=\"q\">Your sources note that Packer was paroled in 1901 and died in 1907, but you have no details about his daily life, where he lived, or his interactions with the public during those final six years.</span> When telling the full biography of a notorious figure who gained public sympathy and parole, you need to show the resolution of their life after release. <span class=\"go\">&rarr; Look into local Littleton or Denver census records and local obituary archives from 1907 covering Packer&#x27;s final years in Deer Creek.</span></li></ol></section><section><div class=\"sec-head\"><span class=\"sec-num\">SECTION 8</span><h2>Source Trail</h2></div><div class=\"trail\"><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_1</span><span class=\"what\"><b>His Horrifying Ordeal in Colorado Mountains</b> (youtube, 2023-11-19T00:00:00.000Z, Scary Interesting) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely describes Packer bursting into the Los Pi\u00f1os mess hall during a snowstorm in foot rags, carrying a coffee pot and rifle, and vomiting after eating too quickly.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_2</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Alferd Packer</b> (article, 2009-02-08, Author Gulliford; Andrew) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely reports Packer\u2019s claim that the starving party cooked and ate their rawhide moccasins after three or four days without provisions.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_3</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Alfred Packer - Wikipedia</b> (article, 2003-08-05, Authority control databases) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely notes that Packer\u2019s convictions included murder convictions later overturned and five counts of voluntary manslaughter, with his death sentence commuted to 40 years.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_4</span><span class=\"what\"><b>The Colorado Cannibal: The Story of Alferd Packer - Law Week Colorado</b> (article, 2022-02-09, Jess Brovsky-Eaker) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely records that Packer gave three confessions\u2014in 1874, 1883, and 1897\u2014and that his first version said Shannon Bell killed \u201cCalifornia\u201d before Packer killed Bell in self-defense.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_5</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Alferd Packer: The Truth is Out There (Or Right Here)</b> (article, 2015-10-21, history.denverlibrary.org) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely reports that Packer escaped the Saguache jail, remained at large for eight years, and was convicted of Bell\u2019s murder in 1883.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_6</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Alfred Packer</b> (article, 2026-01-01, archives.colorado.gov) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely identifies the Colorado State Archives\u2019 online Alfred Packer Collection, which includes penitentiary, parole, court, confession, gubernatorial-pardon, and mugshot records.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_7</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Alfred Packer ate &#x27;em, forensic expert says - UPI Archives</b> (article, 2026-08-29, KAREN TIMMONS) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely reports forensic professor James Starrs\u2019s claim that exhumed remains proved Packer butchered and skinned all five companions.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_8</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Was the \u2018Colorado Cannibal\u2019 a villain or a victim? You decide.</b> (article, 2023-03-28, Brendan Bures; Benjamin Rasmussen) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely states that Packer suffered epilepsy, was discharged from the Union Army because of seizures, and that an exhumed skull had a hole in its forehead.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_9</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Polly Pry - Wikipedia</b> (article, 2007-01-08, Authority control databases International VIAF National United States France BnF data Other Yale LUX) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely identifies Polly Pry as Leonel Campbell Ross O\u2019Bryan, the Denver Post\u2019s first female reporter, whose career connected her to the Packer case.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_10</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Alferd Packer</b> (article, 2025-05-05, Town of Lake City) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely details the 21-man expedition\u2019s division at the Ute encampment into three factions after Chief Ouray warned them about the winter conditions.</span></span></div><div class=\"trail-row\"><span class=\"sid\">SRC_11</span><span class=\"what\"><b>Cannibal Correspondence - True West Magazine</b> (article, 2022-09-06, Kellen Cutsforth) <span class=\"contrib\">&mdash; It uniquely describes Packer receiving romantic fan mail from adoring women while incarcerated, framing this as an early example of what is now called hybristophilia.</span></span></div></div></section><footer>Briefing generated from job 0f7e0818-5def-49dd-bec9-a16b1b534979 &middot; every claim traces to raw source text via the IDs shown &middot; Section 1 is written for reading; Sections 2&ndash;8 are the reference layer</footer></div>\